window.PhoenixSiteSearchIndex={"generated_at_utc":"2026-08-13T16:21:58.350478Z","count":91,"pages":[{"title":"UFO Noises","url":"/ufo-noises/","description":"Reports of strange \"UFO noises\" have circulated for decades, often describing deep humming, metallic droning, booming explosions, or eerie trumpet-like...","breadcrumb":"","level":1,"text":"UFO Noises UFO Noises UFO Noises UFO Noises Reports of strange \"UFO noises\" have circulated for decades, often describing deep humming, metallic droning, booming explosions, or eerie trumpet like... Reports of strange \"UFO noises\" have circulated for decades, often describing deep humming, metallic droning, booming explosions, or eerie trumpet like sounds that appear to come from the sky. ufo noises What people mean by \"UFO noises\" Descriptions of alleged UFO noises vary considerably. Witnesses commonly report: A low frequency hum that seems to come from above. Loud explosive booms without visible aircraft or storms. Metallic grinding or scraping sounds. Long, horn like or trumpet like tones. Pulsing vibrations that can sometimes be felt indoors. The diversity of these reports is important. Unlike well defined natural phenomena such as thunder or sonic booms, there is no single acoustic signature consistently associated with UFO sightings. The sounds differ in duration, pitch, volume, weather conditions and accompanying visual observations. Many widely shared internet videos labelled as \"UFO sounds\" actually contain recordings with little verifiable information about where or when they were captured, making independent analysis difficult. Why these sounds are often called skyquakes A phenomenon known as a skyquake provides the closest documented parallel to many reports of mysterious aerial noises. Skyquakes are loud sounds that appear to originate from the sky, often resembling cannon fire, distant explosions or massive brass instruments. They have been reported around the world for more than two centuries. [sciencefocus.com] sciencefocus.com Science FocusMystery ‘skyquakes’ are ripping through the world. And nobody knows why BBC Science Focus MagazineApril 12, 2026... Published: April 12, 2026 What scientists think may cause them No single explanation accounts for every skyquake. Researchers have proposed multiple mechanisms, including: Sonic booms from aircraft or meteors. Atmospheric temperature layers bending sound over long distances. Small earthquakes transmitting sound unusually efficiently. Offshore wave activity. Underground gas releases. Quarry blasting or military exercises. Rare combinations of weather and terrain that amplify distant sounds. Different cases likely have different causes. Rather than representing one unexplained phenomenon, \"skyquakes\" may be a label covering several unrelated processes. [Science Focus] sciencefocus.com Science FocusMystery ‘skyquakes’ are ripping through the world. And nobody knows why BBC Science Focus MagazineApril 12, 2026... Published: April 12, 2026 51:24 Breaking Down UAP Footage with the Head of The Pentagon’s UAP Taskforce, Dr. Jon Kosloski Channel: StarTalk · Views: 1.5M · Uploaded: August 2025 · Length: 51 minutes Open on YouTube Are UFO sightings commonly accompanied by unusual sounds? Surprisingly, many famous UFO reports involve little or no reported sound. Witnesses often describe objects moving silently despite apparently high speeds. This silence has itself become part of UFO folklore because conventional aircraft normally produce significant engine noise. When sounds are reported alongside unidentified objects, they are inconsistent. Accounts include: Buzzing. Electrical crackling. Low frequency humming. Whistling. Mechanical vibration. Because these reports rely primarily on eyewitness testimony rather than calibrated acoustic measurements, they cannot establish a consistent physical pattern. NASA's independent UAP study concluded that the available evidence is too limited and inconsistent to support firm conclusions about the nature of unidentified aerial phenomena, including any characteristic sounds. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 Famous cases involving mysterious noises Several well known incidents are repeatedly cited in discussions of UFO noises, although none has produced conclusive evidence of an extraterrestrial origin. The \"sky trumpet\" recordings Beginning around the early 2010s, videos appeared online featuring loud metallic horn like sounds echoing through urban environments. Many accumulated millions of views and were presented as evidence of UFO activity. Subsequent investigations found numerous problems: Original recording locations were often uncertain. Several videos contained obvious audio editing. Similar sound effects were traced to commercial audio libraries in some cases. Independent verification was usually impossible. While unusual sounds have certainly been recorded in some locations, viral compilations frequently combine authentic recordings with fabricated material. 59:22 Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Independent Study Report Channel: NASA · Views: 107.4K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Persistent humming reports Some communities report ongoing low frequency hums, sometimes called simply \"the Hum.\" These reports have occasionally become associated with UFO speculation. However, investigations have suggested many possible explanations, including industrial equipment, electrical infrastructure, distant traffic, environmental acoustics and, in some individuals, auditory perception differences. No consistent link to unidentified aerial phenomena has been demonstrated. Why mysterious sounds are difficult to investigate Acoustic investigations face several challenges. First, sound travels differently depending on weather, temperature, humidity and wind. Under certain atmospheric conditions, noises from factories, ships or distant highways can travel tens of kilometres while appearing to originate overhead. Second, humans are poor at locating low frequency sounds. Bass frequencies provide relatively little directional information, making it easy to misjudge where a sound originated. Finally, many reports occur unexpectedly and last only a few seconds, leaving investigators without high quality recordings or multiple independent measurements. These limitations mean that even genuine unexplained noises often remain unresolved without implying an extraordinary cause. What scientific investigations of UAP say Recent scientific reviews have approached unidentified aerial phenomena cautiously rather than dismissively. NASA's independent UAP study emphasised that the principal obstacle is not a lack of theories but a lack of high quality data. The agency found no evidence that existing reports indicate extraterrestrial technology, while acknowledging that some observations remain unidentified because available information is insufficient. The recommended approach is better sensors, improved reporting standards and rigorous data analysis rather than speculation. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 Similarly, recent releases of U.S. government UAP files include reports of unusual lights, radar contacts and witness statements, but they do not establish mysterious sounds as a recurring or defining feature of these incidents. Most investigated cases ultimately receive conventional explanations or remain unresolved because the evidence is incomplete rather than because it points to alien technology. [AP News] apnews.com Spurred by former President Donald Trump's push for transparency, the files include declassified State Department cables, FBI interviews... Common misconceptions Several popular beliefs deserve clarification. \"All mysterious sky sounds are UFOs.\" There is no evidence supporting this claim. Most investigated cases have ordinary or plausible natural explanations. \"Trumpet sounds only started recently.\" Reports of unexplained booming or horn like noises date back at least to the nineteenth century, long before modern social media. [discoveryuk.com] discoveryuk.com Skyquake: The Mysterious Sounds from the Sky Discovery UK... \"Scientists ignore these reports.\" Atmospheric scientists, geologists, acousticians and government agencies have investigated unexplained sounds and UAP reports, although many individual events remain unresolved due to limited evidence rather than lack of interest. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 4:00:26 Public Meeting on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (Official NASA Broadcast) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 358.7K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 4 hours Open on YouTube The current state of the evidence The strongest evidence indicates that mysterious aerial noises are a real observational phenomenon in the sense that people genuinely hear unusual sounds. However, the available data do not support the conclusion that these noises are characteristic of UFOs or extraterrestrial vehicles. Instead, the evidence points to a mixture of identifiable human activity, unusual atmospheric sound propagation, geological processes, occasional"},{"title":"Aircraft Noise","url":"/aircraft-noise/","description":"Jets, helicopters, and military aircraft can create whining or roaring sounds that arrive late, shift direction, or seem detached from visible lights.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises","level":2,"text":"Aircraft Noise When Aircraft Noise Sounds Like a UFO UFO Noises Aircraft Noise Aircraft Noise When Aircraft Noise Sounds Like a UFO Jets, helicopters, and military aircraft can create whining or roaring sounds that arrive late, shift direction, or seem detached from visible lights. Jets, helicopters, and military aircraft can create whining or roaring sounds that arrive late, shift direction, or seem detached from visible lights. entity Jet whine, rotor noise, and engine roar Delayed and displaced sound Military flights and unfamiliar silhouettes ufo noises aircraft noise Introduction Many reports of mysterious overhead noises that are initially associated with UFOs are ultimately traced to ordinary aircraft. Jet airliners, helicopters and military aircraft can produce whining, roaring or pulsating sounds that seem disconnected from what observers see in the sky. At night or in poor visibility, an aircraft may be difficult to identify visually, while its sound can arrive from an unexpected direction or several seconds after the aircraft has already passed overhead. These perfectly ordinary acoustic effects can make familiar aircraft seem unusually mysterious, even to experienced observers. Understanding how aircraft generate and transmit sound helps explain why a distant jet may resemble a strange humming object, why a helicopter can appear to roar without being immediately visible, and why witnesses sometimes report a loud \"UFO\" before locating an aircraft. Why aircraft often sound stranger than they look Aircraft are moving sound sources rather than stationary ones. Their engines, rotors and airframes emit noise continuously while travelling at high speed through an atmosphere that bends, reflects and absorbs sound in complex ways. Several factors combine to create unusual impressions: Sound travels far more slowly than aircraft. A jet flying hundreds of kilometres per hour can move a considerable distance while its earlier sound is still travelling towards an observer. Wind and temperature layers can refract, or bend, sound waves, making noise appear to come from an unexpected location. Terrain, buildings and cloud layers may reflect or scatter sound, further disguising its true source. At night, the lack of visual reference points makes it much harder to judge an aircraft's distance, altitude and direction. These effects are well understood in aviation acoustics and are among the reasons that aircraft noise studies consider not only the aircraft itself but also weather, flight path and local geography. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation Administration Aircraft Noise Federal Aviation Administration Federal Aviation AdministrationAircraft Noise Federal Aviation AdministrationJanuary 31, 2025... Published: January 31, 2025 Jet whine, rotor noise and engine roar Different aircraft produce distinct sound signatures , but those signatures often overlap enough to confuse listeners. Jet engine whine Modern turbofan engines generate a characteristic high pitched whine, particularly during climb or when engine power changes. As the aircraft approaches, the pitch appears higher because of the Doppler effect. Once it passes, the pitch drops noticeably before gradually fading into the distance. To someone who cannot see the aircraft, this changing pitch may sound as though an unidentified object accelerated, slowed or changed direction, even though the aircraft maintained a steady course. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Open source on faa.gov. Helicopter rotor noise Helicopters produce a more complicated acoustic signature than fixed wing aircraft because several components contribute simultaneously: the main rotor, the tail rotor, the engine, interactions between rotor blades and disturbed air. One of the best known sounds is the rhythmic \" blade slap \", a sharp repetitive beat created by changing pressure around the rotor blades. Depending on viewing angle and weather, this may resemble anything from a deep thumping noise to a continuous mechanical roar. Studies of helicopter acoustics show that the loudest sound does not always originate from the engine itself. Rotor systems frequently dominate what listeners hear, especially when the aircraft passes nearly overhead. [NTRL] ntrl.ntis.gov Open source on ntis.gov. The sound barrier, shockwaves, sonic booms and the shock wave cone. Channel: Zak's Lab Open on YouTube Engine roar Large transport aircraft generate broad band low frequency noise that many witnesses describe simply as a roar. During take off, climb or military training exercises, this roar may be audible long before the aircraft becomes visible, particularly under cloud cover. Because lower frequencies travel efficiently over long distances, listeners may first notice a distant rumble without immediately identifying its source. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Open source on faa.gov. Why the sound arrives late or seems to come from elsewhere One of the most common reasons people associate aircraft with unidentified aerial phenomena is that sound and sight rarely arrive together. Light reaches an observer almost instantly, whereas sound travels through air at roughly 343 metres per second under typical conditions. A high altitude aircraft therefore appears overhead before its engine noise reaches the ground. As the aircraft continues moving, the sound arriving at the observer was actually produced when the aircraft occupied an earlier position. The result is that: a visible aircraft may seem almost silent initially; loud engine noise can suddenly appear after the aircraft has passed; [faa.gov] faa.gov Aircraft Noise Levels & Stages Federal Aviation Administration Aircraft Noise Levels & Stages Federal Aviation Administration the apparent source of the sound may trail well behind the visible aircraft; listeners may mistakenly search the wrong part of the sky. Wind direction can exaggerate these effects by carrying sound away from or towards observers, while temperature inversions may bend sound back towards the ground, making distant aircraft seem surprisingly close. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Open source on faa.gov. Why military aircraft are especially prone to UFO reports Military aircraft account for a disproportionate number of mistaken UFO reports because they often behave differently from routine commercial traffic. Characteristics that contribute include: rapid climbs after take off; high power engine settings; unusual flight paths; formation flying; operations at dusk or night; unfamiliar silhouettes; aircraft types rarely seen by the public. Training flights may also involve repeated turns, holding patterns or variable engine power, producing rapidly changing noise that appears inconsistent with a single aircraft. In addition, military aircraft may operate in areas where observers are unfamiliar with their normal acoustic signatures. A loud, fast moving roar from an unseen aircraft can therefore appear far more mysterious than the steady sound of a scheduled passenger flight. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation Administration Aircraft Noise Federal Aviation Administration Federal Aviation AdministrationAircraft Noise Federal Aviation AdministrationJanuary 31, 2025... Published: January 31, 2025 Breaking The Sound Barrier: Can Pilots Hear Sonic Booms? Channel: ScienceABC II Open on YouTube Why aircraft can seem invisible A witness may hear an unmistakable aircraft like sound without locating any aircraft at all. Several ordinary explanations are common: Cloud cover: Thick cloud can completely obscure an aircraft while transmitting its sound efficiently. Night time observation: Navigation lights may not be visible from every angle or may be mistaken for stars. High altitude: Aircraft at cruising altitude can be difficult to detect visually even when their engines remain audible. Sun position: Bright sunlight can hide aircraft against the sky despite strong engine noise. Viewing direction: A listener searching where the sound appears to originate may look away from the aircraft's true location because sound has been displaced by atmospheric conditions. These circumstances can leave witnesses convinced that a loud overhead object produced no visible aircraft. Practical clues that the source is an aircraft When investigating an unusual whining or roaring sound, several observations favour an aircraft explanation: The pitch rises before overhead passage and falls afterwards, indicating the Doppler effect. The sound gradually fades rather than stopping abruptly. A visible light or aircraft appears shortly before or after the loudest noise. [faa.gov] faa.gov Aircraft Noise Levels & Stages Federal Aviation Administration Aircraft Noise Levels & Stages Federal Aviation Administration Similar sounds occur repeatedly along the same flight corridor. The event coincides with known airport arrival or departure routes, or military training activity. None"},{"title":"Delayed Noise","url":"/delayed-noise/","description":"Because sound travels slowly, the loudest engine noise may reach the ground seconds after the aircraft has already moved on.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Aircraft Noise","level":3,"text":"Delayed Noise Why the Roar Comes After the Aircraft Passes Aircraft Noise Delayed Noise Delayed Noise Why the Roar Comes After the Aircraft Passes Because sound travels slowly, the loudest engine noise may reach the ground seconds after the aircraft has already moved on. Because sound travels slowly, the loudest engine noise may reach the ground seconds after the aircraft has already moved on. mechanism How far sound lags behind a moving aircraft Why the apparent source trails the visible plane Simple timing checks for witness reports ufo noises aircraft noise delayed noise Introduction One of the most common reasons an ordinary aircraft is mistaken for something unusual is that the loudest noise often arrives after the aircraft has already passed overhead. To an observer, this can feel counter intuitive: the aircraft is visibly moving away, yet the roar appears to come from where it was a few seconds earlier. In UFO witness reports, this timing mismatch sometimes creates the impression that the sound belongs to a different object or that an unidentified craft has suddenly disappeared. The explanation is straightforward. Light reaches your eyes almost instantly, while sound travels through air at only about 343 metres per second under typical conditions. A fast moving aircraft covers a significant distance while the sound it produced earlier is still travelling towards the ground. The result is a natural delay between what you see and what you hear, sometimes amplified by the aircraft's speed, altitude and the atmosphere. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation AdministrationFundamentals of Noise and Sound Federal Aviation AdministrationMarch 29, 2022... Published: March 29, 2022 How Far Sound Lags Behind a Moving Aircraft The key point is that every sound you hear was generated in the past. Suppose an airliner is flying at around 250 metres per second (roughly 900 km/h) at an altitude of 3,000 metres. Even if it flies directly overhead, the sound produced when it was nearest to you requires approximately 8–9 seconds to travel down to ground level. During that time, the aircraft has travelled more than 2 kilometres along its flight path. This means that: you see the aircraft overhead almost immediately because light reaches you effectively instantaneously; several seconds later, you hear the loudest engine noise; by then, the aircraft is already well ahead of the point where that sound originated. The delay varies continuously with altitude and geometry. A low aircraft on approach may produce only a brief lag, while a high altitude jet can seem almost disconnected from its sound. FAA guidance on moving noise sources notes that aircraft sound levels naturally rise as the aircraft approaches and decrease after it passes, but the observer is always hearing sound emitted earlier because of the finite speed of sound. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation AdministrationFundamentals of Noise and Sound Federal Aviation AdministrationMarch 29, 2022... Published: March 29, 2022 Breaking The Sound Barrier: Can Pilots Hear Sonic Booms? Channel: ScienceABC II Open on YouTube Why the Apparent Source Trails the Visible Plane People often assume they are hearing sound from the aircraft's current position. In reality, they are hearing sound emitted when the aircraft occupied an earlier point along its route. Imagine taking a series of snapshots every second. Each snapshot shows the aircraft somewhere new, but each also launches expanding sound waves that spread outward in all directions. By the time one of those expanding wavefronts reaches a listener on the ground, the aircraft has already moved on. This produces two common perceptions: The roar seems to come from behind the aircraft. Your ears naturally associate the arriving sound with the location where it originated rather than where the aircraft is now. The aircraft appears unusually quiet overhead. The strongest engine noise may not coincide with the instant the aircraft is directly above you because that sound has not yet reached your ears. NASA's aircraft noise modelling explicitly accounts for aircraft motion, Doppler effects and atmospheric sound propagation because all three influence what listeners actually perceive during a flyover. [NASA] nasa.gov New Acoustics Techniques Clear Path for Quieter Aviation New Acoustics Techniques Clear Path for Quieter Aviation NASA... Why the Delay Can Seem Even Longer The basic travel time delay explains most cases, but several factors can increase the apparent separation between sight and sound. Greater altitude. The higher the aircraft, the farther the sound must travel before reaching the ground. Aircraft speed. Faster aircraft cover more distance during the sound's travel time, increasing the apparent gap between the visible aircraft and the arriving noise. Wind. Wind can speed up or slow down sound travelling towards a listener. A headwind or tailwind changes the effective travel time, making the delay seem slightly shorter or longer depending on conditions. Temperature layering. Differences in air temperature with height can bend sound waves (a process called refraction), causing the loudest sound to arrive from an unexpected direction or making certain parts of the flight path seem louder than others. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation AdministrationFundamentals of Noise and Sound Federal Aviation AdministrationMarch 29, 2022... Published: March 29, 2022 High Speed Flight : Part 1 Approaching the Speed of Sound Channel: National Aerospace Library Open on YouTube Why This Can Confuse UFO Witnesses Within reports of unexplained aerial sounds, delayed aircraft noise is a recurring source of confusion because human perception naturally links simultaneous events. A witness may: see an aircraft already moving away; hear a sudden roar overhead; conclude the visible aircraft cannot be responsible because it appears too far away. Without considering sound travel time, it is easy to assume the noise belongs to another unseen object. At night or under cloud cover, where visual references are limited, this disconnect becomes even more convincing. The effect is particularly noticeable with large transport aircraft and military jets, whose powerful engines remain audible over long distances. Because these aircraft continue moving rapidly while earlier sound propagates through the atmosphere, the visible aircraft and the audible source can appear strikingly separated. Simple Timing Checks for Witness Reports When evaluating reports of mysterious overhead sounds, a few practical observations can help determine whether delayed aircraft noise is a likely explanation. Estimate the aircraft's altitude. A high aircraft naturally produces a longer delay than a low one. Notice whether the loudest roar follows the flyover. A delay of several seconds is entirely consistent with ordinary aircraft acoustics. Watch the aircraft after it passes. If it continues steadily away while the noise reaches its peak, the timing matches expected sound propagation. Consider weather conditions. Wind and temperature inversions can make the sound seem displaced or unusually loud without requiring an unusual source. These simple checks cannot identify every aircraft, but they often explain why an apparently mysterious roaring sound seems disconnected from the visible aeroplane. In many cases, the \"late arriving\" noise is simply the unavoidable consequence of sound travelling much more slowly than light, combined with an aircraft moving rapidly across the sky. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation AdministrationFundamentals of Noise and Sound Federal Aviation AdministrationMarch 29, 2022... Published: March 29, 2022 Speed of Sound in a Medium & Doppler Effect Explained Waves, Motion & Worked Examples Channel: YouScience Lab Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why the Roar Comes After the Aircraft Passes. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Master Handbook of Acoustics By F. Alton Everest The goal of this book is to apply the principles of acoustics to the audio arts. This involves serving as an interpreter of major trends... See on Amazon Book The Science of Sound By Thomas D. Rossing, Paul A. Wheeler et al. Written for students without a background in mathematics or physics, this textbook provides an introduction to the study of acoustics. It... See on Amazon Book Understanding Flight By David W. Anderson, Scott Eberhardt The simplest, most intuitive book on the toughest lessons of flight addresses the science of flying in terms, explanations, and illustra... See on Amazon Book Why You Hear What You Hear By Eric J. Heller Why You Hear What You Hear is the first book on the physics of sound for the nonspecialist to empower readers with a hands on, ears open... See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: Master of"},{"title":"Blade Slap","url":"/blade-slap/","description":"Helicopter rotors can create pulsing thumps or a continuous roar that remains hard to place when the aircraft is hidden.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Aircraft Noise","level":3,"text":"Blade Slap When Helicopter Rotors Sound Like an Unseen Craft Aircraft Noise Blade Slap Blade Slap When Helicopter Rotors Sound Like an Unseen Craft Helicopter rotors can create pulsing thumps or a continuous roar that remains hard to place when the aircraft is hidden. Helicopter rotors can create pulsing thumps or a continuous roar that remains hard to place when the aircraft is hidden. mechanism What creates helicopter blade slap Why the sound changes with angle and weather How to distinguish rotor noise from jet noise ufo noises aircraft noise blade slap Introduction Among ordinary aircraft sounds that are sometimes mistaken for mysterious “ UFO noises ”, helicopter blade slap is one of the most distinctive. A helicopter hidden behind hills, trees or cloud can produce a deep pulsing thump or broad mechanical roar that seems detached from any visible aircraft. The effect is not produced simply by the engine. Instead, it arises primarily from complex aerodynamic interactions between the rotating blades and the swirling air they leave behind. Under the right flight conditions, these interactions generate short, powerful pressure pulses that can travel surprising distances and create the impression of an unseen object moving overhead. Decades of rotorcraft research have identified this mechanism as blade–vortex interaction (BVI), a well studied source of helicopter noise rather than an unexplained phenomenon. [rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov] rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov In Flight Rotorcraft Acoustics Program Introduction... What creates helicopter blade slap? The familiar \"chop chop\" sound of a helicopter comes from several overlapping sources, including the main rotor, tail rotor, engine and gearbox. Blade slap is a specific component of that sound. It occurs when one rotor blade passes through the turbulent tip vortex shed by the blade ahead of it. The encounter causes a rapid change in aerodynamic loading, producing a sharp pulse of sound that radiates away from the rotor. [rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov] rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov In Flight Rotorcraft Acoustics Program Introduction... Researchers have shown that the strength of the sound depends on factors such as: the shape and strength of the trailing vortex, the distance between the blade and the vortex, blade speed and loading, the helicopter's forward speed and rate of descent. Even small changes in these conditions can make blade slap much louder or almost disappear. This explains why the same helicopter may sound completely different from one flight to the next. [NASA Technical Reports Server] ntrs.nasa.gov NASA Technical Reports ServerThe effect of tip vortex structure on helicopter noise due to blade/vortex interaction NASA Technical Repo... NASA and other rotorcraft researchers have spent decades studying blade–vortex interaction because it is one of the principal external noise sources affecting communities near helicopter routes and landing sites. [rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov] rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov In Flight Rotorcraft Acoustics Program Introduction... Decoding Blade Vortex Interaction (BVI): The Science Behind Helicopter Noise Channel: News & Books Open on YouTube Why the sound changes with viewing angle and weather One reason blade slap can seem mysterious is that listeners rarely hear the same sound from every direction. Directional radiation Blade slap is highly directional. The strongest pulses are projected into particular regions around the helicopter rather than equally in every direction. Someone beneath the flight path may hear sharp rhythmic impacts, while another observer a short distance away hears only a subdued rotor beat. NASA flight test programmes were specifically designed to measure how blade vortex interaction changes with observer position because the sound field varies so dramatically. [rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov] rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov In Flight Rotorcraft Acoustics Program Introduction... This directional behaviour explains why witnesses often disagree about how loud or unusual a helicopter sounded even when describing the same aircraft. Flight condition matters Blade slap becomes especially noticeable during certain manoeuvres, particularly forward flight with moderate descent, such as approaches for landing. Under these conditions the rotor blades are more likely to intersect the vortices produced by preceding blades, strengthening the impulsive noise. Hovering helicopters generally produce a different acoustic character because the geometry of the rotor wake changes. [rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov] rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov In Flight Rotorcraft Acoustics Program Introduction... Atmospheric effects Weather also alters how the sound is perceived. Wind can carry the louder part of the acoustic pattern towards or away from an observer, while temperature gradients can refract sound so that it appears to originate from an unexpected direction. Moist or cloudy conditions may further reduce visual cues, leaving listeners with a loud, apparently disembodied roar before they identify the helicopter itself. These effects are consistent with established principles of outdoor acoustics and aircraft noise propagation. [rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov] rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov In Flight Rotorcraft Acoustics Program Introduction... Why Are Helicopters So Loud? + more videos aumsum kids science education whatif Channel: It's AumSum Time Open on YouTube Why blade slap can resemble an unidentified roaring object People often expect helicopters to produce a smooth, continuous whir. In reality, blade slap consists of repeated pressure pulses that merge together at distance. Close to the helicopter, the sound is usually recognisable as rhythmic chopping. Farther away, atmospheric absorption removes more of the higher frequencies than the lower ones. The remaining low frequency energy blends into a heavy rumble or mechanical roar. Reflections from hillsides, buildings or woodland can further smear the individual pulses, making the source difficult to locate by ear. Because the helicopter may be hidden behind terrain or cloud, the listener may first encounter only the sound. Without visual confirmation, the combination of delayed arrival, shifting direction and deep pulsing can easily be interpreted as an unfamiliar airborne object rather than a conventional rotorcraft. [NASA Technical Reports Server] ntrs.nasa.gov NASA Technical Reports ServerThe effect of tip vortex structure on helicopter noise due to blade/vortex interaction NASA Technical Repo... How to distinguish rotor noise from jet noise Although helicopters and jets can both produce loud roaring sounds, several features help separate them. CharacteristicHelicopter blade slapJet aircraftRhythmRepeating pulses or chopping that may merge into a roarMostly continuous broadband roar with changing pitchMain sourceRotor blades interacting with vorticesTurbofan or turbojet exhaust and fan noiseStrongest duringCertain forward flight and descent conditionsHigh engine power, especially take off and climbPitch behaviourRepetitive beat often remains audiblePronounced Doppler pitch shift before and after passing A helicopter's sound also tends to fluctuate more noticeably as the aircraft turns or changes its flight path because the directional radiation pattern of blade slap swings relative to the listener. Jets generally produce a steadier noise field dominated by engine thrust rather than discrete aerodynamic pulses. [rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov] rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov In Flight Rotorcraft Acoustics Program Introduction... Can We Really Have Stealth Helicopters When They’re So Loud? Channel: Seeker Open on YouTube Why this matters in reports of unusual aerial sounds Many reports of unexplained aerial noises begin with descriptions such as \"a deep roar\", \"heavy mechanical thumping\", or \"something huge passing overhead without being seen\". Blade slap provides a well supported explanation for a significant subset of these accounts when helicopters are operating nearby but remain concealed by darkness, weather or terrain. This does not mean every unexplained sound is caused by a helicopter. However, rotorcraft acoustics are sufficiently complex that even experienced observers can misjudge the source, distance or direction of the noise. Controlled experiments, flight testing and decades of aerodynamic modelling consistently show that blade–vortex interaction can generate unusually intense, directional and rapidly changing sounds that match many descriptions of an unseen roaring aircraft. [nasa.gov] rotorcraft.arc.nasa.gov In Flight Rotorcraft Acoustics Program Introduction... Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to When Helicopter Rotors Sound Like an Unseen Craft. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Principles of Helicopter Aerodynamics By J. Gordon Leishman An internationally recognized expert explains the aerodynamic principles of helicopters and other rotating wing vertical lift aircraft."},{"title":"Jet Whine","url":"/jet-whine/","description":"Doppler shifts can make a steady jet sound as though it accelerates, slows or changes direction across the sky.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Aircraft Noise","level":3,"text":"Jet Whine Why a Steady Jet Sounds Like It Accelerates Aircraft Noise Jet Whine Jet Whine Why a Steady Jet Sounds Like It Accelerates Doppler shifts can make a steady jet sound as though it accelerates, slows or changes direction across the sky. Doppler shifts can make a steady jet sound as though it accelerates, slows or changes direction across the sky. mechanism How approaching and departing pitch changes work Why listeners misread pitch as motion Clues that identify a normal jet pass ufo noises aircraft noise jet whine Introduction A steady jet does not normally speed up, slow down or change direction as dramatically as it may sound from the ground. One of the main reasons people mistake an ordinary aircraft for something unusual is that the engine's pitch changes continuously during a fly past. Human hearing naturally associates a rising pitch with increasing speed and a falling pitch with deceleration, even when the aircraft is maintaining a nearly constant course and airspeed. The combination of the Doppler effect, the directionality of jet engine noise and the delay between light and sound can create the convincing illusion that an object is accelerating across the sky or making unexpected manoeuvres. [NASA GRC] grc.nasa.gov GRCDoppler Effect NASA GRCDoppler EffectMay 13, 2021... Published: May 13, 2021 How approaching and departing pitch changes work The primary mechanism is the Doppler effect. A jet engine emits sound at a nearly steady frequency, but if the aircraft is moving towards an observer, each new sound wave is emitted from a point slightly closer than the previous one. The waves become compressed, increasing the apparent frequency and making the engine sound higher pitched. After the aircraft passes, the opposite happens: the waves are stretched farther apart, lowering the apparent frequency and producing the familiar drop in pitch. The engine itself has not suddenly changed note; only the sound reaching the listener has changed. [NASA GRC] grc.nasa.gov GRCDoppler Effect NASA GRCDoppler EffectMay 13, 2021... Published: May 13, 2021 The effect is strongest when the aircraft passes closest to the observer. Instead of hearing a gradual transition, many people perceive a sudden \"gear change\" in the sound. That abrupt shift often coincides with the moment when the aircraft crosses overhead, making it seem as though it has accelerated away or altered course, even if its flight path is perfectly straight. [NASA GRC] grc.nasa.gov GRCDoppler Effect NASA GRCDoppler EffectMay 13, 2021... Published: May 13, 2021 The Doppler Effect Explained: Why Sound Changes Pitch Channel: eduvids Open on YouTube Why listeners mistake pitch for motion People rarely judge an aircraft's speed directly from sound alone. Instead, the brain combines several cues, including pitch, loudness and timing, to estimate movement. A rising engine whine is commonly interpreted as an engine increasing power because that is what people experience with cars, motorcycles and other familiar machines. When the pitch suddenly drops, listeners often interpret it as braking, climbing steeply or turning away. In reality, a constant speed airliner flying overhead can produce exactly the same sequence simply because of its changing position relative to the observer. [NASA GRC] grc.nasa.gov GRCDoppler Effect NASA GRCDoppler EffectMay 13, 2021... Published: May 13, 2021 This impression becomes even stronger when the aircraft is difficult to see. At night, under cloud or at high altitude, visual information is weak or absent, so the auditory illusion dominates. The brain fills in the missing motion using the changing pitch, sometimes producing reports of an object that seemed to \"race away\" despite never changing speed significantly. [NASA] nasa.gov New Acoustics Techniques Clear Path for Quieter Aviation New Acoustics Techniques Clear Path for Quieter Aviation NASA... Why a jet may sound as though it is revving up Not every pitch change comes solely from Doppler shift. Jet engines radiate different frequencies more strongly in different directions, and the parts of the engine that dominate the sound change as the aircraft's orientation changes during a fly by. Compressor tones may be more prominent while the aircraft approaches, whereas turbine and exhaust noise can dominate after it passes. This changing balance of frequencies can reinforce the impression that the engine is winding up or winding down. [NASA] nasa.gov New Acoustics Techniques Clear Path for Quieter Aviation New Acoustics Techniques Clear Path for Quieter Aviation NASA... Aircraft also occasionally make genuine thrust adjustments during climb, descent or approach. These real engine power changes can combine with Doppler shift, making the perceived acceleration seem larger than it actually is. However, even without any throttle movement, a constant power fly past still produces a characteristic rise and fall in pitch. [NASA] nasa.gov New Acoustics Techniques Clear Path for Quieter Aviation New Acoustics Techniques Clear Path for Quieter Aviation NASA... Doppler Shift & Sonic Booms Neil deGrasse Tyson Explains… Channel: StarTalk Open on YouTube Why the illusion can be surprisingly convincing Several additional effects amplify the sensation of changing speed: Sound arrives after the aircraft has moved. Because sound travels much more slowly than a jet flies, the aircraft is already in a new position by the time earlier engine noise reaches the listener. Changing loudness affects perceived motion. The aircraft usually becomes louder as it approaches its closest point and quieter afterwards, reinforcing the impression of acceleration followed by retreat. Atmospheric conditions alter transmission. Wind and temperature layers can strengthen or weaken different frequencies, making the pitch change seem uneven or unusually dramatic. Limited visual references increase reliance on hearing. Without a clearly visible aircraft, the brain interprets the changing sound as evidence of changing motion rather than changing geometry. [nasa.gov] nasa.gov New Acoustics Techniques Clear Path for Quieter Aviation New Acoustics Techniques Clear Path for Quieter Aviation NASA... Clues that identify a normal jet pass Although the illusion can be striking, a normal jet fly over usually has a recognisable pattern. A typical sequence is a steadily increasing high pitched whine as the aircraft approaches, a rapid pitch transition near the closest point overhead, followed by a lower pitched sound that gradually fades into the distance. The apparent \"acceleration\" is closely tied to the moment the aircraft passes the observer rather than to any visible change in its flight path. [NASA GRC] grc.nasa.gov GRCDoppler Effect NASA GRCDoppler EffectMay 13, 2021... Published: May 13, 2021 If the aircraft remains visible throughout the event and continues along a straight course while the sound changes exactly as it passes, the changing pitch is almost certainly an ordinary consequence of Doppler shift and viewing geometry rather than evidence of unusual flight behaviour. Understanding this mechanism explains why routine airline traffic can sometimes produce surprisingly dramatic sounds that are later remembered as mysterious \" UFO noises .\" [NASA GRC] grc.nasa.gov GRCDoppler Effect NASA GRCDoppler EffectMay 13, 2021... Published: May 13, 2021 Supersonic Doppler effect explained Channel: Srinath Srinivasan Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why a Steady Jet Sounds Like It Accelerates. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Science of Sound By Thomas D. Rossing, Paul A. Wheeler et al. Written for students without a background in mathematics or physics, this textbook provides an introduction to the study of acoustics. It... See on Amazon Book Fundamentals of Aerodynamics By John David Anderson (Jr.) Rating: 4.0/5 from 8 Google Books ratings This book is for students?to be read, understood, and enjoyed. It is consciously written i n a clear, informal, and direct styie designed... See on Amazon Book Understanding Flight By David W. Anderson, Scott Eberhardt The simplest, most intuitive book on the toughest lessons of flight addresses the science of flying in terms, explanations, and illustra... See on Amazon Book Why You Hear What You Hear By Eric J. Heller Why You Hear What You Hear is the first book on the physics of sound for the nonspecialist to empower readers with a hands on, ears open... See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: The Science of Sound Fundamentals of Aerodynamics Understanding Flight As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. eBay marketplace picks Marketplace Samples Live tested eBay searches with available results related to this page. Shop location Using USA USA USA UK Canada Australia Ireland USA UK Canada Australia Ireland Selected from aircraft model on eBay.co.uk . Related eBay result Harrier GR3 Pewter Jet Model"},{"title":"Military Flights","url":"/military-flights/","description":"Rapid climbs, formation flying and high-power manoeuvres can make military aircraft sound unfamiliar even when their behaviour is routine.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Aircraft Noise","level":3,"text":"Military Flights Why Military Aircraft Sound More Mysterious at Night Aircraft Noise Military Flights Military Flights Why Military Aircraft Sound More Mysterious at Night Rapid climbs, formation flying and high power manoeuvres can make military aircraft sound unfamiliar even when their behaviour is routine. Rapid climbs, formation flying and high power manoeuvres can make military aircraft sound unfamiliar even when their behaviour is routine. population context Flight patterns that create changing noise Why military silhouettes and lights are hard to identify Evidence to check near training areas ufo noises aircraft noise military flights Introduction Military aircraft account for a significant share of reports describing mysterious roaring, whining or rapidly changing sounds that are initially interpreted as UFOs. The reason is not that military flights are inherently mysterious, but that they often operate differently from scheduled passenger traffic. Fast climbs, abrupt power changes, formation flying, night training and low level routes can produce unfamiliar sounds while making the aircraft themselves difficult to identify. In darkness, observers may hear an intense engine note long before seeing any lights, or may see only a single bright light without recognising the aircraft's shape. These ordinary military operations can therefore create convincing UFO noise reports, especially near training areas or air bases. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK Low flying military aircraft: Overview Low flying military aircraft: Overview Flight patterns that create changing noise Unlike commercial airliners that generally follow predictable airways and relatively smooth climb profiles, military aircraft regularly practise manoeuvres designed to simulate operational conditions. These activities can produce sound patterns that seem inconsistent with everyday aviation: Rapid climbs and descents change engine power quickly, creating dramatic shifts in pitch and loudness. High power turns redirect engine exhaust and noise toward or away from observers, making the sound swell unexpectedly. Formation flying means multiple aircraft may be heard as one continuously changing source rather than several separate aircraft. Low level navigation training brings aircraft much closer to listeners than commercial traffic, greatly increasing perceived loudness. Because sound travels much more slowly than a fast moving jet, witnesses may hear an aircraft after it has already moved elsewhere in the sky. The combination of delayed sound, changing engine settings and limited visual references can give the impression that an unknown object accelerated, hovered or changed direction when the aircraft was simply following a planned training profile. Military authorities openly acknowledge that low flying training is conducted because it is necessary to prepare crews for operational missions, including flights at altitudes far below those normally used by civil aviation. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK Low flying military aircraft: Overview Low flying military aircraft: Overview Why Fighter Jets Are So Much Louder Than Passenger Planes WION Podcast Channel: WION Open on YouTube Why military aircraft sound different from passenger jets Military aircraft often use engines at power settings rarely encountered on commercial flights. For example, fighter aircraft may: accelerate aggressively after take off; repeatedly increase and reduce thrust during exercises; remain at relatively low altitude for extended periods; perform training circuits that pass the same location several times. These behaviours generate rapidly changing acoustic signatures rather than the steady engine noise typical of an airliner cruising overhead. Helicopters used by military forces also contribute distinctive sounds. Heavy lift helicopters produce deep rotor beats that carry over long distances, while attack or utility helicopters can generate rhythmic blade noise that changes dramatically with manoeuvring. When several aircraft operate together, overlapping frequencies can create unusual pulsing or \"wavering\" sounds that listeners may struggle to identify. Listen To The Deafening Roar Of F 16 Jet Engine Full Afterburner Channel: AiirSource Military Open on YouTube Why military silhouettes and lights are hard to identify The sound alone rarely convinces people they have encountered something unusual. The uncertainty usually comes from the combination of unfamiliar noise and poor visual information. Night operations reduce the visual clues that normally identify an aircraft: only one or two lights may be visible at long range; the aircraft's outline often disappears against the night sky; dark camouflage colours make military aircraft especially difficult to see; viewing angle can hide wings or tail surfaces, leaving only a bright point of light. Perspective further complicates matters. A fighter flying directly towards an observer may appear almost stationary despite approaching at high speed because its sideways motion is minimal from that viewpoint. If engine noise grows rapidly while the apparent movement remains slow, observers may conclude they are watching an unidentified object rather than an aircraft. These visual limitations are one reason aviation experts caution against identifying aircraft solely from isolated lights seen at night. [AP News] apnews.com AP News Is that a drone or a plane? Experts help explain the differencesDecember 16, 2024 — Distinguishing drones from planes and helicopters can be challenging from a dista... Published: December 16, 2024 Why reports increase near military training areas Many well known UFO noise reports occur close to locations where military aviation is common. Training areas differ from ordinary air routes because they may involve: repeated circuits over the same landscape; multiple aircraft arriving from different directions; temporary periods of unusually intense flying; exercises continuing into the evening or night. People who live near these areas often become familiar with routine aircraft sounds. Visitors or residents experiencing an unusually busy training period, however, may encounter combinations of noise that seem entirely unfamiliar. In the United Kingdom, for example, the Ministry of Defence publishes information explaining that low flying military training occurs across designated areas and that Tactical Training Areas allow especially realistic low level exercises during scheduled periods. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK Military low flying Military low flying Evidence to check near training areas When an unexplained roaring or whining sound is reported, several practical checks can help determine whether military aviation is the most likely explanation. Useful evidence includes: proximity to known military airfields or training routes; published low flying or tactical training schedules where available; reports from nearby residents describing similar aircraft activity; weather conditions that may enhance or redirect sound; multiple independent witnesses describing conventional aircraft shortly afterwards. It is also worth remembering that not every military aircraft appears on publicly accessible flight tracking websites. Operational, training and security considerations mean some flights may have limited or delayed public tracking data, so the absence of an aircraft from a consumer flight tracking service does not by itself demonstrate that an unidentified object was present. Aviation regulators likewise caution that consumer tracking platforms are not definitive evidence of an aircraft's location. [Civil Aviation Authority] caa.co.uk Open source on caa.co.uk. Pure F 15 Strike Eagle Low Level Flying Sounds Without Music. \" The Roundabout / Mach Loop \" Channel: Wonkabar007 Open on YouTube Why military flights remain a persuasive explanation Military aviation combines several characteristics that naturally generate convincing UFO noise reports: higher engine power, lower operating altitudes, rapid manoeuvres, formation flying and frequent night exercises. Each factor independently makes aircraft harder to recognise, while together they can create striking experiences in which loud, unfamiliar sounds seem disconnected from any visible aircraft. This does not mean every unexplained report has a military origin. Rather, it explains why investigators routinely consider nearby military activity before concluding that an unusual noise requires an extraordinary explanation. In many cases, the combination of aircraft acoustics, night time perception and realistic training operations provides a complete and evidence based account of sounds that initially seemed mysterious. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK Low flying military aircraft: Overview Low flying military aircraft: Overview Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Military Aircraft Sound More Mysterious at Night. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Aircraft Book By"},{"title":"Bent Sound","url":"/bent-sound/","description":"Wind and temperature layers can redirect aircraft noise, making distant flights sound closer or come from the wrong direction.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Aircraft Noise","level":3,"text":"Bent Sound How Weather Makes Aircraft Sound Close and Misplaced Aircraft Noise Bent Sound Bent Sound How Weather Makes Aircraft Sound Close and Misplaced Wind and temperature layers can redirect aircraft noise, making distant flights sound closer or come from the wrong direction. Wind and temperature layers can redirect aircraft noise, making distant flights sound closer or come from the wrong direction. population context How wind shifts apparent sound direction Why temperature inversions carry noise farther Terrain and cloud reflections that confuse listeners ufo noises aircraft noise bent sound Introduction Many reports of mysterious whining, roaring or humming overhead that are later linked to aircraft share one important feature: the atmosphere between the aircraft and the listener was not acoustically neutral. Wind, temperature layers and local terrain can bend, redirect and reinforce sound waves, making an ordinary aircraft seem much closer than it is or making the noise appear to come from the wrong part of the sky. These effects are especially noticeable at night, during calm weather following sunset, or beneath low cloud, when visual cues are weak and atmospheric conditions often favour long distance sound propagation. Rather than indicating an unusual flying object, these acoustic distortions are well understood in aviation noise research and atmospheric acoustics. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Toward inclusion of atmospheric effects in the aircraft community noise predictions PubMed... How wind shifts apparent sound direction Sound does not travel through still air in the real atmosphere. Winds usually change speed and direction with height, creating a vertical wind gradient that alters the path of sound waves. Instead of travelling in straight lines, sound can be refracted, meaning it curves as it moves through layers of moving air. When the wind strengthens with altitude in the direction from the aircraft towards the listener, sound waves tend to bend downward. This keeps more acoustic energy close to the ground, allowing aircraft to be heard at greater distances than expected. Conversely, when sound travels against increasing winds, the waves bend upward, creating \"shadow zones\" where an aircraft may seem surprisingly quiet despite being nearby. Modern aircraft noise prediction models explicitly include these atmospheric effects because they can alter ground level noise patterns by several decibels. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Toward inclusion of atmospheric effects in the aircraft community noise predictions PubMed... For someone trying to identify an unfamiliar overhead sound, this bending creates several perceptual traps: the aircraft may already have moved well beyond the point where the sound appears to originate; the loudest sound may arrive from one side rather than directly beneath the aircraft; changes in loudness can resemble a manoeuvring object rather than a steadily flying aircraft. These effects become more confusing because observers naturally expect sound to travel in straight lines, even though the atmosphere often prevents that. Refraction of Sound: How Sound Waves Bend Through Different Media Sound Physics Explained Channel: CodeLucky Open on YouTube Why temperature inversions carry noise farther One of the most important weather conditions affecting aircraft sound is a temperature inversion. Under normal daytime conditions, air becomes cooler with increasing height. During an inversion, typically after clear, calm nights, the opposite occurs for a layer of the atmosphere: warmer air sits above cooler surface air. Because the speed of sound varies with temperature, this structure bends sound waves back towards the ground instead of allowing them to spread upward. The result is that aircraft noise can remain concentrated over much longer distances, making flights many kilometres away seem unexpectedly loud. This is one reason communities around airports often report that aircraft sound travels farther during the early morning or overnight hours. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com Meteorological and ground effects on the propagation of aircraft noise close to the Earth's surface ScienceDirectSeptember... Importantly, a temperature inversion does not make aircraft louder at the source. Instead, it changes how efficiently the atmosphere transports their sound to the listener. Someone unfamiliar with the effect may conclude that an unusually low aircraft has passed overhead when the aircraft is actually following a routine flight path at normal altitude. Historical research into airport acoustics has shown that unusually low sound attenuation at long distances is often explained by a combination of atmospheric refraction and interactions with the ground rather than by any increase in aircraft engine output. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Meteorological and ground effects on the propagation of aircraft noise close to the Earth's surface ScienceDirectSeptember... Terrain and cloud reflections that confuse listeners Weather is only part of the story. The landscape itself can further distort where aircraft seem to be. Hillsides, cliffs, large buildings and even extensive urban environments can reflect or diffract sound, creating delayed echoes or multiple arrival paths. A listener may therefore hear one aircraft as though several independent sound sources exist overhead. Modern aviation noise models devote significant attention to predicting these terrain effects because they materially change perceived noise around airports. [DOI.org] doi.org Open source on doi.org. Low cloud often receives credit for \"reflecting\" aircraft noise back to the ground. In practice, the situation is more subtle. Clouds themselves are generally much less effective reflectors than popular belief suggests. Instead, the stable atmospheric conditions commonly associated with low cloud or overcast weather often reduce atmospheric mixing and coincide with wind and temperature profiles that favour downward refraction. Listeners therefore correctly notice louder aircraft under these conditions, but the primary cause is usually the atmosphere as a whole rather than the cloud layer acting as a giant acoustic mirror. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Toward inclusion of atmospheric effects in the aircraft community noise predictions PubMed... Why Crowded Shows Sound Dull Impact of Heat on Live Sound Coverage Channel: Dave Rat Open on YouTube Why these conditions produce convincing \"UFO noises\" Within the broader context of reports of unexplained aerial sounds, weather driven sound propagation explains several recurring witness descriptions without requiring an unusual airborne source. Common observations include: A distant aircraft sounding directly overhead. Downward refraction concentrates sound near the surface, making remote traffic appear surprisingly close. Noise arriving from the wrong direction. Curved sound paths and reflections disconnect the apparent sound source from the aircraft's true position. Aircraft heard before they are seen—or after they appear to have passed. Because aircraft move much faster than sound, atmospheric bending can exaggerate the normal delay between visual and acoustic cues. Exceptionally loud nights followed by ordinary mornings. Small changes in wind profile or the breakdown of an overnight temperature inversion can dramatically change how aircraft are heard without any change in flight operations. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Toward inclusion of atmospheric effects in the aircraft community noise predictions PubMed... These effects are strongest when visual information is poor. Darkness, haze or broken cloud removes the visual reference points that would normally allow observers to associate the sound with an identifiable aircraft, increasing the likelihood that an ordinary flight is perceived as an unidentified aerial phenomenon. What weather can—and cannot—explain Atmospheric acoustics provides a robust explanation for many reports of misplaced, unusually loud or oddly directed aircraft sounds. Wind gradients, temperature inversions and interactions with terrain are established physical mechanisms that are routinely incorporated into aviation noise modelling because they measurably alter where and how strongly aircraft are heard on the ground. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Toward inclusion of atmospheric effects in the aircraft community noise predictions PubMed... However, these mechanisms do not create new sounds or transform an aircraft into something fundamentally different. They alter the path, timing and apparent location of existing aircraft noise. In investigations of alleged \" UFO noises \", recognising these weather dependent acoustic effects helps distinguish genuine atmospheric illusions from claims that require other explanations, reducing the risk of mistaking ordinary aviation under unusual propagation conditions for evidence of an unidentified object."},{"title":"Sound Travel","url":"/sound-travel/","description":"Wind, temperature layers, clouds, and terrain can bend, weaken, or redirect sound until its source seems distant or airborne.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises","level":2,"text":"Sound Travel How Weather Makes Sky Sounds Seem Mysterious UFO Noises Sound Travel Sound Travel How Weather Makes Sky Sounds Seem Mysterious Wind, temperature layers, clouds, and terrain can bend, weaken, or redirect sound until its source seems distant or airborne. Wind, temperature layers, clouds, and terrain can bend, weaken, or redirect sound until its source seems distant or airborne. mechanism Temperature inversions and sound refraction Wind direction and acoustic shadows Terrain, clouds, and delayed arrival ufo noises sound travel Introduction Many reports of mysterious \" UFO noises \" involve sounds that seem detached from any visible source, arrive from the wrong direction, or appear to hover overhead despite originating many kilometres away. In many cases, atmospheric acoustics provide a well understood explanation. The atmosphere is not a uniform medium: temperature, wind, humidity and terrain continuously alter the way sound travels. Under the right conditions, ordinary aircraft, distant industry, explosions or traffic can sound unexpectedly loud, strangely quiet or as though they are coming from the sky itself. Understanding these mechanisms helps explain why unusual sounds are often sincere observations without requiring an unusual source. [RMetS] rmets.org RMet SUnder a cloud or walking on sunshine? Royal Meteorological Society Under a cloud or walking on sunshine? Royal Meteorological SocietyJanuary 13, 2022... Published: January 13, 2022 Temperature inversions and sound refraction The single most important weather condition that alters how sky sounds are perceived is a temperature inversion . Normally, air becomes colder with height. During an inversion, however, a layer of warmer air sits above cooler air near the ground. This often develops on clear, calm nights or beneath persistent high pressure systems. [RMetS] rmets.org RMet SUnder a cloud or walking on sunshine? Royal Meteorological Society Under a cloud or walking on sunshine? Royal Meteorological SocietyJanuary 13, 2022... Published: January 13, 2022 Because the speed of sound increases in warmer air, sound waves passing into warmer layers bend, or refract , back towards the cooler air below instead of continuing upwards. The result is similar to a giant invisible acoustic lens. For someone on the ground, this can produce several surprising effects: sounds travel much farther than expected; distant aircraft become unusually prominent; industrial machinery many kilometres away becomes audible; the apparent direction of a sound shifts away from its true source; sounds may seem to emerge from the sky rather than the horizon. People commonly notice this effect on still winter evenings, when trains, motorways or airports suddenly seem much closer than usual. Although dramatic, this behaviour follows ordinary acoustic physics rather than indicating an unusual airborne object. [RMetS] rmets.org RMet SUnder a cloud or walking on sunshine? Royal Meteorological Society Under a cloud or walking on sunshine? Royal Meteorological SocietyJanuary 13, 2022... Published: January 13, 2022 Why aircraft sometimes sound overhead when they are not Aircraft are particularly prone to acoustic misidentification because observers often see them at one location while hearing sound that has followed a different path. An aircraft flying tens of kilometres away may be hidden by darkness or cloud while its engine noise is bent towards listeners by an inversion layer. Conversely, an aircraft that is clearly visible may appear almost silent if atmospheric conditions direct much of its sound away from the observer. This helps explain reports in which witnesses describe humming or roaring overhead without locating any corresponding aircraft, even though radar or flight records later identify conventional traffic in the area. Wind direction and acoustic shadows Wind changes more than the movement of air—it also changes how sound propagates through it. If wind speed increases with altitude, as it often does, sound travelling downwind is gradually bent back towards the ground. Listeners positioned downwind therefore receive stronger, clearer sound over longer distances. By contrast, sound travelling upwind bends upward, creating regions where surprisingly little sound reaches the ground. [arXiv] arxiv.org Observational Evidence for Wind Driven Low Pass Filtering of Infrasound at Short RangeJanuary 31, 2026... Published: January 31, 2026 This creates what acousticians call an acoustic shadow . A person standing within such a shadow may experience: an aircraft becoming suddenly quieter despite remaining visible; an engine apparently switching off before becoming audible again; sounds that appear to \"jump\" from one location to another; inconsistent loudness between nearby observers. Because wind speed and direction change with height rather than remaining constant, these effects are often patchy. Two witnesses only a short distance apart may report noticeably different sound levels from the same aircraft. Recent field experiments using controlled explosions have shown that atmospheric wind structure alone can significantly alter the frequencies and apparent characteristics of low frequency sound, even over relatively short distances. [arXiv] arxiv.org Observational Evidence for Wind Driven Low Pass Filtering of Infrasound at Short RangeJanuary 31, 2026... Published: January 31, 2026 What Are the Mystery Booms, Hums and \"Trumpets\" in the Sky? Make This Make Sense Channel: New York Post Open on YouTube Terrain, clouds and delayed arrival Hills and valleys reshape sound paths Topography strongly influences outdoor acoustics. Mountain ridges, escarpments and large hills can block direct sound while reflecting or diffracting portions around obstacles. Valleys may channel sound for considerable distances, allowing aircraft or industrial noise to emerge from unexpected directions. For example: a jet beyond a ridge may remain inaudible until it reaches a point where reflected sound becomes dominant; a quarry hidden behind hills may sound airborne because only reflected sound reaches the listener; a valley can carry engine noise much farther than surrounding open ground. These effects become stronger when combined with stable atmospheric conditions. Clouds are rarely the main cause Clouds themselves are often blamed for mysterious sky sounds, but their direct effect on audible sound is generally modest. Low cloud can contribute slightly by scattering or reflecting some higher frequency sound, and cloud often accompanies the stable weather patterns that also favour temperature inversions. However, the atmosphere's temperature and wind structure usually play a much larger role than the cloud layer itself in determining how far sound travels. [RMetS] rmets.org RMet SUnder a cloud or walking on sunshine? Royal Meteorological Society Under a cloud or walking on sunshine? Royal Meteorological SocietyJanuary 13, 2022... Published: January 13, 2022 Why sound sometimes arrives late Many reports describe seeing an object before hearing it. This delay has several ordinary causes: sound travels at roughly 343 metres per second under typical conditions, much slower than light; aircraft may already have moved significantly before their sound arrives; atmospheric refraction can lengthen the sound's path; terrain may block the direct route while allowing a later reflected path to reach the observer. The resulting delay can make a conventional aircraft appear disconnected from its own sound, especially at night when judging distance is difficult. What’s Behind These Mysterious Sky Sounds? WION Podcast Channel: WION Open on YouTube Why atmospheric distortion makes sound hard to locate Humans are remarkably poor at locating certain outdoor sounds. Our brains estimate direction primarily by comparing the tiny differences in arrival time and loudness between the two ears. When atmospheric refraction bends sound along curved paths instead of straight lines, these cues become less reliable. Low frequency sounds—often described in UFO reports as humming, droning or distant rumbling—are especially difficult to pinpoint because their long wavelengths provide weaker directional information. When refraction and wind are added, listeners may perceive the sound as: directly overhead; moving independently of any visible object; stationary despite an aircraft passing; coming from multiple directions at once. This uncertainty is a recognised feature of atmospheric acoustics rather than evidence that the sound has no ordinary source. Practical examples linked to UFO sound reports Several common situations illustrate how atmospheric conditions can create convincing but misleading impressions: Night time airport traffic: During strong inversions, aircraft many kilometres away may produce loud engine noise while remaining visually inconspicuous. Military training flights: High altitude aircraft may appear nearly silent until changing wind"},{"title":"Cloud Myth","url":"/cloud-myth/","description":"Low cloud may alter some higher frequencies, but temperature and wind structure usually explain long-range sky sounds far better.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Sound Travel","level":3,"text":"Cloud Myth Do Low Clouds Really Amplify Sky Sounds? Sound Travel Cloud Myth Cloud Myth Do Low Clouds Really Amplify Sky Sounds? Low cloud may alter some higher frequencies, but temperature and wind structure usually explain long range sky sounds far better. Low cloud may alter some higher frequencies, but temperature and wind structure usually explain long range sky sounds far better. dataset evidence What clouds can and cannot do to audible sound Why inversions often occur beneath low cloud How to avoid mistaking correlation for the main cause ufo noises sound travel cloud myth Introduction Reports of mysterious “ UFO noises ” are often linked with low, grey cloud decks because witnesses notice unusual humming, roaring or droning on overcast nights. It is therefore tempting to assume that the clouds themselves are reflecting or amplifying sound. The evidence, however, points to a more subtle explanation. Low clouds can make a modest difference to how some sound frequencies behave, but the dominant factor is usually the structure of the atmosphere beneath the cloud base—particularly temperature inversions and wind gradients that bend sound back towards the ground. In many situations, the cloud layer is simply a visible sign that these favourable acoustic conditions already exist, leading observers to credit the cloud when the invisible air layers are doing most of the work. [metoffice.gov.uk] weather.metoffice.gov.uk Met Office What is a temperature inversion? Met OfficeWhat is a temperature inversion? Met Office... Do Low Clouds Really Amplify Sky Sounds? The popular idea that a blanket of low cloud acts like the ceiling of a concert hall is only partly correct. Water droplets in clouds interact with sound, but for the audible frequencies associated with aircraft, traffic, industrial machinery and many reported \"sky hums\", the effect is generally much weaker than commonly imagined. Clouds are not solid surfaces. They consist of tiny droplets or ice crystals suspended in air, so they do not produce the strong mirror like reflections that many people picture. Instead, they may scatter or slightly attenuate some higher frequency sound while having comparatively little direct influence on lower frequencies that travel long distances most effectively. [Institute of Acoustics] ioa.org.uk Institute of Acoustics Proceedings of the Institute of Acoustics Institute of Acoustics Proceedings of the Institute of Acoustics This distinction matters because many sounds described in UFO reports—deep rumbles, humming and distant engine like drones —contain substantial low frequency energy. Those frequencies are far more strongly affected by the temperature and wind profile of the atmosphere than by the presence of cloud droplets. What Clouds Can and Cannot Do to Audible Sound Low cloud is not acoustically irrelevant, but its role is limited. Clouds can: slightly scatter or absorb some higher frequency components; contribute to a generally damped acoustic environment under certain conditions; coincide with weather patterns that favour long range sound transmission. Clouds usually cannot: dramatically amplify sound by themselves; redirect sound over many kilometres without favourable atmospheric layering; explain why distant aircraft or industrial noise suddenly appears overhead. Outdoor acoustics studies consistently show that atmospheric refraction caused by temperature and wind gradients dominates long range sound propagation. Even where cloud cover is present, researchers typically model the air's temperature profile and wind shear as the primary controls on how sound bends. [Institute of Acoustics] ioa.org.uk Institute of Acoustics Proceedings of the Institute of Acoustics Institute of Acoustics Proceedings of the Institute of Acoustics This explains why two overcast days can sound completely different. One cloudy day may seem unusually quiet, while another carries motorway or airport noise for many kilometres, depending on the invisible structure of the air below the clouds. What Are Inversions? Channel: NorCast Weather School Open on YouTube Why Inversions Often Occur Beneath Low Cloud The reason low cloud receives so much blame is that it frequently appears alongside temperature inversions. A temperature inversion occurs when warmer air overlies cooler air near the surface. Instead of allowing sound to disperse upward, this structure bends sound waves back towards the ground. The result can be surprisingly loud and distant noises that appear to come from above rather than from their true source. [Met Office] weather.metoffice.gov.uk Met Office What is a temperature inversion? Met OfficeWhat is a temperature inversion? Met Office... Low stratus or fog often forms because moisture becomes trapped beneath the same stable layer. In other words, the cloud is frequently a visible symptom of the inversion rather than its acoustic cause. The inversion acts as an atmospheric lid, limiting vertical mixing while also creating conditions that favour downward refraction of sound. [Met Office] weather.metoffice.gov.uk Met Office What is a temperature inversion? Met OfficeWhat is a temperature inversion? Met Office... This relationship explains why many witnesses sincerely associate unusual sounds with cloudy nights. Their observation is often accurate—the sounds do coincide with cloud—but the underlying physical mechanism is usually the stable atmospheric layering that produced both the cloud and the altered acoustics. Historical and Scientific Examples Meteorologists have documented the effects of inversions for decades, particularly in relation to thunder, aircraft noise and industrial sound. The US National Weather Service and NOAA both explain that thunder can seem louder and carry farther during inversions because sound waves are refracted back towards the Earth's surface. In these situations, the inversion—not the cloud itself—effectively concentrates more of the sound where listeners are standing. [National Weather Service] weather.gov National Weather Service NWS Jet Stream National Weather ServiceNWS JetStream The Sound of Thunder... Studies of aircraft noise have reached similar conclusions. Classical investigations found that unexpectedly strong aircraft sound often results from a combination of atmospheric refraction and interactions with the ground rather than simple \"reflection\" from weather layers. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Meteorological and ground effects on the propagation of aircraft noise close to the Earth's surface ScienceDirect... An interesting comparison comes from research on low frequency infrasound. A reinterpretation of historic pigeon navigation experiments found that temperature inversions associated with low stratus clouds could trap infrasonic waves beneath the cloud layer. Birds released above the inversion apparently lacked access to those signals, illustrating that it was the atmospheric duct beneath the clouds—not the clouds themselves—that controlled long range propagation. Although this concerns frequencies below human hearing, it demonstrates how strongly atmospheric layers can dominate sound transmission. [USGS] usgs.gov A reinterpretation of “Homing pigeons’ flight over and under low stratus” based on atmospheric propagation modeling of infrasonic nav... Temperature Inversions Channel: Met Office Learn About Weather Open on YouTube How to Avoid Mistaking Correlation for the Main Cause When assessing reports of unexplained sky sounds, it helps to separate visible weather from the invisible atmospheric structure. Questions that are often more informative than simply asking whether it was cloudy include: Was the weather calm, with little surface wind? Did the event occur after sunset or before sunrise, when inversions commonly strengthen? Was there fog, mist or low stratus suggesting stable air? Were there airports, motorways, railways or industrial sites tens of kilometres away? Did the apparent direction of the sound change over time? If several of these conditions are present, atmospheric refraction provides a well established explanation for why ordinary sounds may seem detached from their true source. Conversely, overcast skies alone are poor evidence that clouds amplified the sound. Without the accompanying temperature and wind structure, a low cloud deck may have little noticeable influence on what reaches the listener. The Practical Takeaway Within the broader question of atmospheric conditions that distort sky sounds, low clouds are best understood as indicators rather than principal actors. They often accompany the stable air masses that allow distant sounds to travel unusually well, making them easy to blame for mysterious noises. Yet decades of atmospheric acoustics research consistently show that temperature inversions and wind structure are the primary mechanisms responsible for long range, seemingly overhead sounds. For reports of unusual humming, roaring or droning associated with alleged"},{"title":"Inversion Noise","url":"/inversion-noise/","description":"A nighttime inversion can make refineries, quarries and power stations sound airborne even when they are many kilometres away.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Sound Travel","level":3,"text":"Inversion Noise Why Distant Industry Can Sound Overhead Sound Travel Inversion Noise Inversion Noise Why Distant Industry Can Sound Overhead A nighttime inversion can make refineries, quarries and power stations sound airborne even when they are many kilometres away. A nighttime inversion can make refineries, quarries and power stations sound airborne even when they are many kilometres away. mechanism How inversions bend sound back to the ground Industrial sources most likely to travel long distances Clues that separate airborne noise from ground machinery ufo noises sound travel inversion noise Introduction Many reports of unexplained nighttime “ UFO noises ” turn out to have an ordinary acoustic cause rather than an unusual airborne source. One of the most important mechanisms is a temperature inversion , where a layer of warmer air sits above cooler air near the ground. Under these stable conditions, the rumble of refineries, power stations, quarries, factories or other heavy industry can travel far beyond its usual range and appear to come from directly overhead. The machinery itself has not become louder; instead, the atmosphere has changed the path the sound takes. This makes inversion driven industrial noise a distinctive explanation for mysterious humming, droning or roaring reported on calm evenings, especially when no obvious source is visible. [Met Office] weather.metoffice.gov.uk Met Office What is a temperature inversion? Met OfficeWhat is a temperature inversion? Met Office... How inversions bend sound back to the ground Under normal daytime conditions, sound from ground level sources gradually spreads upward and weakens with distance. A temperature inversion changes that behaviour. Because sound travels slightly faster through warmer air than colder air, a warm layer above cooler surface air causes sound waves to bend back towards the ground instead of continuing upward. Rather than escaping into the atmosphere, some of the acoustic energy repeatedly returns towards the surface. The result is an extended zone in which distant sounds remain unexpectedly strong. [Met Office] weather.metoffice.gov.uk Met Office What is a temperature inversion? Met OfficeWhat is a temperature inversion? Met Office... For a listener many kilometres away, this creates several effects at once: industrial noise carries much farther than expected; low frequency rumbling remains particularly noticeable; the sound may seem detached from the true location of the machinery; echoes and multiple propagation paths make the source difficult to pinpoint; the noise can appear to float above the listener instead of coming from the horizon. This is why witnesses sometimes describe an invisible object hovering overhead when the actual source is fixed on the ground. Night time inversions are especially effective because they often form during clear, calm, high pressure weather, when the atmosphere is stable and vertical mixing is weak. These same conditions frequently accompany reports of mysterious humming or distant droning. [Met Office] weather.metoffice.gov.uk Met Office What is a temperature inversion? Met OfficeWhat is a temperature inversion? Met Office... ACOUSTICS: Refraction and Temperature Inversion Channel: The Point Studios Open on YouTube Industrial sources most likely to travel long distances Not every industrial sound propagates equally well. The most common long range sources share one important characteristic: they produce continuous low frequency energy. Examples include: large refinery flare systems and compressors; power station cooling fans and turbines; quarry crushers and conveyor systems; steelworks and heavy manufacturing plants; large ventilation systems; pumping stations and industrial gas compressors. Low frequency sound loses less energy to the atmosphere than higher pitched noise. During an inversion, these frequencies can remain audible over many kilometres while higher frequency components fade away. The listener therefore hears a broad hum or distant roar rather than distinct machinery. This filtering effect contributes to misidentification. Without the sharper mechanical noises normally associated with industrial equipment, the remaining sound may resemble an aircraft engine, hovering rotorcraft or an unidentified aerial source. Why the sound often seems to come from the sky Human hearing estimates direction by comparing tiny differences in arrival time and loudness between the two ears. Outdoors, this works best when sound travels directly from its source. Temperature inversions disrupt those assumptions. The listener may receive sound that has followed a curved atmospheric path rather than a straight line. Reflections from terrain, buildings or multiple atmospheric layers can further confuse localisation. Instead of pointing towards the actual refinery or quarry, the brain may interpret the strongest arriving wavefront as coming from above or from an entirely different compass direction. Unlike visual illusions, this is not a failure of hearing. It is the consequence of the sound field itself being reshaped before it reaches the observer. Why the effect is strongest after dark Many reports occur shortly after sunset or during the early hours of the morning because these are the periods when radiational cooling produces the strongest surface inversions. Typical favourable conditions include: clear skies; [thetimes.co.uk] thetimes.co.uk Moist air drawn in by high pressure systems from surrounding seas creates a blanket of low cloud cover, particularly during autumn and wi... light or calm winds; high pressure weather; [thetimes.co.uk] thetimes.co.uk Moist air drawn in by high pressure systems from surrounding seas creates a blanket of low cloud cover, particularly during autumn and wi... winter evenings or cool nights in any season; valleys where cold air pools near the surface. These same weather patterns are also associated with trapped fog and pollution because the inversion suppresses vertical mixing throughout the lower atmosphere. [Met Office] weather.metoffice.gov.uk Met Office What is a temperature inversion? Met OfficeWhat is a temperature inversion? Met Office... Once daytime sunshine warms the ground and breaks up the inversion, the unusual sound propagation usually disappears. A factory that seemed only a few kilometres away overnight may become effectively inaudible by late morning despite operating continuously. why sound travels farther at night Channel: Code the Universee Open on YouTube Clues that separate airborne noise from ground machinery Several practical observations help distinguish inversion carried industrial sound from a genuine airborne object. The sound remains steady. Industrial plants usually produce continuous humming or droning rather than rapidly changing engine notes. No movement is apparent. The apparent position may stay fixed for long periods even though no aircraft or visible object is present. The weather is unusually calm. Reports commonly occur during still evenings with little wind, clear skies or persistent fog. Multiple people hear the same hum across a wide area. A single industrial source may become audible simultaneously over many communities lying beneath favourable acoustic conditions. The sound disappears after sunrise. As atmospheric mixing resumes, long distance propagation weakens quickly. These features do not prove an industrial origin on their own, but together they are consistent with well understood atmospheric acoustics rather than an unidentified object. A real world illustration of long range sound propagation Extreme events demonstrate how dramatically stable atmospheric conditions can extend the reach of sound. Following the 2005 explosion at the Buncefield oil storage depot in Hertfordshire, reports documented the blast being heard hundreds of kilometres away, including across parts of the Netherlands. While an explosion is far more energetic than ordinary industrial operations, it illustrates the same underlying principle: under suitable atmospheric conditions, sound can travel far beyond everyday expectations and be perceived in surprising locations. [thetimes.co.uk] thetimes.co.uk Moist air drawn in by high pressure systems from surrounding seas creates a blanket of low cloud cover, particularly during autumn and wi... Routine industrial machinery produces a much weaker effect, but the same physics applies. Instead of a single explosive event, a continuous hum may be carried over unexpectedly long distances, making an ordinary ground installation seem like an invisible source in the night sky. Refraction of Sound: How Sound Waves Bend Through Different Media Sound Physics Explained Channel: CodeLucky Open on YouTube Why this matters for reports of unexplained sky sounds Temperature inversions do not create mysterious noises—they redirect familiar ones. When heavy industrial machinery operates beneath a stable nocturnal atmosphere, its sound can be"},{"title":"Directionless Hums","url":"/directionless-hums/","description":"Long-wavelength humming and rumbling are hard to locate, especially when wind and refraction distort the timing cues used by both ears.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Sound Travel","level":3,"text":"Directionless Hums Why Low Hums Seem to Fill the Sky Sound Travel Directionless Hums Directionless Hums Why Low Hums Seem to Fill the Sky Long wavelength humming and rumbling are hard to locate, especially when wind and refraction distort the timing cues used by both ears. Long wavelength humming and rumbling are hard to locate, especially when wind and refraction distort the timing cues used by both ears. mechanism Why low frequencies provide weak directional cues How refraction disrupts binaural localisation Practical checks for locating a persistent outdoor hum ufo noises sound travel directionless hums Introduction Reports of unexplained \"UFO noises\" often describe a deep hum or distant rumble that seems to come from everywhere at once. Unlike sharp sounds, which are usually easy to point towards, low frequency outdoor noise can be remarkably difficult for the human auditory system to locate. When long wavelength sound combines with atmospheric refraction, wind gradients and reflections from the landscape, listeners may sincerely perceive a source as hovering overhead, surrounding them or remaining fixed despite movement. This does not mean the sound has no physical origin. Instead, it reflects well understood limits of human sound localisation under challenging outdoor conditions. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med[Sound localization cues of binaural hearing PubMed[Sound localization cues of binaural hearing] PubMed... Within the broader context of atmospheric conditions that distort sky sounds, low frequency humming deserves separate attention because the mechanism differs from simple sound bending. The atmosphere may alter where sound energy arrives, but the listener's hearing also struggles to assign an accurate direction when the sound itself contains few reliable localisation cues. Why low frequencies provide weak directional cues Human hearing estimates a sound's direction by comparing what reaches each ear. At higher frequencies, the head casts an acoustic shadow, making the sound noticeably louder in one ear than the other. The folds of the outer ear also alter high frequency sound differently depending on whether it comes from above, below, in front or behind. Low frequency sounds behave differently. Their wavelengths are much larger than the width of the human head, so they bend around it instead of producing a strong acoustic shadow. As a result, the loudness difference between the ears becomes very small. The brain must rely mainly on tiny differences in arrival time—known as interaural time differences—which become increasingly ambiguous for distant, slowly varying hums. [nih.gov] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... This creates several characteristic effects: a hum may seem centred overhead even when originating near the horizon; listeners may disagree about its direction despite standing close together; turning the head often provides little improvement; the sound can appear stationary even while the true source is moving. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com Sound source localization August 1, 2018 — EUROPEAN ANNALS OF OTORHINOLARYNGOLOGY, HEAD AND NECK DISEASES Volume 135, Issue 4, August 2018, Pages 259... Published: August 1, 2018 Researchers have long described these ambiguities as part of the \"cone of confusion\": different physical source locations can produce almost identical timing differences between the ears. Normally, head movement and higher frequency content resolve this ambiguity. A nearly pure outdoor hum provides far fewer clues. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med[Sound localization cues of binaural hearing PubMed[Sound localization cues of binaural hearing] PubMed... The Mysterious Hum Learn English with Mystery Channel: MysteryEnglish Open on YouTube How atmospheric refraction disrupts binaural localisation Atmospheric refraction does not merely carry sound farther. It also changes the path by which sound reaches the listener. Temperature inversions and wind speed gradients can bend sound downward over long distances, allowing machinery, aircraft engines or heavy traffic to remain audible far beyond their expected range. By the time these waves arrive, they may have followed curved paths through multiple layers of air rather than travelling directly from the source. Their timing, phase relationships and mixture of frequencies therefore differ from what the auditory system expects for a nearby source. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... Low frequency energy is particularly resistant to atmospheric absorption, allowing it to survive these long propagation paths better than higher frequency components. As the higher frequencies fade, the listener is left with a smoother, bass heavy signal containing fewer directional landmarks. The result is often described as a hum that seems to \"fill the sky\" rather than coming from a single point. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMCJuly 10, 2024... Published: July 10, 2024 Importantly, refraction does not create new sounds or make them intrinsically directionless. Instead, it reduces the reliability of the cues that humans normally use to identify direction. Why distant hums can seem overhead or everywhere Several mechanisms can combine simultaneously. First, a distant industrial fan, compressor, motorway or aircraft engine may be refracted back towards the ground by an inversion layer. Second, because low frequencies spread efficiently around obstacles and terrain, reflections from buildings, hills or woodland may arrive alongside the direct wave. These overlapping arrivals reduce the contrast between the ears even further. Third, the remaining signal often contains little high frequency detail. Without those spectral cues from the outer ear, the brain has difficulty distinguishing whether the source is above, behind or far away. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMCJuly 10, 2024... Published: July 10, 2024 These factors explain why eyewitnesses sometimes describe a deep humming sound as: directly overhead despite an empty sky; surrounding an entire neighbourhood; impossible to point towards; apparently unchanged while walking several hundred metres. Such descriptions are genuine perceptual experiences, but they do not reliably indicate the physical position of the source. Interaural Time Difference and Interaural Level Difference Channel: Mark Anderson Audio Open on YouTube Practical checks for locating a persistent outdoor hum A persistent low frequency hum can still be investigated systematically, even though localisation is difficult. Move a significant distance. Walking tens rather than just a few metres may reveal changes hidden by local reflections. If the apparent direction never changes, the sound may be arriving through multiple propagation paths rather than directly. Compare different elevations. Listening from an upstairs window, an open field or a sheltered valley can alter the balance between direct and refracted sound. Marked changes suggest atmospheric or terrain effects. Listen at different times. Temperature inversions commonly strengthen after sunset and weaken after sunrise. A hum that becomes much clearer on calm nights than during windy afternoons is consistent with atmospheric propagation changes rather than a new source appearing. Atmospheric conditions can substantially alter long distance audibility from one period to the next. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... Look for broadband clues. If only a narrow bass tone remains while higher pitched components disappear, distance and atmospheric filtering may be responsible. A nearby machine usually produces a broader spectrum of audible frequencies. Compare with independent information. Flight tracking services, industrial operating schedules or traffic patterns may identify ordinary sources active at the relevant time. This should be treated as corroborating evidence rather than proof, because several distant sources can overlap acoustically. What this means for reports of unexplained sky hums Low frequency humming illustrates an important limitation of human perception rather than evidence for an unusual aerial object. The combination of long wavelengths, weak directional cues and atmospheric refraction can produce a compelling illusion of a sound that is overhead, omnipresent or impossible to trace. For investigations of alleged UFO noises , this means witness confidence about hearing a hum should not automatically be interpreted as confidence about where it originated. Deep"},{"title":"Terrain Echoes","url":"/terrain-echoes/","description":"Hills, ridges and valleys can block direct noise while reflecting or channelling it from a direction that appears to be in the sky.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Sound Travel","level":3,"text":"Terrain Echoes How Terrain Makes Ground Noise Seem Airborne Sound Travel Terrain Echoes Terrain Echoes How Terrain Makes Ground Noise Seem Airborne Hills, ridges and valleys can block direct noise while reflecting or channelling it from a direction that appears to be in the sky. Hills, ridges and valleys can block direct noise while reflecting or channelling it from a direction that appears to be in the sky. population context How ridges block direct sound paths Why valleys channel engines and industrial noise Using local geography to test a reported sky sound ufo noises sound travel terrain echoes Introduction Reports of mysterious “ UFO noises ” often describe a hum, roar or droning sound that appears to come from the sky even though no aircraft or visible object can be seen. While atmospheric conditions such as temperature inversions can bend sound over long distances, local terrain frequently completes the illusion. Hills, ridges and valleys reshape the paths that sound follows, blocking direct routes while reflecting, diffracting or channelling noise from entirely different directions. The result is that an ordinary quarry, motorway, industrial plant or distant aircraft may seem to hover overhead or emerge from an empty stretch of sky rather than from its true location. Understanding these terrain effects helps explain why sincere witnesses can misjudge both the direction and apparent height of unfamiliar sounds without requiring an unusual airborne source. [Annual Reviews] annualreviews.org Annual Reviews SOUND PROPAGATION CLOSE TO THE GROUND Annual Reviews Annual ReviewsSOUND PROPAGATION CLOSE TO THE GROUND Annual ReviewsJanuary 1, 2002... Published: January 1, 2002 How ridges block direct sound paths A ridge does more than reduce the loudness of a sound source hidden behind it. It also changes which parts of the sound field reach a listener. If a hill or ridge blocks the direct line of sight between a source and an observer, the strongest sound waves are interrupted. However, lower frequency sound, such as the deep rumble of heavy machinery, aircraft engines or large diesel vehicles, bends around the obstacle through a process called diffraction. Higher frequencies are blocked more effectively, leaving a muffled sound that is harder for the human ear to locate accurately. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect On the effect of terrain profile on sound propagation outdoors On the effect of terrain profile on sound propagation outdoors ScienceDirectJanuary 8, 1985... Published: January 8, 1985 This matters because people determine the direction of a sound using small differences in timing and frequency between their ears. When the direct sound is removed and only diffracted or reflected sound remains, those directional cues become much less reliable. Instead of identifying a source hidden beyond a ridge, listeners may perceive the remaining sound as coming from above, from a nearby hillside or from open sky. Terrain also creates acoustic shadow zones . These are areas immediately behind hills where direct sound is greatly weakened. As wind and atmospheric conditions change, sound may fade in and out as it enters or leaves these shadowed regions. Someone walking only a few hundred metres may experience dramatic changes in loudness and apparent direction without the source moving at all. [Annual Reviews] annualreviews.org Annual Reviews SOUND PROPAGATION CLOSE TO THE GROUND Annual Reviews Annual ReviewsSOUND PROPAGATION CLOSE TO THE GROUND Annual ReviewsJanuary 1, 2002... Published: January 1, 2002 Refraction of Sound: How Sound Waves Bend Through Different Media Sound Physics Explained Channel: CodeLucky Open on YouTube Why valleys channel engines and industrial noise Valleys can behave like broad natural corridors that guide sound over surprisingly long distances. Rather than spreading evenly in all directions, sound travelling along a valley often encounters repeated reflections from opposing slopes. Although natural terrain is not as reflective as concrete walls, the combination of multiple reflections, reduced sideways spreading and favourable atmospheric conditions can preserve low frequency sound far beyond what would be expected over flat ground. [NASA Technical Reports Server] ntrs.nasa.gov NASA Technical Reports ServerSound propagation over uneven ground and irregular topography NASA Technical Reports Server (NTRS)... Common sources that become unusually prominent include: heavy lorries travelling through distant valleys; quarry blasting and stone crushing; rail traffic following river valleys; industrial plants located beyond intervening hills; low flying aircraft using valley routes. Low frequency components travel particularly well because they are less easily scattered by vegetation and small terrain features than higher frequencies. A distant engine may therefore be heard as a deep, directionless rumble while the higher frequency sounds that would normally reveal its location have been absorbed or blocked. [Annual Reviews] annualreviews.org Annual Reviews SOUND PROPAGATION CLOSE TO THE GROUND Annual Reviews Annual ReviewsSOUND PROPAGATION CLOSE TO THE GROUND Annual ReviewsJanuary 1, 2002... Published: January 1, 2002 In mountainous regions, this effect sometimes leads observers to believe that an unseen aircraft is circling overhead when the actual source is travelling many kilometres away along a valley outside the observer's line of sight. Why terrain can make ground noise seem airborne The illusion of an airborne source usually develops from several terrain effects acting together rather than from a single acoustic phenomenon. A typical sequence looks like this: 1. A ridge blocks the direct path between the listener and the true source. 2. Low frequency sound bends around the ridge or reflects from valley slopes. 3. Atmospheric refraction, especially during stable evening conditions, further redirects the sound. 4. The listener receives delayed or redirected sound but lacks the normal directional cues. 5. The brain assigns the source to the most plausible open direction, often the sky above or a nearby hillside. This combination explains why witnesses sometimes report a hovering hum despite finding no visible aircraft or machinery nearby. The sound is real, but its propagation path has been reshaped by both the atmosphere and the landscape. [annualreviews.org] annualreviews.org Annual Reviews SOUND PROPAGATION CLOSE TO THE GROUND Annual Reviews Annual ReviewsSOUND PROPAGATION CLOSE TO THE GROUND Annual ReviewsJanuary 1, 2002... Published: January 1, 2002 Understanding \"Acoustic Shadow\": A Deep Dive into Sound Phenomena Channel: Speak & Spell English Open on YouTube Practical examples of terrain driven misidentification Several familiar situations demonstrate how easily topography can mislead listeners. A quarry hidden behind a ridge may be inaudible at one location yet clearly audible several kilometres away where a valley opens towards the listener. Because only the low frequency components arrive, the sound resembles a distant aircraft or an unidentified aerial hum rather than industrial machinery. Similarly, motorway traffic can appear to originate from elevated ground instead of the road itself when intervening hills block direct sound but allow refracted or diffracted energy to reach neighbouring valleys. Residents in hilly regions often notice that traffic noise changes dramatically between calm evenings and windy afternoons, even though vehicle volumes remain similar. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect A Simple Model of Outdoor Noise Propagation A Simple Model of Outdoor Noise Propagation ScienceDirect... Aircraft can produce similar confusion. A jet flying beyond a mountain ridge may remain hidden from view while its engine noise follows a path around terrain features and into a valley. Conversely, an aircraft directly overhead may sound unexpectedly quiet if terrain and atmospheric conditions redirect most of its acoustic energy elsewhere. [NASA Technical Reports Server] ntrs.nasa.gov NASA Technical Reports ServerSound propagation over uneven ground and irregular topography NASA Technical Reports Server (NTRS)... Using local geography to test a reported sky sound When investigating reports of unexplained sky noises, local terrain should be examined before assuming the source was actually airborne. Useful checks include: Study elevation maps. Identify ridges, escarpments and valleys between the observation point and nearby roads, railways, airports, quarries or industrial sites. Look for hidden infrastructure. A motorway or quarry concealed behind high ground may still project sound into neighbouring valleys. Compare multiple listening locations. If moving a short distance causes the sound to weaken, strengthen or shift direction dramatically, terrain effects are a likely contributor. Note the weather. Calm evenings, light winds and stable atmospheric conditions often enhance"},{"title":"Sound Shadows","url":"/sound-shadows/","description":"Changing winds can make a visible aircraft fade, return or seem to switch its engine off without any change in the aircraft itself.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Sound Travel","level":3,"text":"Sound Shadows Why Visible Aircraft Suddenly Become Quiet Sound Travel Sound Shadows Sound Shadows Why Visible Aircraft Suddenly Become Quiet Changing winds can make a visible aircraft fade, return or seem to switch its engine off without any change in the aircraft itself. Changing winds can make a visible aircraft fade, return or seem to switch its engine off without any change in the aircraft itself. population context How wind bends sound upwind and downwind Why nearby witnesses may hear different loudness How to recognise a moving acoustic shadow ufo noises sound travel sound shadows Introduction A visible aircraft can sometimes become unexpectedly quiet even though it continues flying normally and remains in clear view. To an observer, the engine noise may seem to fade away, return a few moments later, or even appear as though the engines have briefly shut down. In many cases, this is not a change in the aircraft at all but an example of a wind driven acoustic shadow —a region where atmospheric conditions bend sound away from listeners on the ground. This phenomenon is a well established aspect of outdoor acoustics and helps explain some reports of unusual or puzzling \" UFO noises \" in which a visible aircraft does not appear to match the sounds being heard. [MDPI] mdpi.com Sound Propagation Modelling for Manned and Unmanned Aircraft Noise Assessment and Mitigation: A ReviewOctober 28, 2021... Published: October 28, 2021 How wind bends sound upwind and downwind The key factor is not simply the wind at ground level but how wind speed changes with height , a property known as wind shear . In the lower atmosphere, wind usually becomes stronger with altitude because surface friction slows the air close to the ground. Since sound travels through moving air, this changing wind profile alters the effective speed of sound at different heights. Rather than travelling in straight lines, sound waves gradually bend, or refract, as they move through these changing conditions. The result is asymmetric sound propagation: Downwind , sound rays tend to bend back towards the ground, making aircraft seem louder and audible over greater distances. Upwind , the same sound bends upward, away from listeners, creating an acoustic shadow where much less sound reaches the surface. The aircraft itself is unaffected; only the path taken by its sound changes. [mdpi.com] mdpi.com Sound Propagation Modelling for Manned and Unmanned Aircraft Noise Assessment and Mitigation: A ReviewOctober 28, 2021... Published: October 28, 2021 This behaviour explains why people standing on opposite sides of the same flight path can report dramatically different loudness from an aircraft that is equally visible to both. Aicraft flyover auralization using Atmospheric Ray Tracing Comparing two wind directions Channel: Hearing Technology and Acoustics (RWTH) Open on YouTube Why a visible aircraft can suddenly become quiet An acoustic shadow is not an all or nothing effect. Instead, it is usually a region where sound energy reaching the ground is substantially reduced. As an aircraft moves, or as the atmosphere slowly changes, a listener may pass into or out of this shadow. The experience can include: engine noise fading over several seconds; apparent silence while the aircraft remains visible; sound returning unexpectedly without any visible change in the aircraft; the impression that the engine has restarted. These observations can feel counter intuitive because people naturally expect seeing and hearing an aircraft to remain closely linked. In reality, light travels essentially unaffected through the atmosphere, while sound is continuously refracted by changing wind and temperature profiles. The aircraft therefore remains visible even as much of its sound is diverted elsewhere. [MDPI] mdpi.com Sound Propagation Modelling for Manned and Unmanned Aircraft Noise Assessment and Mitigation: A ReviewOctober 28, 2021... Published: October 28, 2021 Modern aircraft noise prediction models used by researchers increasingly include realistic vertical wind profiles precisely because these atmospheric effects can noticeably change where noise reaches the ground. Simulations have shown that weather related refraction can alter predicted aircraft noise levels by several decibels and produce distinct refractive shadow zones. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Toward inclusion of atmospheric effects in the aircraft community noise predictions PubMed... Why nearby witnesses may hear different loudness One of the strongest pieces of evidence for wind driven acoustic shadows is that nearby observers often disagree about how loud the same aircraft sounded. The atmosphere is rarely uniform. Wind direction and speed can vary with height and across short horizontal distances. As a result, the boundary between illuminated and shadowed regions is often irregular rather than sharply defined. Consequently: one witness may hear a loud engine rumble; another only a few hundred metres away may hear very little; a third may hear the sound disappear and then reappear. This does not require different aircraft, equipment failures or unusual flight manoeuvres. It follows directly from the way refracted sound intersects the ground at different locations. Outdoor acoustics research has modelled these shadow regions for decades, demonstrating that wind and temperature gradients naturally produce areas of strong and weak sound reception. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com The acoustic shadow produced by wind speed and temperature gradients close to the ground ScienceDirect... Blue Sky Science: How do temperature and wind affect traffic noise? Channel: madison.com Open on YouTube How to recognise a moving acoustic shadow Several features distinguish an atmospheric acoustic shadow from an actual change in the aircraft. A moving acoustic shadow typically shows a combination of the following characteristics: The aircraft's appearance remains steady. There is no visible reduction in speed, altitude or engine exhaust. The quiet period is gradual. Sound usually fades over seconds rather than stopping instantly. The effect is directional. Observers downwind often continue hearing the aircraft clearly while those upwind report much weaker sound. The sound returns naturally. As the aircraft or the refracted sound path changes, engine noise gradually becomes audible again. Different aircraft produce different experiences. Aircraft at different altitudes or headings can encounter different wind profiles, leading to varying amounts of refraction. Because wind fields evolve continuously, the acoustic shadow itself can drift across the landscape. From a stationary observer's perspective, it can feel as though an invisible quiet zone is moving through the area. Why this matters when assessing unusual sky sounds Reports associated with \"UFO noises\" sometimes describe a visible aircraft that appears impossibly quiet or seems to stop making noise altogether. By itself, this observation is not strong evidence of anything unusual. Wind driven acoustic shadows provide a physically understood explanation for several otherwise surprising perceptions: a visible aircraft producing little or no audible engine noise; engine sound appearing disconnected from the aircraft's position; sudden changes in loudness despite smooth, level flight; conflicting witness descriptions from nearby locations. These effects are especially plausible when atmospheric wind shear is present, because the atmosphere acts less like a transparent medium for sound and more like a shifting acoustic lens. Recognising this mechanism helps separate genuine mysteries from well established behaviour of sound travelling through a changing atmosphere. [mdpi.com] mdpi.com Sound Propagation Modelling for Manned and Unmanned Aircraft Noise Assessment and Mitigation: A ReviewOctober 28, 2021... Published: October 28, 2021 Noise Pollution L 7: Outdoor Sound Propagation Environmental Science UGC NET Channel: EnviroPioneers Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Visible Aircraft Suddenly Become Quiet. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Fundamentals of Acoustics By Lawrence E. Kinsler, Austin R. Frey et al. Dargestellt werden die physikalischen und mathematischen Konzepte zur Beschreibung der Erzeugung, Weiterleitung und Wahrnehmung von Schal... See on Amazon Book Predicting Outdoor Sound By Keith Attenborough, Kai Ming Li et al. Predicting Outdoor Sound provides a scholarly yet practical examination of the phenomena that affect outdoor sound close to the ground an... See on Amazon Book Engineering Noise Control: Theory and Practice By David A. Bies, Colin H. Hansen The practice of engineering noise control demands a solid understanding of the fundamentals of acoustics, the practical application of cu... See on Amazon Book Foundations of Engineering Acoustics By Frank J. Fahy Foundations of"},{"title":"Low Frequency","url":"/low-frequency/","description":"Deep sounds are difficult to localize, so a hum or rumble may seem to fill the sky even when it comes from distant machinery.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises","level":2,"text":"Low Frequency Why Strange Hums Seem to Come From Everywhere UFO Noises Low Frequency Low Frequency Why Strange Hums Seem to Come From Everywhere Deep sounds are difficult to localize, so a hum or rumble may seem to fill the sky even when it comes from distant machinery. Deep sounds are difficult to localize, so a hum or rumble may seem to fill the sky even when it comes from distant machinery. mechanism How humans locate sound Why bass frequencies confuse direction Indoor resonance and distant sources ufo noises low frequency Introduction Low frequency sounds are one reason that reports of mysterious \"UFO noises\" can feel especially convincing. A deep hum or rumble often seems to fill the entire environment rather than coming from one clear point. This is not because bass sounds have supernatural properties, but because the human auditory system is less precise at identifying their origin under many real world conditions. When combined with night time listening, distant machinery, atmospheric sound propagation or echoes from buildings, an ordinary source can seem to hover overhead or come from \"everywhere at once\". Research in auditory science shows that these effects arise from the way the brain interprets timing, loudness and reflections rather than from any unique characteristic of unexplained aerial phenomena. [nih.gov] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... How humans locate sound The brain normally determines the direction of a sound by comparing what reaches each ear. Two main cues are used: Interaural time difference (ITD): the tiny difference in arrival time between the ears, measured in microseconds. Interaural level difference (ILD): the difference in loudness caused by the head partially blocking sound before it reaches the far ear. Together these cues allow people to identify the horizontal direction of many everyday sounds with remarkable accuracy. Additional clues come from the shape of the outer ear, head movements and echoes from the surrounding environment, which help distinguish whether a sound is above, below, in front or behind. [nih.gov] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... Why bass frequencies confuse direction Low frequency sounds behave differently from higher pitched sounds because their wavelengths are much longer. The head casts little acoustic shadow High frequency sounds have relatively short wavelengths, so the head blocks part of the sound from reaching the far ear. This creates a noticeable loudness difference that the brain can use for localisation. Deep bass waves, however, bend around the head much more easily. Both ears therefore receive almost the same sound level, leaving the brain with far less directional information. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... Timing cues are useful but not perfect For lower frequencies, the auditory system relies mainly on tiny differences in arrival time rather than loudness. This works well under controlled conditions, but it becomes less reliable when the sound is: continuous rather than impulsive, weak compared with background noise, masked by echoes, or dominated by a narrow band of low frequencies rather than a broad spectrum. Modern hearing research shows that localisation depends on a combination of timing and level cues rather than a strict separation between low and high frequencies. Nevertheless, low frequency sounds generally provide less precise directional information than sounds containing strong higher frequency components. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Transaural experiments and a revised duplex theory for the localization of low frequency tones PMCFebruary 24, 2016... Published: February 24, 2016 Is YOUR SUBWOOFER facing the WRONG way? How we locate sounds explained! Channel: CarAudioFabrication Open on YouTube Why a distant hum can seem to fill the sky Many sounds associated with reported \"sky hums\" originate from sources that are both distant and rich in low frequencies. Examples include: heavy industrial equipment, electrical infrastructure, large ventilation systems, diesel engines, ships or trains many kilometres away, and aircraft heard from long distances. As sound travels, higher frequencies are absorbed by the atmosphere more readily than lower ones. By the time the sound reaches a distant listener, much of the sharper, more directional content has disappeared, leaving mainly the low frequency rumble. Without those higher frequency cues, the brain struggles to determine where the sound began. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... This is one reason people may describe a mysterious hum as surrounding them or coming from directly overhead even when the physical source is on the ground and several kilometres away. Indoor resonance and reflected sound Buildings can make localisation even more difficult. Low frequency waves readily excite resonances in: walls, floors, ceilings, window panes, and large rooms. Instead of hearing a single incoming wave, listeners receive a mixture of direct sound and multiple reflections arriving from different directions. Standing waves can also create \"hot spots\" where the bass seems unusually strong in one part of a room and much weaker a few steps away. The result is that the sound appears detached from its true source. A distant industrial fan, for example, may seem to come from the ceiling, a corner of the room or nowhere identifiable at all because the room itself is vibrating in response to the incoming low frequency energy. Research into indoor acoustics consistently shows that reflections and reverberation reduce localisation accuracy, particularly at low frequencies. [Institute of Acoustics] ioa.org.uk Institute of AcousticsProceedings of the Institute of AcousticsLOW FREQUENCY SOUND SOURCE LOCALIZATION ASMarch 26, 2026... Published: March 26, 2026 How Sound Localization Works: Timing + Intensity Differences 20th Week in Medical School Channel: Doctor Rex Open on YouTube Why mysterious hums are often reported at night Night time conditions can make these effects more noticeable without requiring an unusual source. Several factors combine: everyday background noise falls, making faint low frequency sounds easier to notice; temperature and wind profiles can alter how sound propagates over long distances; visual reference points are reduced, making people more dependent on hearing; and the absence of clear directional cues encourages the impression that the sound is widespread rather than localised. These conditions do not create mysterious sounds by themselves, but they can make ordinary low frequency sources seem unfamiliar and difficult to place. What this means for reports of UFO noises The fact that a humming sound seems to come from everywhere is not, by itself, evidence of an aerial or unexplained source. Auditory research provides a well supported explanation for why deep sounds are frequently mislocalised. Low frequency energy supplies fewer reliable directional cues, travels efficiently over long distances, survives atmospheric filtering better than higher frequencies, and is easily altered by reflections from terrain and buildings. Under those circumstances, a listener may sincerely perceive a hum as filling the sky even when it originates from ordinary infrastructure beyond immediate view. This perceptual limitation helps explain why reports of unexplained humming are common in UFO accounts while remaining entirely consistent with established principles of acoustics and human hearing. [nih.gov] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... Interaural time difference and how to find your phone instantly Channel: Steve Mould Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Strange Hums Seem to Come From Everywhere. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book An Introduction to the Psychology of Hearing By Brian C. J. Moore Now available in a Fourth Edition, Introduction to the Psychology of Hearing is the leading textbook in the field of auditory perception ... See on Amazon Book Spatial Hearing: The Psychophysics of Human Sound Localization By Jens Blauert The field of spatial hearing has exploded in the decade or so since Jens Blauert's classic work on acoustics was first published in Engli... See on Amazon Book The Universal Sense: How Hearing Shapes the Mind By Seth Horowitz A surprising, entertaining book about how what we hear with our ears affects what's between them. See on Amazon Book Why You Hear What You Hear: An Experiential Approach to Sound... By Eric J. Heller First published 2012. See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: An to the Psychology of Hearing books Spatial Hearing: The Psychophysics of Human Sound"},{"title":"Bass Direction","url":"/bass-direction/","description":"Long bass wavelengths bend around the head, so both ears receive similar sound levels and the source becomes harder to place.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Low Frequency","level":3,"text":"Bass Direction Why Your Ears Struggle to Place Deep Bass Low Frequency Bass Direction Bass Direction Why Your Ears Struggle to Place Deep Bass Long bass wavelengths bend around the head, so both ears receive similar sound levels and the source becomes harder to place. Long bass wavelengths bend around the head, so both ears receive similar sound levels and the source becomes harder to place. mechanism How the head creates a sound shadow Why long wavelengths reach both ears evenly What missing loudness differences mean for UFO hum reports ufo noises low frequency bass direction Introduction One reason deep, low frequency sounds are so difficult to locate is that they largely defeat one of the brain's most important directional tools: comparing how loud a sound is at each ear. For many everyday sounds, the head creates an acoustic \"shadow\", making the sound slightly quieter at the ear furthest from the source. The brain interprets this loudness difference—known as the interaural level difference (ILD) —to determine where the sound is coming from. With deep bass, however, this cue almost disappears because the sound's long wavelengths bend around the head instead of being blocked by it. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMCJuly 10, 2024... Published: July 10, 2024 This mechanism helps explain why low frequency rumbles often seem to come from \"everywhere\" rather than a single location. In reports of mysterious UFO hums or sky noises, the absence of strong loudness differences can make an ordinary distant source feel detached from the ground, even though nothing unusual is happening acoustically. How the Head Creates a Sound Shadow The human head is an obstacle to sound waves, but not equally for every frequency. High frequency sounds have short wavelengths that are similar in size to, or smaller than, the width of the head. When these sounds arrive from one side, the head blocks and reflects part of the energy before it reaches the far ear. As a result: the nearer ear receives a stronger signal, the farther ear receives a weaker signal, the brain measures this difference in level and estimates the sound's direction. This acoustic shadow grows larger as frequency increases and as the sound source moves further from directly in front of the listener. It is one of the principal reasons why the direction of a whistle, bird call or snapping twig can often be identified almost instantly. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMCJuly 10, 2024... Published: July 10, 2024 Is YOUR SUBWOOFER facing the WRONG way? How we locate sounds explained! Channel: CarAudioFabrication Open on YouTube Why Long Wavelengths Reach Both Ears Almost Equally Deep bass behaves differently because its wavelengths are enormous compared with the size of the human head. For example: a 100 Hz tone has a wavelength of roughly 3.4 metres, a 50 Hz tone stretches to about 6.9 metres, both are vastly larger than a head measuring only around 17–20 centimetres across. When an obstacle is much smaller than a wave's wavelength, the wave diffracts—it bends around the obstacle instead of being strongly blocked. The head therefore casts very little acoustic shadow for these frequencies. Nearly the same sound level reaches both ears, leaving almost no useful ILD for the auditory system to measure. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMCJuly 10, 2024... Published: July 10, 2024 Rather than producing two noticeably different signals, deep bass delivers two remarkably similar ones. What Missing Loudness Differences Mean The absence of a strong loudness difference does not make bass impossible to localise, but it removes one of the brain's most reliable directional cues. Instead of comparing sound levels, the auditory system must depend much more heavily on tiny differences in arrival time between the ears, known as interaural time differences (ITDs) . These timing cues work well under ideal listening conditions, especially for low frequency sounds, but they are easier to disrupt when a sound is: continuous rather than sharply starting, masked by environmental noise, reflected from buildings or terrain, dominated by a narrow band of low frequencies. Without the supporting evidence provided by ILDs, directional judgements become less certain, and perceived source locations become broader or more ambiguous. [nih.gov] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... Importantly, modern auditory research shows that localisation is not governed by a rigid split between timing cues for bass and loudness cues for treble. Instead, the brain combines multiple cues whenever they are available. The difficulty with deep bass is that one of those cues—the loudness difference—is physically much weaker to begin with. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMCJuly 10, 2024... Published: July 10, 2024 How Sound Localization Works: Timing + Intensity Differences 20th Week in Medical School Channel: Doctor Rex Open on YouTube Why This Matters for Reports of UFO Hums Many reports of mysterious aerial hums describe a low, steady vibration that seems impossible to point towards. The missing ILD cue contributes directly to that impression. Imagine a distant industrial fan, large diesel engine or ventilation system producing mostly low frequency energy. By the time the sound reaches a listener: higher frequency components may have been absorbed more strongly by the atmosphere, reflections from buildings or the landscape may blur timing information, the remaining bass reaches both ears at almost identical levels. Because neither ear receives a clearly louder signal, listeners often cannot confidently identify a horizontal direction. The sound may instead seem to surround them, drift overhead or appear to fill the entire environment rather than originate from a specific point. This perceptual ambiguity can make an ordinary terrestrial source feel unusually mysterious, especially at night when visual reference points are limited. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... Bass Direction Is Reduced, Not Eliminated A common misconception is that humans cannot localise bass at all. That is not correct. Low frequency sounds can still be located using timing differences, head movements and, when available, higher frequency harmonics that accompany the bass. Problems arise when the sound contains little besides deep low frequencies or when echoes and background noise degrade the remaining cues. This is why a full range sound containing both bass and higher frequencies is generally much easier to place than an isolated low frequency rumble. The high frequency content restores the head shadow effect, giving the brain the loudness differences it needs for more confident localisation. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... ITD vs ILD Channel: Robert Willey Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Your Ears Struggle to Place Deep Bass. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book An Introduction to the Psychology of Hearing By Brian C. J. Moore Now available in a Fourth Edition, Introduction to the Psychology of Hearing is the leading textbook in the field of auditory perception ... See on Amazon Book Spatial Hearing: The Psychophysics of Human Sound Localization By Jens Blauert The field of spatial hearing has exploded in the decade or so since Jens Blauert's classic work on acoustics was first published in Engli... See on Amazon Book Auditory Perception: An Analysis and Synthesis By Richard M. Warren This revised and updated third edition describes the nature of sound, how sound is analyzed by the auditory system, and the rules and pri... See on Amazon Book Why You Hear What You Hear: An Experiential Approach to Sound... By Eric J. Heller First published 2012. See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: An to the Psychology of Hearing books Spatial Hearing: The Psychophysics of Human Sound Localization Auditory Perception: An Analysis and Synthesis As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. eBay marketplace picks Marketplace Samples Live tested eBay searches with available results related to this page. Shop location Using USA USA USA UK Canada Australia Ireland USA"},{"title":"Distant Rumbles","url":"/distant-rumbles/","description":"Atmospheric travel removes much of a distant sound's sharper content, leaving a bass-heavy rumble with few clues to its source.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Low Frequency","level":3,"text":"Distant Rumbles How Distant Noise Loses Its Sense of Direction Low Frequency Distant Rumbles Distant Rumbles How Distant Noise Loses Its Sense of Direction Atmospheric travel removes much of a distant sound's sharper content, leaving a bass heavy rumble with few clues to its source. Atmospheric travel removes much of a distant sound's sharper content, leaving a bass heavy rumble with few clues to its source. event time window Why high frequencies fade faster over distance How bass survives from remote ground sources Why filtered rumbles can be mistaken for sky sounds ufo noises low frequency distant rumbles Introduction One reason distant noises are often reported as mysterious \"UFO sounds\" is that long distance travel changes what reaches the listener. Over several kilometres, the atmosphere removes much of a sound's higher frequency content while allowing lower frequency energy to travel much farther. The result is a deep, bass heavy rumble that has lost many of the directional clues the human auditory system normally relies on. A train, industrial fan, cargo ship or distant aircraft may therefore sound detached from its true location, appearing to come from the sky, the horizon or even from \"everywhere at once\" rather than from a single identifiable source. This effect is well understood in acoustics and hearing science and helps explain why remote, ordinary sound sources can be perceived as unfamiliar or difficult to locate. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... Why High Frequencies Fade Faster Over Distance Air does not transmit every frequency equally well. As sound propagates, higher frequencies are absorbed more strongly by the atmosphere than lower frequencies. The amount depends on humidity, temperature and atmospheric pressure, but the overall trend is consistent: sharp, high pitched components weaken much faster than deep bass. This selective filtering matters because many everyday sound sources contain a broad range of frequencies. For example: A diesel locomotive produces deep engine pulses alongside metallic wheel noise and higher frequency mechanical sounds. A large industrial compressor emits both a low frequency hum and higher pitched motor components. An aircraft engine generates broadband noise extending from bass into high frequencies. Close to the source, these combined frequencies help listeners recognise both the type of sound and its direction. After travelling long distances, however, much of the higher frequency information has disappeared, leaving mainly the lower frequency rumble. The sound becomes less distinctive and much harder to place accurately. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... Is YOUR SUBWOOFER facing the WRONG way? How we locate sounds explained! Channel: CarAudioFabrication Open on YouTube How Bass Survives From Remote Ground Sources Low frequency sound has wavelengths measured in metres. These long wavelengths interact differently with obstacles and the atmosphere than shorter wavelengths. Because of their size, bass waves: diffract, or bend, around buildings, hills and vegetation more effectively; lose less energy to atmospheric absorption over long distances; can continue propagating after higher frequency components have largely disappeared. This does not mean low frequencies travel without loss. Their level still decreases with distance through geometric spreading and ground interaction. However, compared with higher frequency sound, they often remain the dominant part of the signal over several kilometres. Large ground based sources therefore become acoustically \"simplified\" with distance. What began as a complex industrial or transport sound may eventually resemble a smooth, continuous low frequency drone with few identifying features. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... Why Filtered Rumbles Can Be Mistaken for Sky Sounds Human directional hearing depends heavily on comparing what reaches each ear. When higher frequencies are present, the head creates an acoustic shadow, making the nearer ear receive a noticeably louder signal. This interaural level difference is one of the strongest directional cues available. After atmospheric filtering removes much of that high frequency content, several problems arise: the sound reaching both ears becomes much more similar; remaining directional information relies mainly on tiny timing differences; continuous low frequency rumbles provide fewer distinct features for the brain to analyse. The result is increased uncertainty rather than complete inability to localise. Instead of confidently pointing to a factory, railway or distant aircraft, listeners may simply perceive the sound as surrounding them or hovering somewhere above. Hearing research shows that localisation becomes progressively less precise when these spectral cues are reduced, especially for sustained low frequency sounds in real outdoor environments. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMC... Where is Sound Coming From? How Humans Use Sound LOCALIZATION Channel: Doctor Cliff, AuD Open on YouTube Why \"Overhead\" Is a Common Impression Reports of unexplained aerial noises often describe a sound that seems suspended in the sky despite having no visible source. Several factors contribute to this perception : No obvious horizontal direction. With weakened localisation cues, the listener cannot confidently assign the sound to a point on the ground. Broad environmental coverage. Low frequency waves can arrive along multiple paths after bending around terrain and structures, making the sound feel widespread rather than point like. Lack of visual confirmation. When no obvious train, ship or industrial site is visible, the brain searches for alternative explanations. The perception of an overhead source therefore does not necessarily indicate that the source is actually airborne. Instead, it reflects uncertainty created by the loss of directional information during long distance propagation. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMC... Everyday Sources That Change Character With Distance Several common sources illustrate this transformation particularly well. Freight trains may lose the distinctive clatter of wheels and retain mainly the deep engine or rolling rumble, making them sound like a distant continuous vibration. Ships operating many kilometres offshore often produce persistent low frequency engine noise that travels inland after much of the sharper machinery noise has been absorbed. Industrial facilities such as refineries, mines or large ventilation systems frequently emit broadband noise. At long range, listeners may hear only a featureless hum that bears little resemblance to the original machinery. Aircraft can also change dramatically with distance. Rather than the familiar close range engine sound, a remote aircraft may produce a diffuse low frequency rumble that is difficult to connect with the visible object—or may be heard before the aircraft is noticed at all. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... Refraction of Sound: How Sound Waves Bend Through Different Media Sound Physics Explained Channel: CodeLucky Open on YouTube Why This Matters When Interpreting Unusual Noise Reports Distance acts as a natural acoustic filter. It removes many of the spectral details that identify a sound source while leaving behind the frequencies that humans find hardest to localise precisely. This combination can make entirely ordinary machinery, transport or industrial activity sound unfamiliar and directionless. For reports of unexplained \" UFO noises \", this mechanism offers a well supported physical explanation for why witnesses sometimes describe deep rumbles as coming from nowhere in particular or from directly overhead. The effect does not require an unusual sound source. Instead, it arises because atmospheric propagation strips away much of the information the auditory system normally uses to determine where a sound originates. [nih.gov] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIBasics of Sound, the Ear, and Hearing Hearing Loss NCBI Bookshelf... Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to How Distant Noise Loses Its Sense of Direction. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Sound Book By Trevor Cox A professor of acoustic engineering provides a tour of the world's most amazing sound phenomena, including creaking glaciers, whispering... See on Amazon Book Master Handbook of Acoustics By F. Alton Everest,"},{"title":"Night Hums","url":"/night-hums/","description":"Quieter surroundings, altered sound propagation and fewer visual references can make ordinary low-frequency noise seem unusually broad at night.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Low Frequency","level":3,"text":"Night Hums Why Mysterious Hums Feel Bigger After Dark Low Frequency Night Hums Night Hums Why Mysterious Hums Feel Bigger After Dark Quieter surroundings, altered sound propagation and fewer visual references can make ordinary low frequency noise seem unusually broad at night. Quieter surroundings, altered sound propagation and fewer visual references can make ordinary low frequency noise seem unusually broad at night. population context How falling background noise reveals faint bass How night air can carry distant sound Why darkness removes useful location clues ufo noises low frequency night hums Introduction Reports of mysterious low frequency \"UFO noises\" often become more common after dark, but night itself does not create unexplained hums. Instead, several ordinary effects combine to make distant bass rich sounds seem louder, broader and much harder to locate. Traffic and daytime activity decline, leaving faint low frequency sounds exposed. Under suitable weather conditions, the atmosphere can also carry some sounds farther than during the day. At the same time, darkness removes many visual cues that the brain normally uses to confirm where a sound originates. The result is that an otherwise ordinary industrial fan, ship engine, ventilation system or distant transport source may appear to surround the listener or hover overhead rather than coming from a specific point. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)An Overview of the Major Phenomena of the Localization of Sound Sources by Normal Hearing, Hearing Impaired, and Aide... How quieter nights reveal sounds that daytime hides One of the biggest changes after sunset is not that low frequency sounds become dramatically stronger, but that competing sounds become weaker. During the day, nearby traffic, aircraft, conversation, construction and wind generated noise mask faint background hums. When those higher level sounds diminish at night, the same distant low frequency source can suddenly become noticeable even if it has not increased in volume. This change in contrast is especially important for bass frequencies. Deep tones are often produced continuously by machinery such as industrial compressors, large ventilation systems, electrical infrastructure, marine engines and transport networks. During busy daylight hours they blend into the overall environmental soundscape. At night they can emerge as a distinct, persistent hum simply because there is less masking noise. This explains why people frequently report hearing a mysterious hum only after dark, even when measurements show that the underlying source has been operating continuously throughout the day. How night air can carry distant sound Night time weather sometimes alters how sound travels , although the effect depends on local conditions and should not be assumed every evening. Temperature Inversions Channel: Met Office Learn About Weather Open on YouTube Temperature inversions can bend sound downward After sunset, the ground often cools faster than the air above it, creating a nocturnal temperature inversion. Instead of warm air near the surface and cooler air higher up, the pattern briefly reverses. Meteorologists recognise this as a common overnight atmospheric condition. [National Weather Service] forecast.weather.gov National Weather Service NOAA's National Weather Service National Weather ServiceNOAA's National Weather Service Glossary... Because the speed of sound varies with air temperature, these layers can refract, or bend, sound waves. Under favourable conditions, sound that would normally disperse upward can instead remain closer to the ground, allowing distant sources to be heard over greater distances. The effect is well known in environmental acoustics and helps explain why trains, ships, industrial facilities or major roads may sound unexpectedly prominent on some calm nights but not on others. Low frequencies survive long journeys better Even without an inversion, low frequency sound generally loses less energy to atmospheric absorption than higher frequencies over long distances. As the sharper, higher pitched components fade, what remains is often a deep rumble with relatively poor directional information. This selective survival means a listener may hear only the bass portion of a distant machine. Without the higher frequency details that normally help identify location, the sound can seem detached from its true source and appear to fill the surrounding environment rather than arriving from one direction. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMC... Why darkness removes useful location clues Humans rarely rely on hearing alone to determine where a sound comes from. Vision constantly reinforces auditory perception . When a lorry passes, an aircraft crosses the sky or factory machinery is visible in the distance, the brain combines what it sees with what it hears to produce a stable interpretation. Research on auditory localisation shows that visual information significantly influences perceived sound position, often improving localisation accuracy. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)An Overview of the Major Phenomena of the Localization of Sound Sources by Normal Hearing, Hearing Impaired, and Aide... At night many of those confirming cues disappear. Buildings, roads and moving vehicles become difficult to see, particularly when the sound source is kilometres away or hidden behind terrain. Without visual confirmation, listeners are more likely to experience: uncertainty about whether the sound is in front, behind or overhead; difficulty estimating distance; the impression that the sound surrounds them rather than coming from one location; and [youtube.com] youtube.com Where is Sound Coming From? How Humans Use Sound LOCALIZATION Engineering Acoustics: Outdoor Sound Propagation... repeated changes in perceived direction as they listen. These effects are particularly noticeable for continuous low frequency hums because they already contain fewer localisation cues than broadband sounds. Refraction of Sound: How Sound Waves Bend Through Different Media Sound Physics Explained Channel: CodeLucky Open on YouTube Why low hums seem to come from \"everywhere\" A persistent bass tone gives the auditory system relatively little information compared with a short, broadband sound such as a hand clap or a bird call. Low frequency sounds produce only small differences in loudness between the ears because their long wavelengths wrap around the head rather than being blocked by it. The brain therefore relies heavily on subtle timing differences and on additional cues such as reflections, head movements and visual information. When some of those cues are missing or degraded, localisation becomes much less reliable. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMC... At night, the combination of reduced background noise, occasional favourable atmospheric propagation and missing visual references can therefore produce a powerful illusion that a distant hum is spread across the entire landscape. This helps explain why witnesses sometimes describe a mysterious sound as \"coming from the sky\", \"everywhere at once\" or \"impossible to point at\", even though the source may remain firmly on the ground. Why night time reports do not necessarily indicate an unusual source Many reports associated with alleged UFO noises begin with an honest observation: the sound was clearer, stranger or more intrusive after dark. However, that timing alone does not distinguish an ordinary source from an extraordinary one. Environmental acoustics provides several well understood mechanisms that make familiar sounds seem unfamiliar at night, especially when they are rich in low frequencies and originate at considerable distance. For investigators, the important question is therefore not simply whether the sound occurred after sunset, but whether it remains consistent with known acoustic behaviour. In many cases, quieter surroundings, occasional atmospheric refraction and the loss of visual orientation together provide a sufficient explanation for why an otherwise ordinary hum feels unusually widespread and difficult to identify. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)An Overview of the Major Phenomena of the Localization of Sound Sources by Normal Hearing, Hearing Impaired, and Aide... Where is Sound Coming From? How Humans Use Sound LOCALIZATION Channel: Doctor Cliff, AuD Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Mysterious Hums Feel Bigger After Dark. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Sound Book"},{"title":"Room Resonance","url":"/room-resonance/","description":"Walls, floors and windows can resonate with incoming bass, making an outdoor source appear to come from the ceiling or room itself.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Low Frequency","level":3,"text":"Room Resonance Why An Outside Hum Can Seem Inside Low Frequency Room Resonance Room Resonance Why An Outside Hum Can Seem Inside Walls, floors and windows can resonate with incoming bass, making an outdoor source appear to come from the ceiling or room itself. Walls, floors and windows can resonate with incoming bass, making an outdoor source appear to come from the ceiling or room itself. population context How building surfaces vibrate with low frequencies Why reflections detach sound from its source How to identify standing wave hot spots in a room ufo noises low frequency room resonance Introduction A common reason that reported \" UFO noises \" seem to come from inside a house is that the building itself changes how low frequency sound is heard. Deep outdoor hums from distant machinery, transport or ventilation systems can cause walls, floors, ceilings and windows to vibrate slightly. At the same time, reflections inside enclosed spaces create standing waves that strengthen the sound in some places and weaken it in others. The result is that an external source can appear to originate from the ceiling, a corner of the room or even from inside the listener's own home rather than from outdoors. This effect is well understood in architectural acoustics and helps explain why the location of a persistent low frequency hum can be surprisingly difficult to identify. [Aalto University's research portal] research.aalto.fi Aalto University's research portalThe Impact of Standing Waves on Localization Ability of Low Frequency Sound Sources Aalto University'... How building surfaces vibrate with low frequencies Low frequency sound has wavelengths measured in several metres, allowing it to interact with entire rooms rather than just individual objects. Instead of simply passing through an open window as a clearly identifiable sound, bass energy can excite structural elements of the building. Large surfaces such as plasterboard walls, timber floors, concrete slabs and window panes all have natural vibration frequencies. When incoming sound contains energy near those frequencies, the structure may resonate and re radiate the sound into the room. The listener therefore hears not only the original airborne sound but also vibrations emitted by the building itself. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Infrasonic sound pressure in dwellings at the Helmholtz resonance actuated by environmental noise and vibration ScienceDirect... This explains why someone may notice that: a window seems to \"hum\" even though the sound source is outside; a suspended ceiling appears to vibrate faintly; one wall sounds louder than another despite facing away from the source; or the hum is strongest when touching a floor or partition. The effect is usually subtle rather than dramatic, but because the vibrating surface surrounds the listener, the brain may attribute the sound to the room instead of to a distant outdoor source. Standing Waves and Harmonics Channel: Professor Dave Explains Open on YouTube Why reflections detach sound from its source Indoors, the listener rarely hears only the direct sound arriving from outside. Every hard surface reflects part of the incoming wave. With higher pitched sounds these reflections are brief and directional cues remain relatively strong. Deep continuous hums behave differently. Their long wavelengths produce overlapping reflections that persist throughout the room, reducing the contrast between the direct sound and its echoes. Instead of pointing clearly towards the window or exterior wall, the sound field becomes diffuse. Because the auditory system already struggles to determine the direction of narrow band low frequency sounds, the additional reflected energy can make localisation even less reliable. Rather than identifying an outdoor origin, listeners often report that the hum seems to fill the room or hover overhead. [Aalto University's research portal] research.aalto.fi Aalto University's research portalThe Impact of Standing Waves on Localization Ability of Low Frequency Sound Sources Aalto University'... The practical consequence is that moving only a short distance may completely change the perceived direction of the sound, even though the external source has not moved at all. Why standing waves create indoor \"hot spots\" One of the most important room effects is the standing wave, also called a room mode. When low frequency sound repeatedly reflects between parallel walls, floor and ceiling, certain frequencies reinforce themselves. This creates fixed regions where sound pressure is unusually high (antinodes) and others where it is greatly reduced (nodes). Instead of experiencing a uniform hum throughout the house, a person may find that: one corner is noticeably louder than the centre of the room; the sound almost disappears a metre away; an upstairs bedroom hums while the room below does not; or a hallway seems quieter despite being closer to the outside wall. Recent listening experiments show that these standing wave patterns can significantly impair a person's ability to judge where a very low frequency sound is coming from. Interestingly, locations near pressure nodes—where the room cancels much of the direct sound—can be especially disruptive to localisation, because the reflected sound dominates what reaches the ears. [Aalto University's research portal] research.aalto.fi Aalto University's research portalThe Impact of Standing Waves on Localization Ability of Low Frequency Sound Sources Aalto University'... In other words, the room itself becomes part of the acoustic signal. What Are Standing Waves In Rooms? www.AcousticFields.com Channel: Acoustic Fields Open on YouTube How to identify standing wave hot spots in a room A practical way to distinguish room resonance from a genuinely indoor source is to observe how the sound changes with position. Signs that room modes are influencing what you hear include: Large changes over short distances. Walking half a metre can transform a loud hum into a barely audible one. Different rooms behaving differently. A bedroom may resonate strongly while the kitchen remains relatively quiet. Frequency specific behaviour. Only one or two bass notes seem exaggerated instead of all low sounds. Little correlation with windows or doors. The loudest point is not necessarily nearest the outside. Simple measurements with a calibrated microphone or acoustic analysis software often reveal narrow frequency peaks that match expected room modes. Professional acousticians use these measurements to separate structural resonance from the characteristics of the external sound source itself. [Tantrum Audio] tantrumaudio.co.uk Tantrum Audio Understanding Room Modes in Studio Acoustics Tantrum Audio Tantrum Audio Understanding Room Modes in Studio Acoustics Tantrum Audio Why this matters when investigating mysterious hums Reports of unexplained hums frequently describe sounds that seem to come from \"inside the walls\", \"the ceiling\", or \"everywhere at once\". These descriptions are consistent with known acoustic behaviour and do not necessarily indicate that the sound originated within the building. A distant industrial fan, electrical installation, transport corridor or other low frequency source may enter through the building envelope, excite structural vibration, and then be reshaped by room resonances before reaching the listener. By the time the brain interprets the sound, many of the directional cues that normally reveal its origin have been altered or lost. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Infrasonic sound pressure in dwellings at the Helmholtz resonance actuated by environmental noise and vibration ScienceDirect... Within the broader discussion of why low frequency sounds seem directionless, room resonance provides an important final piece of the puzzle. It shows that even when the source is entirely ordinary and located outdoors, the acoustics of an enclosed space can relocate the perceived origin, making an external hum feel as though it has emerged from the building itself. [Aalto University's research portal] research.aalto.fi Aalto University's research portalThe Impact of Standing Waves on Localization Ability of Low Frequency Sound Sources Aalto University'... How Sound Works (In Rooms) Channel: Acoustic Geometry Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why An Outside Hum Can Seem Inside. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Master Handbook of Acoustics By F. Alton Everest, Ken Pohlmann Practical Instruction on the Art and Science of Acoustic Design and Architecture Build your own acoustic environments such as recording s... See on Amazon Book The Sound Book By Trevor Cox A professor of acoustic engineering provides a tour of the world's most amazing sound phenomena, including creaking glaciers, whispering... See on Amazon Book Architectural Acoustics By Marshall Long Architectural Acoustics, Second"},{"title":"Steady Hums","url":"/steady-hums/","description":"Continuous narrow-band hums give the brain fewer clear timing landmarks than knocks, clicks or other sudden broadband sounds.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Low Frequency","level":3,"text":"Steady Hums Why A Steady Hum Has No Clear Origin Low Frequency Steady Hums Steady Hums Why A Steady Hum Has No Clear Origin Continuous narrow band hums give the brain fewer clear timing landmarks than knocks, clicks or other sudden broadband sounds. Continuous narrow band hums give the brain fewer clear timing landmarks than knocks, clicks or other sudden broadband sounds. mechanism Why sudden sounds provide stronger timing clues How narrow frequency bands create directional ambiguity Why background noise and echoes worsen continuous hums ufo noises low frequency steady hums Introduction When people describe a mysterious \"UFO hum\" as coming from everywhere at once, the continuous nature of the sound is often as important as its low frequency . The human auditory system is generally much better at locating a brief knock, click or bang than an unbroken, narrow band hum. Sudden sounds provide clear timing landmarks that allow the brain to compare what reaches each ear, whereas a steady tone offers far fewer distinct reference points. If that hum is also low in frequency, distant or reflected by buildings, directional information becomes even less reliable. Rather than indicating an unusual source, this combination of acoustic properties can make an ordinary sound seem detached from any obvious origin. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMedJuly 10, 2024... Published: July 10, 2024 Why sudden sounds provide stronger timing clues A short, broadband sound such as a hand clap, hammer strike or door slam contains a rapid onset and energy across many frequencies. Those features give the auditory system multiple independent cues to compare between the two ears. The brain can measure tiny differences in arrival time—known as interaural time differences (ITDs)—and combine them with loudness differences and frequency dependent filtering caused by the head and outer ears to estimate direction with remarkable precision. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMedJuly 10, 2024... Published: July 10, 2024 A continuous hum is different. Once the sound has settled into a stable tone, there is no repeating \"start\" for the brain to use as a fresh timing reference. Instead, the auditory system must rely on the ongoing phase or fine structure of the waveform, which becomes more vulnerable to ambiguity, masking and reverberation. This does not mean continuous sounds cannot be localised, but they generally provide weaker spatial information than abrupt sounds containing rich spectral content. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Historically, psychoacoustic experiments consistently showed that listeners identify the direction of clicks, impulses and broadband noise bursts more accurately than isolated pure tones. This distinction has become a cornerstone of modern auditory localisation research because it demonstrates that sound duration alone is not the deciding factor; the richness of the available timing and frequency cues is equally important. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMedJuly 10, 2024... Published: July 10, 2024 How Your Brain Uses Sound to Map Space Channel: Oddment Open on YouTube How narrow frequency bands create directional ambiguity Many reported environmental hums are dominated by a relatively narrow range of low frequencies rather than a wide spectrum. That limits the amount of directional information reaching the brain. Several mechanisms contribute: Few independent localisation cues: A narrow band tone contains much less spectral information than broadband sound, reducing opportunities for the brain to cross check direction. Phase ambiguity: Pure or nearly pure tones can produce similar phase relationships at more than one possible source direction, making some positions difficult to distinguish. Minimal head shadow: At low frequencies, long wavelengths bend around the head, so both ears receive nearly the same sound level, weakening interaural level differences. Reduced spectral shaping: High frequency components normally interact with the folds of the outer ear, helping distinguish front from back and above from below. A low, narrow band hum lacks much of this information. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMC... The result is not complete loss of localisation but greater uncertainty. Listeners may know that a hum is present without confidently identifying whether it originates ahead, behind, above or from one side. Why background noise and echoes worsen continuous hums Real environments rarely contain only one sound. Wind, traffic, ventilation systems and other background noises partially mask steady tones, making already subtle localisation cues even harder to detect. Reflections further complicate the problem. Indoors, walls, ceilings and floors create multiple delayed copies of the original sound. Outdoors, large buildings, hillsides or other structures can produce similar reflections. Because a continuous hum never truly stops, these reflected waves overlap with the direct sound instead of arriving as clearly separated events. The auditory system must interpret a constantly changing mixture rather than one clean directional signal. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Research on binaural hearing has shown that reduced interaural coherence—the similarity of what reaches each ear after reflections—degrades sensitivity to timing cues. As coherence falls, listeners become less certain about source direction even when the sound itself remains audible. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. This helps explain why a distant industrial hum or large ventilation system may seem to \"fill the air\" instead of pointing back to a single building. Auditory Localization and the Precedence Effect Channel: Abilene Prewitt Open on YouTube Why pulses often reveal a source that hums conceal A useful comparison is the difference between hearing an idling diesel engine and hearing a hammer striking metal nearby. The engine produces a largely continuous low frequency sound. Even if it is loud, the listener may struggle to judge its exact location because the signal changes slowly over time and contains relatively weak localisation cues. A hammer strike, by contrast, generates: a sudden onset, broadband energy extending into higher frequencies, distinct timing information, and a short duration that separates the direct sound from later echoes. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Sound Localization Sound Localization an overview ScienceDirect Topics... These characteristics allow the auditory system to identify the source far more accurately, often within a few degrees under favourable conditions. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMedJuly 10, 2024... Published: July 10, 2024 Why this matters when interpreting reported UFO hums Many reports of unexplained aerial hums describe a sound that appears stationary, overhead or impossible to trace. The acoustic properties of a continuous, low frequency hum provide a well established explanation for why such impressions occur without requiring an unusual source. When a sound is steady instead of impulsive, dominated by a narrow low frequency band instead of broad frequencies, and further degraded by distance, background noise or reflections, the auditory system loses many of the cues it normally uses to determine direction. In those circumstances, the sound may genuinely seem to come from everywhere at once even though it originates from an ordinary terrestrial source. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMedJuly 10, 2024... Published: July 10, 2024 Where is Sound Coming From? How Humans Use Sound LOCALIZATION Channel: Doctor Cliff, AuD Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why A Steady Hum Has No Clear Origin. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Sound Book By Trevor Cox A professor of acoustic engineering provides a tour of the world's most amazing sound phenomena, including creaking glaciers, whispering... See on Amazon Book An Introduction to the Psychology of Hearing By Brian C. J. Moore Now available in a Fourth Edition, Introduction to the Psychology of Hearing is the"},{"title":"Earth Booms","url":"/earth-booms/","description":"Some unexplained booms blamed on objects in the sky may originate from small earthquakes or regional acoustic events known as Seneca Guns.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises","level":2,"text":"Earth Booms Can Earthquakes Sound Like UFOs Overhead? UFO Noises Earth Booms Earth Booms Can Earthquakes Sound Like UFOs Overhead? Some unexplained booms blamed on objects in the sky may originate from small earthquakes or regional acoustic events known as Seneca Guns. Some unexplained booms blamed on objects in the sky may originate from small earthquakes or regional acoustic events known as Seneca Guns. event time window What earthquake booms sound like The mystery of Seneca Guns How ground events seem airborne ufo noises earth booms Introduction People sometimes report a deafening boom overhead, look into the sky expecting an aircraft or meteor, and instead find nothing at all. These incidents have often become folded into UFO folklore because the sound appears to come from above and may be accompanied by little or no obvious ground movement. However, geology provides an important alternative explanation. Small, very shallow earthquakes can produce audible booming sounds, and a long standing family of mysterious regional noises known as Seneca Guns has, in at least some cases, been linked to natural Earth processes rather than anything in the sky. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. This does not mean every unexplained boom has a single cause. Scientists emphasise that \"Seneca Guns\" is a descriptive label rather than the name of one proven phenomenon. Some reports are now attributed to shallow earthquakes, others to atmospheric events such as sonic booms, and some remain unexplained because there was insufficient evidence to identify a source. For anyone investigating alleged UFO noises , Earth generated booms are therefore an important possibility to consider before assuming an aerial origin. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. What earthquake booms sound like Although earthquakes are usually associated with shaking, witnesses have long reported hearing them as well. Descriptions commonly include: A cannon like explosion. [strangesounds.org] strangesounds.org Earthquake Booms & Seneca Guns – Cannon Like Skyquakes Linked to Shallow Quakes Strange SoundsJanuary 6, 2026 — Earthquake Booms & Sene... Published: January 6, 2026 A single heavy bang. A rolling or rumbling sound. A deep roar similar to distant thunder. A sharp crack followed by brief vibration. The reason is that very shallow earthquakes can generate high frequency vibrations that reach the surface efficiently enough to become audible. If the earthquake occurs only a few kilometres—or even less than two kilometres—below ground, nearby observers may hear a boom even when the shaking is weak or barely noticeable. Deeper earthquakes generally lose these higher frequency vibrations before they reach the surface, making audible sounds much less common. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Because humans are poor at locating the direction of low frequency sounds, these booms often seem to come from the sky rather than beneath the ground. That perceptual effect helps explain why witnesses sometimes search overhead for aircraft or unidentified objects despite the source being geological. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. The mystery of Seneca Guns The term Seneca Guns refers to unexplained booming sounds reported for centuries around Seneca Lake in New York's Finger Lakes region. Similar reports have also been documented along parts of the Atlantic coast, particularly in North Carolina, South Carolina and Virginia, although the name's historical origin remains uncertain. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Historical descriptions are remarkably consistent. Witnesses describe sudden detonations resembling heavy artillery, often without storms, visible explosions or nearby aircraft. The sounds may rattle windows, be heard across many kilometres, and then cease without any obvious source. The phenomenon became well known in nineteenth century literature, with writers including James Fenimore Cooper referring to mysterious \"lake guns\". Local traditions variously attributed the sounds to spirits, distant battles or unknown natural forces long before aviation existed. These accounts demonstrate that the reports predate modern UFO culture by many generations. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Modern scientific opinion is more cautious than folklore. Rather than treating Seneca Guns as a single unexplained mystery, researchers regard the label as covering several different kinds of booming events that may have different causes in different locations. How ground events seem airborne One reason earthquake booms become associated with UFO reports is that they often violate everyday expectations. Normally, people expect: Aircraft to produce sounds from above. Earthquakes to produce shaking from below. Shallow earthquakes blur that distinction. A loud acoustic wave may arrive before the brain registers subtle ground motion, or the shaking may be so weak that only the boom is noticed. Without an obvious visual source, observers naturally scan the sky. Atmospheric conditions can reinforce this illusion. Calm air, temperature inversions and local topography may affect how sound travels , making its origin difficult to judge. Even experienced observers can misidentify the direction of low frequency sounds. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey The result is a familiar pattern in witness reports: a loud explosion, no visible aircraft, no obvious damage, and speculation about something passing overhead when the actual source may have been beneath the surface. The Sky Just BOOMED… But No One Knows Why Channel: History of Simple Things Open on YouTube Historical examples linking booms and earthquakes Several well documented earthquakes included reports of audible booming or roaring sounds. The 1886 Charleston earthquake in South Carolina (approximately magnitude 6.9) was accompanied by widespread descriptions of roaring and booming as seismic waves crossed the region. During the weeks of aftershocks that followed, many witnesses reported loud detonations accompanying smaller tremors. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. During the 1811–1812 New Madrid earthquake sequence in the central United States, contemporary accounts likewise described artillery like sounds before or during major shocks. While eyewitness descriptions from the period cannot always distinguish sound from shaking, they show that audible earthquakes have been recognised for centuries. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Modern instrumental observations support these historical accounts. During earthquake swarms near Spokane, Washington, and Mammoth Mountain, California, USGS researchers documented very shallow earthquakes that coincided with booming or rumbling sounds reported by nearby residents and scientists in the field. In some cases, observers heard the sounds even when they felt little or no shaking. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Why not every mysterious boom is an earthquake Although earthquakes explain some cases, they do not explain them all. The USGS notes that most unexplained booms investigated ultimately turn out to be more ordinary events, including: Sonic booms from supersonic aircraft. Construction blasting. Quarry explosions. Heavy vehicles. Meteor or bolide airbursts. Frost quakes caused by freezing ground. Distinguishing among these possibilities requires seismic records, timing and sometimes weather or aviation data. For example, sonic booms travel through the atmosphere rather than through rock, producing different timing patterns on seismic instruments. Bright fireballs accompanying a boom may indicate a meteor, while frost quakes occur during intense cold when freezing groundwater fractures soil and rock. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. The key point is that a loud unexplained boom, by itself, is not diagnostic of any single phenomenon. Are Seneca Guns finally explained? There is no universal solution to the mystery. The USGS continues to state that while many unexplained booms have plausible explanations, not every historical report has been conclusively resolved. Evidence from western North America strongly suggests that some mysterious booms are generated by very shallow earthquakes, but this cannot automatically be extended to every location traditionally associated with Seneca Guns. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. More recently, researchers studying Seneca Lake identified numerous large craters in the lakebed and found evidence consistent with methane and other gases escaping from sediments. They proposed that exceptionally large gas releases could create explosive surface bursts capable of producing cannon like reports,"},{"title":"Source Checks","url":"/source-checks/","description":"Seismic timing, weather, aviation records and witness details help distinguish shallow earthquakes from sonic booms, meteors and explosions.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Earth Booms","level":3,"text":"Source Checks Was That Boom an Earthquake or Something Else Earth Booms Source Checks Source Checks Was That Boom an Earthquake or Something Else Seismic timing, weather, aviation records and witness details help distinguish shallow earthquakes from sonic booms, meteors and explosions. Seismic timing, weather, aviation records and witness details help distinguish shallow earthquakes from sonic booms, meteors and explosions. dataset evidence What seismic instruments can confirm Clues that point to aircraft, meteors or blasting Why some boom reports remain unresolved ufo noises earth booms source checks Introduction A loud unexplained boom is not, by itself, evidence of an earthquake, a meteor, a military aircraft or anything more exotic. Investigators rarely identify the source from witness descriptions alone. Instead, they compare several independent datasets—including seismic recordings, the timing and distribution of reports, weather conditions, aviation activity and, where available, astronomical observations. This multi source approach is especially important when investigating reports that later become associated with UFO folklore or longstanding mysteries such as Seneca Guns, because many different natural and human made events can produce remarkably similar sounds. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. In practice, most cases are resolved by asking a simple question: does the evidence point to energy travelling through the ground, through the atmosphere, or from a local explosion? The answer comes from comparing physical measurements rather than relying on how dramatic the boom sounded. What seismic instruments can confirm The first step is checking whether regional seismometers recorded an event. Modern seismic networks continuously monitor ground motion, allowing investigators to determine whether the boom coincided with an earthquake or another disturbance. A genuine earthquake usually produces a recognisable sequence of seismic waves. Even very small, shallow earthquakes often generate characteristic waveforms that can be detected at several stations. By comparing arrival times, seismologists estimate the source location and depth. When an earthquake is extremely shallow—sometimes only one or two miles (roughly 1.5 to 3 kilometres) below the surface—it may generate an audible boom even if the shaking is weak. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Sonic booms look different. If they appear on seismometers at all, they generally create short, high frequency signals rather than the familiar earthquake pattern. The timing between stations also reflects the speed of sound travelling through the atmosphere, which is much slower than seismic waves moving through rock. This distinction is one of the strongest diagnostic tools available to investigators. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey Investigators also compare the geographic pattern: Earthquakes typically produce a coherent seismic source that can be located underground. Atmospheric events may be heard across a broad corridor while producing little or no conventional earthquake signal. Local explosions or quarry blasts usually affect a relatively limited area and have different waveform characteristics from natural earthquakes. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey Clues that point to aircraft, meteors or blasting Once the seismic evidence has been reviewed, investigators compare it with other independent records. The Sky Just BOOMED… But No One Knows Why Channel: History of Simple Things Open on YouTube Aircraft and sonic booms A widespread boom with no corresponding earthquake is often investigated as a possible sonic boom. Analysts examine aviation activity, military training schedules where available, radar information and the pattern of public reports. One important clue is the distribution of witnesses. A supersonic aircraft can produce a boom heard over a long, narrow corridor rather than around a single point. If seismic stations record only weak atmospheric coupling while reports extend across many kilometres, an atmospheric source becomes more likely than a local explosion. Official confirmation may later come from military or aviation authorities, although this is not always available. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey Meteors and bolides Large meteors can also produce explosive sounds, but they often leave additional evidence. Investigators look for: reports of bright fireballs or daylight flashes; observations from meteor camera networks; [arxiv.org] arxiv.org Review of synergic meteor observations: linking the results from cameras, ionosondes, infrasound and seismic detectorsJuly 2, 2021... Published: July 2, 2021 satellite detections where available; infrasound recordings of atmospheric shock waves; consistent eyewitness descriptions of an object crossing the sky. Many significant bolides produce both optical observations and low frequency atmospheric waves, allowing scientists to match visual records with acoustic measurements. When neither visual nor astronomical evidence exists, a meteor explanation becomes less persuasive. [arXiv] arxiv.org Review of synergic meteor observations: linking the results from cameras, ionosondes, infrasound and seismic detectorsJuly 2, 2021... Published: July 2, 2021 Quarry blasting and industrial explosions Industrial blasting usually follows predictable patterns. Investigators compare: local quarry operating schedules; mining records; known demolition work; the reported direction of the sound; the limited geographic extent of reports. Because blasting occurs at fixed locations, repeated reports from the same area often become easier to identify than isolated mysterious booms. What Are the Mystery Booms, Hums and \"Trumpets\" in the Sky? Make This Make Sense Channel: New York Post Open on YouTube Why witness accounts still matter Although eyewitness testimony cannot identify the cause by itself, it provides valuable context when combined with instrument records. Investigators pay attention to details such as: the exact time of the boom; whether windows rattled or the ground shook; whether one bang or multiple reports were heard; whether the sound rolled like thunder or arrived as a sharp crack; whether any flash, smoke or aircraft was observed. Time is especially important. Even a difference of a minute or two can determine whether a report matches a recorded earthquake, an aircraft track or a known blast. Investigators also compare reports from many locations. Consistent timing across a wide area can reveal the direction of travel of an atmospheric disturbance, while tightly clustered reports may indicate a local source. Why some boom reports remain unresolved Not every mysterious boom receives a definitive explanation. Several factors can prevent a firm identification: the event may be too small to produce a clear seismic record; weather conditions can refract sound unusually far from its source; aviation information may be unavailable or incomplete; there may be too few reliable witness reports to reconstruct what happened; no meteor observations or industrial records match the reported time. The USGS notes that many reported booms are ultimately linked to ordinary human activity such as explosions, heavy vehicles, construction or sonic booms, while others are associated with shallow earthquakes. However, some reports remain unexplained simply because there is insufficient evidence—not because they necessarily represent an unknown physical phenomenon. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. For investigations of alleged UFO noises or historic reports of Seneca Guns, this distinction is crucial. An unexplained boom is a starting point for evidence gathering rather than a conclusion. Only by comparing seismic data, atmospheric observations, aviation records, weather information and carefully documented witness accounts can investigators distinguish an earthquake boom from a sonic boom, meteor airburst or local explosion with confidence. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Scientists Finally Know What Caused the Sonic Booms on the US East Coast Channel: BRIGHT SIDE Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Was That Boom an Earthquake or Something Else. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Sound Book By Trevor Cox A professor of acoustic engineering provides a tour of the world's most amazing sound phenomena, including creaking glaciers, whispering... See on Amazon Book Earthquakes By Bruce Bolt The ultimate introduction"},{"title":"Charleston Booms","url":"/charleston-booms/","description":"Reports from the 1886 Charleston earthquake show how roaring main shocks and explosive-sounding aftershocks can shape regional boom folklore.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Earth Booms","level":3,"text":"Charleston Booms What Charleston's Earthquake Booms Sounded Like Earth Booms Charleston Booms Charleston Booms What Charleston's Earthquake Booms Sounded Like Reports from the 1886 Charleston earthquake show how roaring main shocks and explosive sounding aftershocks can shape regional boom folklore. Reports from the 1886 Charleston earthquake show how roaring main shocks and explosive sounding aftershocks can shape regional boom folklore. event time window Eyewitness descriptions during the main shock Why aftershocks produced repeated detonations How the case informs modern mystery boom reports ufo noises earth booms charleston booms Introduction The 1886 Charleston earthquake remains one of the strongest historical examples of how earthquakes can generate loud, mysterious sounds that are easily mistaken for explosions or other unexplained phenomena. Within the broader subject of earthquake booms and Seneca Guns, Charleston is especially important because contemporary witnesses documented not only the roaring noise of the main earthquake but also weeks of aftershocks accompanied by repeated cannon like detonations. These accounts provide one of the clearest historical records linking audible booms directly to seismic activity rather than to atmospheric or unidentified aerial events. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Unlike isolated reports of unexplained bangs, the Charleston sequence combined thousands of eyewitness observations with an extended series of aftershocks. That unusually rich historical record allows modern seismologists to compare reported sounds with known earthquake behaviour and better understand why some shallow earthquakes produce booming noises while others do not. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov 1886 charleston south carolina earthquake intensities and ground motions U.S. Geological SurveyThe 1886 Charleston, South Carolina, earthquake: Intensities and ground motions U.S. Geological Survey... What Charleston's earthquake booms sounded like When the main earthquake struck on the evening of 31 August 1886, many witnesses described hearing the sound before, during or immediately alongside the strongest shaking. Contemporary reports consistently compared it to: heavy artillery fire; a freight train approaching at speed; continuous roaring; rolling thunder; violent underground explosions. The roaring became part of the event's defining memory because it spread across an exceptionally large area. The earthquake itself, now generally estimated at about magnitude 7.3, was felt across much of the eastern United States, and reports of the accompanying roar appeared throughout the region. [U.S. Geological Survey Publications] pubs.usgs.gov Charleston, South Carolina, Earthquake March 28, 2012... Published: March 28, 2012 Modern seismology explains these descriptions without requiring any unusual acoustic source. As seismic waves travelled through the shallow crust, high frequency vibrations reached the ground surface and produced audible sound. Near the epicentral region, the sound and shaking were experienced almost simultaneously, making the earth itself appear to roar. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. The Sky Just BOOMED… But No One Knows Why Channel: History of Simple Things Open on YouTube Why the aftershocks produced repeated detonations The main earthquake was only the beginning. For weeks afterwards, Charleston experienced frequent aftershocks, many of which were accompanied by loud explosive noises. The US Geological Survey specifically notes historical reports describing \"loud detonations\" during this prolonged aftershock sequence. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Several factors made these aftershocks particularly noticeable. First, the fault system remained highly active. Large earthquakes redistribute stress through surrounding rocks, allowing numerous smaller ruptures to occur as the crust gradually adjusted. Some of these aftershocks were strong enough to damage already weakened buildings. Modern reassessments of historical records have even identified a previously unrecognised sizeable aftershock roughly nine to ten minutes after the main shock, estimated at about magnitude 5.6. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov 1886 charleston south carolina earthquake intensities and ground motions U.S. Geological SurveyThe 1886 Charleston, South Carolina, earthquake: Intensities and ground motions U.S. Geological Survey... Second, many aftershocks were probably relatively shallow. Shallow earthquakes transmit higher frequency vibrations to the surface more efficiently than deeper events, making audible booming much more likely. This is the same mechanism now recognised in many modern reports of earthquake sounds. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Third, people were already on alert. Following the destruction of the main earthquake, residents remained outside or slept in temporary shelters because they feared additional shocks. This meant thousands of observers were awake and listening during every significant aftershock, greatly increasing the number of documented sound reports . [U.S. Geological Survey Publications] pubs.usgs.gov Charleston, South Carolina, Earthquake March 28, 2012... Published: March 28, 2012 Why Charleston became such an important historical case Many mysterious boom reports suffer from one major problem: there is no confirmed source. Charleston differs because the source was unmistakable. Researchers can directly compare: the timing of documented aftershocks; reports of shaking; descriptions of booming or roaring; patterns of structural damage. Because these independent observations align so well, Charleston serves as a benchmark demonstrating that earthquakes can genuinely produce loud, explosion like sounds rather than merely being associated with them through coincidence. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov 1886 charleston south carolina earthquake intensities and ground motions U.S. Geological SurveyThe 1886 Charleston, South Carolina, earthquake: Intensities and ground motions U.S. Geological Survey... The event also illustrates that audible earthquake sounds are not restricted to the initial rupture. A prolonged aftershock sequence can generate repeated booms over days or weeks, reinforcing local traditions that an area is haunted by mysterious cannon like noises. 139 YEARS LATER: The Charleston Earthquake of 1886 Channel: WCBD NEWS 2 Open on YouTube How Charleston helps explain later mystery booms The Charleston sequence provides a useful comparison for later reports of unexplained booms along the Atlantic coast. Without historical context, a resident hearing a sudden cannon like bang with little obvious shaking might reasonably suspect: an explosion; military activity; a sonic boom; an unidentified object overhead. Charleston demonstrates that another explanation must also be considered: a small, shallow earthquake whose acoustic energy is more noticeable than its ground motion. The USGS specifically discusses the Charleston earthquake alongside Seneca Guns because both involve reports of loud detonations that can originate from natural geological processes. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. This comparison does not mean every unexplained boom has a seismic origin. Seneca Guns almost certainly encompass several different natural and human made causes depending on location and circumstance. Instead, Charleston provides a well documented example showing that earthquakes belong on the list of plausible explanations whenever loud unexplained booms occur in seismically active regions. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. What the Charleston record teaches modern investigators The Charleston earthquake highlights several practical lessons that remain relevant when evaluating reports of unexplained booming sounds: Booms may accompany only modest shaking. Especially during shallow events, witnesses may remember the sound more vividly than the ground motion itself. Repeated detonations do not necessarily indicate repeated explosions. They may instead reflect an ongoing sequence of aftershocks as stressed faults continue to adjust. Historical eyewitness accounts have scientific value. Thousands of descriptions collected after the Charleston earthquake have helped researchers reconstruct both shaking intensity and the behaviour of the aftershock sequence. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov 1886 charleston south carolina earthquake intensities and ground motions U.S. Geological SurveyThe 1886 Charleston, South Carolina, earthquake: Intensities and ground motions U.S. Geological Survey... Context matters. A confirmed earthquake sequence provides a much stronger explanation for mysterious booms than speculation about aerial phenomena when the timing and location are consistent with seismic activity. Within the wider study of UFO noises and other"},{"title":"Sky Illusion","url":"/sky-illusion/","description":"Low-frequency sound, weak shaking and atmospheric conditions can make a geological boom seem as though it came from an aircraft overhead.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Earth Booms","level":3,"text":"Sky Illusion Why an Earthquake Boom Can Sound Airborne Earth Booms Sky Illusion Sky Illusion Why an Earthquake Boom Can Sound Airborne Low frequency sound, weak shaking and atmospheric conditions can make a geological boom seem as though it came from an aircraft overhead. Low frequency sound, weak shaking and atmospheric conditions can make a geological boom seem as though it came from an aircraft overhead. population context Why listeners misjudge low frequency direction How sound can arrive before noticeable shaking How weather and terrain distort perceived origin ufo noises earth booms sky illusion Introduction When people hear a sudden, powerful boom with little or no visible cause, many instinctively look upwards for an aircraft, meteor or even something more unusual. That reaction is understandable, but it is not always correct. Some booms that appear to come from the sky are actually produced by very shallow geological events, including small earthquakes. The apparent contradiction arises because human hearing is surprisingly poor at locating deep, low frequency sounds, while atmospheric conditions and local terrain can further distort where a sound seems to originate. In the context of UFO noise reports and discussions of earthquake booms or Seneca Guns, this perceptual illusion is one of the most important reasons why an Earth generated event can initially be mistaken for an airborne one. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Why listeners misjudge the direction of low frequency booms A loud crack from a nearby object is usually easy to locate because it contains abundant high frequency sound. Those higher frequencies are filtered differently by each ear and by the folds of the outer ear, allowing the brain to estimate direction with reasonable accuracy. Deep booming sounds behave differently. Their long wavelengths wrap around the head rather than being strongly blocked by it, reducing the differences between what each ear receives. With fewer directional clues available, the brain must rely more heavily on context and expectation. If there is no obvious source on the ground, people often assume the sound came from above because aircraft, thunder and explosions are familiar overhead causes of sudden booms. This is a limitation of normal human hearing rather than evidence that the source was actually airborne. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. This effect is especially relevant for shallow earthquake booms. The audible sound may consist largely of low frequency energy combined with a brief impulse, giving listeners an impression of an explosion without providing reliable information about its true location. The result is that witnesses frequently report the boom as \"overhead\" even when the source lies beneath their feet. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. The Unsolved Mystery of ‘Skyquakes’ Unexplained Loud Sounds Baffle Scientists Podcast Ep. 2 Channel: SciTech Vault Open on YouTube Why the boom may be heard before noticeable shaking One reason earthquake booms are so confusing is that people often remember hearing the sound before they consciously noticed any ground movement. This does not necessarily mean the sound travelled faster than the seismic waves. Instead, several factors affect perception : Very shallow earthquakes can generate audible vibrations that emerge almost immediately at the surface near the observer. Weak shaking may fall below conscious awareness until loose objects rattle or a second, stronger pulse arrives. The brain detects an abrupt explosive sound more readily than subtle vibration, causing the sound to dominate the memory of the event. The USGS notes that some shallow earthquakes produce booming sounds even when nearby people report little or no obvious shaking. Because the expected sequence—earth moving first, sound second—is absent, witnesses naturally search for an atmospheric explanation instead. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. This perceptual sequence helps explain why many reports begin with statements such as \"something exploded overhead\" rather than \"I felt an earthquake.\" What Are the Mystery Booms, Hums and \"Trumpets\" in the Sky? Make This Make Sense Channel: New York Post Open on YouTube How weather and terrain distort perceived origin The atmosphere is not acoustically uniform. Temperature, humidity and wind alter how sound travels , while hills, valleys, forests and bodies of water can reflect or refract sound waves. Several effects can reinforce the illusion of an aerial source: Temperature inversions can bend sound back towards the ground, allowing distant booms to remain surprisingly loud. Wind layers may carry sound farther in one direction than another, making the source seem displaced. Valleys, cliffs and water surfaces can reflect sound, producing echoes that obscure the original direction. Urban environments create multiple reflections from buildings, reducing the brain's ability to identify the first arriving sound. The same atmospheric processes are well known to influence genuine sonic booms, which is why investigators cannot rely solely on witness descriptions when distinguishing between aircraft, meteors and geological sources. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey Why witnesses instinctively scan the sky Human expectations strongly influence perception. Most people have little experience with audible earthquakes but are familiar with aircraft, thunder and explosions occurring above them. When an unexpected boom is heard without an immediately obvious cause, the brain quickly matches it to familiar experiences. Looking upwards is therefore a natural response, particularly if: no significant shaking is felt; the boom is singular rather than a prolonged rumble; windows or walls vibrate without visible ground movement; or there is clear weather that rules out nearby thunder. This expectation bias does not imply that witnesses are unreliable. Instead, it demonstrates that perception combines physical sound with prior experience. Two people hearing the same boom may sincerely report different apparent directions depending on where they were standing, surrounding structures and what they expected to hear. The Sky Just BOOMED… But No One Knows Why Channel: History of Simple Things Open on YouTube Why this matters when evaluating UFO noise reports The tendency for ground generated booms to sound airborne provides an important caution when interpreting unexplained noises associated with UFO reports. A loud bang with no visible aircraft does not automatically imply an unidentified object overhead. Investigators instead compare multiple lines of evidence, including: whether seismic instruments detected a shallow earthquake; whether the sound was reported over a broad region or only locally; whether atmospheric conditions favoured unusual sound propagation; whether any shaking, however slight, accompanied the boom; and whether aviation, meteor or industrial sources can be independently confirmed. The USGS emphasises that mysterious booms have multiple causes. Some are attributable to shallow earthquakes, some to atmospheric phenomena such as sonic booms, and others remain unexplained because available evidence is insufficient. The important point is that the perceived direction of the sound, by itself, is not a reliable indicator of where the event actually originated. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why an Earthquake Boom Can Sound Airborne. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Earthquakes By Bruce Bolt The ultimate introduction to seismology, written by distinguished scholar and Professor Bruce Bolt, of the University of California, Berk... See on Amazon Book The Sound Book By Trevor Cox A professor of acoustic engineering provides a tour of the world's most amazing sound phenomena, including creaking glaciers, whispering... See on Amazon Book The Universal Sense By Seth Horowitz A surprising, entertaining book about how what we hear with our ears affects what's between them. See on Amazon Book Why You Hear What You Hear By Eric J. Heller Why You Hear What You Hear is the first book on the physics of sound for the nonspecialist to empower readers with a hands on, ears open... See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: Earthquakes The Sound Book The Universal Sense As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. eBay marketplace picks Marketplace Samples Live tested eBay searches with available results related to this page. Shop location Using USA USA USA UK Canada Australia Ireland USA UK Canada Australia Ireland Selected from UFO poster on eBay.co.uk . Current eBay listing VINTAGE UFO FLYING SAUCERS COMIC ADVERTISING A2 POSTER"},{"title":"Lakebed Gas","url":"/lakebed-gas/","description":"Methane-rich sediments and large lakebed craters offer a location-specific hypothesis for some historic cannon-like reports at Seneca Lake.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Earth Booms","level":3,"text":"Lakebed Gas Could Gas Bursts Explain Seneca Lake's Booms Earth Booms Lakebed Gas Lakebed Gas Could Gas Bursts Explain Seneca Lake's Booms Methane rich sediments and large lakebed craters offer a location specific hypothesis for some historic cannon like reports at Seneca Lake. Methane rich sediments and large lakebed craters offer a location specific hypothesis for some historic cannon like reports at Seneca Lake. entity What researchers found on the lakebed How trapped gas could produce a surface blast Why the idea cannot explain every Seneca Gun ufo noises earth booms lakebed gas Introduction For centuries, people around New York's Seneca Lake have reported sudden cannon like explosions known as the Seneca Guns . While shallow earthquakes and atmospheric phenomena remain possible explanations for some mysterious booms, a growing body of geological evidence has revived an older idea: that methane and other gases trapped beneath the lakebed may occasionally escape in violent bursts. The hypothesis has gained attention because recent surveys have revealed numerous large craters on the floor of Seneca Lake together with evidence of gas rich sediments. Even so, researchers emphasise that this mechanism is unlikely to account for every reported Seneca Gun. Instead, it may explain a specific subset of events in a phenomenon that probably has more than one natural cause. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. What researchers found on the lakebed The gas burst hypothesis moved beyond speculation after high resolution sonar surveys of Seneca Lake revealed an unexpectedly rugged lake floor. Researchers identified more than one hundred large circular depressions, or pockmarks, many measuring roughly 120 metres across and around 9 metres deep. These features resemble gas expulsion craters found in other aquatic environments. [Popular Mechanics] popularmechanics.com Popular Mechanics Massive Booms Shook the East Coast for over 300 Years Scientists Finally Found the Source of the Sonic Explosions.For over 300 years, mysterious booming sounds—known locally as the \"Seneca Gu... Follow up sampling strengthened the case that gas is involved. Sediment and water collected from deep parts of the lake contained methane together with other naturally occurring gases. These findings suggest that organic material buried beneath the lakebed continues to generate gas, which can accumulate below relatively impermeable sediment layers before eventually escaping. [Popular Mechanics] popularmechanics.com Popular Mechanics Massive Booms Shook the East Coast for over 300 Years Scientists Finally Found the Source of the Sonic Explosions.For over 300 years, mysterious booming sounds—known locally as the \"Seneca Gu... The idea itself is not entirely new. As early as the nineteenth and early twentieth centuries, geologists proposed that natural gas escaping through the lake might be responsible for the mysterious sounds. At the time, however, they lacked the underwater mapping technology and sampling techniques needed to test the hypothesis directly. Recent surveys have provided the first substantial physical evidence consistent with those earlier suggestions. [Popular Mechanics] popularmechanics.com Popular Mechanics Massive Booms Shook the East Coast for over 300 Years Scientists Finally Found the Source of the Sonic Explosions.For over 300 years, mysterious booming sounds—known locally as the \"Seneca Gu... The Sky Just BOOMED… But No One Knows Why Channel: History of Simple Things Open on YouTube How trapped gas could produce a surface blast Methane forms naturally when microorganisms break down organic matter in oxygen poor sediments. In deep lakes such as Seneca, this gas can become trapped beneath compact sediment layers for long periods. If pressure builds sufficiently, the sediment cap may fail suddenly. A large bubble would then rise rapidly through hundreds of metres of water before bursting at the surface. Researchers proposing the hypothesis argue that such an eruption could generate a pressure wave perceived as a loud boom, particularly because Seneca Lake is exceptionally deep and capable of transmitting low frequency sound efficiently. [Popular Mechanics] popularmechanics.com Popular Mechanics Massive Booms Shook the East Coast for over 300 Years Scientists Finally Found the Source of the Sonic Explosions.For over 300 years, mysterious booming sounds—known locally as the \"Seneca Gu... Studies of methane release in other lakes show that bubbling is often highly episodic rather than continuous. Changes in water pressure, seasonal conditions and sediment stability can all influence when gas is released. That behaviour is consistent with the irregular timing of many historical Seneca Gun reports, which sometimes occur after long quiet periods. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. The cratered lake floor also fits this mechanism. Repeated episodes of gas escape can disturb sediments and leave circular depressions, although the precise processes involved vary between lakes and geological settings. Similar looking pockmarks elsewhere have been linked to fluid escape, while others have later proved to have different origins, illustrating why the features alone are not conclusive evidence of methane eruptions. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Why the idea cannot explain every Seneca Gun Despite the intriguing evidence, the methane explanation remains a working hypothesis rather than a settled conclusion. One limitation is historical geography. Reports described as Seneca Guns have been recorded not only around Seneca Lake but also along parts of the Atlantic coast and in other regions with no obvious connection to methane rich lake sediments. Those cases almost certainly require different explanations. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Even around Seneca Lake, researchers have not yet directly recorded a large methane eruption occurring simultaneously with a characteristic boom. The physical evidence indicates that gas exists beneath the lake and that large craters are present, but capturing an actual eruption together with acoustic and seismic measurements remains an important missing piece. That gap has motivated new monitoring efforts designed to detect both shallow seismic activity and possible gas related events. [fdsn.org] fdsn.org YA 2026 YA (2026 2027): Seneca Drums Pilot Project... Scientists also caution against assuming that every pockmark necessarily formed through methane explosions. Studies from other regions demonstrate that superficially similar depressions can result from underwater sediment flows rather than escaping gas. Consequently, each lake must be evaluated using its own geological evidence rather than by analogy alone. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. What Are the Mystery Booms, Hums and \"Trumpets\" in the Sky? Make This Make Sense Channel: New York Post Open on YouTube Scientists Solve Seneca Lake Booming Mystery WION Podcast Channel: WION Open on YouTube What the hypothesis means for understanding Seneca Lake booms The lakebed gas hypothesis is important because it offers a location specific mechanism supported by tangible geological observations rather than folklore alone. Instead of treating the Seneca Guns as entirely mysterious—or attributing them to aircraft, UFOs or other extraordinary causes—it points investigators toward measurable natural processes beneath the lake. At the same time, the evidence argues for caution rather than certainty. The presence of methane, gas rich sediments and large lakebed craters makes gas eruptions a plausible explanation for some historic cannon like reports at Seneca Lake. However, until researchers directly observe a gas release producing the characteristic sound, the hypothesis remains one of several scientifically credible explanations. In the broader context of unexplained booming noises, Seneca Lake appears increasingly likely to represent a combination of local geological processes rather than a single phenomenon with a single cause. [usgs.gov] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Could Gas Bursts Explain Seneca Lake's Booms. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Story of Earth By Robert M. Hazen Hailed by The New York Times for writing “with wonderful clarity about science . . . that effortlessly teaches as it zips along,” nationa... See on Amazon Book Underland By Robert Macfarlane Follow Robert Macfarlane to the furthest corners of the globe.... A SUNDAY TIMES BESTSELLER WINNER OF THE WAINWRIGHT PRIZE 2019 WINNER OF... See on Amazon Book Annals of the Former World By John McPhee Like the terrain it covers, Annals of the Former World tells a many layered tale, and the reader may choose one of many paths through it,... See on Amazon Book The Deep Hot Biosphere"},{"title":"Shallow Quakes","url":"/shallow-quakes/","description":"Earthquakes near the surface can release high-frequency vibrations that reach listeners as a bang, roar or cannon-like boom.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Earth Booms","level":3,"text":"Shallow Quakes Why Some Earthquakes Sound Like Cannon Fire Earth Booms Shallow Quakes Shallow Quakes Why Some Earthquakes Sound Like Cannon Fire Earthquakes near the surface can release high frequency vibrations that reach listeners as a bang, roar or cannon like boom. Earthquakes near the surface can release high frequency vibrations that reach listeners as a bang, roar or cannon like boom. mechanism How shallow depth preserves audible vibrations Why weak shaking can accompany a loud boom What field reports and earthquake swarms reveal ufo noises earth booms shallow quakes Introduction One of the most surprising explanations for an apparent \"explosion\" in the absence of aircraft, storms or visible impacts is an earthquake occurring extremely close to the Earth's surface. Although earthquakes are normally associated with shaking, very shallow events can generate sharp bangs, cannon like reports or rolling booms that are heard more clearly than they are felt. This makes them especially relevant when assessing reports of mysterious \" UFO noises \" or unexplained booms. The key factor is not simply the earthquake's magnitude, but its depth: when the rupture occurs only a few kilometres below ground, or even shallower, high frequency vibrations can reach the surface before they are absorbed, producing audible sound. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Unlike broader discussions of earthquake booms or the historical mystery of Seneca Guns, this mechanism focuses on why shallow earthquakes can sound remarkably like explosions despite producing only modest ground motion. How shallow depth preserves audible vibrations Earthquakes release energy across a broad range of frequencies. Most of the energy from large or deep earthquakes reaches people as ground motion rather than audible sound because higher frequency vibrations are rapidly weakened as they travel through rock. By the time seismic waves from a deep earthquake reach the surface, much of the energy capable of creating audible vibrations has already dissipated. [USGS Earthquake Hazards Program] earthquake.usgs.gov Earthquake Hazards Program Listening to Earthquakes USGS Earthquake Hazards ProgramListening to Earthquakes... Very shallow earthquakes behave differently. Because the rupture occurs close to the surface: High frequency seismic waves have much less rock through which to lose energy. Those vibrations can reach the ground with enough strength to set nearby air into motion. The resulting pressure waves are perceived as a sharp bang, crack, boom or short roar rather than a prolonged rumble. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. This explains why two earthquakes of similar magnitude can produce very different experiences. A deeper event may cause obvious shaking but little or no sound, whereas a much smaller earthquake only a kilometre or two below the surface may generate a startling explosive noise. The Sky Just BOOMED… But No One Knows Why Channel: History of Simple Things Open on YouTube Why weak shaking can accompany a loud boom Many eyewitnesses assume that a loud explosion should correspond to a powerful earthquake. In reality, sound and felt shaking do not always scale together. A small, shallow rupture concentrates high frequency energy close to the epicentre. These frequencies are efficient at producing short, impulsive sounds but do not necessarily produce prolonged or widespread ground motion. Someone standing very near the rupture may hear what resembles a heavy detonation while experiencing only a brief jolt or almost imperceptible vibration. The USGS notes that some shallow earthquakes create booming sounds even when people report no noticeable shaking at all. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Distance also changes perception . High frequency waves fade much faster than low frequency waves. Close to the source, people may hear a sharp crack or bang. Further away, those higher frequencies disappear, leaving only lower frequency shaking or nothing noticeable at all. [USGS Earthquake Hazards Program] earthquake.usgs.gov Earthquake Hazards Program Earthquake Distance Effects USGS Earthquake Hazards ProgramEarthquake Distance Effects... This combination explains why emergency services sometimes receive reports of an \"explosion\" without obvious earthquake damage, particularly when the seismic event is too small to attract immediate public attention. Why the sound resembles cannon fire The comparison with artillery or cannon fire appears repeatedly in historical earthquake accounts because the acoustic signal is usually brief, impulsive and rich in higher frequencies. Witnesses commonly describe: a single cannon shot; an explosion beneath the ground; a heavy door slamming; a quarry blast; a deep rolling boom similar to distant thunder. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. These descriptions arise because the audible component is generated by an abrupt release of energy rather than a continuous vibration. The sound often lasts only a fraction of a second before any noticeable shaking arrives or while it is occurring. People are also relatively poor at locating the direction of low frequency sounds. As a result, a geological source beneath the surface may be perceived as originating somewhere overhead, contributing to reports that initially seem consistent with aircraft, meteors or unidentified aerial phenomena. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Loud Booms Reported During Earthquake Channel: WMUR TV Open on YouTube What field reports and earthquake swarms reveal Several well documented earthquake sequences illustrate the importance of shallow depth rather than large magnitude. Spokane earthquake swarm During a 2001 earthquake swarm near Spokane, Washington, numerous residents reported repeated booming sounds. According to the USGS, the earthquakes occurred at unusually shallow depths—sometimes only 1–2 miles (roughly 1.6–3.2 km)—which probably explains why the noises were so widely reported despite the modest size of the events. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Mammoth Mountain and Imperial Valley USGS scientists working near Mammoth Mountain, California, reported hearing muffled booms without feeling noticeable shaking. Seismographs later showed that several very small, shallow earthquakes, each below magnitude 2, had occurred simultaneously. Similar observations were made during an earthquake swarm in California's Imperial Valley, where researchers both heard low rumbling sounds and experienced local shaking. These field observations demonstrate that audible earthquake sounds are not merely folklore but have been directly observed alongside instrumental recordings. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Historical examples Historical earthquakes provide comparable descriptions over much longer timescales. Reports from the 1886 Charleston earthquake describe roaring sounds accompanying both the main shock and numerous aftershocks. Earlier accounts from the New Madrid earthquake sequence of 1811–1812 also mention artillery like detonations. While those were much larger earthquakes than the shallow swarms discussed above, they show that explosive sounds have been recognised features of some earthquakes for centuries. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. Why this matters when investigating mysterious booms For reports collected under labels such as \"earthquake booms\" or \"Seneca Guns\", the presence of an explosion like sound does not automatically indicate an atmospheric source. A shallow earthquake should be considered whenever a boom is: localised rather than heard across hundreds of kilometres; accompanied by a brief jolt or slight vibration; reported near an area with known seismic activity or earthquake swarms; unsupported by evidence of aircraft, industrial explosions or meteor activity. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov Open source on usgs.gov. At the same time, scientists emphasise that most unexplained booms are not earthquakes. Sonic booms, industrial blasts, quarry explosions and other atmospheric or human made sources account for many reports. The significance of very shallow earthquakes is that they provide a well established geological mechanism capable of producing sounds that listeners naturally interpret as explosions, even when the accompanying earthquake is too small or too shallow to fit everyday expectations. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey Why can i hear the rumble of an earthquake, what makes the noise? Channel: Earth Sciences New Zealand Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Some Earthquakes Sound Like Cannon Fire. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book"},{"title":"Perception","url":"/perception/","description":"Media coverage, surprise, and uncertain viewing conditions can influence how witnesses connect an unfamiliar noise with a strange light.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises","level":2,"text":"Perception How Expectation Shapes UFO Sound Memories UFO Noises Perception Perception How Expectation Shapes UFO Sound Memories Media coverage, surprise, and uncertain viewing conditions can influence how witnesses connect an unfamiliar noise with a strange light. Media coverage, surprise, and uncertain viewing conditions can influence how witnesses connect an unfamiliar noise with a strange light. population context Linking simultaneous sights and sounds Expectation after media coverage How memory changes uncertain events ufo noises perception Introduction When people report hearing a strange hum, buzz or other unusual sound at the same time as seeing an unidentified light or object, it is tempting to assume the two experiences came from the same source. Research on perception and eyewitness memory suggests a more cautious interpretation. Under uncertain conditions—particularly at night, during unexpected events or after exposure to UFO stories—people can sincerely connect sights and sounds that were unrelated, misremember when they occurred, or gradually reshape their recollection as they discuss the event or encounter new information. This does not imply dishonesty. Instead, it reflects well established features of human perception and memory that become especially important when interpreting reports of unidentified aerial phenomena (UAP). [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Misinformation and memory: the creation of new memories Misinformation and memory: the creation of new memories PubMed... Why unfamiliar sounds become linked to unusual lights A key feature of perception is that the brain tries to combine information from different senses into a single, coherent account of what happened. If a bright light appears overhead and an unusual sound is heard at roughly the same time, people naturally infer that both came from the same object, even when the true source of the sound is uncertain. This process, known as cross modal binding, is normally helpful because sights and sounds from the same event usually do belong together. However, in ambiguous situations it can produce mistaken associations. Night time viewing, poor distance cues, echoes, wind, delayed sound propagation and competing background noises all increase uncertainty about where a sound originated or whether it was connected to the observed light. Experimental neuroscience shows that the brain actively integrates auditory and visual signals into unified percepts, even when the relationship between them is not perfectly clear. [Nature] nature.com Neural correlates of cross modal binding Nature Neuroscience Neural correlates of cross modal binding Nature Neuroscience... In UFO sound reports , this means that a witness may accurately remember seeing an unusual light and accurately remember hearing an unfamiliar noise, yet still be mistaken in concluding that the sound came from the object itself. Linking simultaneous sights and sounds Several factors make these links especially compelling: Low frequency sounds are difficult for humans to locate accurately. Darkness reduces reliable visual reference points for estimating distance and size. Unexpected events encourage rapid interpretation before complete information is available. Aircraft, distant industry, wildlife or atmospheric sounds may overlap with an unrelated visual event. Because the brain prefers one coherent explanation over multiple unrelated ones, witnesses often recall the experience as a single integrated event rather than separate sensory observations. [Nature] nature.com Neural correlates of cross modal binding Nature Neuroscience Neural correlates of cross modal binding Nature Neuroscience... How expectation influences interpretation Expectation affects perception long before anyone gives a formal witness statement. When people already have a mental model of what a UFO encounter \"should\" be like, ambiguous sounds are more likely to be interpreted within that framework. Psychologists refer to this as top down processing: existing knowledge, beliefs and expectations help interpret incomplete sensory information. If someone has recently watched documentaries, read news coverage about unexplained aerial phenomena or discussed UFO cases, those ideas become more readily available when an unusual experience occurs. This does not mean media exposure creates sightings. Rather, it influences which interpretation feels most plausible when the available sensory information is incomplete. Statistical analysis of large UFO reporting databases has found that reporting activity itself is sensitive to media attention, suggesting that publicity affects both the likelihood of reporting and the way experiences are interpreted. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect On the dynamics of reporting data: A case study of UFO sightings On the dynamics of reporting data: A case study of UFO sightings ScienceDirectOctober 1, 2022... Published: October 1, 2022 Expectation after media coverage Following periods of intense publicity, witnesses may be more inclined to: Notice unusual environmental sounds that would otherwise be ignored. Interpret an unidentified light and unrelated sound as belonging together. Describe sounds using familiar UFO related language such as \"electrical hum\", \"buzzing\" or \"vibration\". Recall additional details after repeated discussion or media exposure. These influences operate through ordinary cognitive processes and are not unique to UFO reports; similar effects have been documented across eyewitness testimony more generally. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Misinformation and memory: the creation of new memories Misinformation and memory: the creation of new memories PubMed... 3:06 Elizabeth F. Loftus False Memories Channel: The Brainwaves Video Anthology · Views: 84.2K · Uploaded: June 2017 · Length: 3 minutes 6 seconds Open on YouTube How memory changes uncertain events Memory is reconstructive rather than a perfect recording. Each recollection involves rebuilding the event from stored information, current knowledge and later experiences. As a result, memories can become more detailed, more coherent or more confident over time without necessarily becoming more accurate. Research led by Elizabeth Loftus and colleagues has repeatedly shown that post event information can alter what people later remember. Misleading questions, repeated discussions or exposure to alternative accounts may introduce details that become incorporated into an individual's recollection while still feeling entirely genuine. [nih.gov] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Misinformation and memory: the creation of new memories Misinformation and memory: the creation of new memories PubMed... For UFO sound reports, this can mean that an initially vague memory such as \"I heard something unusual\" gradually becomes \"the object emitted a deep electrical humming noise\" after repeated retelling, especially if similar descriptions are encountered elsewhere. Why uncertain events are especially vulnerable Several circumstances increase the likelihood of memory reconstruction: Brief observations. High emotional arousal or surprise. Darkness or poor visibility. Divided attention between multiple unusual events. Delays before writing or recording an account. Repeated conversations with other witnesses. Laboratory studies also show that high perceptual load reduces recall accuracy and increases susceptibility to suggestion, affecting both visual and auditory aspects of eyewitness memory. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Perceptual Load Affects Eyewitness Accuracy and Susceptibility to Leading Questions PubMed... Why sincere witnesses can disagree One common misconception is that differing accounts automatically indicate fabrication. Cognitive psychology suggests the opposite: honest observers frequently remember different aspects of the same event because attention, viewpoint, stress and later reconstruction vary between individuals. One witness may primarily remember the unusual sound, another the movement of the light and a third the emotional impact of the experience. As memories evolve independently, later comparisons can reveal genuine disagreements over timing, sequence or the characteristics of the reported noise. Modern memory research further indicates that expectations at the time of recall—not only at the time of observation—can influence remembered event details, reinforcing the importance of documenting witness accounts as soon as possible after an incident. [PLOS] journals.plos.org Shared memories of event details in the human brain are altered by misinformation and test expectations PLOS BiologyJuly 6, 2026... Published: July 6, 2026 Where is Sound Coming From? How Humans Use Sound LOCALIZATION Channel: Doctor Cliff, AuD Open on YouTube What this means when evaluating UFO sound reports Expectation and memory research neither proves nor disproves any individual UFO report. Instead, it provides a framework for"},{"title":"Early Interviews","url":"/early-interviews/","description":"Prompt, separate and neutral witness interviews can preserve uncertainty before discussion and suggestion reshape a sound report.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Perception","level":3,"text":"Early Interviews How to Record UFO Sound Reports Before Memory Shifts Perception Early Interviews Early Interviews How to Record UFO Sound Reports Before Memory Shifts Prompt, separate and neutral witness interviews can preserve uncertainty before discussion and suggestion reshape a sound report. Prompt, separate and neutral witness interviews can preserve uncertainty before discussion and suggestion reshape a sound report. policy intervention Why delays increase reconstruction risks How to separate witnesses and avoid leading questions What sensory details to record immediately ufo noises perception early interviews Introduction When a witness reports hearing an unusual noise during a UFO or unidentified aerial phenomenon (UAP) sighting, the most valuable evidence is often the account recorded before memory begins to change. Psychological research consistently shows that human memory is reconstructive rather than a perfect recording. As time passes, conversations with other witnesses, exposure to media coverage, repeated questioning and leading interview techniques can all reshape what people sincerely believe they heard. For investigators interested in evaluating reported UFO sounds , prompt, independent and carefully documented interviews therefore serve an important purpose: they preserve uncertainty where uncertainty genuinely exists and help distinguish direct perception from later interpretation. [ojp.gov] nij.ojp.gov National Institute of JusticeInterviewing Victims and Witnesses of Crime National Institute of JusticeJanuary 1, 1995... Published: January 1, 1995 This approach does not assume that unusual sounds are real, misidentified or extraordinary. Instead, it improves the quality of the evidence by recording the witness's original sensory experience before post event influences have an opportunity to alter it. Why delays increase reconstruction risks Memory begins changing almost immediately after an event. Every act of recall can strengthen some details while altering or omitting others. In ambiguous situations—such as hearing an unfamiliar hum, buzz or vibration at night while observing an unidentified light—later information can become incorporated into the witness's recollection without conscious awareness. This process is one of the best established findings in eyewitness memory research. [Office of Justice Programs] ojp.gov Office of Justice Programs EYEWITNESS TESTIMONY Office of Justice ProgramsEYEWITNESS TESTIMONY FINAL REPORT, SEPTEMBER 1975 Office of Justice Programs... Published: SEPTEMBER 1975 For UFO sound reports, delays create several specific risks: Interpretation replaces observation. A witness may initially report, \"I heard a low humming sound,\" but later remember, \"The object emitted a low electrical hum,\" even if they never directly perceived the sound's source. Descriptions become more specific over time. Initially uncertain impressions may evolve into detailed narratives after discussion, despite no new sensory evidence. Confidence can increase independently of accuracy. Repeated retelling often makes memories feel more certain, even when details have shifted. [National Institute of Justice] nij.ojp.gov National Institute of JusticeInterviewing Victims and Witnesses of Crime National Institute of JusticeJanuary 1, 1995... Published: January 1, 1995 These changes are especially relevant for sounds because people are often less certain about the location, distance and origin of an unfamiliar noise than they are about whether they heard something at all. Early documentation therefore captures both the reported sound and the witness's original level of confidence. 9:22 The Cognitive Interview: How to Improve Eyewitness Testimony AQA Psychology Channel: Bear it in MIND · Views: 24.6K · Uploaded: April 2023 · Length: 9 minutes Open on YouTube How separate interviews reduce contamination One of the simplest ways to preserve the integrity of witness accounts is to interview people individually before they discuss the event with one another. Laboratory studies repeatedly demonstrate that exposure to another witness's account can introduce details a person never observed directly. This \"co witness contamination\" can occur even when everyone involved is acting honestly and trying to remember accurately. Some witnesses later report another person's observations as though they were their own. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Co witness information can have immediate effects on eyewitness memory reports PubMed... For UFO sound investigations, separating witnesses is particularly valuable because auditory experiences are often incomplete. One witness may remember a humming noise, another may not have heard anything unusual, while a third may recall wind or aircraft noise . Recording these differences before discussion preserves potentially important evidence instead of allowing the accounts to converge artificially. Guidance developed for eyewitness investigations in criminal contexts similarly recommends separating witnesses during initial evidence collection to reduce inadvertent sharing of information. Although UFO investigations differ in purpose, the same memory principles apply. [National Institute of Justice] nij.ojp.gov National Institute of JusticeEyewitness Evidence: A Guide for Law Enforcement National Institute of JusticeOctober 1, 1999... Published: October 1, 1999 How to avoid leading questions The wording of interview questions matters because suggestions can unintentionally alter later memory. Questions that assume a sound existed or imply its characteristics should be avoided. Instead of asking: \"Did the UFO make the electrical humming sound?\" \"How loud was the buzzing?\" an interviewer can ask neutral questions such as: \"What, if anything, did you hear?\" \"Can you describe every sound you noticed during the event?\" \"How certain are you that the sound came from the object you observed?\" Neutral wording allows witnesses to distinguish between direct perception and personal inference. Research pioneered by Elizabeth Loftus and many subsequent studies has shown that even subtle wording differences can influence later recollection of events. [Office of Justice Programs] ojp.gov Office of Justice Programs EYEWITNESS TESTIMONY Office of Justice ProgramsEYEWITNESS TESTIMONY FINAL REPORT, SEPTEMBER 1975 Office of Justice Programs... Published: SEPTEMBER 1975 Equally important is allowing witnesses to say they are unsure. Recording uncertainty accurately is often more informative than encouraging a complete but potentially reconstructed account. 3:06 Elizabeth F. Loftus False Memories Channel: The Brainwaves Video Anthology · Views: 84.2K · Uploaded: June 2017 · Length: 3 minutes 6 seconds Open on YouTube What sensory details should be recorded immediately? The first interview should focus on observations rather than explanations. Practical documentation includes: Description of the sound: humming, buzzing, clicking, roaring, silence or another description using the witness's own words. Timing: when the sound was first noticed relative to the visual observation. Duration: whether it was continuous, intermittent or brief. Perceived direction: where the witness believed the sound originated, while noting any uncertainty. Changes over time: whether the sound became louder, softer or changed character. Environmental conditions: wind, traffic, nearby machinery, animals or aircraft that could have contributed competing sounds. Confidence statements: how certain the witness feels about each observation rather than treating all details as equally reliable. Recording exact wording is valuable because later summaries often replace the witness's original language with interpretations. A statement such as \"it reminded me of electricity\" conveys something different from \"it was an electrical sound.\" Preserving perception rather than producing agreement An early interview is not intended to create a single, consistent narrative. In fact, differences between independent accounts may help investigators understand which details were genuinely perceived and which emerged later. For example, if three witnesses independently report an unusual low frequency hum before speaking with one another, that consistency deserves attention. Conversely, if only later interviews contain highly specific descriptions such as \"oscillating electromagnetic vibration\" after witnesses discussed the event together or consumed related media, investigators have reason to treat those additions more cautiously. The aim is therefore not to eliminate disagreement but to preserve the original distribution of observations, uncertainty and confidence. That distinction allows later analysis to evaluate which elements remained stable from the earliest available accounts. Why early interviews strengthen sound evidence Early witness interviews cannot determine whether an unusual sound originated from an unidentified object, a conventional source or a"},{"title":"Sensory Binding","url":"/sensory-binding/","description":"The brain can merge a strange light and nearby noise into one event even when they came from separate sources.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Perception","level":3,"text":"Sensory Binding Why Separate Sights and Sounds Feel Like One Event Perception Sensory Binding Sensory Binding Why Separate Sights and Sounds Feel Like One Event The brain can merge a strange light and nearby noise into one event even when they came from separate sources. The brain can merge a strange light and nearby noise into one event even when they came from separate sources. mechanism How the brain binds sights and sounds Why darkness and poor distance cues increase errors How to separate observation from inferred source ufo noises perception sensory binding Introduction When people report a mysterious light and an unusual sound during the same UFO sighting, it is natural to assume they came from the same source. However, research on multisensory perception shows that this conclusion does not necessarily follow from the observations themselves. The human brain is designed to combine information from different senses into a single, coherent event whenever the timing and circumstances seem compatible. This process is usually highly effective, but in situations with poor visibility, uncertain distances or unfamiliar sounds, it can lead people to connect events that were actually independent. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Audiovisual temporal integration: Cognitive processing, neural mechanisms, developmental trajectory and potential interventi... Within UFO reports, this means a witness may accurately remember both seeing an unusual light and hearing an unusual noise while being mistaken only about the causal link between them. Understanding this distinction helps explain why sincere eyewitnesses can disagree about whether an unidentified object produced a sound without implying dishonesty or faulty observation. How the brain binds sights and sounds The brain constantly solves what psychologists call the binding problem : deciding which sensory signals belong together as parts of the same event. Rather than processing vision and hearing independently, it combines them whenever they occur close together in time and appear likely to share a common cause. This integration reduces uncertainty and normally produces an accurate understanding of everyday events such as watching a person speak or seeing a vehicle pass while hearing its engine. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com Audiovisual temporal integration: Cognitive processing, neural mechanisms, developmental trajectory and potential interventi... Researchers describe one important factor as the temporal binding window —a brief interval during which sounds and sights are likely to be treated as belonging to one occurrence even if they are not perfectly simultaneous. The width of this window is flexible rather than fixed. It changes with attention, expectations, stimulus quality and environmental conditions, making the brain more willing to fuse uncertain sensory information into one percept. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com Audiovisual temporal integration: Cognitive processing, neural mechanisms, developmental trajectory and potential interventi... This tendency is generally adaptive. In ordinary life, a sound heard immediately after a visible event usually does come from that event. The same mechanism, however, can encourage mistaken associations when several unrelated stimuli happen within a short period. Why darkness and poor distance cues increase errors Night time observations create exactly the conditions in which sensory binding becomes more uncertain. Visual information is reduced because landmarks disappear into darkness, making it harder to estimate: how far away a light is; how large it might be; whether it is moving or stationary; and whether multiple lights belong to one object or several. At the same time, hearing provides only limited information about distance and direction. Humans are generally much less accurate at locating sounds than visible objects, particularly for low frequency noises or sounds that have reflected from terrain, buildings or cloud layers. Echoes, wind and atmospheric conditions can all alter where a sound appears to originate. When both senses are uncertain, the brain often favours the simplest explanation: that the unusual light and unusual sound belong to the same object. This \"common source\" assumption is an efficient shortcut in most circumstances, but under ambiguous viewing conditions it increases the chance of combining unrelated events into a single experience. [go.jp] jstage.jst.go.jp J STAGEAudiovisual multisensory integration J STAGEAudiovisual multisensory integration Perception Features & the Binding Problem Cognitive Psychology Channel: Puppet Psychology Open on YouTube Laboratory demonstrations of sensory binding Scientists have demonstrated these mechanisms using controlled perceptual illusions rather than UFO related experiments. One of the best known examples is the ventriloquist effect . Even when listeners know intellectually where a loudspeaker is located, they often perceive the sound as coming from the visible moving mouth of a puppet or actor. The visual signal effectively \"captures\" the perceived location of the sound because the brain assumes both originated from one source. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)The Ventriloquist Illusion as a Tool to Study Multisensory Processing: An Update PMC... Another example is the sound induced flash illusion . Under carefully controlled conditions, a single flash accompanied by multiple beeps can lead observers to report seeing multiple flashes. Here, auditory information changes visual perception itself rather than simply influencing interpretation. Neuroimaging and brain stimulation studies indicate that multisensory integration and attention jointly contribute to these effects. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Parietal disruption alters audiovisual binding in the sound induced flash illusion PubMed... These experiments do not imply that UFO witnesses experience laboratory illusions directly. Instead, they demonstrate that combining sight and sound is an active interpretive process rather than a perfect recording of external reality. Why UFO observations are especially vulnerable Several features common to UFO reports encourage the brain to bind separate sensory events together. Unexpected timing. Witnesses are rarely prepared to observe an unusual aerial event. Because attention is rapidly redirected, there is little opportunity to establish whether a sound began before, during or after the visual observation. Sparse environmental information. A bright light against a dark sky provides few reliable reference points for judging altitude or distance. Multiple possible sound sources. Aircraft, road traffic, industrial equipment, insects, wind, wildlife or electrical infrastructure may all generate unfamiliar sounds that overlap in time with an unrelated visual event. Limited observation time. Many sightings last only seconds. When information is incomplete, the perceptual system tends to favour one coherent explanation over several unrelated ones. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com Audiovisual temporal integration: Cognitive processing, neural mechanisms, developmental trajectory and potential interventi... These conditions increase the probability of an honest but mistaken causal inference: \"the object made that sound.\" Separating observation from inferred source A useful way to evaluate witness testimony is to distinguish between observations and interpretations . Observations are direct sensory experiences, such as: \"I saw a bright white light moving north.\" \"I heard a low humming sound.\" \"The sound became louder after several seconds.\" Interpretations add a causal conclusion, for example: \"The humming came from the object.\" \"The light produced the vibration.\" \"The object accelerated silently and then generated the buzz.\" The observations may be entirely accurate even if the inferred relationship is not. Investigators in aviation, accident reconstruction and eyewitness psychology often encourage witnesses to report what they directly perceived before discussing explanations, because later reasoning can become difficult to separate from the original memory. Applied to UFO sound reports , this distinction allows researchers to preserve valuable witness observations while recognising that causal links between sights and sounds require independent supporting evidence rather than assumption alone. Where is Sound Coming From? How Humans Use Sound LOCALIZATION Channel: Doctor Cliff, AuD Open on YouTube What this mechanism does—and does not—explain Sensory binding offers a well supported explanation for why unrelated lights and sounds may feel like parts of one event. It explains a cognitive mechanism, not the identity of any observed object. The mechanism does not imply that every reported UFO sound came from an unrelated source. Some aerial phenomena—including conventional aircraft, helicopters, drones , meteors and atmospheric"},{"title":"Media Effects","url":"/media-effects/","description":"Recent documentaries, news stories and discussions can make ambiguous noises seem more consistent with familiar UFO encounter narratives.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Perception","level":3,"text":"Media Effects Can UFO Stories Change What Witnesses Hear? Perception Media Effects Media Effects Can UFO Stories Change What Witnesses Hear? Recent documentaries, news stories and discussions can make ambiguous noises seem more consistent with familiar UFO encounter narratives. Recent documentaries, news stories and discussions can make ambiguous noises seem more consistent with familiar UFO encounter narratives. population context How expectations guide ambiguous perception Why publicity can increase reporting activity How familiar UFO language enters later accounts ufo noises perception media effects Introduction Media coverage of unidentified flying objects (UFOs) and unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) can influence how people interpret ambiguous sounds without creating the underlying event itself. When someone hears a distant hum, buzz or vibration while observing an unusual light, prior exposure to documentaries, news reports, films or online discussions can make a UFO explanation feel more immediately plausible than competing explanations. This effect reflects well established psychological processes involving expectation, top down perception and reconstructive memory rather than evidence that media generate sightings. Research on perception and eyewitness memory suggests that expectations are particularly influential when sensory information is incomplete or uncertain, making this an important consideration when evaluating reports of alleged UFO noises . [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Top down influences on ambiguous perception: the role of stable and transient states of the observer PubMedDecember 8, 2014... Published: December 8, 2014 How expectations guide ambiguous perception Perception is not a passive recording of the outside world. The brain continually combines incoming sensory information with previous knowledge, beliefs and recent experiences to arrive at the most likely interpretation of an event. This process, known as top down processing, is especially important when sights or sounds are faint, distant or difficult to identify. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Top down influences on ambiguous perception: the role of stable and transient states of the observer PubMedDecember 8, 2014... Published: December 8, 2014 In the context of UFO reports, media exposure supplies ready made expectations about what an encounter should sound like. Popular documentaries and dramatised reconstructions often describe mysterious craft as producing: A low frequency hum. An electrical buzzing noise. A pulsating vibration. An unusual silence interrupted by mechanical sounds. After repeated exposure, these descriptions become familiar reference points. If a person later encounters an unexplained environmental sound while observing an unusual light, those familiar patterns may become the first explanation that comes to mind. This does not mean the witness invents the sound. Rather, expectation influences how an already ambiguous experience is interpreted. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Top down influences on ambiguous perception: the role of stable and transient states of the observer PubMedDecember 8, 2014... Published: December 8, 2014 Importantly, the same ambiguous sound could have been interpreted differently in another cultural or historical setting. Before the modern UFO era, unfamiliar aerial sounds were more likely to be attributed to atmospheric phenomena, secret aircraft or other locally familiar explanations. How We Make Memories: Crash Course Psychology 13 Channel: CrashCourse Open on YouTube Why publicity can increase reporting activity Periods of intense publicity surrounding UFO stories have long been associated with increases in reported sightings. Researchers studying UFO reporting databases have noted that reporting rates fluctuate alongside major media attention, suggesting that publicity affects whether people decide that an unusual experience is worth reporting and how they categorise it. This pattern is consistent with broader psychological research on attention and expectation rather than serving as evidence that the underlying phenomenon itself has become more common. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Top down influences on ambiguous perception: the role of stable and transient states of the observer PubMedDecember 8, 2014... Published: December 8, 2014 Several mechanisms help explain this relationship. Greater vigilance. People who have recently watched a high profile documentary or followed extensive news coverage may become more attentive to unusual lights and unfamiliar environmental sounds that they would normally ignore. Lower reporting threshold. An experience that previously might have been dismissed as an odd aircraft noise may instead be regarded as potentially significant because similar descriptions have recently appeared in the media. Shared interpretive framework. Publicity provides common language and expectations that make people more confident that their experience resembles those described by other witnesses. This helps explain why clusters of reports sometimes follow widely publicised cases even when there is no independent evidence that unusual aerial activity increased during the same period. How familiar UFO language enters later accounts Witness statements often evolve over time rather than remaining fixed. Psychological research on eyewitness memory shows that memories are reconstructed whenever they are recalled, making them vulnerable to new information introduced after the original event. [UW Staff] staff.washington.edu UW StaffMisinformation and Memory The Creation of New Memories (1989)March 1, 1989... Published: March 1, 1989 For UFO reports, this means that descriptions of sound may gradually become more specific after: Watching television programmes about famous UFO encounters. Reading articles comparing multiple witness accounts . Participating in online discussion forums. Talking repeatedly with other witnesses or investigators. Instead of recalling an uncertain \"strange noise\", a witness may later remember an \"electrical hum\", \"vibrating resonance\" or \"metallic buzzing\" because those labels have become familiar within UFO culture. The change may occur sincerely rather than deliberately, reflecting the normal reconstructive nature of memory. [washington.edu] staff.washington.edu UW StaffMisinformation and Memory The Creation of New Memories (1989)March 1, 1989... Published: March 1, 1989 Research on eyewitness testimony also shows that ambiguous events are especially susceptible to schema driven recall. A schema is a mental framework for understanding familiar situations. When information is incomplete, people naturally fill gaps using the schema that seems to fit best. In experimental settings, participants exposed to ambiguous events are more likely to recall details consistent with an activated schema, even when those details were absent from the original experience. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov The influence of schemas, stimulus ambiguity, and interview schedule on eyewitness memory over time PubMed... The Confirmation Bias Channel: Sprouts Open on YouTube Historical changes in what witnesses report hearing Descriptions of UFO sounds have not remained constant across decades. Historical collections of reports show that witness language often reflects the technology and cultural imagery of the period. During the 1950s and 1960s, reports frequently referred to humming discs, jet like roaring or mechanical whirring, echoing contemporary aviation and industrial technology. Later decades saw more references to electronic buzzing, electromagnetic effects and sophisticated propulsion concepts as these ideas became common in science fiction, documentaries and public discussion. The broad pattern does not necessarily indicate that witnesses were describing different external phenomena. Instead, it illustrates how available cultural vocabulary provides convenient ways of describing unusual experiences whose acoustic characteristics may have been difficult to identify precisely. Sensation and Perception: Crash Course Psychology 5 Channel: CrashCourse Open on YouTube Media influence has limits The influence of media should not be overstated. Exposure to UFO stories does not automatically produce sightings, nor does it explain every report involving unusual sounds. Many witnesses describe experiences before learning of similar accounts, while others report sounds that bear little resemblance to common UFO narratives. Psychological evidence instead supports a more limited conclusion: media exposure can alter which interpretation feels most convincing when sensory evidence is uncertain. It may also influence the language used during later recollection, particularly after repeated discussion or prolonged reflection. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Top down influences on ambiguous perception: the role of stable and transient states of the observer PubMedDecember 8, 2014..."},{"title":"Confidence Gap","url":"/confidence-gap/","description":"A witness can become highly certain about a UFO noise even when timing, source or descriptive details have shifted in memory.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Perception","level":3,"text":"Confidence Gap Why Confident UFO Sound Memories Can Still Be Wrong Perception Confidence Gap Confidence Gap Why Confident UFO Sound Memories Can Still Be Wrong A witness can become highly certain about a UFO noise even when timing, source or descriptive details have shifted in memory. A witness can become highly certain about a UFO noise even when timing, source or descriptive details have shifted in memory. dataset evidence How confidence grows through repeated recall Why confidence and accuracy can diverge What investigators should ask beyond certainty ufo noises perception confidence gap Introduction A witness who is absolutely certain that a UFO emitted a humming, buzzing or mechanical sound may be giving a sincere account, but confidence alone does not establish that every remembered detail is accurate. Decades of psychological research show that confidence and memory accuracy are related in complex ways. Under some carefully controlled conditions, an immediate, uncontaminated memory can be both highly confident and highly accurate. However, confidence can also increase after repeated retelling, discussion, media exposure or suggestive questioning even when specific details have drifted from the original experience. This distinction is especially important when evaluating reports of unusual sounds associated with unidentified aerial phenomena (UAP), where the sound source, timing and acoustic characteristics are often difficult to verify. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological ScienceThe Relationship Between Eyewitness Confidence and Identification Accuracy: A New Synthesis Psycho... How confidence grows through repeated recall Memory is not a fixed recording that is replayed unchanged. Each act of remembering involves reconstruction, and the recalled version can become the basis for later memories. As a result, people often become more fluent and more certain when describing an event repeatedly, even if some details have gradually changed. [Wiley Online Library] onlinelibrary.wiley.com Wiley Online LibraryRepeated recall, retention interval and the accuracy–confidence relation in eyewitness memory Odinot 2006 Appli... For reports of alleged UFO sounds, this process may unfold over weeks or years: A witness initially recalls hearing an indistinct low frequency hum. Later conversations encourage comparison with other UFO reports mentioning electrical buzzing or vibration. Subsequent retellings adopt increasingly specific descriptions. The story becomes easier to recall, and that ease is experienced as confidence rather than as evidence that the memory has remained unchanged. Research on eyewitness memory has found that repetition itself can increase confidence in misinformation. Repeated exposure to the same claim makes it feel more familiar and believable, even when the claim originated after the original event rather than during it. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Repetition, not number of sources, increases both susceptibility to misinformation and confidence in the accuracy of eyewitn... This does not imply deliberate exaggeration. Instead, confidence often reflects how coherent and well rehearsed a memory has become rather than whether every remembered sound matches what was originally heard. Why confidence and accuracy can diverge The relationship between confidence and accuracy is more nuanced than the simple assumption that \"confident means correct\" or \"confident means unreliable.\" Psychological research shows both positions are too simplistic. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological ScienceThe Relationship Between Eyewitness Confidence and Identification Accuracy: A New Synthesis Psycho... Several mechanisms can produce a gap between certainty and sound accuracy. 3:06 Elizabeth F. Loftus False Memories Channel: The Brainwaves Video Anthology · Views: 84.2K · Uploaded: June 2017 · Length: 3 minutes 6 seconds Open on YouTube Source confusion People may accurately remember hearing a sound while becoming less certain where that sound originated. A distant aircraft engine, industrial machinery, insects or wind induced vibration may later become mentally linked with the unusual object that was being observed. The memory of the sound itself may remain genuine, while the remembered source gradually shifts through repeated reconstruction. This phenomenon, often discussed as source monitoring or source confusion, is well established in memory research and becomes more likely when events are unexpected or ambiguous. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIReference Guide on Eyewitness Identification Reference Manual on Scientific Evidence NCBI Bookshelf... Confidence inflation after feedback Confidence can increase when witnesses receive explicit or implicit confirmation from others. Examples include: investigators appearing to endorse a description; friends reporting similar experiences; reading published UFO accounts that resemble one's own; online discussions reinforcing a particular interpretation. Eyewitness research shows that confirming feedback can substantially inflate confidence without improving underlying memory accuracy. This is one reason forensic psychologists recommend recording a witness's initial confidence before outside influences accumulate. [American Psychological Association] apa.org American Psychological Association Eyewitness accuracy in police lineups American Psychological AssociationEyewitness accuracy in police lineupsApril 1, 2014... Published: April 1, 2014 Narrative coherence Humans naturally prefer a coherent story over disconnected observations. Instead of remembering: \"I saw an unusual light, and separately I heard an unfamiliar noise.\" people often come to remember: \"The object made a loud electrical hum.\" The second version is easier to understand, easier to tell and therefore easier to remember confidently, even if the original evidence linking the sound to the object was uncertain. [NCBI] ncbi.nlm.nih.gov NCBIReference Guide on Eyewitness Identification Reference Manual on Scientific Evidence NCBI Bookshelf... Why incompetent people think they're amazing David Dunning Channel: TED Ed Open on YouTube Evidence from eyewitness research Most scientific evidence on confidence and memory comes from eyewitness studies rather than UFO investigations specifically. Although identifying a suspect differs from recalling an unexplained aerial event, the underlying cognitive processes are highly relevant. Early research consistently found that confidence alone was a poor predictor of whether individual memories were accurate. In some experiments, the statistical relationship between confidence and correctness was surprisingly weak, demonstrating that people could express high certainty about incorrect recollections. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Eyewitness accuracy and confidence: within versus between subjects correlations PubMed... More recent work has refined that conclusion rather than overturning it. Studies indicate that an immediate confidence judgement made under carefully controlled conditions—before feedback, repeated interviews or contamination—can provide useful information about accuracy. Once memories have been repeatedly discussed or influenced by later information, however, confidence becomes much less informative because it may have been inflated independently of memory accuracy. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological ScienceThe Relationship Between Eyewitness Confidence and Identification Accuracy: A New Synthesis Psycho... For historical UFO cases, witnesses are frequently interviewed days, months or even decades after the reported event. By then, opportunities for memory reconstruction are far greater than in the controlled conditions where confidence has stronger evidential value. Are all of your memories real? Daniel L. Schacter Channel: TED Ed Open on YouTube What investigators should ask beyond certainty Because confidence and accuracy are not identical, investigators examining reported UFO sounds should treat certainty as one piece of evidence rather than as proof. More informative questions include: When was the sound first noticed? Determining whether the sound preceded, coincided with or followed the visual observation helps separate perception from later reconstruction. How was the sound described immediately after the event? Contemporary notes or recordings are generally more informative than memories recalled years later. Could the witness identify the sound's direction? Humans often struggle to localise low frequency or distant sounds accurately. Has the description changed over time? Comparing early and later accounts can reveal whether details became more elaborate through repeated recall. Were there possible conventional sound sources? Nearby aircraft, traffic, industrial equipment, wildlife or weather"},{"title":"Memory Drift","url":"/memory-drift/","description":"Repeated discussion and later information can turn a vague remembered noise into a detailed and confident account.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Perception","level":3,"text":"Memory Drift How Retelling Can Rewrite a UFO Sound Memory Perception Memory Drift Memory Drift How Retelling Can Rewrite a UFO Sound Memory Repeated discussion and later information can turn a vague remembered noise into a detailed and confident account. Repeated discussion and later information can turn a vague remembered noise into a detailed and confident account. mechanism Why memory is reconstructed during recall How conversations and leading questions add detail Why vivid memories may still be inaccurate ufo noises perception memory drift Introduction When people later describe the sound they associate with a UFO sighting, the account may be more detailed and more confidently told than it was immediately after the event. That increased coherence does not necessarily mean the memory has become more accurate. Decades of psychological research show that human memory is reconstructive rather than a literal recording. Each act of remembering is also an opportunity for the memory to be updated, reshaped or supplemented by later information. In the context of UFO reports, this means an initially uncertain recollection—such as \"I heard an odd noise\"—can gradually become a vivid description of a humming, buzzing or pulsing sound that feels entirely authentic to the witness. [cambridge.org] cambridge.org Cambridge University PressRE EVALUATING THE CREDIBILITY OF EYEWITNESS TESTIMONY: THE MISINFORMATION EFFECT AND THE OVERCRITICAL JUROR E... Understanding this process does not imply that witnesses are dishonest or imagining events. Instead, it highlights a well established feature of eyewitness memory that is especially relevant when unusual experiences are discussed repeatedly, compared with other accounts or explored through suggestive questioning. Why memory is reconstructed during recall Remembering is an active process. Rather than retrieving a perfect recording, the brain rebuilds an event using fragments of stored information together with current knowledge, expectations and beliefs. Every recall episode can therefore slightly alter what is remembered the next time. Psychologists have shown that recalling an event does not simply strengthen the original memory. Once a memory is reactivated, it can become vulnerable to incorporating new information before it is stored again. Experimental work suggests that even an early interview can increase susceptibility to later misleading information under some circumstances, illustrating that repeated retrieval and later exposure interact in complex ways. [Association for Psychological Science] psychologicalscience.org Association for Psychological ScienceRecalling a Witnessed Event Increases Eyewitness Suggestibility Psychological Science APS... For reported UFO sounds , this mechanism is particularly important because auditory memories are often less precise than visual memories. A witness may genuinely remember hearing something unusual while remaining uncertain about its exact qualities. Later attempts to explain or describe the sound naturally encourage the brain to fill gaps with details that make the overall experience more coherent. Instead of remaining as an uncertain impression, the remembered sound may gradually acquire: A clearer pitch or rhythm. A specific label such as \"electrical hum\", \"metallic buzz\" or \" low frequency vibration\". A stronger perceived connection to the observed object. A more definite sequence of events. These additions may reflect reconstruction rather than deliberate invention. 3:06 Elizabeth F. Loftus False Memories Channel: The Brainwaves Video Anthology · Views: 84.2K · Uploaded: June 2017 · Length: 3 minutes 6 seconds Open on YouTube How conversations and leading questions add detail Much of the reshaping of memory happens socially. After an unusual event, people often compare experiences with family members, friends, investigators or other witnesses. Each conversation introduces new descriptions, interpretations and assumptions that can become woven into later recollections. For example, a witness who originally says, \"There was some kind of noise,\" may later hear others describe a humming sound. During subsequent retellings, that description can become incorporated into the witness's own account without conscious awareness of its external origin. Psychologists describe this as a source monitoring problem: people may remember information accurately while forgetting where it came from. [Cambridge University Press] cambridge.org Cambridge University PressRE EVALUATING THE CREDIBILITY OF EYEWITNESS TESTIMONY: THE MISINFORMATION EFFECT AND THE OVERCRITICAL JUROR E... Leading questions can have a similar effect. Questions such as: \"Did the object make a buzzing sound?\" \"How loud was the humming?\" \"Could you feel the vibration?\" implicitly suggest that such characteristics existed. Even when asked innocently, these prompts encourage witnesses to search for matching details in memory instead of simply reporting uncertainty. Research on eyewitness testimony consistently finds that post event information can alter later recollections without witnesses recognising that their memories have changed. [cambridge.org] cambridge.org Cambridge University PressRE EVALUATING THE CREDIBILITY OF EYEWITNESS TESTIMONY: THE MISINFORMATION EFFECT AND THE OVERCRITICAL JUROR E... Why repeated retelling increases confidence One of the more counter intuitive findings in memory research is that confidence and accuracy do not always increase together. Repeatedly telling the same story makes it easier to retrieve. Because the account becomes more fluent and familiar, it often feels increasingly true to the person remembering it. Experimental studies have shown that repetition alone—not necessarily confirmation from multiple independent people—can increase both belief in misleading information and confidence that it is accurate. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Open source on nih.gov. Applied to UFO sound reports, this means a witness who recounts the experience many times may sincerely become more certain that: the sound had a particular tone; it came directly from the observed object; the sequence of events is remembered precisely; or specific acoustic features were noticed at the time. Greater confidence therefore cannot be treated as independent evidence that the remembered sound has become more faithful to the original experience. 7:28 EYE WITNESS TESTIMONY Loftus & Palmer (1974) Car Crash Experiment Cognitive Psychology Channel: Psychology Unlocked · Views: 45.3K · Uploaded: February 2022 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube Why vivid memories may still be inaccurate People often assume that especially vivid memories must be especially reliable. Psychological evidence does not support such a simple relationship. An unusual or emotionally significant event can certainly produce a powerful memory, but vividness reflects the subjective experience of remembering rather than objective accuracy. A detailed recollection may contain genuine sensory impressions mixed with later inferences, conversations, expectations or culturally familiar descriptions. This distinction matters in UFO investigations because witnesses frequently report striking experiences that they have reflected on for years. During that time, memories may become internally consistent while drifting away from the original sensory experience. A witness may honestly believe that every remembered detail—including the precise quality of an unusual sound—was perceived during the event, even if some elements emerged gradually through reconstruction. Memory research therefore encourages investigators to distinguish between the sincerity of a witness and the historical accuracy of every remembered detail. [cambridge.org] cambridge.org Cambridge University PressRE EVALUATING THE CREDIBILITY OF EYEWITNESS TESTIMONY: THE MISINFORMATION EFFECT AND THE OVERCRITICAL JUROR E... What this means for evaluating UFO sound reports Recognising memory drift does not require dismissing eyewitness testimony. Instead, it suggests that investigators should give particular weight to accounts recorded as close as possible to the original event and should preserve early descriptions before repeated interviews or public discussion influence later recollections. When comparing multiple versions of the same account, changes are not necessarily signs of deception. They may instead reflect the ordinary way human memory develops over time. An initially hesitant report of \"an unusual noise\" becoming a confident description of a distinctive humming or buzzing sound can be understood as a predictable consequence of reconstructive memory rather than evidence that the later account is either wholly true or wholly false. Within the broader study of expectation and memory in UFO sound reports, this mechanism helps explain why detailed narratives often emerge gradually. As memories are repeatedly recalled, discussed and reorganised into coherent stories, they"},{"title":"Identify Sources","url":"/identify-sources/","description":"Aircraft trackers, weather records, local activity, and witness locations can narrow the source of a strange sound before speculation begins.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises","level":2,"text":"Identify Sources A Practical Check Before Calling It a UFO UFO Noises Identify Sources Identify Sources A Practical Check Before Calling It a UFO Aircraft trackers, weather records, local activity, and witness locations can narrow the source of a strange sound before speculation begins. Aircraft trackers, weather records, local activity, and witness locations can narrow the source of a strange sound before speculation begins. policy intervention Check aircraft, drones, and helicopters Review weather and local activity Compare timing across multiple locations ufo noises identify sources Introduction When an unusual sound overhead is associated with a suspected UFO, the most useful first step is to rule out ordinary explanations before considering extraordinary ones. Most reports of unexplained humming, booming, buzzing or roaring can be investigated using publicly available information, including aircraft tracking data, weather records, local activity reports and observations from other locations. This approach does not assume that every unusual sound has a simple explanation, but it helps separate genuinely unexplained events from those caused by aircraft, helicopters, drones , atmospheric conditions or nearby industrial activity. A careful, evidence based check is often more informative than relying on memory or first impressions. Check Aircraft, Drones and Helicopters First Aircraft are among the most common sources of unexpected overhead noise, particularly at night or in poor visibility when the sound is heard before the aircraft is seen. Flight tracking services can show whether a commercial aircraft, business jet or many general aviation flights were overhead at the relevant time. Most modern aircraft broadcast their position using Automatic Dependent Surveillance–Broadcast (ADS B), allowing websites and mobile apps to reconstruct their flight path in near real time. [Flightradar24] flightradar24.com Automatic Dependent Surveillance Automatic Dependent Surveillance Broadcast (ADS B) Flightradar24... When comparing a flight track with a reported sound, note: The exact time the sound began and ended. The observer's location. The aircraft's altitude and direction of travel. Whether the sound continued after the aircraft had passed. A delay between seeing an aircraft and hearing it is normal because sound travels much more slowly than light. High altitude jets may be several kilometres beyond the point directly overhead before their noise reaches an observer. Helicopters deserve separate attention because they can hover, circle or fly slowly while producing distinctive low frequency rotor noise that may seem difficult to locate. Air ambulances, police helicopters, military training flights and utility inspections can all generate prolonged sounds that unfamiliar listeners may interpret as unusual. It is also important to recognise the limits of flight trackers. Not every aircraft appears on every service. Some military flights , older aircraft, aircraft with disabled or unavailable transponders, and some low level operations may be absent or only partially tracked. A missing aircraft on one website should therefore not be treated as proof that no aircraft was present. [support.adsbexchange.com] support.adsbexchange.com May 19, 2025... Published: May 19, 2025 Drones present a different challenge. Consumer drones usually operate at much lower altitudes and produce a high pitched buzzing sound rather than a deep hum. They are often difficult to see at dusk or night, particularly when flying against a dark background. In many countries there is no universally accessible public tracker showing every drone flight, so visual observation and local context remain important. Review Weather and Local Activity Atmospheric conditions can significantly alter how sound travels. Temperature inversions, wind direction and humidity may allow distant noises to carry much farther than expected while making their apparent direction difficult to judge. For example: Wind can carry aircraft or industrial noise well beyond its usual range. Temperature inversions may refract sound back towards the ground, making distant sources seem surprisingly close. Calm night time conditions reduce background noise, allowing faint mechanical sounds to become much more noticeable. Low cloud can reflect sound, creating the impression that it originates directly overhead rather than from the horizon. [arXiv] arxiv.org Observational Evidence for Wind Driven Low Pass Filtering of Infrasound at Short RangeJanuary 31, 2026... Published: January 31, 2026 Checking historical weather records for the date and time of an event can therefore provide valuable context. Wind speed, wind direction and cloud cover often explain why a familiar source sounded unfamiliar. Local activity should also be considered. Possible sources include: Construction work. Quarry blasting. Railway traffic. Industrial plants. Large ventilation systems. Agricultural machinery. Sporting events or concerts using sound systems. Fireworks or ceremonial displays. These activities may not be visible from the observer's location but can still produce sounds that travel considerable distances under favourable atmospheric conditions. 1:58 How does ADS B work? Channel: Flightradar24 · Views: 66.1K · Uploaded: April 2024 · Length: 1 minute 58 seconds Open on YouTube Compare Timing Across Multiple Locations One witness can accurately report what they experienced, but multiple independent observations provide a stronger basis for identifying a sound's origin. If several people in neighbouring areas report hearing the same noise, compare: The reported time. The apparent direction. The duration. Whether the sound changed pitch or intensity. Whether it appeared stationary or moving. A moving aircraft generally produces reports that progress across locations in a predictable sequence. By contrast, a fixed industrial source affected by wind may be heard over a broad area simultaneously. Social media posts, neighbourhood forums and local community groups can sometimes reveal whether many people heard the same sound. These reports should be treated cautiously because later accounts may be influenced by earlier descriptions, but independently timed posts can help establish whether an event was widespread. Record the Details Before Memory Changes Witness memory naturally changes over time, especially after discussion or media coverage. Recording observations immediately makes later comparison much more reliable. Useful details include: Date and exact time. Precise location. Weather conditions. Estimated direction. Duration. Whether the sound rose or fell in pitch. Whether any lights or aircraft were visible. Whether nearby animals reacted. Even simple smartphone recordings can provide objective evidence for later analysis, although many phone microphones reduce low frequency sounds automatically and may not reproduce what the listener actually heard. 22:59 How to Track Filtered Aircraft with ADS B Exchange Channel: 0x4rk0 · Views: 5.9K · Uploaded: April 2022 · Length: 22 minutes Open on YouTube Common Misidentifications Several ordinary sources repeatedly appear in reports initially described as mysterious overhead noises: High altitude jet traffic: Deep rumbling may arrive long after the aircraft passes overhead. Helicopters: Rotor beats can echo and appear to come from unexpected directions. Drones: High frequency buzzing may seem unusually loud when flying nearby at night. Military exercises: Fast jets, transport aircraft and helicopters often operate outside familiar commercial routes and may not always be visible on public trackers. Industrial equipment: Compressors, turbines and ventilation systems can create persistent humming that varies with wind conditions. Atmospheric acoustics: Temperature inversions and changing winds can make distant sources appear local or overhead. [flightradar24.com] flightradar24.com How Flightradar24 works Flightradar24 How Flightradar24 works Flightradar24... These explanations account for many reports without implying that every unusual sound will always receive a definitive identification. A Practical Decision Path When confronted with a genuinely unusual overhead sound, a systematic approach is more reliable than immediate speculation. 1. Record the exact time and location. 2. Check aircraft tracking services for nearby flights, remembering that not every aircraft will appear. 3. Consider helicopters, local airports and known flight paths. 4. Review weather conditions, particularly wind direction and cloud cover. 5. Look for reports of nearby industrial work, construction, events or military activity. 6. Compare observations with independent witnesses in surrounding locations. 7. Assess whether the available evidence consistently supports an ordinary source before concluding that the event remains unexplained. Following these steps does not guarantee an identification, but it"},{"title":"Timing Clues","url":"/timing-clues/","description":"Reports from several locations can show whether a sound moved like an aircraft or remained fixed like distant machinery.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Identify Sources","level":3,"text":"Timing Clues Can Neighboring Reports Trace a Sound's Path? Identify Sources Timing Clues Timing Clues Can Neighboring Reports Trace a Sound's Path? Reports from several locations can show whether a sound moved like an aircraft or remained fixed like distant machinery. Reports from several locations can show whether a sound moved like an aircraft or remained fixed like distant machinery. population context Building a reliable witness timeline Patterns that indicate a moving aircraft Patterns that suggest a fixed source ufo noises identify sources timing clues Introduction When several people in different neighbourhoods report the same unusual overhead sound, the timing of those reports can become one of the strongest tests of whether the source was actually moving. Instead of treating each witness account in isolation, comparing when the sound was first heard, when it was loudest and when it faded can reveal a consistent travel path that matches an aircraft or another mobile source. If the reports do not show a coherent progression, they may instead point towards a fixed industrial source whose sound was carried unusually far by atmospheric conditions. Used carefully, cross neighbourhood timing transforms scattered anecdotes into evidence that can be checked against known flight paths, weather conditions and geography rather than relying on impressions alone. [faa.gov] faa.gov Community Response to Noise Federal Aviation Administration July 22, 2022... Published: July 22, 2022 Can neighbouring reports trace a sound's path? A single witness can easily misjudge where a low frequency sound originated. Human hearing is poor at locating deep, sustained tones, especially at night or under cloudy skies. However, a series of independent reports from different locations provides information that no individual observer possesses. If a sound source is moving, the reports should usually show a logical sequence. For example, residents in one district hear the sound first, people in the next neighbourhood report it a minute or two later, and those farther along the same line hear it last. Even allowing for differences in reaction time and the delay caused by the finite speed of sound, the overall pattern often follows the movement of an aircraft. By contrast, if people across a wide area report hearing the sound begin and end at nearly the same time, the explanation may be different. A stationary industrial source, large ventilation system or distant motorway can become audible over many kilometres when atmospheric conditions refract sound back towards the ground. Under those conditions, people in several neighbourhoods may all notice the same distant source without it having travelled between them. [faa.gov] faa.gov Community Response to Noise Federal Aviation Administration July 22, 2022... Published: July 22, 2022 Building a reliable witness timeline The value of multiple reports depends more on consistency than on the number of witnesses. A handful of accurately timed observations is often more useful than dozens of vague recollections. Useful information includes: The exact or approximate time the sound was first noticed. When it became loudest. When it faded or stopped. The observer's location. Whether the sound appeared to move or remained in one direction. Whether any visual observations accompanied the sound. Independent accounts are especially valuable because they reduce the risk that later discussion influences people's memories. Reports collected from social media, neighbourhood groups or emergency call logs can sometimes be arranged chronologically to reveal a clear progression across an urban area. Maps become particularly informative when each report is plotted with its timestamp. Even without sophisticated acoustic modelling, a moving pattern often becomes visually obvious once locations are arranged in time order. 1:44 How does multilateration (MLAT) work? Channel: Flightradar24 · Views: 17.6K · Uploaded: July 2024 · Length: 1 minute 44 seconds Open on YouTube Patterns that indicate a moving aircraft A moving aircraft generally produces several recognisable timing characteristics. A consistent geographic progression. Reports advance steadily across neighbouring districts in the same direction rather than appearing randomly. Reasonable travel times. The intervals between reports broadly match the speed of an aircraft after allowing for sound propagation delays. Witnesses rarely experience perfectly sequential timing because sound from a high altitude aircraft may reach different observers after the aircraft has already moved well beyond its closest point. Changing sound character. Observers ahead of the aircraft's path may describe an approaching rumble, while those behind it report a receding roar. Different neighbourhoods often describe different phases of the same event. Agreement with known operations. When the inferred movement aligns with recorded commercial flights, known helicopter routes or military training areas, the timing evidence becomes substantially stronger. Aircraft noise depends not only on the flight path but also on aircraft type, engine power, altitude and local weather, meaning neighbouring communities can experience noticeably different sound levels from the same flight. [faa.gov] faa.gov Community Response to Noise Federal Aviation Administration July 22, 2022... Published: July 22, 2022 Patterns that suggest a fixed source Some timing patterns point away from an overhead moving object. A nearly simultaneous onset across multiple neighbourhoods is difficult to reconcile with a single aircraft passing overhead unless the witnesses are very close together. Instead, it may indicate that changing atmospheric conditions suddenly made a distant fixed source audible over a much larger area. Other indicators include: Reports remaining strongest from the same compass direction regardless of observer location. Similar descriptions continuing for long periods without a clear progression. Multiple evenings showing the same timing under comparable weather conditions. Correlation with industrial operating schedules rather than aircraft movements. Temperature inversions deserve particular attention. During an inversion, warmer air overlies cooler surface air, bending sound waves back towards the ground. This can allow distant machinery, transport corridors or airport operations to be heard across neighbourhoods that would normally be outside the audible range, creating the impression of an unexplained overhead source. [NOAA] prod 01 alb www noaa.woc.noaa.gov The Sound of Thunder National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration The Sound of Thunder National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration... How Does Air Traffic Turns Chaos into Order? The Hidden Map Above the Clouds Channel: History of Simple Things Open on YouTube Interpreting disagreements between witnesses Conflicting reports do not necessarily invalidate the evidence. Several factors naturally produce variation: People's clocks may differ by a minute or more. Many witnesses estimate times after the event rather than recording them immediately. Buildings, hills and vegetation alter local sound levels. Low frequency sounds are difficult for humans to localise accurately. Wind and atmospheric layering can cause one neighbourhood to hear the sound much more clearly than another. Rather than expecting perfect agreement, investigators should look for the overall trend. If independent reports consistently form a directional sequence, that pattern is often more informative than minor disagreements over exact times. Why timing evidence matters Cross neighbourhood timing is one of the simplest ways to distinguish between an apparently mysterious overhead sound and an ordinary moving source. It does not identify the source by itself, but it provides a testable hypothesis that can then be compared with aircraft tracking data, weather records and local activity. Within the broader task of identifying ordinary explanations for reported UFO noises , this approach shifts the discussion from isolated impressions to measurable patterns. A sound that appears to \"travel\" through successive neighbourhoods is far more consistent with a moving aircraft or similar vehicle than with a stationary mystery, while reports that remain synchronised across a wide area may instead reflect unusual sound propagation from a fixed source rather than anything overhead. [faa.gov] faa.gov Community Response to Noise Federal Aviation Administration July 22, 2022... Published: July 22, 2022 Standardized analyses of acoustic telemetry data using the R packages actel and RSP Channel: European Tracking Network Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Can Neighboring Reports Trace a Sound's Path?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Sound Book: The Science of the Sonic Wonders of the World By Trevor Cox A"},{"title":"Tracker Gaps","url":"/tracker-gaps/","description":"A missing flight track does not prove the sky was empty, because military, low-level and poorly received aircraft may not appear.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Identify Sources","level":3,"text":"Tracker Gaps When Flight Trackers Miss the Aircraft Overhead Identify Sources Tracker Gaps Tracker Gaps When Flight Trackers Miss the Aircraft Overhead A missing flight track does not prove the sky was empty, because military, low level and poorly received aircraft may not appear. A missing flight track does not prove the sky was empty, because military, low level and poorly received aircraft may not appear. dataset evidence Why aircraft disappear from public trackers How to compare several tracking sources What evidence can still confirm a flight ufo noises identify sources tracker gaps Introduction When investigating an unusual sound overhead, a public flight tracker is one of the quickest ways to test whether an aircraft could have been responsible. However, the absence of a flight on a tracking website is not evidence that the sky was empty. Public tracking services depend on aircraft transmitting suitable signals and on those signals being received, processed and displayed. Any weakness in that chain can leave a genuine aircraft unconfirmed. For anyone trying to rule out ordinary explanations for a suspected UFO related noise, the practical lesson is straightforward: treat flight trackers as powerful screening tools rather than complete records of everything flying overhead. Understanding where their gaps come from makes it much easier to interpret a missing track correctly and avoid drawing conclusions that the available evidence cannot support. Why Aircraft Disappear from Public Trackers Most public flight tracking websites rely primarily on Automatic Dependent Surveillance–Broadcast (ADS B) transmissions. Aircraft equipped with ADS B Out periodically broadcast their identity, GPS derived position, altitude and other information, which is received by networks of volunteer or commercial ground stations. If either the transmission or reception fails, the aircraft may disappear from public maps. [Flightradar24] flightradar24.com How We Track Flights with ADS B Flightradar24 Blog How We Track Flights with ADS B Flightradar24 BlogMay 22, 2015... Published: May 22, 2015 Several common situations create gaps. Aircraft without ADS B position broadcasts. Some older aircraft, certain categories of general aviation and aircraft operating outside airspace where ADS B is mandatory may not transmit the information required for public tracking. Some may only broadcast older Mode S or Mode C transponder signals, which provide less information or cannot be converted into a usable public track. [support.adsbexchange.com] support.adsbexchange.com May 19, 2025... Published: May 19, 2025 Low altitude reception limits. ADS B uses line of sight radio signals. Aircraft flying low behind terrain, buildings or vegetation may simply be invisible to nearby receivers even though they are audible on the ground. Coverage improves rapidly with altitude because the radio horizon expands. [Flightradar24] flightradar24.com How We Track Flights with ADS B Flightradar24 Blog How We Track Flights with ADS B Flightradar24 BlogMay 22, 2015... Published: May 22, 2015 Sparse receiver coverage. Public services depend on their own receiver networks. Remote rural areas, mountainous regions and some coastal or oceanic areas have fewer receivers, increasing the likelihood of incomplete or missing tracks. [Flightradar24] flightradar24.com How We Track Flights with ADS B Flightradar24 Blog How We Track Flights with ADS B Flightradar24 BlogMay 22, 2015... Published: May 22, 2015 Intentional operational choices. Certain military, government or security sensitive flights may use temporary identifiers, disable transmissions where regulations permit, or operate under procedures that reduce their visibility on some public services. [faa.gov] faa.gov AD S B Privacy Federal Aviation Administration ADS B Privacy Federal Aviation AdministrationMarch 22, 2025... Published: March 22, 2025 These limitations mean that a loud aircraft can still be physically present even if no corresponding symbol appears on a familiar flight tracking app. 1:44 How does multilateration (MLAT) work? Channel: Flightradar24 · Views: 17.6K · Uploaded: July 2024 · Length: 1 minute 44 seconds Open on YouTube Why Different Tracking Websites Show Different Results A common source of confusion is that one tracking service may display an aircraft while another does not. This difference usually reflects how each service collects and presents data , not necessarily whether an aircraft existed. Some services combine multiple data sources, including ADS B, multilateration (MLAT) derived from Mode S transmissions and, in some countries, additional official data feeds. Others rely almost entirely on direct radio reception from volunteer receivers. Their filtering policies also differ. ADS B Exchange, for example, states that it displays aircraft whenever its receivers detect valid broadcasts and does not voluntarily remove flights from its public display, whereas other services may honour privacy or display restrictions in some circumstances. [support.adsbexchange.com] support.adsbexchange.com June 13, 2025... Published: June 13, 2025 Differences also arise because receiver networks vary in density. One service may have excellent local coverage while another has few nearby receivers, leading to different maps despite observing the same airspace. [Flightradar24] flightradar24.com How We Track Flights with ADS B Flightradar24 Blog How We Track Flights with ADS B Flightradar24 BlogMay 22, 2015... Published: May 22, 2015 For someone checking an unexplained overhead sound, disagreement between tracking websites should therefore be interpreted as a cue to gather more evidence rather than as proof that one map is correct and the other is wrong. How to Compare Several Tracking Sources Comparing multiple sources can reduce the chance of overlooking an ordinary aircraft. A practical workflow is: 1. Check at least two independent public tracking services rather than relying on a single application. 2. Compare both live and historical playback if the event happened earlier. 3. Look for partial tracks that begin or end suddenly, which may indicate reception gaps rather than an aircraft appearing or disappearing physically. 4. Note the reported altitude and position. A distant high altitude jet can produce delayed sound, while a nearby low level helicopter may never have been tracked consistently. 5. If one service shows only fragments of a route, avoid assuming the missing portions represent periods when the aircraft was absent. This comparison helps distinguish between genuine tracking gaps and situations where every available source consistently shows empty airspace. 1:58 How does ADS B work? Channel: Flightradar24 · Views: 66.1K · Uploaded: April 2024 · Length: 1 minute 58 seconds Open on YouTube What Evidence Can Still Confirm a Flight? Even when no complete public track exists, other evidence may still indicate that an aircraft caused the sound. Useful supporting evidence includes: Visual observations from multiple witnesses describing navigation lights, anti collision strobes or aircraft silhouettes. Characteristic sound signatures , such as rotor beats from helicopters or the delayed rumble of high altitude jet engines. Airport activity , including departures or arrivals matching the reported time. Air traffic control recordings where legally available, especially near controlled airports. Photographs or videos containing navigation lights or contrails that can be compared with known aircraft behaviour. Consistent reports from nearby observers , particularly if they describe the same direction of travel and timing. No single piece of evidence is definitive on its own, but several independent indicators together often provide a stronger explanation than a missing entry on a flight tracker. Interpreting Missing Flight Data Carefully One of the most common reasoning errors in UFO investigations is treating an absent flight track as positive evidence that no aircraft could have produced the observed sound. Public flight trackers are excellent investigative tools, but they were never designed as complete surveillance systems. Their coverage depends on aircraft equipment, regulatory requirements, radio reception, network density and each provider's processing policies. Consequently, a missing track should be regarded as an information gap , not as confirmation that an overhead sound lacked an ordinary aviation source. [adsbexchange.com] support.adsbexchange.com May 19, 2025... Published: May 19, 2025 Within a broader process of identifying ordinary sources overhead, the most reliable approach is to combine flight tracking data with weather conditions, witness observations and other contextual evidence. That combination is far more robust than relying on any single tracking map in isolation. Why Most Planes Don't Need RADAR Tracking Any More Channel: Scott Manley Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field"},{"title":"Local Sources","url":"/local-sources/","description":"Construction, railways, quarries, events and industrial systems can create distant sounds that seem aerial under favorable conditions.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Identify Sources","level":3,"text":"Local Sources Which Ground Activities Can Sound Like UFOs? Identify Sources Local Sources Local Sources Which Ground Activities Can Sound Like UFOs? Construction, railways, quarries, events and industrial systems can create distant sounds that seem aerial under favorable conditions. Construction, railways, quarries, events and industrial systems can create distant sounds that seem aerial under favorable conditions. event time window Matching sound types to local activities Finding permits, notices and event schedules Using wind and terrain to test a source ufo noises identify sources local sources Introduction Many reports of mysterious booms, rumbles and persistent humming that seem to come from the sky are ultimately traced to ordinary activity on the ground. Distance, darkness, wind, terrain and atmospheric conditions can make familiar sources appear to be overhead, especially when the sound itself is heard more clearly than its origin can be seen. Before treating an unexplained noise as evidence of something unusual, it is worth checking whether construction, rail operations, quarry blasting, industrial machinery or large public events were taking place within several kilometres—or sometimes much farther away. Sound can travel unexpectedly well under certain conditions, and low frequency noise is particularly difficult for people to locate accurately. [gov.uk] GOV.UK Noise and vibration management: environmental permits 23, 2021... Which Ground Activities Can Sound Like UFOs? The most common sources differ in the type of sound they produce. Matching the character of the noise with likely local activity is often more productive than searching for visual explanations. Sound reportedCommon ground sourceTypical cluesSingle deep boomQuarry or mining blast, demolition, sonic industrial processOccurs during working hours, sometimes felt as vibrationRepeated boomingBlasting programme, heavy pile driving, military training nearbyRegular intervals over minutes or hoursContinuous low humFactories, power infrastructure, ventilation systems, pumping stationsStrongest at night or during calm weatherRhythmic rumbleFreight trains, rail maintenance, heavy road trafficBuilds and fades, sometimes accompanied by vibrationLow roaring overheadLarge industrial fans, cooling towers, motorway traffic carried by windAppears directionless during temperature inversionsPulsing mechanical droneConstruction cranes, compressors, generatorsOften begins and ends with work shifts Low frequency sounds deserve particular attention because human hearing is poor at determining their direction. A distant industrial fan or train can therefore appear to be directly above an observer, especially if reflections from clouds or nearby buildings obscure the true origin. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK Noise and vibration management: environmental permits 23, 2021... Matching Sound Types to Local Activities Construction and infrastructure work Large construction projects generate many sounds that can seem unfamiliar outside normal working areas. Pile driving produces sharp impacts, while tunnelling equipment, compressors and generators create continuous mechanical hums. Bridge works, road resurfacing and concrete pumping can continue after dark in urban areas to reduce disruption to daytime traffic. Useful indicators include: Recently installed roadworks. Crane lighting visible at night. Temporary traffic diversions. Public notices about overnight engineering work. Many local councils publish advance notices for major infrastructure projects, particularly where overnight work is expected. Why Bristol Is Plagued By A Mysterious Noise🎵🌉 Channel: Paul And Sanja Open on YouTube Railways Railways produce a wider variety of sounds than many people realise. Freight trains create long duration low frequency rumbling that can carry considerable distances. Curved track can generate wheel squeal, while maintenance grinding, ballast tamping and engineering trains often operate overnight when passenger services are reduced. Wind direction and atmospheric conditions may make trains audible from several kilometres away while hiding the tracks themselves behind hills or buildings. Federal Railroad Administration guidance notes that railway sound propagation depends on distance, ground conditions and atmospheric effects, all of which influence how loud or directional a train appears. [Railroad Administration] railroads.dot.gov Railroad Administration Guidance on Assessing Noise and Vibration Impacts FRA Railroad Administration Guidance on Assessing Noise and Vibration Impacts FRA Quarries and blasting Quarry blasting frequently produces reports of unexplained booms because the visible explosion is usually hidden behind terrain or occurs many kilometres away. Depending on local geology, observers may experience both airborne sound and ground vibration. In many jurisdictions, blasting follows published schedules or permit conditions, with regular daytime operating windows except under approved special circumstances. Checking quarry operator announcements or regulatory notices can quickly determine whether blasting coincided with the reported event. [Legal Information Institute] law.cornell.edu Legal Information Institute30 CFR § 816.64 Use of explosives: Blasting schedule. Electronic Code of Federal Regulations (e CFR) US Law LII / Legal Information InstituteNovemb... Industrial facilities Factories, refineries, wastewater treatment plants and distribution centres often operate continuously. Cooling systems, extraction fans, refrigeration equipment and pumps can create persistent humming that becomes much more noticeable after sunset when background traffic declines. Some industrial sounds vary with production cycles, meaning the hum may begin suddenly, continue for several hours and then disappear without any obvious explanation to nearby residents. Finding Permits, Notices and Event Schedules Many seemingly mysterious sounds can be investigated using publicly available information before assuming an unknown source. Useful places to check include: Local council planning and construction notices. Highway authority announcements about overnight roadworks. Railway engineering possession schedules where publicly available. Quarry operator websites or mining authority announcements. Environmental permit registers for major industrial facilities. Stadium, concert venue and festival event calendars. Firework display announcements during holidays or local celebrations. Environmental permitting systems in several countries require industrial operators to assess and manage noise impacts, and significant facilities may publish or provide information about expected noisy operations. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK Noise and vibration management: environmental permits 23, 2021... Large concerts also deserve consideration. Powerful sound systems, particularly those emphasising bass frequencies, can produce distant low frequency pulses that travel farther than speech or music at higher frequencies. Under favourable weather conditions these bass components may be audible long after the rest of the performance is inaudible. Blasting Area Awarenes Stay Safe Around Blasting Operations Online Training Certificate Channel: OSHA Online Center Open on YouTube Using Wind and Terrain to Test a Source One practical way to investigate an unusual sound is to determine whether weather conditions favour long distance sound propagation. Questions worth asking include: Was the wind blowing from a known industrial area towards the observation point? Did the sound become louder after sunset? Was there low cloud, fog or a temperature inversion? Are there valleys, water surfaces or open farmland between the suspected source and the observer? These conditions can refract or reflect sound, making distant machinery seem both louder and closer than expected. Environmental noise assessment guidance recommends considering propagation conditions because weather significantly alters how sound reaches listeners. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK Noise and vibration management: environmental permits Noise and vibration management: environmental permits Terrain can also produce misleading impressions. Hills may block direct sight of a quarry while allowing sound to pass over the ridge. Urban streets can channel low frequency noise between buildings, making it appear to descend vertically. A Practical Time Window Investigation When investigating a reported boom or persistent hum, work through the relevant time window rather than relying on memory alone. 1. Record the exact start and end times as accurately as possible. 2. Mark the observation location. 3. Check whether construction, rail engineering, blasting or public events were scheduled during that period. 4. Compare wind direction with the location of possible sources. 5. Look for reports from nearby communities rather than only immediate neighbours. Independent reports from several locations often help distinguish a widespread"},{"title":"Sound Weather","url":"/sound-weather/","description":"Low cloud, calm air and temperature inversions can make distant engines sound louder, closer and more directly overhead.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Identify Sources","level":3,"text":"Sound Weather Why Distant Noise Can Sound Directly Overhead Identify Sources Sound Weather Sound Weather Why Distant Noise Can Sound Directly Overhead Low cloud, calm air and temperature inversions can make distant engines sound louder, closer and more directly overhead. Low cloud, calm air and temperature inversions can make distant engines sound louder, closer and more directly overhead. mechanism How low cloud reflects mechanical noise How inversions bend sound toward the ground Which weather records matter most ufo noises identify sources sound weather Introduction When people report a mysterious humming, roaring or droning noise during a suspected UFO event, the sound itself is often genuine even if its apparent location is misleading. One of the most important reasons is that the atmosphere does not transmit sound in a simple straight line. Low cloud, calm night time conditions and temperature inversions can make distant aircraft, trains, industrial plants or traffic sound unusually loud and seem to come from directly overhead rather than from the horizon. Understanding these weather effects helps explain why an ordinary mechanical source can appear to have no obvious origin, especially on quiet evenings when visual cues are limited. [Met Office] weather.metoffice.gov.uk Met Office What is a temperature inversion? Met OfficeWhat is a temperature inversion? Met Office... How Low Cloud Reflects Mechanical Noise A low cloud deck can act as a partial acoustic reflector. While clouds are not perfect mirrors for sound, extensive low cloud reduces the amount of sound energy escaping upward. Instead, some of the energy is scattered back towards the ground, where it combines with the direct sound from the source. For an observer standing beneath an overcast sky, this can produce several effects: Aircraft engines may seem louder than on a clear day. Engine noise can linger after the aircraft has passed. [faa.gov] faa.gov Aircraft Noise Federal Aviation Administration January 31, 2025 — AIRCRAFT NOISE Who to contact about noise complaints TOPICS ON THIS PAGE Aircraft and airport noise programs High... Published: January 31, 2025 Sounds from different directions may blend together, making the source difficult to pinpoint. Noise may appear to descend from the cloud base instead of arriving from a distant horizon. This effect is particularly noticeable with continuous low frequency sounds such as turbofan engines, helicopters and large industrial machinery. Human hearing estimates direction partly by comparing tiny timing and loudness differences between the two ears. When reflections arrive from above as well as from the true source direction, those cues become less reliable, increasing the impression that the sound is overhead rather than several kilometres away. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Meteorological and ground effects on the propagation of aircraft noise close to the Earth's surface ScienceDirect... The phenomenon becomes even more convincing when the cloud base is only a few hundred metres above the ground, because reflected sound returns quickly enough to merge with the original noise rather than being recognised as a separate echo. ACOUSTICS: Refraction and Temperature Inversion Channel: The Point Studios Open on YouTube How Temperature Inversions Bend Sound Towards the Ground A temperature inversion occurs when air becomes warmer with height instead of cooler. These conditions commonly develop during calm, clear or lightly overcast nights under high pressure, especially after the ground has cooled rapidly following sunset. [Met Office] weather.metoffice.gov.uk Met Office What is a temperature inversion? Met OfficeWhat is a temperature inversion? Met Office... From an acoustic perspective, inversions matter because sound travels slightly faster through warmer air than colder air. As sound moves upward into progressively warmer layers, its path bends back towards the cooler air near the ground. Rather than dispersing into the atmosphere, the sound is refracted downwards, allowing it to travel much greater distances than expected. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Meteorological and ground effects on the propagation of aircraft noise close to the Earth's surface ScienceDirect... In practical terms, this means: Aircraft cruising many kilometres away may sound surprisingly close. Motorways, railways or industrial sites normally hidden by distance become clearly audible. Low frequency engine noise often carries farther than higher pitched sounds. Listeners may perceive the sound as moving overhead even though the source never passed nearby. The atmosphere effectively becomes a natural waveguide, repeatedly steering sound back towards the surface instead of allowing it to disperse upward. Importantly, the strongest audible effect often comes from the combination of several mechanisms—including atmospheric refraction, wind structure and interactions with the ground surface—rather than from the inversion acting alone. Long standing acoustic research has shown that these combined effects explain many reports of unexpectedly strong aircraft noise over large distances. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Meteorological and ground effects on the propagation of aircraft noise close to the Earth's surface ScienceDirect... Why Quiet Night Air Makes Distant Engines Stand Out Weather affects not only how sound propagates but also how easily it is noticed. At night: Road traffic usually decreases. Birds and insects are often quieter. Human activity declines. Wind speeds frequently weaken near the surface. With less competing background noise, sounds that would be ignored during the day become obvious. A distant aircraft that would normally blend into daytime ambient noise may become the loudest sound in the environment. This change in perception is significant during UFO reports. Witnesses frequently describe a low hum or steady roar accompanying an unseen object. In many cases, the weather has simply increased the contrast between a distant mechanical source and an unusually quiet environment rather than creating a new sound. [Met Office] weather.metoffice.gov.uk Met Office What is a temperature inversion? Met OfficeWhat is a temperature inversion? Met Office... What is a Temperature Inversion? For Student Pilots Channel: Part Time Pilot Open on YouTube Why Sound Direction Can Be So Misleading Humans locate sound best when it arrives directly from a nearby source. Long distance propagation under unusual weather conditions degrades those directional cues. Several factors contribute: Multiple arrival paths: Direct and reflected sound reach the listener from different angles. Low frequency dominance: Bass sounds are naturally harder to localise than higher frequencies. Lack of visual confirmation: At night or beneath cloud, the brain cannot verify the source visually. Changing wind and atmospheric layers: Slight changes in atmospheric structure alter the apparent direction over time. These effects can produce the convincing impression that an invisible object is hovering overhead even when the true source is several kilometres away. Which Weather Records Matter Most When investigating an unusual overhead sound, historical weather data are often as useful as flight information. The most relevant records include: Cloud base and cloud cover: Extensive low cloud increases the likelihood of reflected sound. Surface wind speed and direction: Calm conditions reduce masking noise, while winds can channel sound towards or away from the observer. Temperature profile: Evidence of an overnight inversion strongly supports enhanced long distance sound propagation. Fog or mist: These often accompany stable air masses in which inversions develop, although the fog itself is not the primary reason sound carries farther. [Met Office] weather.metoffice.gov.uk Met Office What is a temperature inversion? Met OfficeWhat is a temperature inversion? Met Office... Many national meteorological services archive hourly observations that include cloud cover, wind, temperature and pressure, allowing investigators to reconstruct the acoustic environment at the time of a report. Reflection and Refraction of Sound Edexcel IGCSE Physics Revision Channel: IGCSE Science Revision Open on YouTube Interpreting UFO Noise Reports in Context Weather related sound relocation does not prove that every unusual overhead noise has an ordinary explanation. It does, however, demonstrate that atmospheric conditions can substantially alter both the loudness and apparent direction of familiar mechanical sounds. When a UFO report consists mainly of an unexplained humming, roaring or engine like sound without a clearly observed object, checking cloud cover, wind conditions and evidence of a temperature inversion is a practical first step. If those conditions were present, distant aircraft or industrial activity become considerably more plausible explanations than they would under clear,"},{"title":"Phone Audio","url":"/phone-audio/","description":"Smartphones can preserve timing and pitch changes, but their microphones often suppress the deep frequencies witnesses remember most.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Identify Sources","level":3,"text":"Phone Audio Why Your Phone May Miss the Deep Hum Identify Sources Phone Audio Phone Audio Why Your Phone May Miss the Deep Hum Smartphones can preserve timing and pitch changes, but their microphones often suppress the deep frequencies witnesses remember most. Smartphones can preserve timing and pitch changes, but their microphones often suppress the deep frequencies witnesses remember most. policy intervention What phone microphones capture well Why low frequencies are reduced How to make a more useful recording ufo noises identify sources phone audio Introduction If you hear an unusual deep humming, rumbling or droning sound overhead and suspect it may be connected to a UFO report, a phone recording can still be valuable—but it should not be treated as a perfect record of what you heard. Modern smartphones are designed primarily for speech, video and everyday environmental sounds. Their microphones and recording software often alter very low frequency audio through filtering, automatic gain control and noise suppression. As a result, a recording may preserve the timing of a mysterious sound and reveal changes in pitch or rhythm while failing to reproduce the deep bass that made the experience seem unusual in person. [Android Developers] developer.android.com Developers Microphone Info API reference Android Developers Android DevelopersMicrophoneInfo API reference Android DevelopersFebruary 13, 2026... Published: February 13, 2026 Understanding these limitations helps prevent a common mistake during investigations: assuming that a weak or thin recording proves the original sound was equally weak. Instead, recordings should be considered one piece of evidence alongside witness observations, weather conditions and checks for ordinary overhead sources. What Phone Microphones Capture Well Smartphone microphones are remarkably capable within the range they are designed for. They usually capture: Changes in pitch over time. Rhythmic pulsing or modulation. The duration of an event. Relative loudness changes. Directional clues when recorded with multiple microphones. These characteristics can be useful when comparing a reported sound with known aircraft, helicopters or industrial machinery. For example, the distinctive pulse rate of helicopter rotor blades or the gradual frequency shift of an aircraft passing overhead may remain visible in the recording even if the deepest components are reduced. Modern phones often contain multiple MEMS (micro electro mechanical systems) microphones. Operating systems can expose information about each microphone's characteristics, including frequency response, because different microphones and recording modes may behave differently. [Android Developers] developer.android.com Developers Microphone Info API reference Android Developers Android DevelopersMicrophoneInfo API reference Android DevelopersFebruary 13, 2026... Published: February 13, 2026 High Pass Filter: Better Mixes Made Simple Channel: Learn Audio Engineering Open on YouTube Why Low Frequencies Are Reduced The most important limitation for reports involving deep humming is that many phones do not preserve very low frequencies in the same way human listeners perceive them. Several factors contribute: Small microphone design. Tiny MEMS microphones are engineered for compact devices and generally prioritise the frequency range most important for speech rather than extremely deep bass. High pass filtering. Many recording paths intentionally reduce very low frequencies to minimise wind noise, handling noise and mechanical vibration. Noise suppression. Signal processing algorithms may classify steady low frequency sounds as unwanted background noise and reduce them before the recording is saved. Automatic gain control (AGC). Phones frequently adjust recording level automatically. A loud rumble can cause the recording level to drop, making surrounding sounds seem quieter or altering how the event is remembered. [android.com] developer.android.com Developers Noise Suppressor API reference Android Developers Android DevelopersNoiseSuppressor API reference Android Developers... This processing is beneficial for voice calls and video recording but can unintentionally remove exactly the type of low frequency content that witnesses often describe in reports of mysterious overhead sounds. Independent laboratory measurements also show that microphone response varies noticeably between phone models. Some newer devices reproduce low frequencies better than others, but none should be assumed to provide a perfectly flat, laboratory quality recording across the entire audible spectrum. [Faber Acoustical] faberacoustical.com Faber AcousticalMeasured: iPhone 15 Pro Max microphone frequency response and directivity – Faber Acoustical BlogSeptember 12, 2024... Published: September 12, 2024 Why the Recording May Sound Different From What You Heard People do not experience sound in exactly the same way as a phone. Very low frequency sound can be felt as vibration or pressure as much as it is heard. Buildings, furniture and the listener's own body can reinforce these sensations. A phone microphone does not experience these physical effects in the same way. In addition, many playback systems—including laptop speakers, phone speakers and inexpensive headphones—cannot reproduce deep bass accurately. Even if the phone captured some low frequency information, the playback device may remove it again, making the recording seem much less dramatic than the original experience. This explains why witnesses sometimes report that \"the recording doesn't sound anything like it did in person.\" That difference is consistent with known properties of consumer recording equipment and does not automatically indicate either an extraordinary event or a faulty memory. How to Make a More Useful Recording A phone recording can still be highly valuable if it is collected carefully. For the best investigative value: 1. Start recording as soon as possible. Capturing the beginning and end of the event helps establish timing. 2. Keep the phone still. Handling noise can create its own low frequency rumble that obscures genuine sounds. 3. Avoid covering the microphones. Fingers, clothing or phone cases may alter the recording. 4. Record continuously. Do not stop and restart repeatedly, as this can interrupt useful changes in the sound. 5. Narrate key observations quietly. Mention the time, direction you are facing and whether the sound appears to move. Spoken notes can later be matched with the audio. 6. If safe, record from more than one location. A short recording indoors followed by one outdoors may help distinguish building vibrations from airborne sound. These steps improve the recording's usefulness without requiring specialist equipment. Best Microphone for YouTube 2026: What I'd Actually Buy Channel: Justin Brown Primal Video Open on YouTube Interpreting the Recording Carefully A phone recording should be interpreted with an understanding of its technical limitations. A recording that lacks strong bass does not necessarily mean the original sound contained little low frequency energy. Likewise, a recording dominated by low frequency rumble may reflect wind striking the microphones, movement of the phone or nearby mechanical vibration rather than an overhead source. Investigators should compare the recording with other evidence, including: The witness's written description made as soon as possible after the event. Weather conditions that may have affected sound propagation. Aircraft and helicopter activity. Other independent recordings or witness accounts from nearby locations. Looking for agreement across these sources is generally more informative than relying on the audio file alone. When Better Equipment Makes a Difference If unusual low frequency sounds occur repeatedly in the same location, a dedicated audio recorder with an external microphone designed for environmental recording is likely to provide a more accurate record than a smartphone. Professional and scientific microphones can offer a flatter response into much lower frequencies than typical phone microphones, avoiding some of the aggressive filtering built into consumer devices. [Analog Devices] analog.com an 1328 Analog DevicesAN 1328: High Performance, Low Noise Studio Microphone with MEMS Microphones, Analog Beamforming, and Power Management An... For isolated, unexpected events, however, the best recorder is usually the one already in your pocket. Even if the deepest hum is reduced, a smartphone recording can still preserve timing, pitch changes and other acoustic clues that help distinguish ordinary overhead sources from sounds that warrant closer investigation. How to Measure Microphones Channel: Listen, Inc. Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Your Phone May Miss the Deep Hum. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Sound and"},{"title":"Low Hums","url":"/low-hums/","description":"Low humming is frequently mentioned in UFO accounts, but similar sounds commonly come from transformers, machinery, aircraft, and infrastructure.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises","level":2,"text":"Low Hums What Causes the Low Hum in UFO Reports? UFO Noises Low Hums Low Hums What Causes the Low Hum in UFO Reports? Low humming is frequently mentioned in UFO accounts, but similar sounds commonly come from transformers, machinery, aircraft, and infrastructure. Low humming is frequently mentioned in UFO accounts, but similar sounds commonly come from transformers, machinery, aircraft, and infrastructure. mechanism How witnesses describe the hum Common electrical and industrial sources Why low frequencies are hard to locate ufo noises low hums Introduction Low humming is one of the most frequently reported sounds in UFO witness accounts , yet it is also one of the least distinctive. Across decades of reports, witnesses have described a deep, steady vibration resembling an electrical transformer, distant generator, power substation or large industrial motor. At the same time, acoustics research shows that low frequency sounds are notoriously difficult for people to locate accurately, can travel long distances, and often appear to come from no obvious direction. This overlap makes the \"low hum\" an important feature of UFO folklore, but not a unique indicator of an unidentified object. Instead, it illustrates how an unusual sensory experience can arise from a combination of genuine environmental sound, challenging listening conditions and uncertain visual observations. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA How witnesses describe the hum Unlike dramatic reports of roaring engines or explosive noises, the low hum is generally described as continuous, subdued and mechanical rather than loud. Common comparisons include: An electrical transformer or high voltage substation. A large diesel engine heard from a distance. Industrial ventilation equipment. A powerful generator operating under constant load. A deep electrical vibration felt as much as heard. The descriptions are remarkably consistent across many decades of UFO literature despite differences in the reported appearance of the object. Rather than varying with disc, sphere or triangular shapes, the reported sound is usually characterised by its low pitch and steady quality. The British UFO Research Association (BUFORA) notes that while many reported UFOs are completely silent, those that do produce sound are most commonly described as humming, buzzing, whirring or whining. Humming is therefore a recurring—but far from universal—feature of witness testimony. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA Historical collections of alleged vehicle interference cases also include numerous reports in which witnesses claimed to hear a loud or persistent hum while an unidentified object was nearby. These reports are often presented alongside claims of stalled engines or electrical interference, although the underlying causes remain disputed and the cases are observational rather than experimentally verified. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Vehicle interference case compendium Center for UFO StudiesAN UPDATE ON VEHICLEOctober 31, 2025... Published: October 31, 2025 Common electrical and industrial sources One reason the humming motif appears so often is that many ordinary sources generate remarkably similar sounds. Examples include: Electrical transformers vibrating at mains frequency. Power substations. HVAC systems and industrial ventilation fans. Large compressors. Pumping stations. Diesel generators. Factory machinery. Distant road or rail infrastructure under suitable atmospheric conditions. Many of these sources produce energy concentrated in low frequency bands. Because these frequencies lose comparatively little energy over distance, a sound originating hundreds of metres—or even kilometres—away may remain audible after higher frequency components have faded. Environmental acoustics specialists have long noted that persistent low frequency noise often seems detached from its source. Buildings can amplify certain frequencies through resonance, making the sound appear stronger indoors than outdoors. Listeners frequently report that the hum seems to come \"from everywhere\" or shifts location as they move around, making intuitive localisation unreliable. [environmental protection.org.uk] environmental protection.org.uk Low Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... The HUM Sample (Kokomo Hum Taos Hum Bristol Hum) Channel: AVL Designs Open on YouTube Why low frequencies are hard to locate Humans determine the direction of most sounds by comparing tiny differences in arrival time and loudness between the two ears. This process works well for higher frequency sounds because their short wavelengths create clear directional cues. Low frequency sounds behave differently. Their long wavelengths reduce these directional differences, making it much harder for the auditory system to estimate where the sound originated. Additional complications include: Reflection from buildings and terrain. Ground vibration coupling into structures. Indoor resonance. Wind and atmospheric temperature layers altering sound propagation. Masking by background environmental noise. As a result, listeners may sincerely perceive a hum as originating overhead, behind them or from an unidentified location when the physical source is actually a fixed piece of infrastructure some distance away. Environmental health guidance on low frequency noise identifies source localisation as one of the principal practical difficulties encountered during investigations. [environmental protection.org.uk] environmental protection.org.uk Low Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... The connection with \"The Hum\" Reports of UFO humming are sometimes linked with the broader phenomenon known simply as \"The Hum\"—persistent low frequency sounds reported in locations such as Taos in New Mexico, Kokomo in Indiana and several communities in the United Kingdom. Although these phenomena are occasionally connected in popular discussion, they differ in important ways. The Hum is generally reported as: Persistent over weeks or months. Experienced repeatedly at the same location. Often heard only by a subset of residents. Usually unaccompanied by unusual aerial objects. By contrast, humming in UFO reports is normally brief and associated with a single sighting. Investigations into Hum reports have proposed a wide range of explanations, including industrial machinery, ventilation systems, transport infrastructure, electrical equipment, environmental vibration and, in some cases, internal auditory phenomena similar to low frequency tinnitus. No single explanation accounts for every reported Hum, but the phenomenon demonstrates that mysterious low frequency sounds can occur without requiring an unidentified aerial source. [environmental protection.org.uk] environmental protection.org.uk Low Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... The Proof Is Out There: DISTURBING Sounds From Beneath the Earth (Season 1) History Channel: HISTORY Open on YouTube What historical case patterns show Across historical UFO case collections, low humming appears repeatedly but without a consistent accompanying pattern. Some reports describe: A stationary object producing a steady hum. A hovering light accompanied by a transformer like vibration. A humming sound immediately before rapid departure. A hum reported together with alleged electrical disturbances. Other well known cases involve no sound at all, despite apparently low altitude or close proximity. This inconsistency is important. If the hum reflected a single physical propulsion mechanism, researchers might expect broadly similar acoustic characteristics across reports. Instead, the reported sounds range from complete silence to buzzing, roaring, crackling and humming, suggesting either multiple unrelated causes or substantial variability in witness perception and environmental circumstances. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA What the low hum can—and cannot—tell us The recurrence of transformer like humming in UFO reports makes it an interesting historical feature of witness testimony, but not a diagnostic one. A low hum alone cannot distinguish between: An unidentified aerial phenomenon. A conventional aircraft heard under unusual conditions. Nearby electrical infrastructure. Industrial machinery. Atmospheric propagation of distant sound. Structural vibration within buildings. Individual auditory perception. Acoustic research consistently shows that low frequency sounds are among the hardest environmental noises to identify and locate accurately. Consequently, investigators generally treat reports of humming as one observational detail to be evaluated alongside visual evidence, timing, weather conditions, nearby infrastructure and other independent information, rather than as evidence of an extraordinary source. [environmental protection.org.uk] environmental protection.org.uk Low Frequency"},{"title":"Industrial Sources","url":"/industrial-sources/","description":"Generators, compressors, fans and pumping stations can produce persistent mechanical tones that resemble reported UFO humming.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Low Hums","level":3,"text":"Industrial Sources Which Machines Can Mimic a UFO Hum? Low Hums Industrial Sources Industrial Sources Which Machines Can Mimic a UFO Hum? Generators, compressors, fans and pumping stations can produce persistent mechanical tones that resemble reported UFO humming. Generators, compressors, fans and pumping stations can produce persistent mechanical tones that resemble reported UFO humming. population context Common industrial sound sources How distance changes the sound Environmental checks near a sighting ufo noises low hums industrial sources Introduction Many reports of unidentified flying objects describe a persistent, low mechanical hum rather than a dramatic roar. One of the most plausible conventional explanations is that the sound originated not from an unusual object in the sky but from industrial equipment operating nearby or several kilometres away. Large compressors, ventilation systems, pumping stations, transformers and other heavy machinery commonly generate stable low frequency tones that can be difficult for people to locate accurately. Under favourable weather and terrain conditions, these sounds may appear detached from their true source, creating the impression that a hovering or distant object is emitting the hum. Understanding how industrial machinery produces these sounds helps explain why some UFO reports include remarkably similar acoustic descriptions without requiring an extraordinary cause. Which Machines Can Mimic a UFO Hum? Industrial installations are among the most common producers of continuous low frequency sound. Unlike sharp mechanical noises, these systems often emit steady tones that remain audible long after higher frequency components have faded with distance. Common examples include: Electrical transformers and substations: Power transformers naturally vibrate because alternating current causes their magnetic cores to expand and contract slightly, producing the familiar electrical hum. Whether this hum is audible beyond the site depends on transformer design, shielding and surrounding structures. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK National Policy Statement for electricity networks infrastructure (EN 5), 2025 (accessible webpage) GOV.UKJanuary 6, 2026... Published: January 6, 2026 Large air compressors: Reciprocating and rotary compressors generate pronounced low frequency energy, often concentrated around frequencies that travel efficiently through the environment. Industrial noise control guidance specifically identifies compressors as challenging sources because conventional sound absorbers are less effective at these frequencies. [HSE] hse.gov.uk HSETreating low frequency compressor noise March 7, 2025... Published: March 7, 2025 Industrial ventilation and extraction fans: Large axial and centrifugal fans can produce continuous tonal noise related to blade rotation and airflow. These tones may dominate the soundscape even when the machinery itself is hidden inside buildings or rooftop plant rooms. [arXiv] arxiv.org Numerical and experimental study of tonal noise sources at the outlet of an isolated centrifugal fanNovember 27, 2020... Published: November 27, 2020 Pumping stations and boilers: Municipal water systems, sewage pumping stations and large heating plants often operate continuously or cycle on for extended periods, producing an unobtrusive but persistent hum that residents may struggle to identify. [Environmental Protection] environmental protection.org.uk Environmental Protection Low Frequency Noise Environmental ProtectionLow Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... Industrial process equipment: Gas turbines, vibratory screens, forced draft fans and similar machinery are recognised sources of environmental low frequency noise, particularly around manufacturing and energy facilities. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Methodology for the evaluation of low frequency environmental noise: a case study ScienceDirect... The important point is not simply that these machines are noisy, but that many produce stable, almost featureless tones. As distance increases, the distinctive rattles, clicks and mechanical details disappear first, leaving behind the deep humming quality that many witnesses describe. The Source Behind a Mystery Sound Heard Around the World Channel: Inside Edition Open on YouTube How Distance Changes the Sound Industrial machinery rarely sounds the same hundreds of metres away as it does beside the equipment. Low frequency sound behaves differently from higher frequency noise in several important ways: It loses energy more slowly over distance. Long wavelengths bend around obstacles more effectively. Buildings and the ground can reflect and reinforce certain frequencies. Wind direction and temperature layers can carry sound unexpectedly far. As a result, someone standing kilometres from an industrial site may hear only a smooth, continuous drone while all the higher frequency clues that would identify the machinery have been absorbed or scattered. Environmental acoustics specialists note that low frequency noise often appears difficult to trace because listeners lose many of the directional cues normally used to identify where a sound originates. [Environmental Protection] environmental protection.org.uk Environmental Protection Low Frequency Noise Environmental ProtectionLow Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... This explains why witnesses sometimes report that a hum seemed to come \"from everywhere\", drifted across the landscape or appeared disconnected from any visible source. Why Industrial Hums Can Be Mistaken for a Nearby Object When a person is already focused on an unusual light or object in the sky, an unrelated environmental hum may naturally become associated with it. Several acoustic effects contribute to this impression: Poor directional perception : Humans are much less accurate at locating very low frequency sounds than higher pitched ones. Visual dominance: Once attention is fixed on an unusual visual event, the brain tends to associate simultaneous sounds with the object being observed. Feature loss over distance: The sound no longer resembles a recognisable machine and instead becomes a simple, continuous vibration. Changing reflections: Nearby buildings, hills or woodland can alter apparent sound direction as a listener moves. None of these effects proves that a particular UFO report involved industrial machinery, but they demonstrate how an authentic environmental sound can become linked with an unrelated visual observation. Why do transformers make a humming noise? Channel: The Engineering Mindset Open on YouTube Environmental Checks Near a Sighting When evaluating reports that include a mysterious hum, investigators commonly examine the surrounding environment before treating the sound as evidence of an unusual craft. Useful questions include: Is there a factory, warehouse or industrial estate within several kilometres? Are there electrical substations or high voltage transformers nearby? Does the area contain water treatment works or pumping stations? Are there large refrigeration plants, HVAC systems or rooftop ventilation equipment? Was the sound continuous, or did it cycle on and off in a pattern consistent with industrial machinery? Were weather conditions favourable for long distance sound propagation, such as calm winds or temperature inversions? Because many industrial systems operate overnight or outside normal working hours, witnesses may be unaware that nearby equipment was active when the observation occurred. Why the Explanation Is Plausible—but Not Universal Industrial machinery provides a strong explanation for many reports of unexplained humming because its acoustic characteristics closely match numerous witness descriptions. Environmental acoustics research consistently identifies pumps, fans, compressors, transformers and similar installations as common sources of persistent low frequency hums that are difficult to localise and can travel considerable distances. [environmental protection.org.uk] environmental protection.org.uk Environmental Protection Low Frequency Noise Environmental ProtectionLow Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... However, this explanation cannot automatically account for every UFO report involving a humming sound. Some sightings occur in remote areas with no obvious industrial infrastructure, while others include additional observations that require separate evaluation. The presence of a low hum alone is therefore best treated as a clue that warrants checking nearby industrial sources rather than as evidence either for or against an unidentified aerial phenomenon. Why Do Transformers HUM? Normal vs. Concerning Transformer Sounds Explained Channel: Maddox Transformer Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Which Machines Can Mimic a UFO Hum?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Handbook of Noise and Vibration"},{"title":"Overhead Illusion","url":"/overhead-illusion/","description":"Long wavelengths and weak directional cues can make a distant fixed hum feel as though it is coming from the sky.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Low Hums","level":3,"text":"Overhead Illusion Why Does a Low Hum Seem Overhead? Low Hums Overhead Illusion Overhead Illusion Why Does a Low Hum Seem Overhead? Long wavelengths and weak directional cues can make a distant fixed hum feel as though it is coming from the sky. Long wavelengths and weak directional cues can make a distant fixed hum feel as though it is coming from the sky. mechanism How ears locate sound Why low frequencies blur direction How buildings and terrain redirect perception ufo noises low hums overhead illusion Introduction Reports of a low humming sound that seems to come from directly overhead are common in UFO accounts, yet the sensation itself is not unusual from an acoustics perspective. A deep, continuous hum can appear to descend from the sky even when its true source is on the ground or many kilometres away. The effect arises because human hearing relies on directional cues that become much less reliable at low frequencies. Long sound wavelengths, reflections from buildings and terrain, and atmospheric conditions can all blur the apparent origin of a hum until it seems to come from \"everywhere\"—or from above. This mechanism helps explain why witnesses may sincerely perceive an overhead sound without that perception , by itself, demonstrating that an airborne object produced it. [Environmental Protection] environmental protection.org.uk Environmental Protection Low Frequency Noise Environmental ProtectionLow Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... How ears determine where a sound comes from Human hearing locates most sounds by comparing what reaches each ear. Two principal cues are used: Interaural time difference (ITD): the tiny difference in arrival time between the ears. Interaural level difference (ILD): the slight difference in loudness caused by the head blocking part of the sound. For higher pitched sounds, these cues are usually strong because their short wavelengths interact noticeably with the head. The brain can therefore estimate direction with remarkable precision. Deep humming sounds behave differently. Their wavelengths are often much larger than the width of a human head, so they wrap around it rather than being significantly blocked. As a result, the loudness difference between the ears becomes extremely small, depriving the auditory system of one of its strongest directional signals. At low frequencies, listeners rely mainly on timing cues, but these too become less reliable in real environments where reflections and reverberation are present. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMC... ‘The Hum’: The Unexplained Noise 2% of People Can Hear Channel: The Atlantic Open on YouTube Why low frequencies blur direction A persistent low hum is especially difficult to place because several effects reinforce one another. First, low frequency waves spread efficiently around obstacles instead of casting sharp acoustic \"shadows\". Unlike a bird call or a whistle, which may point clearly towards its source, a deep hum reaches both ears with nearly identical characteristics. Second, these frequencies travel long distances with comparatively little loss. By the time they reach a listener, many of the higher frequency components that would normally provide localisation cues may have disappeared, leaving only a diffuse low frequency component. Third, the brain is much better at identifying horizontal direction than elevation. Humans infer whether a sound is above or below largely from subtle filtering by the outer ear, but those spectral cues are strongest at higher frequencies. When a sound contains little high frequency energy, the brain has fewer clues about height, making an overhead interpretation more likely. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMC... How buildings and terrain redirect perception The environment often contributes as much to the illusion as the original sound source. Low frequency sound reflects from large surfaces such as hillsides, warehouse walls, tower blocks and broad roof structures. Multiple reflected paths can arrive almost simultaneously, creating a diffuse sound field in which no single direction dominates. Rather than hearing a sound coming from one side, listeners may perceive it as surrounding them. Buildings can also resonate at particular low frequencies. Structural vibration may make ceilings, walls or windows radiate sound into a room, causing the apparent source to shift from outdoors to the building itself. Because ceilings occupy much of the upper visual field indoors, listeners may naturally interpret the sound as coming from above rather than from a distant industrial source or transport corridor. Environmental noise specialists note that low frequency noise is frequently reported as difficult or impossible to trace, with indoor resonance often making it seem detached from its true origin. [Environmental Protection] environmental protection.org.uk Environmental Protection Low Frequency Noise Environmental ProtectionLow Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... Terrain produces similar effects. Valleys, embankments and uneven ground can redirect or channel sound so that a distant source is heard from an unexpected direction. Under favourable conditions, listeners may therefore attribute an ordinary ground based hum to the sky. How BASS Works (In Rooms) Acoustic Geometry Channel: Acoustic Geometry Open on YouTube Why UFO witnesses may experience an overhead hum Within UFO reports, the perception of an overhead hum often occurs while witnesses are already looking upwards. That visual focus can reinforce the brain's interpretation of an otherwise ambiguous sound. Imagine a witness observing an unusual light while simultaneously hearing a low mechanical hum. If the sound lacks reliable directional information and the visual attention is fixed on the object overhead, the auditory system has little evidence to contradict the impression that both sensations share the same source. This process is a well known characteristic of multisensory perception: when hearing is uncertain, vision often dominates the perceived location of an event. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMC... Importantly, this does not imply that witnesses are fabricating their experience. The impression of an overhead sound can be genuine even if the physical source lies elsewhere. Why \"coming from above\" is not strong evidence by itself An overhead hum is therefore a useful observation but not a diagnostic one. Similar descriptions can arise from: distant transformers or substations; large ventilation or HVAC systems; industrial machinery; road, rail or marine infrastructure under favourable propagation conditions; building resonance transmitting low frequency energy indoors. Environmental investigations of persistent low frequency hums repeatedly show that such sounds can appear detached from their origins and may resist straightforward localisation, even during detailed acoustic surveys. In some cases the exact source remains unresolved, but the difficulty of locating it is itself consistent with the known behaviour of low frequency sound rather than evidence of an aerial origin. [Environmental Protection] environmental protection.org.uk Environmental Protection Low Frequency Noise Environmental ProtectionLow Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... For that reason, investigators evaluating UFO reports generally treat an apparent overhead hum as one observation among many. Its evidential value depends on whether it is supported by independently verifiable data—such as multiple synchronised recordings or instrument measurements—rather than on the perceived direction of the sound alone. What is Binaural Sound Localization? Channel: Hearing and Balance Doctors Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Does a Low Hum Seem Overhead?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Science of Sound By Thomas D. Rossing, Paul A. Wheeler et al. Written for students without a background in mathematics or physics, this textbook provides an introduction to the study of acoustics. It... See on Amazon Book Master Handbook of Acoustics By F. Alton Everest, Ken Pohlmann Practical Instruction on the Art and Science of Acoustic Design and Architecture Build your own acoustic environments such as recording s... See on Amazon Book Fundamentals of Acoustics By Lawrence E. Kinsler, Austin R. Frey et al. Dargestellt werden die physikalischen und mathematischen Konzepte zur Beschreibung"},{"title":"Transformer Hum","url":"/transformer-hum/","description":"Transformer vibration can closely match the steady low hum witnesses sometimes associate with a nearby UFO.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Low Hums","level":3,"text":"Transformer Hum Could a Transformer Explain the UFO Hum? Low Hums Transformer Hum Transformer Hum Could a Transformer Explain the UFO Hum? Transformer vibration can closely match the steady low hum witnesses sometimes associate with a nearby UFO. Transformer vibration can closely match the steady low hum witnesses sometimes associate with a nearby UFO. mechanism How transformer vibration sounds Why the source can seem overhead Clues that distinguish infrastructure noise ufo noises low hums transformer hum Introduction A steady, low electrical hum is one of the most common sounds described by people who report a nearby unidentified flying object (UFO). Yet that same description closely matches the sound produced by electrical transformers and substations. This overlap is significant because transformer hum is a well understood physical phenomenon that can travel surprisingly long distances, penetrate buildings and become difficult to locate accurately. In some situations, a listener may genuinely perceive the sound as coming from above or from an apparently empty area, even when its true source is fixed infrastructure rather than anything airborne. This does not explain every UFO report, but it does provide a plausible, evidence based mechanism for a subset of cases in which the principal auditory feature is a continuous low frequency hum. [gov.uk] GOV.UK National Policy Statement for electricity networks infrastructure (EN 5), 2025 (accessible webpage) GOV.UKJanuary 6, 2026... Published: January 6, 2026 How transformer vibration sounds Electrical transformers generate sound because alternating current causes their laminated steel cores to expand and contract by microscopic amounts through a process called magnetostriction . Although the movement is tiny, it occurs repeatedly with every cycle of the electrical supply, creating a stable tonal vibration that listeners recognise as transformer hum. In countries using a 50 Hz mains supply, the dominant audible tone commonly appears around 100 Hz because the mechanical deformation occurs twice during each electrical cycle. [samaterials.com] samaterials.com Stanford Advanced Materials Magnetostriction and Transformers Stanford Advanced Materials Magnetostriction and Transformers People often describe transformer hum as: A deep, steady drone rather than a roar. More noticeable than directional. Constant in pitch with little fluctuation. Something that can sometimes be felt as vibration as well as heard. These characteristics resemble many UFO witness descriptions that compare the sound to an electrical installation, generator or distant industrial machine rather than an aircraft engine. The similarity is notable because witnesses frequently choose the same real world comparison: \"like a transformer\". The comparison itself does not identify the source—it simply reflects how closely the acoustic signatures resemble one another. Government guidance on electricity infrastructure also recognises transformer hum as a normal by product of substations and notes that whether it is audible outside depends on transformer design, installation and surrounding sound attenuation. [GOV.UK] GOV.UK National Policy Statement for electricity networks infrastructure (EN 5), 2025 (accessible webpage) GOV.UKJanuary 6, 2026... Published: January 6, 2026 The Controversial Sound Only 2% Of People Hear Channel: Benn Jordan Open on YouTube Why the source can seem overhead The most confusing aspect of transformer hum is often not the sound itself but its apparent location. Human hearing determines direction by comparing tiny differences in arrival time and loudness between the two ears. Those cues become much weaker at low frequencies because the wavelengths are very long. As a result, listeners often struggle to determine whether the sound is coming from in front, behind, above or below. [Environmental Protection] environmental protection.org.uk Environmental Protection Low Frequency Noise Environmental ProtectionLow Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... Several environmental effects can make the problem worse: Long distance propagation. Low frequency sound loses less energy over distance than many higher frequency sounds, allowing it to remain audible after sharper components have disappeared. [Environmental Protection] environmental protection.org.uk Environmental Protection Low Frequency Noise Environmental ProtectionLow Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... Building resonance. Walls, floors and rooms may amplify particular frequencies, making the hum stronger indoors than outside or in one room but not another. [Environmental Protection] environmental protection.org.uk Environmental Protection Low Frequency Noise Environmental ProtectionLow Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... Reflections. Buildings, terrain and other structures can reflect low frequency sound, making it appear detached from its physical origin. [Environmental Protection] environmental protection.org.uk Environmental Protection Low Frequency Noise Environmental ProtectionLow Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... Structural vibration. Instead of hearing only airborne sound, people may perceive vibration transmitted through the ground or building framework, making localisation even less reliable. [Environmental Protection] environmental protection.org.uk Environmental Protection Low Frequency Noise Environmental ProtectionLow Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... If a witness is already focused on an unusual light or object in the sky, the brain may naturally associate an otherwise ambiguous hum with that visual stimulus, even if the sound originates from nearby electrical infrastructure. Why transformer hum is often mistaken for something unusual Transformer hum has several properties that encourage misidentification without requiring any unusual acoustic phenomenon. First, it is generally continuous rather than intermittent. Aircraft engines usually change pitch as the aircraft moves relative to the observer, whereas transformer hum tends to remain remarkably stable. Second, the source is stationary while the listener may move. As a person walks around, room resonance and reflections can make the apparent direction shift unexpectedly. This can create the impression that the sound is following them or has no identifiable origin. Environmental health organisations note that tracing persistent low frequency hums is often difficult even for investigators using conventional sound measurements. [environmental protection.org.uk] environmental protection.org.uk Environmental Protection Low Frequency Noise Environmental ProtectionLow Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... Finally, transformer hum often becomes most noticeable at night. Reduced background traffic and other environmental sounds allow the remaining low frequency component to stand out more clearly, increasing the chance that an observer notices it while looking at an unusual light in the sky. [Environmental Protection] environmental protection.org.uk Environmental Protection Low Frequency Noise Environmental ProtectionLow Frequency Noise Environmental Protection UK... The Source Behind a Mystery Sound Heard Around the World Channel: Inside Edition Open on YouTube Clues that distinguish infrastructure noise Although transformer hum can resemble reported UFO sounds , several practical clues favour an electrical source over an airborne one. Features consistent with transformer or substation noise include: The hum maintains a nearly constant pitch. The sound continues after any visual sighting has ended. It is strongest near buildings, substations or overhead electrical infrastructure. Different observers disagree about the direction it comes from. The hum becomes louder in certain rooms or locations indoors. Features that would be less consistent with transformer hum include: A sound that accelerates or changes pitch rapidly in synchrony with an apparently moving object. A clearly directional source that tracks a visible object's motion across the sky. Brief, highly localised bursts rather than a persistent drone. Complex tonal changes unlike the stable spectrum expected from transformer vibration. None of these features alone proves or disproves a UFO interpretation, but they help distinguish a fixed environmental noise source from an object genuinely producing sound while moving through the air. What acoustics research suggests Research into environmental low frequency noise shows that persistent hums can be surprisingly difficult to identify without specialised measurements. Standard sound level readings may underestimate annoyance because low frequency energy behaves differently from higher frequency noise, and identifying the exact source may require narrow band analysis or microphone arrays designed specifically for localisation. [sagepub.com] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsAnnoyance Due to Environmental Low Frequency Noise and"},{"title":"Vehicle Cases","url":"/vehicle-cases/","description":"Some historical cases link a persistent hum with stalled engines or electrical trouble, but the claims remain observational and disputed.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Low Hums","level":3,"text":"Vehicle Cases Did UFO Hums Really Disrupt Vehicle Engines? Low Hums Vehicle Cases Vehicle Cases Did UFO Hums Really Disrupt Vehicle Engines? Some historical cases link a persistent hum with stalled engines or electrical trouble, but the claims remain observational and disputed. Some historical cases link a persistent hum with stalled engines or electrical trouble, but the claims remain observational and disputed. dataset evidence Patterns in vehicle interference reports Why witness accounts are difficult to verify Conventional electrical and mechanical explanations ufo noises low hums vehicle cases Introduction Reports of a low, steady humming sound sometimes appear alongside claims that a nearby unidentified flying object caused a vehicle engine to stall, headlights to dim, or radios to fail. Within UFO literature, these accounts are often grouped as vehicle interference cases and are treated as one of the few categories that allegedly involve measurable physical effects rather than unusual lights alone. However, the evidence remains observational rather than experimental. Witnesses consistently describe a sequence of events, but there has never been controlled testing demonstrating that an unidentified object can produce the reported combination of humming, engine failure and electrical disruption. The result is a body of intriguing case reports that continues to be debated rather than confirmed. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Vehicle interference case compendium Center for UFO StudiesAN UPDATE ON VEHICLEOctober 31, 2025... Published: October 31, 2025 Patterns in vehicle interference reports Historical collections assembled by civilian UFO researchers identify recurring features across many alleged vehicle interference incidents. Although details vary, the reported sequence often follows a recognisable pattern: A driver notices an unusual light or structured object near the road. A low humming, buzzing or whirring sound is heard, sometimes described as resembling a transformer or generator. The vehicle develops problems such as engine stalling, dimming headlights, radio static or erratic dashboard instruments. After the object departs, the vehicle starts again without obvious repairs. Compilations produced by organisations such as the Centre for UFO Studies (CUFOS) and the British UFO Research Association (BUFORA) include dozens of reports fitting some variation of this pattern. These catalogues are valuable as historical datasets because they preserve witness testimony in a consistent format, but they are not controlled scientific studies and therefore cannot establish causation. [cufos.org] cufos.org Vehicle interference case compendium Center for UFO StudiesAN UPDATE ON VEHICLEOctober 31, 2025... Published: October 31, 2025 One notable feature is that the reported hum is rarely described as painfully loud. Instead, witnesses commonly refer to a persistent mechanical vibration accompanying the alleged electrical disturbance. This makes the sound notable within the reports but not distinctive enough to identify a unique physical mechanism. UFOs That Shut Down Engines: The Shocking Levelland 1957 Case Channel: Omen Lights Open on YouTube The Levelland case and similar reports The best known historical example is the Levelland, Texas incident of November 1957. During a stormy night, police received numerous independent calls from motorists reporting a brightly illuminated object near roads outside the town. Several witnesses stated that their engines stopped, headlights failed or dimmed, and normal operation resumed after the object disappeared. Some descriptions also included a loud humming or rushing sound accompanying the object's movement. [uaprad.org] uaprad.org Levelland Incident Research Deep Dive UAPRAD Levelland Incident Research Deep Dive UAPRAD Levelland remains influential because it involved multiple witnesses reporting similar effects over several hours rather than a single isolated account. Even so, similarities between reports do not by themselves demonstrate a common external cause. Many of the witnesses learned that others had experienced unusual events that night, making it difficult to separate entirely independent observations from developing local awareness. Other cases collected in vehicle interference catalogues report comparable combinations of humming and temporary vehicle malfunction. Examples include accounts in which drivers described: engines stopping despite no immediately obvious mechanical fault; radios filling with static before an object appeared; humming or swishing sounds while an illuminated object hovered nearby; vehicles restarting normally once the object departed. These reports span different countries and decades, but nearly all depend primarily on witness recollection rather than preserved physical evidence. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Vehicle interference case compendium Center for UFO StudiesAN UPDATE ON VEHICLEOctober 31, 2025... Published: October 31, 2025 Why witness accounts are difficult to verify Vehicle interference reports are often presented as stronger than ordinary sighting reports because they involve an observable effect on machinery. In practice, however, several factors make them difficult to evaluate rigorously. First, most cases occurred unexpectedly at night on isolated roads. Few included contemporaneous instrument recordings, mechanical inspections performed immediately after the event, or environmental measurements of electrical or magnetic conditions. Second, many reports describe vehicles that resumed normal operation before mechanics could identify any fault. A vehicle restarting successfully does not by itself indicate an external influence; intermittent ignition faults, moisture related electrical problems and temporary fuel delivery issues can produce similar behaviour. Third, the reported humming itself cannot usually be reconstructed. Human memory is reasonably good at recalling that an unusual sound occurred but much less reliable at preserving precise acoustic characteristics such as frequency, loudness and duration, particularly during stressful events. Because of these limitations, historical catalogues document consistent testimony rather than experimentally verified physical interactions. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Vehicle interference case compendium Center for UFO StudiesAN UPDATE ON VEHICLEOctober 31, 2025... Published: October 31, 2025 UFO Encounter: The Levelland Incident The Texas Sightings That Stopped Cars in Their Tracks — E... Channel: State of the Unknown Open on YouTube Conventional electrical and mechanical explanations Several conventional explanations have been proposed for the combination of humming sounds and apparent vehicle interference. Atmospheric electricity. The US Air Force's Project Blue Book investigation of the Levelland incident concluded that severe weather, including electrical atmospheric phenomena such as ball lightning, offered the most likely explanation. Storm conditions were present in the area, and investigators suggested that moisture and electrical effects could contribute to temporary ignition problems in vehicles. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Levelland UFO case Levelland UFO case Temporary ignition faults. Many vehicles involved in classic reports from the 1950s and 1960s relied on mechanical ignition systems that were more vulnerable to moisture, loose electrical connections and intermittent failures than modern electronic systems. Engines that stopped unexpectedly could sometimes restart after only a brief pause. Misidentified environmental sounds. A distant transformer, industrial equipment, electrical infrastructure or other low frequency source may produce a steady hum that becomes psychologically linked to an unexpected visual event. Low frequency sounds are particularly difficult for listeners to locate accurately, making them seem associated with an object that is not actually generating the sound. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA These explanations do not account conclusively for every reported detail, but they demonstrate that ordinary mechanisms can reproduce important elements of the claimed pattern without invoking an unknown technology. What the vehicle interference datasets actually show The historical collections are best understood as archives of recurring witness narratives rather than proof of a physical phenomenon. They demonstrate that: claims linking humming sounds with temporary vehicle failures have appeared repeatedly since the 1950s; similar descriptions occur across multiple independent reports and investigators; the available evidence consists largely of testimony gathered after the events rather than controlled measurements; no reproducible experiment has confirmed that unidentified aerial phenomena can generate the alleged electrical effects. Consequently, the recurring association between humming and vehicle interference"},{"title":"The Hum","url":"/the-hum-56f665/","description":"Brief humming during a sighting differs from persistent community Hum reports, despite their similar low-frequency character.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Low Hums","level":3,"text":"The Hum Is UFO Humming the Same as The Hum? Low Hums The Hum The Hum Is UFO Humming the Same as The Hum? Brief humming during a sighting differs from persistent community Hum reports, despite their similar low frequency character. Brief humming during a sighting differs from persistent community Hum reports, despite their similar low frequency character. dataset evidence How the two report patterns differ Places associated with persistent Hum reports Explanations proposed for each phenomenon ufo noises low hums the hum Introduction Brief humming reported during an alleged UFO sighting is not the same phenomenon as The Hum , even though both are commonly described as deep, low frequency sounds resembling a distant diesel engine, electrical transformer or industrial machinery. The key distinction is one of context and duration. In UFO reports, humming is typically a short lived feature associated with the appearance of an unusual object. By contrast, The Hum refers to persistent or recurring low frequency sounds reported by residents over weeks, months or even years, usually without any accompanying aerial phenomenon. Although the two experiences share descriptive language, they have developed as separate bodies of evidence with different investigative approaches and proposed explanations. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Manifestations of a low frequency sound of unknown origin perceived worldwide, also known as \"the Hum\" or the \"Taos Hum\" PubMedJu... How the two report patterns differ The similarities between UFO humming and The Hum can make them appear related at first glance. Witnesses in both cases often compare the sound to: A diesel engine idling in the distance. An electrical transformer. A large generator. A deep mechanical vibration felt as much as heard. The resemblance largely ends there. Reports of UFO humming are generally characterised by: A clearly defined beginning and end. A sound occurring simultaneously with a reported object in the sky. Duration measured in seconds or minutes. Witness attention focused primarily on the visual event. The Hum follows a very different pattern: The sound recurs repeatedly or remains almost continuous. No unusual aerial object is normally reported. The experience may continue for months or years. The sound often disrupts sleep, particularly at night. Only a minority of people in the same area typically report hearing it. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Manifestations of a low frequency sound of unknown origin perceived worldwide, also known as \"the Hum\" or the \"Taos Hum\" PubMedJu... This distinction is important because the evidential questions differ. UFO investigators ask whether the reported sound is associated with an observed object, while researchers studying The Hum investigate whether there is a persistent environmental, physiological or psychological cause independent of any aerial sighting. The UFO Phenomenon Full Documentary 2021 7NEWS Spotlight Channel: 7 News Spotlight Open on YouTube Places associated with persistent Hum reports Several locations have become well known because of sustained reports of unexplained low frequency noise. These cases differ fundamentally from isolated UFO encounters because the reports accumulated over long periods and involved multiple residents rather than individual sightings. The best known examples include: Bristol, England , where widespread reports emerged during the 1970s. Taos, New Mexico , where reports in the early 1990s prompted formal scientific investigation. Kokomo, Indiana , where investigators identified multiple industrial low frequency sources contributing to at least some complaints. Windsor, Ontario , where residents reported an intermittent hum for years, with studies examining possible industrial activity across the nearby international border. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Manifestations of a low frequency sound of unknown origin perceived worldwide, also known as \"the Hum\" or the \"Taos Hum\" PubMedJu... These locations illustrate that The Hum is treated primarily as an environmental acoustics problem rather than a UFO phenomenon. Investigations have typically involved acoustic measurements, industrial surveys and hearing studies instead of eyewitness reconstruction of aerial events. Why the same descriptions appear in both subjects The overlap in vocabulary does not necessarily imply a shared cause. Low frequency sound is difficult for humans to localise accurately because long wavelengths provide relatively weak directional cues. Environmental reflections and structural vibrations can also make the source seem to come from everywhere at once. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sources and effects of low frequency noise Sources and effects of low frequency noise PubMed... As a result, people encountering very different situations may independently describe what they hear using the same familiar comparisons: distant engine, electrical transformer, industrial machinery, steady vibration, low drone. This convergence of language reflects common human auditory perception rather than evidence that the underlying events are identical. Explanations proposed for each phenomenon Although both topics involve low frequency sound, the explanations usually considered are different because the reported circumstances differ. UFO humming In UFO reports, the humming is interpreted in several ways depending on the investigator's perspective. Possible explanations include: ordinary environmental sounds mistakenly associated with a visual event; distant industrial or electrical infrastructure; aircraft or helicopter noise altered by atmospheric conditions; witness memory integrating unrelated sounds into an unusual experience; in a minority of UFO interpretations, an unknown propulsion system, although no independently verified physical evidence supports this explanation. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORARESEARCH BUFORA BUFORARESEARCH BUFORA Because most UFO humming reports are brief and cannot be instrumentally recorded after the event, testing these competing explanations is usually difficult. THE HUM: The Windsor Hum (Part 1) Channel: NightDocs Open on YouTube The Hum Research into The Hum has concentrated on persistent environmental and physiological mechanisms rather than unidentified aerial objects. Frequently proposed explanations include: industrial machinery operating at low frequencies; ventilation systems, compressors and pumps; long distance propagation of environmental low frequency noise; building resonance amplifying otherwise weak sounds; individual differences in hearing sensitivity; tinnitus or other internally generated auditory perceptions in at least some cases. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sources and effects of low frequency noise Sources and effects of low frequency noise PubMed... Recent hearing research has increasingly explored whether many reported Hum experiences may originate within the auditory system itself rather than from an unidentified external sound source, while acknowledging that some local Hum cases have later been traced to genuine industrial noise. [Popular Mechanics] popularmechanics.com Popular Mechanics A Mysterious Hum Has Plagued the Earth for 50 Years Scientists May Have Finally Found Its Source.For over 50 years, a mysterious low frequency noise known as \"the Hum\" has been reported glo... Why investigators usually keep the phenomena separate Despite occasional speculation linking The Hum with UFO activity, there is little evidence supporting a direct relationship. Persistent Hum reports generally lack: simultaneous observations of unusual aerial objects, radar or aviation anomalies, concentrated periods of UFO reporting, repeatable physical evidence connecting the sound to an airborne source. Likewise, most UFO humming accounts describe isolated incidents rather than recurring acoustic experiences affecting entire communities. For these reasons, acoustics researchers, audiologists and UFO investigators typically treat the two subjects as distinct categories. The shared descriptions arise because humans have a limited vocabulary for describing deep, sustained mechanical sounds, not because the reports necessarily originate from the same phenomenon. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Sources and effects of low frequency noise Sources and effects of low frequency noise PubMed... ‘The Hum’: The Unexplained Noise 2% of People Can Hear Channel: The Atlantic Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Is UFO Humming the Same as The Hum?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The UFO Experience By J. Allen Hynek Cited by the New York Review of Books as “the best brief for visitation,” this classic study presents an analysis of UFO reports and conc... See on Amazon Book The UFO Enigma: A New Review of the Physical Evidence By Peter A. Sturrock \"A comprehensive investigation of encounters with"},{"title":"Meteor Booms","url":"/meteor-booms/","description":"Bright meteors can generate shock waves, delayed booms, and infrasound that make a dramatic sky event seem mechanical or unexplained.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises","level":2,"text":"Meteor Booms How Meteors Create UFO Like Booms UFO Noises Meteor Booms Meteor Booms How Meteors Create UFO Like Booms Bright meteors can generate shock waves, delayed booms, and infrasound that make a dramatic sky event seem mechanical or unexplained. Bright meteors can generate shock waves, delayed booms, and infrasound that make a dramatic sky event seem mechanical or unexplained. event time window How fireballs produce shock waves Why sound may arrive after the flash Clues that point to a meteor ufo noises meteor booms Introduction A loud boom a few minutes after a brilliant streak of light is one of the easiest natural events to mistake for something mysterious. Witnesses sometimes describe a fireball crossing the sky, followed by a powerful bang that rattles windows or shakes buildings. Because the sound can arrive well after the visible event—and because many people never see the meteor at all—the experience is occasionally interpreted as an unidentified flying object or unexplained aerial phenomenon. In reality, bright meteors known as fireballs or bolides are well documented sources of delayed sonic booms and low frequency atmospheric waves. Understanding how these sounds are produced helps explain why dramatic meteor events have repeatedly entered UFO folklore despite having a natural origin. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... How fireballs produce shock waves Most meteors burn up silently high in the atmosphere. Only the largest and brightest fireballs penetrate deeply enough to generate strong pressure waves that can reach the ground. As a meteoroid enters Earth's atmosphere at tens of kilometres per second, it compresses the air in front of it. The resulting shock wave travels outward as the object slows, fragments or explodes. If enough energy is released at relatively low altitude, observers beneath or near the flight path may hear one or more loud reports resembling: A cannon blast. A distant explosion. A thunderclap without clouds. A jet like sonic boom.( endnote 5 \"Endnote 5\") [amsmeteors.org] amsmeteors.org Search Results sonic boom Search Results sonic boom A series of rapid bangs caused by fragmentation. The brightest events, known as bolides , often end in a brilliant flash as the meteoroid breaks apart. That fragmentation can strengthen the pressure wave reaching the ground. The American Meteor Society notes that audible sonic booms are uncommon and generally require exceptionally bright fireballs that descend below roughly 50 km altitude before breaking up. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... 3:12 Watch: Meteor Causes Thunderous Boom Over Ohio and Pennsylvania Channel: TODAY · Views: 389.8K · Uploaded: March 2026 · Length: 3 minutes 12 seconds Open on YouTube Why the sound may arrive long after the flash One feature that frequently surprises witnesses—and contributes to UFO speculation—is the delay between seeing the meteor and hearing the boom. Light from a meteor reaches observers almost instantly, but sound travels through air at only about 343 metres per second under typical conditions. Depending on the altitude and distance of the explosion, the boom may not arrive for several minutes. For large fireballs: [ntrs.nasa.gov] ntrs.nasa.gov Source details in endnotes. A delay of around 1.5 to 4 minutes is common. More distant observers may wait even longer. People who hear only the boom may never connect it with the earlier flash. [amsmeteors.org] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... This separation in time can make the sound appear unrelated to the visual event. Someone who notices only shaking windows or an isolated explosion may reasonably wonder whether an aircraft, military activity or something unexplained caused the noise. The American Meteor Society advises observers to continue listening for several minutes after a bright fireball because delayed sonic booms are a recognised feature of these events. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... Original document Meteor Exploding Over Pennsylvania (2022 01 01 GOES 16) NOAA · 10 Memorable Images Captured by NOAA’s GOES Satellites · 20 November 2025 (upload date) Taken on 1 January 2022 · 178797544 Clear GOES‑16 satellite capture of an actual meteor explosion, directly illustrating the fireball–boom mechanism described in the article. Public domain · View original record Why meteor booms are sometimes linked to UFO reports Meteor generated booms share several characteristics with reports that later become associated with unidentified aerial phenomena. First, the source is often invisible by the time the sound arrives. The meteor has already burned up or disappeared beyond the horizon before the pressure wave reaches observers. Second, the boom can affect a wide geographic area. Residents many kilometres apart may report hearing the same explosion, giving the impression of a large, mysterious event. Third, witnesses often describe physical effects such as: Windows rattling. Doors vibrating. Houses briefly shaking. Pets reacting suddenly. Multiple loud reports rather than a single bang. Without an obvious source, these effects may initially generate reports of unidentified aircraft, explosions or UFOs until astronomical observations, camera footage and meteor networks reconstruct the event. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey Real events that illustrate the pattern Numerous documented fireballs demonstrate how easily meteor booms can be mistaken for unexplained aerial sounds before investigators identify the cause. In February 2015, a bright fireball over northern Florida generated hundreds of eyewitness reports. Many people described delayed booms powerful enough to shake houses and rattle windows. By comparing witness observations, investigators reconstructed the meteor's trajectory and showed that reports of the sonic boom clustered near the end of the object's atmospheric flight. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Florida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFlorida Fireball with Boom American Meteor Society... A similar pattern occurred during a major New England fireball in 2026. Residents across several states reported loud explosions and ground shaking, prompting initial speculation about earthquakes or other unusual causes. NASA, the American Meteor Society and the U.S. Geological Survey concluded that the disturbance resulted from a bolide exploding high in the atmosphere, with an estimated energy release equivalent to hundreds of tonnes of TNT. The USGS also noted that atmospheric explosions such as meteor bolides can sometimes produce signals distinguishable from earthquakes. [apnews.com] apnews.com The American Meteor Society and NASA confirmed that a meteor, about three feet wide, entered the atmosphere around 2:06 p.m. near the New... These events show that widespread reports of mysterious booms do not necessarily imply an unknown aircraft or technology; they are consistent with rare but well understood meteor entries. Meteor boom shocks Massachusetts: Residents hear loud explosion across region Channel: Boston 25 News Open on YouTube A rare complication: simultaneous meteor sounds A small number of witnesses report hearing hissing, crackling or sizzling at exactly the same moment they see a bright meteor rather than minutes later. These reports are known as electrophonic sounds . Unlike delayed sonic booms, they cannot be explained by ordinary sound propagation because the meteor is usually far too distant for acoustic waves to arrive instantly. Researchers have investigated whether very low frequency (VLF) electromagnetic waves generated by the meteor could induce nearby objects—such as metal frames, eyeglasses, dry vegetation or even hair—to vibrate and create audible sound. Although many historical reports exist, the phenomenon remains rare and its physical mechanism has not been conclusively demonstrated. It should therefore be distinguished from the much better understood delayed sonic booms produced by fireballs. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... Meteor triggers sonic boom over New England Channel: CBS News Open on YouTube Clues that point to a meteor rather than an unknown craft Several features strongly favour a meteor explanation when a mysterious boom follows an unusual event in the sky. A brilliant streak or fireball observed shortly beforehand. A delay of several minutes between the"},{"title":"Instant Sounds","url":"/instant-sounds/","description":"Rare reports of hissing or crackling during a visible meteor may involve local vibrations triggered by electromagnetic waves, but the mechanism remains","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Meteor Booms","level":3,"text":"Instant Sounds Can a Meteor Be Heard Instantly? Meteor Booms Instant Sounds Instant Sounds Can a Meteor Be Heard Instantly? Rare reports of hissing or crackling during a visible meteor may involve local vibrations triggered by electromagnetic waves, but the mechanism remains Rare reports of hissing or crackling during a visible meteor may involve local vibrations triggered by electromagnetic waves, but the mechanism remains mechanism Why ordinary sound cannot arrive with the flash The electromagnetic vibration hypothesis Why the evidence remains unsettled ufo noises meteor booms instant sounds Introduction Most meteor sounds are heard minutes after a brilliant fireball, when the shock wave finally reaches the ground. Yet a much rarer class of reports describes something very different: a faint hiss, crackle, sizzle or pop heard at exactly the same moment as the meteor flashes across the sky. These so called electrophonic meteor sounds have intrigued astronomers for centuries because ordinary sound cannot travel from a meteor tens of kilometres overhead quickly enough to explain them. Rather than overturning physics, the reports have prompted researchers to investigate whether a meteor might generate electromagnetic energy that causes nearby objects—or even hair, glasses or foliage—to vibrate locally. Although the phenomenon has not been conclusively verified under controlled conditions, it remains one of the most unusual unresolved questions associated with bright meteors and is occasionally mistaken for a mysterious \"UFO noise\". [amsmeteors.org] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... Why ordinary sound cannot arrive with the flash The central mystery begins with a simple physical constraint. Light from a meteor reaches an observer almost instantly, while sound in air travels at roughly 343 metres per second. A meteor burning 80–100 km above Earth cannot produce an audible hiss or crackle that arrives simultaneously through normal acoustic propagation. This distinction is important because it separates two very different phenomena: Delayed sonic booms are well understood. They result from shock waves produced by large fireballs and typically arrive between about 1.5 and 4 minutes after the meteor is seen. Instantaneous reports describe sounds perceived during the visible flight itself, with no noticeable delay. These are the observations referred to as electrophonic sounds. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... Because the timing appears impossible for ordinary sound, early investigators often dismissed such reports as mistakes or coincidence. However, the remarkable consistency of many independent descriptions—often involving words such as \"hissing\", \"sizzling\", \"swishing\" or \"crackling\"—kept the question alive. [NASA Technical Reports Server] ntrs.nasa.gov Technical Reports Server Meteor fireball sounds identified NASA Technical Reports ServerMeteor fireball sounds identified NASA Technical Reports Server (NTRS)... The electromagnetic vibration hypothesis The leading explanation does not suggest that the meteor's sound itself travels instantaneously. Instead, it proposes that the meteor emits very low frequency (VLF) or extremely low frequency (ELF) electromagnetic waves. Unlike sound, electromagnetic waves travel at essentially the speed of light. If sufficiently strong, they could reach an observer almost simultaneously with the visible meteor. The audible sound would then be created locally , not in the sky. Researchers have proposed that the electromagnetic pulse could induce tiny vibrations in nearby objects acting as natural transducers, including: thin metal objects such as wire fences or spectacles; dry leaves, pine needles or grass; aluminium foil or lightweight conductors; even strands of human hair under favourable conditions. The listener would therefore hear a nearby object vibrating, even though the trigger originated with the distant meteor. This neatly resolves the apparent conflict with the speed of sound. [nasa.gov] ntrs.nasa.gov Technical Reports Server Meteor fireball sounds identified NASA Technical Reports ServerMeteor fireball sounds identified NASA Technical Reports Server (NTRS)... One influential series of studies by physicist Colin Keay argued that turbulent plasma surrounding exceptionally bright meteors could distort Earth's magnetic field and generate VLF radiation. Laboratory experiments also demonstrated that ordinary objects can convert electrical excitation into audible vibrations, making the basic transduction mechanism physically plausible even if the atmospheric source remains debated. [NASA Technical Reports Server] ntrs.nasa.gov Technical Reports Server Meteor fireball sounds identified NASA Technical Reports ServerMeteor fireball sounds identified NASA Technical Reports Server (NTRS)... 'Spooky Meteor Noises' Reproduced and Explained by Scientists Video Channel: VideoFromSpace Open on YouTube Why witnesses often describe hissing rather than explosions Reported electrophonic sounds differ noticeably from delayed meteor booms. [nature.com] nature.com Photoacoustic Sounds from Meteors Scientific Reports Photoacoustic Sounds from Meteors Scientific Reports Instead of loud explosive noises, witnesses commonly describe: gentle hissing; frying or sizzling sounds; [livescience.com] livescience.com Can You Hear a Meteor? Electrophonics Live Science Can You Hear a Meteor? Electrophonics Live Science static like crackling; soft swishing; brief popping noises. These descriptions resemble the sort of noises expected from small nearby objects vibrating rather than a powerful explosion many kilometres away. Some surveys have also noted recurring circumstances in witness accounts . Reports sometimes mention the presence of metal objects nearby, exceptionally dry vegetation, or unusually bright fireballs. A few observers even reported their hair appearing to respond at the same moment as the sound. While such anecdotes are intriguing, they remain observational rather than definitive evidence. [amsmeteors.org] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... Why the evidence remains unsettled Although electrophonic sounds are taken more seriously today than they once were, they are not considered a solved phenomenon . Several problems continue to challenge researchers. First, the events are extremely rare. Even among brilliant fireballs, only a small proportion generate simultaneous sound reports , making planned instrumental observations difficult. [NASA Technical Reports Server] ntrs.nasa.gov Technical Reports Server Meteor fireball sounds identified NASA Technical Reports ServerMeteor fireball sounds identified NASA Technical Reports Server (NTRS)... Second, no single physical model explains every reported case. Different researchers have suggested several mechanisms, including: VLF electromagnetic radiation produced by plasma turbulence; electric fields generated during catastrophic fragmentation; local corona discharge around nearby objects; other forms of atmospheric electrical coupling. Each model accounts for some observations while struggling with others. [wiley.com] agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com AGU PublicationsOn the electrophonic generation of audio frequency sound by meteors Kelley 2017 Geophysical Research Letters Wile... Third, direct instrumental confirmation remains limited. The American Meteor Society notes that electrophonic sounds have never been conclusively validated scientifically , and although the VLF hypothesis is the most widely discussed explanation, it has not been definitively demonstrated. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... For this reason, the phenomenon occupies an unusual position: it is no longer routinely dismissed as impossible, yet neither is it regarded as fully explained. Starship seen and (HEARD in real time!) over Puerto Rico Channel: Sociedad de Astronomía del Caribe (SAC) Open on YouTube Why instant meteor sounds can resemble UFO reports Within reports of unexplained aerial noises, electrophonic meteors occupy a unique niche. Unlike delayed sonic booms—which can usually be matched with the meteor's trajectory and timing—an instantaneous hiss may leave witnesses convinced that the sound came directly from an object flying overhead. Since the sound coincides perfectly with the visible streak, it can create the impression that the object itself was \"making noise\" despite its immense altitude. This timing has occasionally contributed to UFO interpretations, particularly when observers were unfamiliar with electrophonic reports or expected all meteor sounds to be delayed. In reality, if electrophonic sounds"},{"title":"Boom Delay","url":"/boom-delay/","description":"A meteor's flash is seen almost instantly, while its shock wave may take several minutes to reach people on the ground.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Meteor Booms","level":3,"text":"Boom Delay Why the Meteor Boom Comes Minutes Later Meteor Booms Boom Delay Boom Delay Why the Meteor Boom Comes Minutes Later A meteor's flash is seen almost instantly, while its shock wave may take several minutes to reach people on the ground. A meteor's flash is seen almost instantly, while its shock wave may take several minutes to reach people on the ground. mechanism How light and sound travel at different speeds How altitude and distance shape the delay Why witnesses may miss the connection ufo noises meteor booms boom delay Introduction A bright fireball can vanish in seconds, yet the loud boom associated with it may not arrive until several minutes later. That delay is one of the main reasons meteor generated sonic booms are sometimes mistaken for unexplained aerial sounds or even UFO related events. The apparent separation between the flash and the explosion is not mysterious: it is a direct consequence of light travelling vastly faster than sound, combined with the great height at which most fireballs fragment. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... For many witnesses, the visual event has already faded from memory by the time windows rattle or a deep explosion is heard. Others never see the meteor at all because they are indoors, looking the wrong way, or because daylight or cloud obscures the fireball. The delayed boom therefore appears to have no obvious source, encouraging speculation about aircraft, explosions or unidentified aerial phenomena when the explanation is simply the travel time of the atmospheric shock wave. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... Bolide meteor falls from the sky over Southeast: Brad Panovich analysis Channel: WCNC Charlotte Weather IQ Open on YouTube How light and sound travel at different speeds The key mechanism is the enormous difference between the speed of light and the speed of sound. The light from a meteor reaches an observer essentially instantaneously over atmospheric distances. Even if a fireball fragments 50 kilometres above the ground, the light takes only a tiny fraction of a second to arrive. Human perception therefore treats the flash as immediate. The pressure wave generated by the same event behaves very differently. Once created, the shock eventually weakens into an audible sonic boom that travels through the atmosphere at roughly the speed of sound—about 343 metres per second near sea level under typical conditions, although temperature, pressure and wind alter the exact value. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... Because sound is comparatively slow, even a simple estimate shows why delays are expected: A boom from around 30 km away can take roughly 90 seconds to arrive. A source about 50 km away can produce a delay approaching two and a half minutes. More distant or higher altitude airbursts can produce waits of three, four or more minutes before the sound reaches observers. The delay is therefore a normal feature of the physics rather than evidence that two separate events occurred. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... Bright, loud meteor passes by Metro Vancouver Channel: CityNews Open on YouTube How altitude and distance shape the delay The waiting time depends on more than the meteor's altitude alone. What matters is the distance the sound must travel through the atmosphere from the point where the strongest shock wave is generated to the listener. A large fireball may fragment between roughly 20 and 50 kilometres above Earth's surface. Someone directly beneath the event experiences a shorter sound path than someone observing it near the horizon, where the boom must travel both downward and sideways through the atmosphere. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... Several additional factors influence when—and even whether—the boom is heard: Fragmentation height: A lower altitude breakup generally produces a shorter delay and a stronger boom. Observer location: People tens of kilometres apart can hear the same event at noticeably different times. Atmospheric conditions: Wind patterns and temperature layers can bend, weaken or focus sound waves, making booms audible much farther away than expected in some situations. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey These effects explain why reports from different communities often describe the same meteor but disagree about exactly when the explosion was heard. Why witnesses often miss the connection The delayed arrival of sound creates a psychological disconnect between what people see and what they later hear. If someone watches a brilliant meteor streak overhead, they may naturally assume the event is over once the light disappears. By the time an explosive bang arrives several minutes later, many have resumed whatever they were doing and no longer associate the sound with the earlier flash. Others never make the connection because they experience only one part of the event: People indoors may hear the boom but never see the meteor. Drivers may notice the explosion after the fireball has passed out of view. Daytime observers may hear windows shake without ever noticing the brief flash against a bright sky. Residents facing away from the meteor's path may hear only the delayed pressure wave. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... This separation in perception makes the boom seem spontaneous, encouraging reports of unexplained explosions, military activity or unidentified flying objects until meteor observations and camera footage reconstruct the event. The Day the Sky Exploded Over a City Channel: OddlyHuman Open on YouTube A real world pattern seen repeatedly Documented meteor events repeatedly follow this sequence: a brilliant fireball appears, several minutes pass, and only then do widespread reports of booming sounds and shaking buildings emerge. For example, a large meteor over the north eastern United States in 2026 produced a daylight fireball followed by delayed sonic booms that were initially mistaken by many residents for an earthquake or explosion. NASA and the American Meteor Society identified the event as a bolide after combining eyewitness reports and observational data, while the U.S. Geological Survey confirmed the disturbance was atmospheric rather than seismic. [apnews.com] apnews.com The American Meteor Society and NASA confirmed that a meteor, about three feet wide, entered the atmosphere around 2:06 p.m. near the New... This pattern is characteristic of large fireballs. The visual event and the audible boom originate from the same phenomenon, but because light arrives almost instantly while sound takes minutes to cross the atmosphere, the two experiences become separated in time. That delay is one of the simplest—and most important—reasons meteor booms are so often reported as mysterious or UFO related noises before their true cause is established. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why the Meteor Boom Comes Minutes Later. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Meteorites: The Story of Our Solar System By Tim Gregory Meteorite tells the fascinating story of the stones from outer space scattered across our planet. From the impact that finished off the d... See on Amazon Book Rocks from Space By O. Richard Norton Appendices A C: Meteorite verification laboratories, Commercial meteorite dealers, Selected list of suspected impact craters worldwide. See on Amazon Book Falling Stars: A Guide to Meteors and Meteorites By Mike D. Reynolds Month by month information on meteor showers and how to make the most of watching them Advice on starting and building a meteorite collec... See on Amazon Book The Cambridge Encyclopedia of Meteorites By O. Richard Norton Beautifully illustrated with over 140 full colour images, The Cambridge Encyclopedia of Meteorites provides a thorough guide to these fas... See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: Meteorites: The Story of Our Solar System Rocks from Space Falling Stars: A to Meteors and Meteorites books As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. eBay marketplace picks Marketplace Samples Live tested eBay searches with available results related to this page. Shop location Using USA USA USA UK Canada Australia Ireland USA UK Canada Australia Ireland Selected from UFO"},{"title":"Florida Case","url":"/florida-case/","description":"The 2015 northern Florida fireball showed how clustered witness reports can map where a meteor's strongest sonic boom reached the ground.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Meteor Booms","level":3,"text":"Florida Case How Florida Witnesses Traced a Fireball Boom Meteor Booms Florida Case Florida Case How Florida Witnesses Traced a Fireball Boom The 2015 northern Florida fireball showed how clustered witness reports can map where a meteor's strongest sonic boom reached the ground. The 2015 northern Florida fireball showed how clustered witness reports can map where a meteor's strongest sonic boom reached the ground. event time window What witnesses saw and heard Where the strongest boom reports clustered How investigators reconstructed the flight ufo noises meteor booms florida case Introduction The northern Florida fireball of 21 February 2015 is a useful case study in how an event that initially sounds mysterious can be reconstructed using hundreds of public observations. For many people, the defining experience was not the brilliant meteor itself but the delayed boom that rattled windows and shook homes. Those reports briefly prompted speculation about explosions or other unusual aerial activity, yet the pattern of eyewitness accounts ultimately helped investigators identify where the meteor ended its flight. Rather than being random anecdotes, the combined reports formed a geographical map that closely matched the fireball's trajectory and the area where its strongest sonic boom reached the ground. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Florida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFlorida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFebruary 22, 2015... Published: February 22, 2015 What witnesses saw and heard Shortly before 11 p.m. Eastern Time on 21 February 2015, residents across northern Florida and southern Georgia observed an exceptionally bright fireball crossing the night sky. Witnesses described a brilliant white or greenish streak, with some reporting that the landscape briefly lit up as though by lightning. The meteor remained visible for only a few seconds before disappearing. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Florida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFlorida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFebruary 22, 2015... Published: February 22, 2015 The sound came later. Dozens of people reported hearing a powerful boom between several seconds and a few minutes after the flash, depending on their distance from the meteor's terminal path. Typical descriptions included: A cannon like blast. A distant explosion. Windows rattling. Houses briefly shaking. Multiple booms rather than a single report. The American Meteor Society (AMS) collected more than 230 eyewitness reports, with over 48 specifically describing delayed sonic effects. Individual accounts from Starke, Sanderson and Lake City consistently mentioned shaking buildings and loud explosive noises rather than continuous aircraft sounds. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Florida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFlorida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFebruary 22, 2015... Published: February 22, 2015 2:37 'Fireball' Seen Shooting Across Southeast Sky Is Confirmed Meteor Channel: TODAY · Views: 35.8K · Uploaded: June 2025 · Length: 2 minutes 37 seconds Open on YouTube Where the strongest boom reports clustered The most revealing feature of the event was not simply the number of reports but their geographical distribution. Using the submitted observations, the AMS produced separate maps showing visual sightings and delayed sonic boom reports. While people across a broad region saw the meteor, reports of the strongest pressure wave were concentrated much more tightly near the end of the meteor's atmospheric flight. Many of the loudest house shaking reports came from the Lake City area, with additional reports extending towards western Jacksonville. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Florida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFlorida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFebruary 22, 2015... Published: February 22, 2015 This clustering reflected a well known property of meteor generated shock waves. The loudest booms generally originate near the final stages of flight, where the meteoroid has descended to lower altitude and often fragments. Instead of being spread evenly beneath the entire visible path, the strongest pressure waves tend to reach the ground in a more focused region. In Florida, that meant the pattern of delayed boom reports effectively highlighted the meteor's terminal section even before detailed instrumental analysis was completed. The witness distribution therefore became evidence rather than merely anecdote. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Florida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFlorida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFebruary 22, 2015... Published: February 22, 2015 How investigators reconstructed the flight The investigation combined two complementary sources of information. First, hundreds of eyewitness reports allowed the AMS to estimate the meteor's apparent track across the sky. Those reports indicated that the object travelled from the south west towards the north east, ending roughly 30 miles (about 48 kilometres) west of Jacksonville. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Florida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFlorida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFebruary 22, 2015... Published: February 22, 2015 Second, NASA's Sky Sentinel all sky camera network independently recorded the event from two observing stations in Melbourne, Florida. The camera based triangulation broadly agreed with the witness derived trajectory, providing confidence that the public reports had accurately captured the meteor's path. NASA estimated the object was roughly one foot (around 30 centimetres) across, weighed about 100 pounds (45 kilograms), travelled at approximately 40,000 miles per hour (64,000 km/h), and burned up before reaching the ground. [NASA] nasa.gov Fireball over Jacksonville, Florida on Feb. 21 Fireball over Jacksonville, Florida on Feb. 21 NASA... The close agreement between instrumental observations and citizen reports demonstrated that large numbers of independent eyewitness accounts can provide surprisingly reliable information when analysed collectively rather than individually. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Florida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFlorida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFebruary 22, 2015... Published: February 22, 2015 7 ton meteor streaks across the sky over multiple states, causing loud boom Channel: CBS Evening News Open on YouTube Blue Green Fireball Lights Up Florida Sky Feb. 2 Meteor Caught on Camera Channel: EarthCam Open on YouTube Why this case matters for reports of mysterious aerial booms The Florida event illustrates why meteor generated sonic booms are sometimes mistaken for unexplained aerial phenomena. Many residents never saw the meteor itself. They experienced only a sudden explosion like noise, vibrating windows or a brief shaking of their homes. Without the earlier visual cue, those sounds could easily be interpreted as industrial explosions, military aircraft or something more mysterious. What distinguished this case was the density of reporting. Instead of isolated accounts, investigators received hundreds of independent observations that could be compared geographically. Visual reports defined the meteor's path, while delayed boom reports identified where the strongest shock wave reached the surface. Together they produced a coherent reconstruction that matched instrumental recordings rather than leaving the event unexplained. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Florida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFlorida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFebruary 22, 2015... Published: February 22, 2015 The Florida fireball therefore remains an instructive example of how organised citizen reporting can transform what initially appears to be an unexplained loud aerial event into a well documented meteor case. It demonstrates that the timing and location of delayed sonic booms are not random but can provide valuable clues to a fireball's final atmospheric flight when many independent observations are analysed together. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Florida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFlorida Fireball with Boom American Meteor SocietyFebruary 22, 2015... Published: February 22, 2015 Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to How Florida Witnesses Traced a Fireball Boom. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Meteorite Hunting: How to Find Treasure from Space By Geoffrey Notkin \"This is the definitive guide to finding meteorites, written by one of the best known personalities in the field and co host of the award... See on Amazon Book Meteorites: The Story"},{"title":"Multiple Bangs","url":"/multiple-bangs/","description":"A bolide that breaks apart can send several pressure pulses toward the ground, producing a rapid series of loud reports.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Meteor Booms","level":3,"text":"Multiple Bangs Why One Fireball Can Produce Several Bangs Meteor Booms Multiple Bangs Multiple Bangs Why One Fireball Can Produce Several Bangs A bolide that breaks apart can send several pressure pulses toward the ground, producing a rapid series of loud reports. A bolide that breaks apart can send several pressure pulses toward the ground, producing a rapid series of loud reports. mechanism What happens when a bolide fragments Why pressure waves may arrive in sequence How repeated bangs differ from engine noise ufo noises meteor booms multiple bangs Introduction A single fireball can produce several loud bangs because a large meteoroid rarely remains intact as it races through the atmosphere. Instead, intense aerodynamic pressure and heating cause it to fracture into multiple pieces. Each major fragmentation episode releases energy into the surrounding air, creating additional shock waves that can travel to the ground. To people below, those pressure waves may arrive as a rapid sequence of explosive reports rather than one isolated boom. This behaviour explains why some meteor events are initially mistaken for aircraft accidents, explosions or even unexplained aerial phenomena, despite having a well understood physical cause. [amsmeteors.org] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... What happens when a bolide fragments? A bolide is an exceptionally bright meteor that undergoes a violent atmospheric breakup. As it enters the atmosphere at many kilometres per second, the air in front of it is compressed into a powerful shock wave. The stresses become so great that cracks spread rapidly through the rock until it separates into multiple fragments. This breakup is rarely a single event. Instead, a large meteoroid often experiences a cascade of failures: An initial crack divides the body into several large fragments. Each fragment continues travelling at hypersonic speed. Some fragments undergo further breakups as aerodynamic forces increase. Every significant fragmentation deposits additional energy into the atmosphere. Rather than behaving like one exploding object, the meteoroid becomes a chain of moving energy sources distributed along its flight path. Researchers modelling major airbursts, including the 2013 Chelyabinsk event, represent the explosion as energy being released over an extended section of the trajectory instead of at a single point. [DOI.org] doi.org Simulation of the airwave caused by the Chelyabinsk superbolide Avramenko 2014 Journal of Geophysical Research: Atmospheres Wiley Online Library... 5:37 Fireball flies across the sky and causes sonic boom Channel: CNN · Views: 466.7K · Uploaded: June 2025 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube Why pressure waves may arrive in sequence The multiple bangs heard on the ground are not necessarily separate explosions in the everyday sense. They result from pressure waves generated at different locations and times along the meteoroid's path. Several factors contribute to the sequence: Successive fragmentation events. Each major breakup produces its own strong pressure pulse. Different distances to the observer. Pressure waves originating at different points along the trajectory travel different distances before reaching the ground. Varying atmospheric paths. Wind, temperature layers and altitude influence how efficiently each wave propagates. Separate fragment shock waves. Large surviving fragments can continue producing strong bow shocks before breaking apart again. Because sound travels much more slowly than light, these waves may reach an observer seconds apart even though the fragmentation happened almost instantaneously from the meteor's perspective. The result is a distinctive pattern of two, three or more explosive reports rather than a single clap. [amsmeteors.org] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... Why repeated bangs differ from engine noise Witnesses often compare meteor sounds to artillery, cannon fire or a succession of explosions. Although repeated bangs can initially resemble aircraft activity, they differ from engine or propulsion noise in several important ways. Meteor fragmentation typically produces: Short, powerful pressure pulses rather than a continuous roar. Little or no audible sound before the first boom because the object is travelling far faster than sound. Several closely spaced reports that may vary in loudness. Window rattling and building vibration over a broad area. By contrast, aircraft engines generate sustained mechanical noise, while a jet sonic boom usually produces a characteristic one time \"double boom\" associated with the aircraft's nose and tail shock waves. A fragmenting bolide can create a more irregular series of reports because numerous fragments and breakup events contribute independently to the pressure field. [usgs.gov] usgs.gov U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey NASA NPP Sees Aftermath of the Chelyabinsk Meteor Channel: NASA Goddard Open on YouTube Chelyabinsk: a clear example of multiple pressure pulses The Chelyabinsk superbolide over Russia on 15 February 2013 remains one of the best studied examples of atmospheric fragmentation producing powerful acoustic effects. High speed video, satellite observations and numerical modelling showed that the meteoroid did not simply explode once. Instead, it underwent catastrophic fragmentation while continuing downrange, depositing enormous amounts of energy over an extended section of its flight. Researchers reconstructed the airburst by matching simulated pressure wave arrival times with recordings from numerous communities, demonstrating that the complex acoustic signature reflected distributed fragmentation rather than a single point explosion. [DOI.org] doi.org Simulation of the airwave caused by the Chelyabinsk superbolide Avramenko 2014 Journal of Geophysical Research: Atmospheres Wiley Online Library... Many recordings from the event captured multiple loud reports separated by short intervals. These reflected the arrival of different components of the atmospheric shock generated as fragmentation progressed, rather than independent mysterious explosions. Fireball stuns the Southeast after meteor explodes over Georgia Channel: CBS Mornings Open on YouTube Why these sounds are sometimes mistaken for UFO activity Multiple explosive bangs can be especially misleading because observers often hear them without seeing the meteor itself. Clouds, daylight or the delay between the flash and the arriving sound may leave only the acoustic evidence. The sequence can resemble reports of: Explosions with no visible source. Military aircraft operating overhead. Industrial accidents. Unidentified aerial phenomena. Scientific investigations, however, consistently show that these sounds correspond to known atmospheric physics. Networks that monitor fireballs, infrasound and seismic signals can often reconstruct the meteor's path and identify fragmentation episodes responsible for the observed pressure waves. Even when witnesses describe several distinct explosions, that pattern is fully consistent with a bolide breaking into multiple energetic fragments rather than requiring multiple unidentified objects. [amsmeteors.org] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireball FAQs American Meteor SocietyFireball FAQs American Meteor Society... Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why One Fireball Can Produce Several Bangs. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Meteorites: The Story of Our Solar System By Tim Gregory Meteorite tells the fascinating story of the stones from outer space scattered across our planet. From the impact that finished off the d... See on Amazon Book Rocks from Space By O. Richard Norton Appendices A C: Meteorite verification laboratories, Commercial meteorite dealers, Selected list of suspected impact craters worldwide. See on Amazon Book Meteorites By Caroline Smith, Sara S. Russell et al. Meteorites give us a unique insight into the nature of material that was present at the formation of the solar system. Learn what they ar... See on Amazon Book The Cambridge Encyclopedia of Meteorites By O. Richard Norton Beautifully illustrated with over 140 full colour images, The Cambridge Encyclopedia of Meteorites provides a thorough guide to these fas... See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: Meteorites: The Story of Our Solar System Rocks from Space Meteorites As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases. eBay marketplace picks Marketplace Samples Live tested eBay searches with available results related to this page. Shop location Using USA USA USA UK Canada Australia Ireland USA UK Canada Australia Ireland Selected from Roswell UFO poster on eBay.co.uk . Current eBay listing"},{"title":"New England","url":"/new-england/","description":"The 2026 New England bolide showed how seismic records, witness reports and astronomical observations can identify an atmospheric blast.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Meteor Booms","level":3,"text":"New England Meteor Blast or Earthquake in New England? Meteor Booms New England New England Meteor Blast or Earthquake in New England? The 2026 New England bolide showed how seismic records, witness reports and astronomical observations can identify an atmospheric blast. The 2026 New England bolide showed how seismic records, witness reports and astronomical observations can identify an atmospheric blast. event time window Why residents suspected an earthquake How atmospheric and seismic signals differ What confirmed the bolide explanation ufo noises meteor booms new england Introduction The New England bolide of 30 May 2026 became a textbook example of how investigators distinguish an atmospheric explosion from an earthquake. Across Massachusetts, New Hampshire, Rhode Island and neighbouring states, residents reported a powerful boom, rattling windows and brief shaking. Many initially suspected an earthquake because the physical effects resembled a shallow seismic event. Within hours, however, evidence from seismic networks, eyewitness accounts, satellite observations and astronomical organisations showed that the source was a large meteor, or bolide, exploding high in the atmosphere rather than movement along a geological fault. [The Verge] theverge.com The Verge NASA confirms exploding meteor caused the sonic boom over Boston The event produced a sonic boom that shook houses across the region, prompting initial speculation of an earthquake. However, the US Geol... The case is especially relevant to reports of mysterious \" UFO noises \" because it demonstrates how dramatic sounds alone are a poor indicator of their origin. By comparing multiple independent data sources, investigators were able to rule out an underground event and identify a natural atmospheric explosion with a high degree of confidence. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey Why residents suspected an earthquake For many people, the event was experienced as sound and vibration rather than as a visible fireball. Reports described one or two loud explosive booms, houses shaking, windows vibrating and pets reacting suddenly. Because the blast was heard across several states, many witnesses assumed an earthquake or industrial explosion had occurred. This reaction was understandable for several reasons: Many people never saw the meteor because it passed in daylight or outside their field of view. The sonic boom reached observers after the meteor had already disappeared. Ground vibration from the pressure wave felt similar to a minor earthquake. Emergency services and the U.S. Geological Survey received numerous enquiries asking whether seismic activity had occurred. [apnews.com] apnews.com NASA confirmed that the meteor, weighing as much as an elephant and measuring 5 feet (1.52 meters) in diameter, was traveling at 42,000 m... The event illustrates why loud unexplained booms often enter UFO or mystery noise discussions before scientific evidence becomes available. Meteor triggers sonic boom over New England Channel: CBS News Open on YouTube How atmospheric and seismic signals differ The decisive evidence came from comparing how earthquakes and atmospheric shock waves appear on monitoring networks. An earthquake produces seismic waves travelling through rock at several kilometres per second. These waves create characteristic patterns on seismographs that allow scientists to locate the underground rupture and estimate its magnitude. A bolide explosion behaves differently. The meteor generates a pressure wave travelling through the atmosphere at roughly the speed of sound. When this wave reaches the ground it can shake buildings and occasionally register on seismic instruments, but the signal differs markedly from that of an earthquake. According to the U.S. Geological Survey, investigators look for several distinguishing features: Either no earthquake signal or only a short, high frequency disturbance on seismic records. Timing between stations that matches the slower speed of sound through air rather than seismic wave propagation through rock. Widespread reports of shaking without any corresponding earthquake located by seismic networks. Frequent reports of loud bangs or explosive noises, suggesting an atmospheric rather than geological source. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey In the New England case, the combination of widespread felt reports and the absence of a matching earthquake immediately pointed investigators away from a tectonic explanation. What confirmed the bolide explanation The atmospheric explanation became increasingly convincing as independent lines of evidence converged. Eyewitness reports matched a meteor Hundreds of observers reported an exceptionally bright daytime fireball crossing the sky before the boom. These reports were collected through the American Meteor Society, allowing researchers to reconstruct the meteor's path from many viewing locations. [American Meteor Society] amsmeteors.org American Meteor Society Fireballs American Meteor SocietyFireballs American Meteor Society... Satellite observations detected the flash The explosion was detected by weather satellites, which recorded the intense flash produced when the object fragmented high above New England. Satellite confirmation independently verified that a powerful atmospheric event had occurred at the same time residents reported hearing the boom. [The Verge] theverge.com The Verge NASA confirms exploding meteor caused the sonic boom over Boston The event produced a sonic boom that shook houses across the region, prompting initial speculation of an earthquake. However, the US Geol... Meteor causes loud 'boom' in New England Channel: NBC 10 WJAR Open on YouTube NASA estimated the explosion energy NASA reported that the meteor entered the atmosphere at roughly 75,000 mph (about 121,000 km/h), fragmented at an altitude of around 40 miles (64 km), and released energy equivalent to approximately 300 tons of TNT. That energy was sufficient to generate a sonic boom heard across much of the region while remaining far above the Earth's surface. [The Verge] theverge.com The Verge NASA confirms exploding meteor caused the sonic boom over Boston The event produced a sonic boom that shook houses across the region, prompting initial speculation of an earthquake. However, the US Geol... Seismic data ruled out an earthquake Perhaps the strongest evidence came from what was missing. Although many residents felt shaking, the regional seismic network did not record an earthquake consistent with the reports. The U.S. Geological Survey therefore classified the event as an atmospheric source rather than a tectonic one. [U.S. Geological Survey] usgs.gov U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey Storm Wrap Up + The “Boom” Explained New England Update Channel: 1DegreeOutside Open on YouTube Why the investigation mattered The New England bolide demonstrated that no single observation is enough to identify the source of an unusual boom. Witness testimony alone suggested an earthquake to some people and an unidentified aerial phenomenon to others. Seismic records alone showed only that no conventional earthquake had occurred. Only by combining several independent datasets could investigators reach a reliable conclusion: seismic monitoring showing no earthquake; eyewitness reports describing a bright fireball; satellite detection of the atmospheric flash; astronomical analysis of the meteor's trajectory and fragmentation; physical modelling showing that the estimated explosion energy could account for the widespread sonic boom. [usgs.gov] usgs.gov U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey U.S. Geological Survey Sonic Booms U.S. Geological Survey For investigations into mysterious aerial sounds, this multi sensor approach is now standard practice. The New England event provides a clear example of how an apparently earthquake like disturbance can instead be traced to a natural bolide exploding high in the atmosphere, resolving initial speculation without invoking unknown or extraordinary causes. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Meteor Blast or Earthquake in New England?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Meteorites: The Story of Our Solar System By Tim Gregory Meteorite tells the fascinating story of the stones from outer space scattered across our planet. From the impact that finished off the d... See on Amazon Book Meteorite Craters By Kathleen Mark The scientific community has argued for decades over the origin of giant craters on the earth. In a highly readable fashion, Kathleen Mar... See on Amazon Book Falling"},{"title":"Official Files","url":"/official-files/","description":"Government UAP inquiries emphasize radar, infrared, and pilot testimony because no unique sound signature has been verified.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises","level":2,"text":"Official Files Do Official UAP Files Reveal a Unique Sound? UFO Noises Official Files Official Files Do Official UAP Files Reveal a Unique Sound? Government UAP inquiries emphasize radar, infrared, and pilot testimony because no unique sound signature has been verified. Government UAP inquiries emphasize radar, infrared, and pilot testimony because no unique sound signature has been verified. policy intervention Why investigations prioritize sensor data What acoustic evidence is available Why no signature has been confirmed ufo noises official files Introduction Official government investigations into unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP), formerly widely called UFOs, have consistently reached the same practical conclusion on sound: there is no verified acoustic signature that can reliably identify a UAP. While witnesses have reported humming, buzzing, roaring, clicking and complete silence, official investigations have not found any unique pattern of noise that distinguishes unexplained objects from conventional aircraft, atmospheric events or sensor errors. Instead, modern UAP programmes place their greatest confidence in corroborated evidence from multiple sensors—including radar, infrared systems, electro optical cameras and trained observers—because isolated reports of unusual sounds have proved too inconsistent to function as reliable evidence. [nasa.gov] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 Why investigations prioritise sensor data over reported sounds Modern UAP investigations are designed around the principle that a single observation rarely provides enough information for a confident assessment. A witness may accurately report hearing an unusual sound, but without supporting data it is difficult to determine its source, direction, distance or relationship to the reported object. Recent US government reports emphasise collecting multiple forms of evidence from the same event. These include: Primary and secondary radar tracks. Infrared imagery. Electro optical video. Flight telemetry. Pilot and aircrew testimony. Weather and air traffic information. When several independent sensors observe the same event, investigators can compare the information for consistency. By contrast, acoustic reports are highly dependent on environmental conditions, human perception and the delay between visual and audible observations. Consequently, sound is treated as supporting information rather than a primary means of identification. [ODNI] dni.gov Open source on dni.gov. NASA's independent UAP study reached a similar conclusion, recommending better calibrated, multi sensor observations rather than focusing on any single category of evidence. The report stresses that progress depends on high quality datasets with known sensor characteristics, not anecdotal descriptions of unusual noises. [nasa.gov] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 What acoustic evidence is actually available? Official case files do contain occasional references to sound, but these descriptions vary so widely that they have not formed a meaningful classification system. Reported sounds include: Low frequency humming. Buzzing. Whining. Roaring. Brief explosive noises. Complete silence despite an apparently nearby object. These descriptions often resemble sounds produced by conventional aircraft, helicopters, drones , atmospheric effects or industrial machinery. In many cases, investigators cannot determine whether the reported sound originated from the observed object at all, particularly when observations occurred at night or over long distances. The variability itself is significant. If a distinctive propulsion system consistently produced a characteristic sound, investigators would expect repeated reports with similar acoustic properties across independent cases. Official datasets have not demonstrated such consistency. [ODNI] odni.gov UNCLASSIFIED... 2:19:06 House holds hearing on UFOs, government transparency full video Channel: CBS News · Views: 3.9M · Uploaded: July 2023 · Length: 2 hours 19 minutes Open on YouTube Why no unique sound signature has been confirmed A useful acoustic signature must be repeatable, measurable and distinguishable from ordinary sources. Current UAP investigations have not found evidence meeting those standards. Several factors explain why: Human hearing is subjective. Witnesses often estimate the direction, intensity and timing of sounds differently, especially during unexpected events. Atmospheric conditions affect sound. Wind, temperature layers and terrain can distort or delay noise. Distance is frequently uncertain. If an object's size or range is misjudged, its expected loudness may also be misinterpreted. Many reports lack recordings. Most historical cases rely solely on memory rather than calibrated acoustic measurements. Multiple explanations remain plausible. Aircraft, drones, balloons , meteors and industrial sources can all produce unusual or unfamiliar sounds. Because of these limitations, official investigators have not identified any frequency range, waveform, modulation pattern or other measurable acoustic characteristic that consistently distinguishes unresolved UAP from known phenomena. [nasa.gov] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 What official investigations have concluded Recent US government reporting reflects an increasingly systematic approach to UAP analysis. Annual reports from the Office of the Director of National Intelligence (ODNI) and the Department of Defense's All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) focus on determining whether reports can be explained through conventional causes, sensor artefacts or insufficient information. Where cases remain unresolved, this does not imply that investigators have identified unknown technology. Instead, it generally means that available evidence is incomplete or conflicting. Even recently released unresolved cases frequently note that the absence of corroborating telemetry or additional sensor data prevents a conclusive assessment. Sound evidence alone has not resolved these investigations. [dni.gov] dni.gov 4020 PANI 2024 ODNI2024 Consolidated Annual Report on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Office of the Director of National Intelligence... Earlier assessments similarly concluded that no single explanation accounts for every reported UAP. Investigators instead expect different incidents to fall into multiple categories, including airborne clutter, natural atmospheric phenomena, foreign systems, classified programmes or other causes. Acoustic reports have not altered that framework. [ODNI] dni.gov Open source on dni.gov. Historical perspective from official files Historical government studies reached similar conclusions long before today's UAP programmes. The UK's Ministry of Defence commissioned the classified Project Condign study during the late 1990s to examine decades of British reports. Although the report discussed witness descriptions of unusual phenomena, it did not identify a distinctive acoustic signature that could reliably identify unexplained aerial events. Instead, it explored a range of possible atmospheric and environmental explanations for many observations while acknowledging that witness reports alone were often insufficient for definitive conclusions. This broader historical pattern reinforces the findings of contemporary investigations: unusual sounds may accompany some reports, but they have never emerged as a dependable forensic marker. 59:12 NASA news conference on UFO report Channel: Sky News · Views: 51.3K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube What this means for reports of \"UFO noises\" For someone investigating an unusual sound today, official UAP research offers a clear practical lesson. A humming, buzzing or silent object is not, by itself, evidence of an unidentified anomalous phenomenon. Government investigations consider sound as one observational detail among many and give far greater weight to independently verifiable measurements from calibrated sensors. After decades of military, intelligence and civilian investigations, no official programme has confirmed a unique or repeatable acoustic signature that identifies a UAP. The absence of such a signature is itself an important finding: despite thousands of reports and increasingly sophisticated investigations, sound has not become a reliable diagnostic tool for distinguishing genuinely unexplained events from ordinary or ultimately explainable phenomena. [nasa.gov] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 Watch again: Nasa UFO task force holds first public meeting Channel: The Independent Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Do Official"},{"title":"Historical Files","url":"/historical-files/","description":"Older official studies reached the same broad conclusion as modern programs: unusual noises were too inconsistent to identify unexplained craft.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Official Files","level":3,"text":"Historical Files Did Earlier UFO Investigations Find a Distinctive Noise? Official Files Historical Files Historical Files Did Earlier UFO Investigations Find a Distinctive Noise? Older official studies reached the same broad conclusion as modern programs: unusual noises were too inconsistent to identify unexplained craft. Older official studies reached the same broad conclusion as modern programs: unusual noises were too inconsistent to identify unexplained craft. event time window How historical files recorded witness sound descriptions What Project Condign concluded about unusual aerial reports Why older findings resemble modern UAP assessments ufo noises official files historical files Introduction Historical government investigations into unidentified flying objects did record reports of unusual sounds, but they did not identify any distinctive or repeatable acoustic signature that could be linked to unexplained craft. From early US Air Force programmes such as Project Sign, Project Grudge and Project Blue Book through to the UK's classified Project Condign study, investigators consistently treated witness descriptions of humming, buzzing, roaring or silence as potentially useful observations rather than decisive evidence. The recurring conclusion was that reported noises were too variable, too dependent on witness perception and too difficult to separate from conventional environmental sources to serve as reliable forensic indicators. That broad assessment closely resembles the position taken by modern official UAP investigations, which continue to prioritise corroborated multi sensor evidence over subjective descriptions of sound. [archives.gov] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 How historical files recorded witness sound descriptions The earliest official UFO investigations in the United States gathered extensive witness testimony, and many reports included comments about sound alongside descriptions of appearance, movement and weather conditions. Witnesses variously described: Low humming or droning. Buzzing similar to electrical equipment or insects. Roaring comparable to aircraft engines. Whistling or rushing noises. Complete silence despite an apparently low flying object. Rather than treating these descriptions as evidence of a unique propulsion system, investigators recorded them as one element of a broader case file. The value of a reported sound depended on whether it could be corroborated by additional witnesses, matched to known aircraft or atmospheric phenomena, or supported by photographs, radar data or physical traces. In practice, that level of corroboration was uncommon. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 The surviving Project Blue Book records illustrate this approach. Individual cases often mention sound when witnesses considered it noteworthy, but there is no official classification built around recurring acoustic characteristics. Instead, reports were assessed in context, with investigators comparing the claimed noise against known aircraft, balloons , meteors, weather effects and other conventional explanations. [National Archives] archives.gov National Archives Project BLUE BOOK National ArchivesProject BLUE BOOK Unidentified Flying Objects National ArchivesJune 25, 2024... Published: June 25, 2024 An important practical limitation also appears throughout historical investigations: witnesses often struggled to judge whether a sound actually originated from the observed object. At night or over long distances, environmental noise, echoes and delayed sound propagation could easily create misleading impressions about timing and direction. 11:22 What Did Project Blue Book Find? Unveiled Channel: Unveiled · Views: 70.9K · Uploaded: March 2020 · Length: 11 minutes Open on YouTube What Project Condign concluded about unusual aerial reports Among historical government studies, the UK's Project Condign is particularly relevant because it attempted a systematic review of decades of unidentified aerial reports rather than evaluating incidents one by one. Completed for the Ministry of Defence in 2000 and released later under freedom of information legislation, the study assembled a database of report characteristics including shapes, colours, motion, witness reliability and reported sounds. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com UFO TransparencyProject Condign, Unidentified Aerial Phenomena in the UK Air Defence Region, Volume 1 (Executive Summary, Chapters 1–5, A... Instead of identifying a recognisable \"UFO noise\", the study found that acoustic descriptions were highly inconsistent across reports. Sound was catalogued as one descriptor among many, but no repeatable pattern emerged that could distinguish genuinely unexplained cases from ordinary phenomena. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com UFO TransparencyProject Condign, Unidentified Aerial Phenomena in the UK Air Defence Region, Volume 1 (Executive Summary, Chapters 1–5, A... Project Condign also proposed that many unusual observations could be explained by poorly understood atmospheric plasma phenomena under certain environmental conditions. Even within that hypothesis, however, reported sounds remained inconsistent. Some witnesses described noise, others reported silence , and many cases lacked enough detail to determine whether any sound was connected to the observed phenomenon at all. The report therefore did not recommend using acoustic reports as an identifying feature of unexplained aerial events. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com UFO TransparencyProject Condign, Unidentified Aerial Phenomena in the UK Air Defence Region, Volume 1 (Executive Summary, Chapters 1–5, A... This distinction is important. Condign did not argue that unusual sounds never occurred. Rather, it concluded that the available reports were too variable and insufficiently documented to establish a distinctive acoustic signature. Why earlier findings resemble modern UAP assessments Although today's investigations use far more sophisticated technology than Cold War era programmes, the treatment of sound has changed remarkably little. Historical investigators recognised several enduring problems: Human memory is imperfect, particularly during unexpected events. Wind, terrain and atmospheric conditions can alter how sound is perceived. Without knowing an object's distance, apparent loudness is difficult to interpret. Similar noises can be produced by many conventional aircraft and environmental sources. Most reports lacked calibrated audio recordings. These same limitations continue to influence official investigations today. Modern programmes therefore regard witness descriptions of sound as supporting information rather than primary evidence. A reported hum or buzz may help reconstruct an event, but it cannot by itself establish the nature of the object involved. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com UFO TransparencyProject Condign, Unidentified Aerial Phenomena in the UK Air Defence Region, Volume 1 (Executive Summary, Chapters 1–5, A... The continuity between historical and contemporary practice is striking. Despite decades of accumulated reports, no government investigation has identified a verified, repeatable acoustic profile that reliably distinguishes unexplained aerial phenomena from known objects or natural processes. 4:46 Project Blue Book UFO Interview USAF (1966) Channel: AIRBOYD · Views: 812 · Uploaded: November 2025 · Length: 4 minutes 46 seconds Open on YouTube 41:31 All the videos from Pentagon's first batch of UFO files Channel: Face the Nation · Views: 61.7K · Uploaded: May 2026 · Length: 41 minutes Open on YouTube What historical files ultimately tell us about UFO sounds Looking across decades of declassified records reveals a consistent pattern rather than a dramatic discovery. Government files show that unusual noises were neither ignored nor treated as proof of extraordinary technology. Instead, investigators documented them carefully while recognising their limitations. Three themes recur throughout the historical record: Sound was considered potentially useful but rarely decisive. Acoustic descriptions formed part of witness testimony, not standalone evidence. No distinctive noise emerged across thousands of reports. Witnesses described everything from loud roaring to complete silence, with no consistent pattern. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com UFO TransparencyProject Condign, Unidentified Aerial Phenomena in the UK Air Defence Region, Volume 1 (Executive Summary, Chapters 1–5, A... Corroboration mattered more than anecdote. Investigators consistently sought agreement between witness accounts , technical observations and conventional explanations before"},{"title":"Unresolved Cases","url":"/unresolved-cases/","description":"An unresolved case usually reflects missing or conflicting data, not official confirmation that the object used extraordinary technology.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Official Files","level":3,"text":"Unresolved Cases What Unresolved UAP Cases Actually Mean Official Files Unresolved Cases Unresolved Cases What Unresolved UAP Cases Actually Mean An unresolved case usually reflects missing or conflicting data, not official confirmation that the object used extraordinary technology. An unresolved case usually reflects missing or conflicting data, not official confirmation that the object used extraordinary technology. policy intervention How official reports define an unresolved event Why missing telemetry prevents firm conclusions How uncertainty differs from evidence of exotic technology ufo noises official files unresolved cases Introduction An unresolved unidentified anomalous phenomenon (UAP) case does not, by itself, demonstrate the existence of unknown or extraordinary technology. In official investigations, \"unresolved\" is a technical classification meaning that available evidence is insufficient to reach a confident identification. It is not a positive finding that an object displayed exotic propulsion, non human origins, or physics beyond current understanding. This distinction is especially important when assessing reports that include unusual or absent sounds. Without reliable telemetry, calibrated sensor data and corroborating observations, investigators cannot determine whether an unusual acoustic report reflects an unconventional object, an ordinary phenomenon seen under unusual conditions, or simply a lack of usable evidence. NASA, the U.S. Office of the Director of National Intelligence (ODNI) and the Department of Defense's All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) have all stressed that uncertainty should not be confused with proof of extraordinary technology. [nasa.gov] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 How official reports define an unresolved event Official UAP programmes use \"unresolved\" as an evidential category rather than a judgement about what an object is. A case may remain unresolved because investigators cannot collect enough reliable information to identify it with confidence. The classification therefore reflects the limits of the available data, not confirmation of an extraordinary explanation. Several factors commonly prevent resolution: Missing or poor quality radar data. Limited or absent electro optical or infrared imagery. No reliable estimate of distance, speed or altitude. Conflicting witness accounts . Incomplete environmental information, such as weather or air traffic records. Lack of calibrated recordings, including acoustic data. NASA's independent UAP study similarly concluded that progress depends on acquiring higher quality, well characterised observations rather than treating unexplained reports as evidence of new technology. The agency recommended improving data collection and sensor calibration precisely because incomplete information leaves many ordinary explanations impossible to confirm or eliminate. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 59:22 Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Independent Study Report Channel: NASA · Views: 107.4K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Why missing telemetry prevents firm conclusions The absence of key measurements is one of the strongest reasons investigators avoid drawing dramatic conclusions from unresolved cases. For example, a witness may report that an object appeared silent despite moving rapidly. Without accurate information about the object's distance, trajectory and atmospheric conditions, investigators cannot determine whether: the object was actually close enough for its sound to be heard; wind or temperature layers redirected the sound; the object was moving in a way that reduced audible noise; the perceived motion resulted from misjudging distance or perspective. The same problem applies to apparent speed and manoeuvrability. If range is unknown, apparent acceleration can be greatly overestimated. Modern investigations therefore rely on multiple independent sensors because they allow estimates of position, motion and timing that eyewitness reports alone cannot provide. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 Within the broader question of UFO noises , this means that an unusual or absent sound cannot be interpreted in isolation. Acoustic observations become meaningful only when they are supported by independently verified measurements. Why uncertainty differs from evidence of exotic technology A common misunderstanding is to treat unresolved cases as if they represent positive evidence for advanced propulsion or non human craft. Logically and scientifically, these are different claims. An unresolved investigation establishes only that investigators could not reach a confident identification from the available evidence. It does not establish that: conventional explanations have been disproved; known physics has been violated; an object demonstrated impossible performance; an unknown technology has been confirmed. This distinction reflects a basic principle of scientific reasoning: failure to explain an observation is not evidence for any particular alternative explanation. Multiple competing explanations may remain plausible simultaneously until additional evidence narrows the possibilities. Official UAP reports consistently apply this principle by separating confidence in the evidence from speculation about origins. [nasa.gov] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 12:21 Drones, Airplanes, Or UAPs? How To Tell The Difference Business Insider Channel: Business Insider · Views: 262.1K · Uploaded: December 2024 · Length: 12 minutes Open on YouTube What recent official investigations show Recent U.S. government reporting illustrates how unresolved cases are handled in practice. AARO has received hundreds of reports while resolving many others as balloons , birds, aircraft, satellites or unmanned aerial systems once additional evidence became available. At the same time, numerous reports remain unresolved because they lack sufficient information for analysis. AARO has repeatedly stated that unresolved status should not be interpreted as evidence of extraterrestrial beings, activity or technology. [newparadigminstitute.org] newparadigminstitute.org New Paradigm Instituteaaro fy24 consolidated report – New Paradigm Institute November 14, 2024... Published: November 14, 2024 Similarly, AARO's historical review of decades of U.S. and international investigations concluded that although many cases remained unexplained, no official investigation reviewed found evidence that unresolved reports demonstrated extraterrestrial origin. The report specifically identified persistent problems such as missing measurements of speed, altitude and size as major barriers to reaching firm conclusions. [AARO] aaro.mil Unclassified Final DSD AARO Historical Report Unclassified Final DSD AARO Historical Report... 4:00:26 Public Meeting on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (Official NASA Broadcast) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 358.7K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 4 hours Open on YouTube Why unresolved cases may later be identified History shows that some cases initially classified as unidentified are eventually explained when better information becomes available. New evidence can include: [newparadigminstitute.org] newparadigminstitute.org Source details in endnotes. higher resolution imagery; additional radar returns; flight records that were unavailable initially; satellite tracking data; [science.nasa.gov] science.nasa.gov satellite data evaluation NASA ScienceJune 30, 2026 — DELIVERING SCIENCE AS A SERVICE NASA’s Commercial Satellite Data Acquisition (CSDA) program delivers “science... Published: June 30, 2026 weather reconstructions; improved analytical techniques. This means an unresolved designation is not necessarily permanent. It represents the current state of knowledge rather than an intrinsic property of the event. As investigative methods improve and additional information emerges, some previously unexplained reports move into identifiable categories. For this reason, investigators generally avoid assigning extraordinary significance to unresolved cases until stronger evidence becomes available. What this means for reports of unusual sounds Within official UAP investigations, reported noises—including humming, buzzing, roaring or complete silence—are treated as contextual observations rather than proof of unusual propulsion. Human hearing is affected by distance, terrain, atmospheric conditions and perception , while most historical cases lack calibrated acoustic recordings. When a case remains unresolved, the uncertainty usually reflects missing or conflicting evidence across multiple sources, not confirmation that"},{"title":"Multi Sensor Proof","url":"/multi-sensor-proof/","description":"Radar, infrared imagery, video and telemetry carry more weight than witness-reported sounds because they can be independently checked.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Official Files","level":3,"text":"Multi Sensor Proof Why Sensors Matter More Than Reported UFO Sounds Official Files Multi Sensor Proof Multi Sensor Proof Why Sensors Matter More Than Reported UFO Sounds Radar, infrared imagery, video and telemetry carry more weight than witness reported sounds because they can be independently checked. Radar, infrared imagery, video and telemetry carry more weight than witness reported sounds because they can be independently checked. dataset evidence How radar, infrared and video corroborate one event Why acoustic testimony is treated as supporting evidence What a strong multi sensor UAP case would contain ufo noises official files multi sensor proof Introduction Official investigations of unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) do not dismiss witness reports of unusual sounds, but they do place far greater weight on evidence that can be independently verified. Radar tracks, infrared imagery, electro optical video, flight telemetry and other calibrated measurements can be compared against one another, checked for consistency and re analysed by different investigators. By contrast, reports of humming, buzzing, roaring or complete silence depend heavily on human perception , environmental conditions and memory. As a result, modern UAP investigations treat acoustic testimony as supporting context rather than decisive evidence. This multi sensor approach reflects a broader principle of scientific and intelligence analysis: confidence increases when several independent systems observe the same event in compatible ways. [AARO] aaro.mil Next AARO UAP Report DocumentsMay 8, 2026... Published: May 8, 2026 Why radar, infrared and video carry more evidential weight The central advantage of instrumented observations is that they produce measurable data. Each sensor captures a different aspect of an event: Radar estimates an object's position, range, altitude and movement. Infrared (IR) sensors detect thermal signatures that may reveal heat sources or temperature contrasts invisible to the human eye. Electro optical cameras record visible imagery that can be stabilised, enlarged and compared frame by frame. Aircraft telemetry and navigation data document the observing platform's own motion, helping investigators distinguish target behaviour from camera or aircraft movement. When several of these systems record the same object at the same time, analysts can compare timing, location and motion across independent datasets. Agreement between different sensors increases confidence that an observation reflects a real external event rather than a single instrument malfunction or human misunderstanding. NASA's independent UAP study specifically emphasised the importance of calibrated, well characterised sensor data over anecdotal reports because reliable science depends upon observations that other investigators can examine and reproduce. [AARO] aaro.mil Next AARO UAP Report DocumentsMay 8, 2026... Published: May 8, 2026 This approach also allows investigators to identify ordinary explanations more effectively. A radar return that coincides with known aircraft transponder information, weather radar data or balloon trajectories can often be resolved quickly. Likewise, infrared footage may reveal characteristics consistent with atmospheric effects, birds or sensor artefacts once additional contextual information is available. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... 4:00:26 Public Meeting on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (Official NASA Broadcast) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 358.7K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 4 hours Open on YouTube Why reported sounds remain supporting evidence Acoustic testimony is rarely ignored, but it is usually considered one element of a much larger evidential picture. Several factors limit the evidential value of reported sounds: Sound propagation varies with wind, humidity, terrain and temperature layers. The apparent direction of a sound can be misleading because of reflections and atmospheric refraction. Visual and acoustic events may not occur simultaneously, especially over long distances where sound arrives several seconds later. Few reports include calibrated audio recordings capable of frequency analysis. Human descriptions such as \"buzzing\", \"humming\" or \"roaring\" are subjective and often overlap with familiar sources including aircraft, helicopters, drones and industrial machinery. Unlike radar recordings or digital imagery, witness descriptions cannot usually be replayed or independently measured after the event. Two observers may describe the same sound differently, while a single observer may unintentionally misjudge its origin, distance or intensity. For that reason, official investigations generally use reported sounds to provide context rather than to establish what was observed. [AARO] aaro.mil Next AARO UAP Report DocumentsMay 8, 2026... Published: May 8, 2026 An unusual sound may become more significant if it coincides precisely with independently verified sensor observations, but on its own it rarely provides enough information to distinguish an unexplained event from a conventional one. How investigators corroborate one event across multiple sensors Modern UAP investigations rely on what is effectively a process of sensor fusion: combining multiple independent observations into a single analytical timeline. Rather than asking whether one witness heard an unusual noise, investigators ask questions such as: Did radar and infrared systems detect the same object at the same time? Does the recorded motion agree across different sensors? Do aircraft position, weather conditions and known traffic explain the observation? Does witness testimony match the instrument data? Are there inconsistencies suggesting sensor artefacts or observational error? This cross checking helps expose weaknesses that would remain hidden if only one source of information were available. For example, an apparent high speed movement in infrared video may later prove consistent with camera tracking behaviour once telemetry is examined. Conversely, agreement between several independent systems increases confidence that an object, rather than a sensor artefact, was actually present. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... Recent AARO public case releases illustrate this principle. In several unresolved infrared cases, the office explicitly states that the absence of corroborating telemetry or additional sensor modalities prevents a conclusive assessment, even where an apparent thermal contrast is visible. The limiting factor is therefore not simply image quality but the lack of independent confirmation from multiple calibrated data sources. [AARO] aaro.mil UAP Imagery AARO UAP Imagery... 59:12 NASA news conference on UFO report Channel: Sky News · Views: 51.3K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube What a strong multi sensor UAP case would contain Investigators generally regard the strongest cases as those supported by several independent, synchronised observations rather than one dramatic piece of evidence. A robust dataset would ideally include: Simultaneous radar tracking , showing consistent position and movement. Infrared imagery , confirming the object's thermal characteristics. Visible spectrum video or photography , documenting its appearance. Platform telemetry , establishing the observing aircraft's own speed, heading and sensor orientation. Environmental information , including weather, wind profiles and air traffic records. Witness testimony , describing what trained observers saw and heard. Accurate timestamps , allowing every source to be aligned in a single chronology. Not every investigation contains all of these elements, but the more independently verifiable information available, the stronger the evidential basis becomes. A witness reported sound may fit naturally within such a dataset, yet it gains credibility primarily because it coincides with objective measurements rather than because the sound itself is distinctive. The practical lesson for acoustic evidence The absence of a verified acoustic signature does not mean reported UFO sounds are irrelevant. Instead, official investigations recognise that sound alone cannot reliably identify an unexplained object. A buzzing noise, unexpected silence or low frequency hum may be accurately remembered, but without independent measurements there is no dependable way to determine whether the sound originated from the observed object, another source nearby or atmospheric effects. Modern UAP programmes therefore prioritise evidence that can be calibrated, preserved and independently reviewed. Multi sensor observations allow analysts to compare radar, infrared imagery, video and telemetry against each other, reducing the influence of perception, memory and environmental ambiguity. Within that framework, acoustic testimony remains valuable as supporting information—but it rarely carries the same evidential weight as corroborated sensor"},{"title":"No Sound Signature","url":"/no-sound-signature/","description":"Official programs have found no repeatable frequency, waveform or noise pattern that reliably separates UAP from ordinary sources.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Official Files","level":3,"text":"No Sound Signature Why UAP Still Have No Confirmed Sound Signature Official Files No Sound Signature No Sound Signature Why UAP Still Have No Confirmed Sound Signature Official programs have found no repeatable frequency, waveform or noise pattern that reliably separates UAP from ordinary sources. Official programs have found no repeatable frequency, waveform or noise pattern that reliably separates UAP from ordinary sources. mechanism What a reliable acoustic signature would require Why reported UAP sounds fail repeatability tests What investigators can and cannot conclude from noise ufo noises official files no sound signature Introduction Official investigations into unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) have reviewed witness descriptions of humming, buzzing, roaring, clicking and, in many cases, complete silence. Despite decades of reports, no government investigation has identified a repeatable acoustic pattern that reliably distinguishes a genuine unexplained event from conventional aircraft, drones , atmospheric phenomena or other ordinary sources. This is not because sound is ignored. Rather, it is because sound has never met the scientific standards required to become a dependable identifier. Investigators therefore treat reported noises as supporting observations rather than diagnostic evidence, relying instead on corroborated measurements from radar, infrared sensors, optical systems and other calibrated instruments. [AARO] aaro.mil Next AARO UAP Report DocumentsMay 8, 2026... Published: May 8, 2026 What a Reliable Acoustic Signature Would Require For sound to identify a class of aerial phenomena, investigators would need to demonstrate a consistent and measurable acoustic signature. In practical terms, this means the same characteristic features would have to appear repeatedly across independent cases recorded under controlled conditions. Such a signature would require: A repeatable frequency spectrum or waveform. Calibrated recordings rather than subjective descriptions. Similar measurements from multiple independent incidents. Clear separation from known aircraft, drones, rockets, weather and industrial noise. Confirmation that the recorded sound originated from the observed object rather than the surrounding environment. These are standard principles in acoustics and signal analysis. Aircraft engines, helicopter rotor systems and many industrial machines can be recognised because they produce stable, measurable acoustic characteristics. Official UAP investigations have not identified any equivalent repeatable pattern. NASA's independent UAP study likewise concluded that progress depends on high quality, calibrated multi sensor observations instead of isolated anecdotal reports. [AARO] aaro.mil Next AARO UAP Report DocumentsMay 8, 2026... Published: May 8, 2026 Why Reported UAP Sounds Fail Repeatability Tests The central problem is not a shortage of reported sounds but a lack of consistency between them. Witnesses have variously described: Low frequency humming. Electrical buzzing. High pitched whining. Loud roaring. Sharp clicking. Complete silence despite apparently nearby objects. These reports do not converge on a distinctive acoustic profile. Instead, they span nearly the entire range of sounds already associated with conventional sources. One witness may describe a quiet hum, another a loud roar, while a third hears nothing at all under apparently similar circumstances. Even when multiple witnesses observe the same event, differences in location, wind conditions, terrain and hearing can produce very different impressions of loudness and tone. Without calibrated microphones positioned near the source, investigators cannot determine whether two descriptions refer to the same physical sound. The absence of repeatability is itself informative. If one propulsion mechanism consistently produced a unique acoustic output, investigators would expect independent reports to begin clustering around the same measurable characteristics. Official reviews have not found such clustering. [AARO] aaro.mil Next AARO UAP Report DocumentsMay 8, 2026... Published: May 8, 2026 2:19:06 House holds hearing on UFOs, government transparency full video Channel: CBS News · Views: 3.9M · Uploaded: July 2023 · Length: 2 hours 19 minutes Open on YouTube Why Human Hearing Is Not Enough Human hearing provides useful context but is an imprecise measurement tool. Unexpected aerial events typically occur under conditions that reduce observational accuracy: The object's distance is unknown. The observer cannot accurately estimate altitude. Sound may arrive several seconds after the visual event. Echoes from buildings, mountains or water can alter apparent direction. Wind and atmospheric temperature layers bend or attenuate sound. Memory also changes over time. Witnesses often describe unusual sounds only after attempting to reconstruct a stressful or surprising event, making it difficult to separate direct perception from later interpretation. For this reason, official investigations generally treat witness descriptions as qualitative observations rather than quantitative evidence. The Problem of Establishing the Source A further obstacle is proving that a reported sound actually originated from the observed object. During many sightings, numerous alternative sound sources are present: Commercial aircraft. Military aircraft . Helicopters. Drones. Ground vehicles. Industrial machinery. Electrical infrastructure. Thunder or atmospheric effects. Without synchronised recordings from multiple microphones or other localisation methods, investigators cannot confidently attribute a particular sound to the object being observed. Modern investigations therefore distinguish between a sound occurring during a sighting and a sound produced by the sighting . Those are very different evidential claims. 59:00 NASA discusses findings from UFO study full video Channel: CBS News · Views: 34.2K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Why Recorded Audio Rarely Resolves a Case Recorded audio is more valuable than memory alone, but it still seldom identifies a UAP. Consumer devices introduce several limitations: Automatic gain control changes volume dynamically. Built in noise reduction removes low level sounds. Compression alters frequency content. Wind noise can mask important details. Microphone quality varies substantially between devices. In addition, many publicly available videos contain incidental background audio unrelated to the object itself. Recent public releases by the All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) explicitly note in some cases that accompanying audio should not be interpreted as evidence about the observed phenomenon because it merely reflects incidental recording conditions rather than a verified sound emitted by the object. [AARO] aaro.mil Next AARO UAP Report DocumentsMay 8, 2026... Published: May 8, 2026 What Official Investigators Can and Cannot Conclude from Noise Current official investigations draw a careful distinction between what sound evidence supports and what it cannot establish. Investigators can conclude that: Witnesses genuinely report a wide variety of unusual sounds. Some cases include apparent silence where conventional aircraft might be expected to be audible. Acoustic observations may help reconstruct the circumstances of an event when combined with other evidence. However, they cannot conclude that: Any specific sound uniquely identifies a UAP. A particular hum, buzz or tone indicates unknown technology. Silence alone demonstrates extraordinary flight characteristics. Reported noises constitute evidence of a common propulsion system. This cautious approach reflects the standards applied throughout modern UAP investigations. Acoustic observations remain part of the evidential record, but they have not demonstrated the consistency required to become an identifying characteristic. [AARO] aaro.mil Next AARO UAP Report DocumentsMay 8, 2026... Published: May 8, 2026 Watch again: Nasa UFO task force holds first public meeting Channel: The Independent Open on YouTube Why Multi Sensor Evidence Remains the Standard The inability to identify UAP by sound has reinforced a broader lesson in official investigations: reliable conclusions depend on multiple independent sources of evidence. When radar tracks, infrared imagery, electro optical sensors, flight data and eyewitness testimony all point to the same event, investigators can compare those datasets for consistency. Sound, by contrast, is highly vulnerable to environmental effects and rarely provides measurements precise enough to stand on its own. This is why both NASA's recommendations and current government investigative practices emphasise better calibrated sensor networks rather than searching for a single characteristic noise. Until repeated, instrument quality recordings reveal a distinctive and reproducible acoustic pattern, official"},{"title":"Sound Distortion","url":"/sound-distortion/","description":"Wind, temperature layers, terrain and uncertain range can make an ordinary aircraft seem strangely quiet, delayed or directionless.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Official Files","level":3,"text":"Sound Distortion Can Weather Make a UFO Sound Unexplained? Official Files Sound Distortion Sound Distortion Can Weather Make a UFO Sound Unexplained? Wind, temperature layers, terrain and uncertain range can make an ordinary aircraft seem strangely quiet, delayed or directionless. Wind, temperature layers, terrain and uncertain range can make an ordinary aircraft seem strangely quiet, delayed or directionless. population context How wind and temperature layers bend sound Why distance errors change expected loudness How investigators separate sound distortion from object behavior ufo noises official files sound distortion Introduction Reports of unusual sounds accompanying unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) often appear puzzling because witnesses compare what they hear with what they expect to hear from an apparently nearby object. However, sound rarely travels through the atmosphere in a simple, straight path. Wind, temperature layers, humidity, terrain and uncertainty about an object's true distance can all make ordinary aircraft or other sound sources seem strangely quiet, unexpectedly loud, delayed or difficult to locate. These well established acoustic effects are one reason official UAP investigations treat witness descriptions of sound as supporting evidence rather than as proof of an object's behaviour. Instead of assuming that an unexplained sound reflects unusual propulsion, investigators first ask whether the atmosphere itself could have altered how the sound reached the listener. [faa.gov] faa.gov Aircraft Noise Federal Aviation Administration January 31, 2025... Published: January 31, 2025 How wind and temperature layers bend sound Sound waves are refracted, or bent, as they move through air whose temperature or wind speed changes with height. Under some conditions, this causes sound to travel much farther than expected; under others, it can create \"shadow zones\" where a noisy aircraft seems almost silent. A common example is a temperature inversion , in which cooler air sits beneath warmer air instead of the normal daytime pattern. Because the speed of sound changes slightly with temperature, these layers can bend sound back towards the ground, allowing engine noise to remain audible over unexpectedly long distances. Similar refraction occurs when wind speed increases with altitude. If the wind blows from the sound source towards an observer, it can curve sound downwards, strengthening what is heard at ground level. Against the wind, sound may bend upwards, making the same aircraft seem much quieter. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Meteorological and ground effects on the propagation of aircraft noise close to the Earth's surface ScienceDirectSeptember... These effects matter because witnesses rarely know the atmospheric structure at the time of an observation. An aircraft that appears visually distant may sound unusually loud, while another that appears nearby may seem almost silent. Either impression can arise from ordinary atmospheric physics rather than from an unconventional propulsion system. [faa.gov] faa.gov Community Response to Noise Federal Aviation Administration Community Response to Noise Federal Aviation Administration Terrain also alters sound propagation. Hills, forests, buildings and valleys can reflect, absorb or scatter acoustic energy. A listener may hear echoes arriving from different directions or receive only part of the original sound spectrum. Low frequency components often travel farther than high frequency ones, causing distant aircraft to produce a deep rumble with little of the sharper engine noise expected at closer range. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Meteorological and ground effects on the propagation of aircraft noise close to the Earth's surface ScienceDirectSeptember... ACOUSTICS: Refraction and Temperature Inversion Channel: The Point Studios Open on YouTube Why distance errors change expected loudness One of the largest sources of confusion in UAP reports is uncertainty about how far away the observed object actually was. Human observers estimate distance using familiar visual cues such as apparent size, contrast and motion. At night, or against an empty sky, many of these cues disappear. If a conventional aircraft is mistakenly perceived as being only hundreds of metres away instead of several kilometres away, its sound can appear surprisingly weak or delayed. Conversely, if a distant object is assumed to be much farther away than it really is, its noise may seem inexplicably loud. The timing between sight and sound can also mislead. Light reaches an observer effectively instantaneously, while sound travels through air at roughly 343 metres per second under typical conditions. An aircraft several kilometres away may therefore be seen well before its sound arrives. Witnesses unfamiliar with the true distance may associate the delayed sound with another object or conclude that the observed object only began producing noise after passing overhead. Motion adds another complication. Aircraft noise changes continuously as the source approaches and recedes, and the familiar Doppler effect alters the perceived pitch. Combined with uncertain range estimates, this can make a routine overflight seem acoustically inconsistent with what the witness expected. [faa.gov] faa.gov Fundamentals of Noise and Sound Federal Aviation Administration Fundamentals of Noise and Sound Federal Aviation Administration Why direction and location can seem wrong People naturally expect to identify where a sound originates, but the atmosphere can interfere with that judgement. Sound reflected from cloud layers, buildings or terrain may appear to come from a different direction than the visual object. Wind driven refraction can shift the apparent source sideways, while multiple reflections may produce echoes that seem detached from the original object. In complex environments, listeners may hear an engine only after it has moved well beyond the point where they first saw it. These effects become especially important during night time observations, when visual information is limited and listeners rely more heavily on hearing. An observer may honestly report that the sound \"came from nowhere\" or \"did not match the object's position\", even though the discrepancy resulted from atmospheric propagation rather than unusual flight characteristics. Speed of Sound: 343 m/s Explained Factors Affecting Sound Propagation in Air Channel: CodeLucky Open on YouTube How investigators separate sound distortion from object behaviour Modern UAP investigations do not assume that unusual acoustic reports are either accurate or inaccurate. Instead, they compare them with independent evidence to determine whether atmospheric effects provide a sufficient explanation. Typical investigative steps include: Comparing witness accounts with local weather observations, including wind profiles and temperature inversions. Reviewing terrain, buildings and topography that could influence sound propagation. Checking known aircraft movements, helicopter operations and drone activity. Comparing reported timing with expected sound travel delays. Looking for recordings from multiple locations to determine whether the same sound behaved consistently over a wider area. Comparing acoustic reports with radar, infrared imagery, flight data and other sensor records before drawing conclusions. This multi sensor approach reflects the fact that atmospheric conditions can alter perceived loudness, direction and timing without changing the object's actual behaviour. Consequently, investigators generally treat sound reports as contextual information that must be interpreted alongside environmental data rather than in isolation. [faa.gov] faa.gov Aircraft Noise Federal Aviation Administration January 31, 2025... Published: January 31, 2025 What this means for reported UAP sounds The absence of an expected sound—or the presence of an unexpected one—is not, by itself, evidence that an observed object used unconventional technology. Atmospheric refraction, wind, terrain and errors in judging distance routinely produce surprising acoustic experiences around conventional aircraft and other airborne sources. For that reason, official investigations into UAP place relatively little weight on isolated descriptions of humming, silence, delayed engine noise or apparently misplaced sounds. Unless these reports are supported by calibrated recordings and corroborating sensor data, they are more reliably interpreted as observations filtered through complex atmospheric acoustics than as indicators of extraordinary object performance. [faa.gov] faa.gov Aircraft Noise Federal Aviation Administration January 31, 2025... Published: January 31, 2025 Refraction of Sound: How Sound Waves Bend Through Different Media Sound Physics Explained Channel: CodeLucky Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Can Weather Make a UFO Sound"},{"title":"Record It","url":"/record-it/","description":"A useful report captures clean audio plus the exact time, direction, weather, duration, and visible activity surrounding the sound.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises","level":2,"text":"Record It How to Document a Strange Sound Overhead UFO Noises Record It Record It How to Document a Strange Sound Overhead A useful report captures clean audio plus the exact time, direction, weather, duration, and visible activity surrounding the sound. A useful report captures clean audio plus the exact time, direction, weather, duration, and visible activity surrounding the sound. policy intervention Recording usable audio and video Logging time, direction, and weather Finding independent witnesses and sources ufo noises record it Introduction If you hear an unusual sound that appears to come from the sky, the most useful response is not to speculate about its origin but to document it carefully. High quality recordings and accurate observations give researchers, journalists and other witnesses the best chance of identifying whether the sound was caused by aircraft, weather, geological activity, industrial sources , wildlife or a genuinely unexplained event. Most reports of mysterious aerial sounds are eventually linked to ordinary causes, but some remain unresolved because the available evidence is incomplete or lacks context. NASA's review of unidentified aerial phenomena (UAP) likewise concluded that poor quality data and missing metadata are major obstacles to meaningful analysis. [nasa.gov] nasa.gov UNIDENTIFIED ANOMALOUS PHENOMENA NASA UNIDENTIFIED ANOMALOUS PHENOMENA IndependentSeptember 13, 2023... Published: September 13, 2023 Record usable audio and video A recording is most valuable when it captures the sound naturally rather than attempting to make it seem louder or stranger. If possible: Start recording as soon as you notice the sound. Keep the phone or recorder still rather than waving it around. Avoid speaking over the recording except to announce useful information such as the current time or compass direction. Continue recording for at least a minute after the sound stops, as this captures ambient background noise for comparison. If safe, record both audio and a wide angle video showing the surrounding sky and horizon. If you have another device available, consider using one device for uninterrupted audio while another records video. Even if nothing unusual appears visually, the video provides evidence of cloud cover, aircraft, weather conditions and other contextual details. Do not apply filters, noise reduction or editing before saving the original file. Keep an untouched copy with its original creation time and metadata intact. NASA's UAP study specifically highlighted the importance of preserving sensor information and recording context because missing metadata makes later scientific analysis much more difficult. [nasa.gov] nasa.gov UNIDENTIFIED ANOMALOUS PHENOMENA NASA UNIDENTIFIED ANOMALOUS PHENOMENA IndependentSeptember 13, 2023... Published: September 13, 2023 Log the details while they are fresh Human memory changes quickly, so write down observations immediately after the event. Record: Exact date and local time (including the time the sound began and ended). Estimated duration. Direction the sound seemed to come from (using a compass app if available). Whether the sound moved or appeared stationary. Estimated loudness and whether windows or objects vibrated. Description of the sound , such as humming, booming, metallic, trumpet like, pulsing or crackling. Whether the sound changed in pitch, rhythm or volume. Avoid describing what you think caused it. Separate direct observations (\"three loud booms\") from interpretations (\"it sounded like a UFO\"). 4:00:26 Public Meeting on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (Official NASA Broadcast) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 358.7K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 4 hours Open on YouTube Record the surrounding conditions Context often explains mysterious sounds better than the recording alone. Note: Weather conditions, including cloud cover, rain, fog and wind. Temperature, if known. Whether thunder, lightning or storms were nearby. Any visible aircraft, helicopters or drones . Bright objects, meteors or unusual lights. Nearby industrial activity, quarry blasting or construction. Whether the sound coincided with an earthquake, vibration or shaking. Researchers studying mysterious booms have found that many reports are eventually associated with atmospheric conditions, sonic booms, small earthquakes or other natural and human made sources rather than a single unexplained phenomenon. [USGS] usgs.gov Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey... Look for independent witnesses Independent observations are more valuable than multiple people discussing the event together before writing their accounts. If others heard the sound: Ask them to record their own recollections separately. Compare the reported time, duration and direction afterwards. Note whether people in different locations heard the same sound. Save any publicly available recordings from nearby residents without altering the originals. Independent accounts can help establish how widely the sound was heard and whether it appeared to move across an area. The Source Behind a Mystery Sound Heard Around the World Channel: Inside Edition Open on YouTube Check ordinary explanations before drawing conclusions Many apparently mysterious sounds have identifiable causes that may not be obvious at first. Useful checks include: Flight tracking services for nearby aircraft. Official weather records for thunderstorms or atmospheric conditions. Earthquake monitoring services if a boom coincided with ground vibration. Local news about quarry blasting, military exercises or industrial incidents. The absence of an immediate explanation does not mean the sound has an extraordinary origin. The US Geological Survey notes that mysterious booms have been reported for centuries, with some linked to shallow earthquakes , sonic booms or other natural processes while others remain unexplained because evidence is insufficient. [USGS] usgs.gov Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey... Preserve the original evidence After recording: Keep the original media files unchanged. Make a backup before sharing them. Record the file names and creation times. If uploading online, retain the original files in case compressed versions lose useful information. Compression by social media platforms often removes metadata and reduces audio quality, making later analysis more difficult. What makes a report genuinely useful? The strongest reports usually include several pieces of mutually supporting evidence rather than a single dramatic recording. A useful report typically contains: ObservationWhy it mattersOriginal audio recordingAllows independent analysis of the sound.Exact timestampsEnables comparison with weather, aircraft and seismic records.Direction of the soundHelps estimate possible sources.Weather observationsAssists in evaluating atmospheric explanations.Video of the surroundingsDocuments visible conditions and nearby activity. Independent witness accountsHelps confirm whether the event was widespread.Notes made immediately afterwardsReduces memory distortion. What Causes Skyquakes That Sound Like Sonic Booms? Strange Science HQ Channel: Strange Science HQ Open on YouTube Common mistakes to avoid Several habits reduce the value of otherwise interesting recordings: Zooming excessively, producing unstable footage. Narrating continuously over the sound. Editing or enhancing the audio before preserving the original. Estimating times from memory instead of checking a clock. Assuming the source without evidence. Sharing only short clips that omit what happened before or after the sound. [usgs.gov] usgs.gov What is that mysterious booming sound? U.S. Geological Survey What is that mysterious booming sound? U.S. Geological Survey... Careful documentation cannot determine the cause of every unexplained sky sound. However, by preserving clean recordings together with accurate information about the time, direction, weather and surrounding activity, you create evidence that is far more useful for independent investigation than a recording presented without context. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to How to Document a Strange Sound Overhead. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Location Sound Bible By Ric Viers Written by a seasoned professional, Viers explains how to achieve Hollywood quality sound that will make productions stand out from the r... See on Amazon Book The Sound Book By Trevor Cox A professor of acoustic engineering provides a tour of the world's most amazing sound phenomena, including creaking glaciers, whispering... See on Amazon Book The Universal Sense By Seth Horowitz A surprising, entertaining book about how what we hear with our ears affects what's between"},{"title":"Witness Accounts","url":"/witness-accounts/","description":"Separate accounts from people in different locations can confirm timing, direction and how widely an unexplained sound was heard.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Record It","level":3,"text":"Witness Accounts When Multiple Witnesses Make a Sound Report Stronger Record It Witness Accounts Witness Accounts When Multiple Witnesses Make a Sound Report Stronger Separate accounts from people in different locations can confirm timing, direction and how widely an unexplained sound was heard. Separate accounts from people in different locations can confirm timing, direction and how widely an unexplained sound was heard. population context Collecting recollections before witnesses compare notes Mapping where each person heard the sound Preserving nearby recordings and original files ufo noises record it witness accounts Introduction When several people independently report hearing the same unexplained sound in the sky, the event becomes easier to investigate—not because multiple witnesses prove an extraordinary explanation, but because separate accounts can establish when the sound occurred, how widely it was heard, and whether different observations agree. For investigations into unusual aerial sounds, including reports sometimes associated with UFO or unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP), independently recorded witness accounts are often more valuable than a single detailed recollection. NASA's guidance on improving UAP investigations similarly stresses that reliable, well documented observations and accompanying metadata are far more useful than isolated anecdotes or poor quality evidence. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 The key principle is independence. Witnesses who document what they heard before discussing it with one another provide a stronger evidential record because their memories are less likely to become unintentionally aligned. Why independent witnesses matter more than agreement alone Several people repeating the same story is not automatically strong evidence. If those people spoke together before recording their memories, later similarities may reflect shared discussion rather than independent observation. Research on eyewitness memory has repeatedly shown that conversations between witnesses can alter recollections. People may unconsciously adopt details they did not personally observe, become more confident in inaccurate memories, or overlook genuine differences that could have helped reconstruct what happened. For that reason, investigators typically seek individual statements before comparing accounts. [University of Portsmouth] researchportal.port.ac.uk University of PortsmouthFebruary 1, 2008... Published: February 1, 2008 For unexplained sky sounds, genuinely independent reports can strengthen confidence in observations such as: the approximate time the sound began and ended; whether it appeared to travel across an area or remain stationary; whether multiple pulses or separate booms occurred; whether vibrations were felt as well as heard; whether the sound was widespread or confined to one neighbourhood. Differences between accounts are not necessarily weaknesses. In fact, natural variations can reveal how the sound propagated across different locations or how local geography affected what people experienced. 57:22 Paranormal activity: science or fiction? with Chris French Channel: The Royal Institution · Views: 63.9K · Uploaded: June 2024 · Length: 57 minutes Open on YouTube Collecting recollections before witnesses compare notes The most valuable witness record is created immediately after the event while memories remain fresh. Each witness should write or record their own account without consulting others. Useful observations include: the exact time, if known; the direction from which the sound seemed to come; estimated duration; whether the sound changed in volume or pitch; whether windows, doors or the ground vibrated; whether anything unusual was visible in the sky. Witnesses should distinguish observations from interpretations. For example: Observation: \"Three loud booms separated by about five seconds.\" Interpretation: \"It was probably a UFO.\" Only the first statement records directly perceived information. The second introduces a hypothesis that may influence other witnesses if shared too early. Even if two independent accounts disagree on some details, those differences may help investigators estimate uncertainty rather than forcing a false consensus. Studies of eyewitness memory indicate that preserving an original independent record before collaboration helps reduce contamination and provides a clearer baseline for later comparison. [BPS Psych Hub] bpspsychub.onlinelibrary.wiley.com BPS Psych Hub When discussion between eyewitnesses helps memory BPS Psych HubWhen discussion between eyewitnesses helps memory Vredeveldt 2017 Legal and Criminological Psychology Wiley Online L... 5:10 Alderney UFO sighting explained Why it's one of the most incredible ever recorded This Is Why Channel: 7NEWS Australia · Views: 745.0K · Uploaded: January 2022 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube Mapping where each person heard the sound Independent reports become much more informative when linked to location. Rather than simply counting witnesses, plot where each person was standing when they heard the sound. Even a simple map with approximate positions can reveal useful patterns. For example: A sound reported over many kilometres may suggest an atmospheric event such as a sonic boom or meteor related airburst. A sound heard only within a small area may indicate a local industrial, geological or infrastructure source. Reports arriving progressively farther away at slightly different times may help estimate the direction of travel. Researchers investigating mysterious booms routinely compare reports from multiple locations with seismic records, weather data, aircraft activity and other measurements. Spatial patterns often prove more informative than individual descriptions. [USGS] usgs.gov Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey... If witnesses independently report similar timing from widely separated locations, investigators gain a better understanding of the event's scale without relying on any single observer. Preserving nearby recordings and original files Witness testimony becomes substantially stronger when supported by independent recordings. Potential sources include: mobile phone videos; home security cameras with audio; dashboard cameras; wildlife recording devices; environmental monitoring equipment. Each recording should be preserved in its original form. Avoid editing, trimming, applying noise reduction or converting file formats before archiving the original. File metadata—including creation time and device information—may later help synchronise recordings from different locations. If several independent recordings capture the same sound, investigators can compare: arrival times at different locations; duration; frequency characteristics; background environmental sounds; evidence of echoes or reflections. NASA's recommendations for future UAP investigations emphasise preserving original data and associated metadata because incomplete or altered records greatly reduce scientific value. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP NASA ScienceUAP NASA ScienceFebruary 23, 2026... Published: February 23, 2026 What multiple witnesses can—and cannot—establish Independent witness accounts can strengthen confidence that an unusual sound occurred, but they do not by themselves identify its cause. Several reliable observers may establish that: a loud sound was genuinely heard across a region; the event occurred at a specific time; its characteristics were broadly consistent across locations. They cannot, on their own, determine whether the source was an aircraft, meteor, atmospheric phenomenon, geological event or something that remains unidentified. This distinction is important because many widely reported mysterious booms have eventually been linked to sonic booms, shallow earthquakes , quarry blasting or other natural and human made causes, while some events remain unresolved because insufficient physical evidence exists. Independent witness reports help narrow the possibilities, but additional evidence is usually required to reach a robust conclusion. [USGS] usgs.gov Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey... Remembering West Michigan's Most Famous UFO Sighting Part 1 Channel: WWMT TV Open on YouTube Common mistakes that reduce evidential value Several avoidable practices can weaken otherwise useful witness evidence: Discussing the event extensively before recording individual memories. Editing audio or video files instead of preserving originals. Estimating times from memory rather than checking device clocks where possible. Allowing one confident witness to shape everyone else's account. Omitting the witness's"},{"title":"File Preservation","url":"/file-preservation/","description":"Backing up untouched recordings protects metadata and audio quality that social platforms may remove through compression.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Record It","level":3,"text":"File Preservation Do Not Let Sharing Ruin Your Best Evidence Record It File Preservation File Preservation Do Not Let Sharing Ruin Your Best Evidence Backing up untouched recordings protects metadata and audio quality that social platforms may remove through compression. Backing up untouched recordings protects metadata and audio quality that social platforms may remove through compression. policy intervention Saving the original media and metadata Creating backups before editing or uploading Understanding compression and quality loss ufo noises record it file preservation Introduction If you record an unexplained sky sound that you think may be significant, the most important file is the one your recording device created—not the version you later upload to a social media platform. Online sharing is useful for finding other witnesses, but many services automatically compress audio and video, alter file formats, reduce resolution, or remove metadata. These changes can make later analysis more difficult and may prevent others from verifying when, where and how the recording was made. For anyone documenting unusual aerial sounds, preserving the untouched original should therefore be treated as a basic evidence preservation step rather than an afterthought. NASA's independent study of unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) similarly emphasised that complete sensor data and metadata are essential because missing contextual information greatly limits scientific evaluation. [nist.gov] nist.gov digital and multimedia evidence September 13, 2013... Published: September 13, 2013 Save the Original Before You Share Anything The safest approach is simple: never let your first upload become your only copy. As soon as practical after making the recording: Leave the original file exactly as your device created it. Copy it to another location before editing, trimming or uploading. Keep the original filename if possible. Avoid opening the file in applications that automatically re encode media on export. Store at least two copies on different devices or storage media. This preserves both the media itself and the information embedded within it. Depending on the device, metadata may include the recording time, file creation details, device model, GPS location (if enabled), recording settings and other technical information that can help reconstruct the circumstances of the event. Apple, for example, notes that original photo and video files may include metadata such as date, time, location and device information, while also providing options to preserve or alter that information during sharing. [Apple Support] support.apple.com Support Share photos and videos on i Phone Apple SupportShare photos and videos on iPhone Apple Support (IE)... Why Social Media Copies Are Usually Poor Evidence A recording that sounds convincing online may be much less useful for technical review than the original stored on your phone or recorder. Most major sharing platforms optimise uploaded media for storage and streaming efficiency. Depending on the service, this may involve: Lowering audio or video bitrates. Changing the codec or container format. Reducing image or video resolution. Recompressing the file, introducing new artefacts. Removing or rewriting metadata. Renaming the file. Research examining major photo sharing platforms has shown that uploaded images are routinely transformed, with changes extending beyond visible quality to filenames and other digital characteristics. Such processing is valuable for platform efficiency but makes downloaded copies less representative of the original recording. [arXiv] arxiv.org Practical Analyses of How Common Social Media Platforms and Photo Storage Services Handle Uploaded ImagesFebruary 23, 2023... Published: February 23, 2023 For an unexplained sky sound, even subtle changes matter. Compression can soften faint background noises, alter frequency content or introduce artefacts that complicate later spectral analysis. Likewise, stripped metadata may remove information needed to compare independent recordings from different witnesses. Create Backups Before Any Editing Many people instinctively trim silence, increase volume or apply noise reduction before sharing. While these edits may make the recording easier to hear casually, they permanently change the evidence unless the original has already been preserved. A practical workflow is: 1. Record the event. 2. Immediately duplicate the file. 3. Archive the untouched original. 4. Perform any editing only on the duplicate. 5. Clearly label edited versions. Using descriptive filenames such as: Original 2026 07 31 2148.wav Edited NoiseBoost v1.wav helps prevent accidentally overwriting the source recording. This approach mirrors established digital evidence practice, where preserving an unmodified original allows later reviewers to distinguish between observed data and subsequent processing. NIST's work on digital and multimedia evidence likewise emphasises the importance of maintaining original digital material for reliable forensic examination. [nist.gov] nist.gov digital and multimedia evidence September 13, 2013... Published: September 13, 2013 Preserve Metadata Whenever Possible Metadata is often invisible to the user but can be highly valuable during later review. Depending on the recording device, useful metadata may include: Date and time of recording. Device model. Recording format and sample rate. GPS coordinates (if enabled). Orientation and other sensor information. Original filename and creation timestamp. If you intentionally remove location information before publishing publicly for privacy reasons, retain a private copy containing the complete metadata. That way, you can still provide the original file to researchers or investigators if needed without exposing personal information online. Some platforms and sharing methods preserve more original information than others. Apple, for instance, allows users in certain sharing workflows to send the original media with edit history and metadata instead of a converted version, although this depends on the chosen sharing option. [Apple Support] support.apple.com Support Share photos and videos on i Phone Apple SupportShare photos and videos on iPhone Apple Support (IE)... Share Copies, Not the Master File Think of the original recording as a master archive. For public discussion, create a separate sharing copy. This allows you to: Reduce file size for easier uploading. Blur personal information if necessary. Trim irrelevant sections without affecting the archive. Add captions or annotations. Normalise playback volume for listeners. If someone later requests the original for detailed analysis, you still have the untouched source available. This distinction is especially important if multiple investigators independently examine the recording. Everyone can compare their analyses against the same unaltered file instead of different edited versions. A Small Habit That Can Preserve Your Best Evidence Most recordings of unusual sky sounds ultimately have ordinary explanations, ranging from aircraft and weather to industrial activity. Regardless of the outcome, preserving the original recording ensures that any investigation begins with the highest quality evidence available. Uploading to social media should therefore be viewed as the final step in documentation rather than the first. By keeping an untouched master copy, making secure backups before editing, and understanding that online platforms frequently recompress media and alter metadata, you maximise the chances that your recording can be assessed fairly and accurately if questions arise later. [arxiv.org] arxiv.org Practical Analyses of How Common Social Media Platforms and Photo Storage Services Handle Uploaded ImagesFebruary 23, 2023... Published: February 23, 2023 Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Do Not Let Sharing Ruin Your Best Evidence. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Digital Evidence and Computer Crime By Eoghan Casey Though an increasing number of criminals are using computers and computer networks, few investigators are well versed in the issues relat... See on Amazon Book File System Forensic Analysis By Brian Carrier The Definitive Guide to File System Analysis: Key Concepts and Hands on Techniques Most digital evidence is stored within the computer's... See on Amazon Book The Location Sound Bible By Ric Viers Written by a seasoned professional, Viers explains how to achieve Hollywood quality sound that will make productions stand out from the r... See on Amazon Book Audio Postproduction for Film and Video By Jay Rose New to this edition: Learn how many Hollywood techniques previously impractical on video can help solve problems on smaller productio... See on Amazon Browse more on Amazon: Digital Evidence Computer Crime File System Forensic Analysis The Location Sound Bible As"},{"title":"Phone Setup","url":"/phone-setup/","description":"A steady phone, uninterrupted audio and a wide view of the sky can preserve details that shaky, narrated clips often lose.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Record It","level":3,"text":"Phone Setup How to Capture a Clear Sky Sound Recording Record It Phone Setup Phone Setup How to Capture a Clear Sky Sound Recording A steady phone, uninterrupted audio and a wide view of the sky can preserve details that shaky, narrated clips often lose. A steady phone, uninterrupted audio and a wide view of the sky can preserve details that shaky, narrated clips often lose. policy intervention Positioning the phone for stable audio Recording the sky and horizon together Keeping an untouched original file ufo noises record it phone setup Introduction When an unusual sound appears to come from the sky, the value of a phone recording depends less on the latest handset and more on how it is used. A steady recording with clear, uninterrupted audio and a wide view of the sky and surrounding horizon gives investigators far more to work with than a shaky clip filled with commentary. NASA's review of unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) highlighted that poor quality sensor data and missing context often prevent meaningful analysis, making careful recording practices more important than speculation. [NASA Technical Reports Server] ntrs.nasa.gov NASA Technical Reports ServerMethods for Recording and Documenting Ambient Environmental Sound for use in Listening Devices NASA Techni... This guide focuses specifically on setting up a phone to maximise the usefulness of an outdoor recording while preserving the original evidence. The aim is not to prove what caused a sound, but to capture it as faithfully as possible. Position the Phone for Stable Audio A phone's built in microphones are surprisingly capable, but they are also sensitive to handling noise. Every adjustment of your grip, every finger brushing the case and every sudden movement can create sounds that may be mistaken for something occurring in the environment. For the clearest recording: Hold the phone with both hands or rest it on a stable surface if one is available. Avoid covering any microphone openings with your fingers or a phone case. Keep movements slow and deliberate rather than repeatedly pointing the phone around the sky. Once recording has started, resist the urge to stop and restart if the sound changes. A continuous recording preserves timing and acoustic transitions. If you have a small tripod, clamp or other support, using it can eliminate much of the handling noise that commonly affects handheld recordings. Even leaning the phone against a fixed object is often better than holding it continuously. Guidance on smartphone audio recording consistently identifies handling noise as one of the easiest problems to avoid through stable positioning. [UC Agriculture and Natural Resources] ucanr.edu UC Agriculture and Natural Resources Recording Communications Toolkit UC Agriculture and Natural Resources Recording Communications Toolkit Field Recording: Capturing Musical Sounds (Tips, Gear & Techniques) 1 of 3 Channel: Output Open on YouTube Record the Sky and Horizon Together The camera should provide environmental context rather than attempting to zoom in on an unknown source. A useful framing usually includes: A broad section of sky. The visible horizon. Nearby buildings, trees or landmarks that establish orientation. Any aircraft, clouds or weather features entering the frame naturally. Avoid digital zoom unless an identifiable object is clearly visible. Digital zoom generally reduces image quality while making camera shake more noticeable. Keeping the horizon in view allows later reviewers to estimate elevation, direction of travel and possible relationships with visible aircraft or weather conditions. Even if nothing unusual appears visually, the footage documents lighting, cloud cover and environmental conditions at the time of the sound. Field Recording For Beginners Essential GEAR & TIPS Channel: Jonas Fasching Open on YouTube Reduce Wind Without Changing the Recording Wind is one of the biggest causes of unusable outdoor audio. Strong gusts striking the microphone can produce low frequency rumbling that overwhelms distant sounds. Rather than relying entirely on software processing: Stand behind a wall, vehicle or building if it safely blocks the wind. Turn your body so it shields the phone from direct airflow. Hold the phone steadily instead of swinging it through the air. If using an external microphone, fit an appropriate windscreen. Some modern smartphones automatically apply wind noise reduction while recording video, and certain devices allow this feature to be enabled or disabled in settings. These tools can improve intelligibility, but because they modify the recorded signal, it is useful to know whether they were active when documenting an unexplained sound. [apple.com] support.apple.com Support Change sound recording options on i Phone – Apple Support (UK Apple SupportChange sound recording options on iPhone – Apple Support (UK)... Avoid Interrupting the Recording The temptation to narrate observations is understandable, but spoken commentary can permanently mask faint environmental sounds. Instead: Begin recording as soon as possible. Allow several seconds of uninterrupted ambient sound before speaking, if speech is necessary at all. If you need to state the time or direction, do so briefly and then remain silent. Continue recording after the sound ends to capture the normal background environment for comparison. The uninterrupted portions are often the most valuable because they preserve changes in loudness, rhythm and duration without introducing additional acoustic interference. Keep an Untouched Original File The original recording is usually the most valuable version. After recording: Save the file immediately. Avoid trimming, filtering or applying noise reduction to the original. Make copies for sharing or enhancement while preserving the first version unchanged. Retain the original filename, creation date and embedded metadata where possible. If later analysis is required, investigators can compare processed versions against the untouched recording. Preserving original metadata also helps establish when and how the recording was created, a point emphasised in NASA's recommendations for improving the quality and traceability of observational data. [NASA Technical Reports Server] ntrs.nasa.gov NASA Technical Reports ServerMethods for Recording and Documenting Ambient Environmental Sound for use in Listening Devices NASA Techni... Useful Phone Settings Before Recording Preparing the phone before an outdoor recording reduces the chance of interruptions. A practical checklist includes: Ensure sufficient battery charge. Confirm adequate free storage space. Switch to aeroplane mode if safe to do so, preventing calls from interrupting recording. Disable unnecessary notifications that could obscure the screen or stop recording. Clean the camera lens and microphone openings. Use the highest practical video quality if storage allows, while remembering that audio quality is usually more important than extreme video resolution for unexplained sky sounds. If your device offers microphone recording options such as mono, stereo or spatial audio, leave the chosen setting consistent throughout the event rather than changing it mid recording. Recording consistency is generally more valuable than experimenting while the sound is occurring. [Apple Support] support.apple.com Support Change sound recording options on i Phone – Apple Support (UK Apple SupportChange sound recording options on iPhone – Apple Support (UK)... How to Easily Record GREAT Audio Outdoors! Channel: Tom Buck Open on YouTube Common Mistakes That Reduce Evidential Value Several common habits can make an otherwise useful recording much harder to interpret: Constantly zooming or panning across the sky. Narrating continuously over faint sounds. Stopping and restarting multiple recordings. Editing the only copy before saving the original. Covering microphones while adjusting grip. Pointing the phone at the ground or only at an empty patch of sky with no visible landmarks. Recording only a few seconds instead of capturing the full event and its aftermath. A calm, continuous recording with stable framing and preserved original audio is generally far more informative than a dramatic but heavily edited clip. Even if the sound is ultimately explained by aircraft, weather or another ordinary source, careful phone setup ensures the recording remains useful for objective analysis rather than speculation. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to How to Capture a Clear Sky Sound Recording. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Location Sound Bible By Ric Viers Written by a seasoned professional, Viers explains how to achieve Hollywood quality sound that will make productions stand out from the r... See on Amazon Book Producing Great Sound for Film and Video By Jay Rose Make your film or video project sound as good as it looks with this"},{"title":"Time and Direction","url":"/time-and-direction/","description":"Exact start times, end times and compass directions make a strange sound easier to compare with aircraft, weather and seismic records.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Record It","level":3,"text":"Time and Direction Why Time and Direction Can Solve the Mystery Record It Time and Direction Time and Direction Why Time and Direction Can Solve the Mystery Exact start times, end times and compass directions make a strange sound easier to compare with aircraft, weather and seismic records. Exact start times, end times and compass directions make a strange sound easier to compare with aircraft, weather and seismic records. dataset evidence Recording exact start and end times Estimating direction with a compass Matching observations to outside records ufo noises record it time and direction Introduction When documenting an unexplained sky sound, the most valuable information may not be the recording itself but the precise record of when the sound occurred and where it appeared to come from. Exact timestamps and estimated compass directions allow investigators to compare a report with independent datasets such as aircraft movements, weather observations, lightning detections, seismic records and satellite observations. Without these details, even a high quality audio recording is often impossible to test against ordinary explanations. NASA's independent study of unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) similarly concluded that poor quality metadata, including missing time and sensor context, is one of the greatest obstacles to scientific analysis. [NASA Science] smd cms.nasa.gov Science NASA UNIDENTIFIED ANOMALOUS PHENOMENA... Why exact times matter more than memory People frequently remember an unusual sound as happening \"around dusk\" or \"just after midnight\", but such estimates can differ from the true time by several minutes or more. That seemingly small error can make comparisons with external records far less reliable. Record three separate times whenever possible: Start time : when the sound was first noticed. End time : when it was last heard. Observation time : when the recording began if it started after the sound. Using the device's automatically recorded creation time is preferable to relying solely on memory. If you begin recording several seconds after hearing the sound, note that delay in your written log. Duration is equally important. A single sharp boom lasting less than a second points investigators towards different possibilities than a low frequency hum that persists for several minutes. Consistent timing also helps determine whether multiple witnesses heard the same event or unrelated sounds. 59:22 NASA UAP Independent Study Report — Press Conference (September 14, 2023) Channel: Lies Above · Views: 26 · Uploaded: April 2026 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube Estimating direction with a compass The apparent direction of the sound helps determine which external records deserve investigation first. A simple compass reading is usually sufficient; precision to the nearest few degrees is rarely necessary. Useful observations include: The compass bearing where the sound appeared strongest. Whether the sound remained fixed or seemed to travel. Whether it rose overhead or faded towards another direction. Whether echoes from buildings, hills or valleys may have affected perception . Because low frequency sounds can refract through the atmosphere or reflect from terrain, the perceived source is not always the true source. Nevertheless, an approximate direction such as \"north west\" or \"110° east south east\" is far more valuable than no directional information at all. If more than one person is present, each should independently estimate the direction before discussing it. Differences between observers can themselves provide useful clues about echoes, movement or local acoustic effects. 1:02:46 UAP Independent Study Event Post Meeting Media Teleconference (May 31, 2023) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 30.3K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 1 hour 2 minutes Open on YouTube Matching observations to independent records The main purpose of logging exact time and direction is to compare the report against datasets that were recorded independently of the observer. Independent records are much harder to influence by expectation or memory. Common comparisons include: Aircraft tracking using publicly available flight data to determine whether commercial or military aircraft were nearby at the recorded time. Weather observations , including thunderstorms, atmospheric pressure changes and strong wind conditions. Lightning detection networks , which can identify distant electrical activity even when no lightning was visible. Seismic records , which may reveal small earthquakes associated with reported booms. Meteor or fireball reports , where available, if witnesses also observed unusual lights. Even when no single source provides a complete explanation, combining several independent datasets often narrows the possibilities considerably. Why start and end times improve verification Many reports record only a single clock time, but an observation window is considerably more informative. For example: A boom heard between 21:14:08 and 21:14:11 can be compared with lightning or seismic detections occurring within seconds. A humming sound lasting from 22:31 until 22:44 can be checked against aircraft tracks crossing the area throughout that period. A repeating sound every two minutes may indicate periodic industrial activity rather than an isolated atmospheric event. Precise timing also allows different recordings from neighbouring locations to be synchronised. If two recordings captured the same event, analysts can compare arrival times and estimate how the sound propagated across the landscape. Direction logs become stronger with multiple witnesses Independent directional estimates are often more valuable than multiple identical stories developed after discussion. If witnesses are separated geographically, each should record: Their exact location. Their own start and end times. Their independent compass bearing. Whether the sound appeared stationary or moving. When plotted on a map, these observations sometimes reveal a consistent pattern that would not be obvious from individual reports alone. Conversely, major disagreements may indicate that echoes, atmospheric refraction or multiple unrelated sounds influenced perception. 3:03 Compass 101 How to Use a Baseplate Compass Channel: MRCAParks · Views: 684.9K · Uploaded: January 2013 · Length: 3 minutes 3 seconds Open on YouTube What investigators look for Investigators generally value reports that distinguish clearly between observations and interpretations . Strong entries include statements such as: \"Sound began at 20:47:13.\" \"Last audible at 20:48:02.\" \"Appeared strongest from approximately 300° (north west).\" \"Direction remained unchanged throughout.\" Weaker entries replace measurable information with conclusions, for example \"a UFO passed overhead\" or \"it came from the sky\". Such statements cannot be independently tested. NASA's recommendations for future UAP research likewise emphasise collecting high quality contextual data and metadata rather than relying on anecdotal descriptions alone. [NASA Science] smd cms.nasa.gov Science NASA UNIDENTIFIED ANOMALOUS PHENOMENA... Time and direction help eliminate ordinary explanations Most reports of mysterious aerial sounds eventually prove consistent with known natural or human made causes once accurate timing and location are available. The U.S. Geological Survey notes that reported booms may result from sonic booms, quarry blasting, construction, meteor events or small earthquakes, while some cases remain unresolved because available information is insufficient to identify a cause. Investigators distinguish among these possibilities by comparing witness reports with independent records rather than by relying on the sound description alone. [USGS] usgs.gov Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey... For that reason, an unexplained sound accompanied by an exact start time, end time and compass direction is far more useful than a longer recording lacking basic contextual information. Those three pieces of metadata often determine whether a report can be verified, compared with external evidence or confidently attributed to an ordinary source. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Time and Direction Can Solve the Mystery. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Be Expert With Map and Compass By Bjorn Kjellstrom Many of the earliest books, particularly those dating back to the 1900s and before, are now extremely scarce and increasingly expensive.... See on Amazon Book The Natural Navigator By Tristan Gooley Before GPS, before the compass, and even before cartography, humankind was navigating. Now this singular guide helps us rediscover what o... See on Amazon Book The Location Sound Bible By Ric Viers Written by a seasoned professional, Viers explains how to achieve Hollywood"},{"title":"Weather Clues","url":"/weather-clues/","description":"Clouds, wind, storms, vibration and nearby shaking can reveal whether a sky sound came from atmospheric or geological activity.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Record It","level":3,"text":"Weather Clues Was the Sound in the Sky or Ground? Record It Weather Clues Weather Clues Was the Sound in the Sky or Ground? Clouds, wind, storms, vibration and nearby shaking can reveal whether a sky sound came from atmospheric or geological activity. Clouds, wind, storms, vibration and nearby shaking can reveal whether a sky sound came from atmospheric or geological activity. mechanism Checking clouds, wind and storm activity Noting vibration and possible seismic clues Distinguishing thunder from isolated booms ufo noises record it weather clues Introduction When an unexplained boom seems to come from the sky, the surrounding weather and any signs of ground movement are often more informative than the sound itself. A loud report that appears airborne may have travelled unusually far because of atmospheric conditions, while a boom that seems to come from overhead may actually originate from a shallow earthquake , frost related ground cracking or another source near the surface. Careful notes about clouds, wind, storms and vibration can therefore help distinguish between competing explanations. Rather than proving what caused the sound, these observations narrow the possibilities and make later analysis far more reliable. Was the Sound in the Sky or Ground? A single loud boom can be surprisingly difficult for the human ear to locate. Low frequency sound bends around obstacles, reflects from cloud layers and terrain, and can be refracted by changes in air temperature. The result is that a sound generated many kilometres away may seem to come directly overhead, while a nearby ground source may be perceived as airborne. This is why weather observations belong alongside audio recordings. Conditions that affect how sound travels can make ordinary events seem mysterious without requiring an unusual source. USGS guidance on mysterious booms likewise notes that many reports are eventually linked to human activity, sonic booms or shallow natural processes, even though some events remain unexplained. [USGS] usgs.gov Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey... Checking Clouds, Wind and Storm Activity Weather changes both how sounds are produced and how they propagate. Cloud cover and nearby storms The first question is whether thunderstorms were present, even if no lightning was visible overhead. Thunder from distant storms can travel tens of kilometres under favourable conditions. A single cloud to ground lightning strike may produce a sharp crack close by or a deep rolling boom when heard from farther away. If rain shafts or storm clouds were visible on one horizon, record their direction. A boom arriving from that same direction is more consistent with thunder than with an isolated unexplained event. NOAA explains that thunder consists of many overlapping shock waves from the lightning channel, creating the familiar rumbling or booming character. [NOAA] prod 01 alb www noaa.woc.noaa.gov The Sound of Thunder National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration The Sound of Thunder National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration... Wind direction and strength Wind affects both loudness and apparent direction. Note whether: The air was calm or gusty. Wind was blowing towards or away from your location. The sound became louder or quieter as the wind changed. Strong winds can scatter and weaken sound, whereas calm conditions often allow low frequency noise to travel farther with less distortion. If several witnesses are spread across an area, comparing wind conditions can help explain why one location heard a sharp boom while another heard only a faint rumble. What Are the Mystery Booms, Hums and \"Trumpets\" in the Sky? Make This Make Sense Channel: New York Post Open on YouTube Temperature inversions One of the most important atmospheric clues is a temperature inversion, where warmer air sits above cooler surface air. Instead of allowing sound to spread upwards, an inversion bends sound waves back towards the ground. Under these conditions: distant thunder may sound unexpectedly loud; [prod 01 alb www noaa.woc.noaa.gov] prod 01 alb www noaa.woc.noaa.gov The Sound of Thunder National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration The Sound of Thunder National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration... sonic booms may be heard much farther than usual; industrial or transport noise may appear to originate from the sky. NOAA notes that inversions can significantly amplify thunder heard at ground level, while USGS also identifies atmospheric refraction as a reason sonic booms sometimes carry much farther than expected. [NOAA] prod 01 alb www noaa.woc.noaa.gov The Sound of Thunder National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration The Sound of Thunder National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration... Noting Vibration and Possible Seismic Clues A useful question is whether you merely heard the boom or also felt it. If windows rattled, shelves vibrated or the floor shook briefly, record exactly what happened. The order also matters: Did the vibration and sound occur together? Did you hear the boom first? Was there a brief jolt before the sound? These details may indicate whether the source was atmospheric or within the ground. USGS notes that very shallow earthquakes can produce audible booms because high frequency vibrations reach the surface. In some cases, people hear a boom without recognising that a small earthquake occurred, especially if the shaking is weak or highly localised. [USGS] usgs.gov Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey... Shallow earthquakes Small earthquakes close to the surface are more likely to produce audible sounds than deeper events. Witnesses commonly describe: a cannon like report; a deep rumble; a heavy thump; brief window rattling. The boom may last only a second while the vibration is subtle enough to be mistaken for a passing lorry or nearby explosion. Because many shallow earthquakes are small, they may only be noticed by people close to the source. [USGS] usgs.gov Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey... Frost quakes and cold weather cracking During severe cold weather, frozen soil and shallow groundwater can crack suddenly as ice expands. These so called frost quakes or cryoseisms may produce loud explosive sounds with little or no sustained shaking. If the boom occurred during an intense freeze, note: recent temperature changes; frozen ground; absence of storms; whether neighbours reported similar sounds. USGS identifies frost quakes as one explanation for wintertime booming sounds reported in some regions. [USGS] usgs.gov Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey Earthquake Booms, Seneca Guns, and Other Sounds U.S. Geological Survey... So Apparently Skyquakes Are A Thing Answers With Joe Channel: Joe Scott Open on YouTube Distinguishing Thunder from Isolated Booms [Thunder] psl.noaa.gov Source details in endnotes. usually leaves several clues beyond the sound itself. Typical characteristics include: repeated rumbles rather than a single report; visible storm clouds; lightning flashes, even if distant; a gradual decrease in loudness as the storm moves away. By contrast, many mysterious boom reports involve: one or two isolated detonations; clear skies; no obvious lightning; no continuing weather activity. However, clear skies alone do not rule out weather as a factor. Sound from storms beyond the visible horizon can still arrive under favourable atmospheric conditions, especially during temperature inversions. [NOAA] prod 01 alb www noaa.woc.noaa.gov The Sound of Thunder National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration The Sound of Thunder National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration... Practical Clues to Record Immediately When documenting an unexplained sky sound, weather and ground observations are as valuable as the recording itself. Useful details include: Cloud type and percentage of sky covered. Wind direction and estimated strength. Rain, hail or lightning anywhere visible. Air temperature or recent rapid temperature changes. Whether the ground, floor or furniture vibrated. Whether windows, doors or loose objects rattled. Whether nearby animals reacted before or after the sound. Any official reports of thunderstorms or minor earthquakes later that day. Separating direct observations from assumptions is especially important. Writing \"windows rattled for about one second\" is far more useful than writing \"it must have been a UFO.\" Frozen Ground Explodes: US Frost Quakes Explained WION Podcast Channel: WION Open on YouTube Why These Clues Matter Many reports of mysterious aerial booms remain unresolved because too little contextual information was recorded. Weather conditions can dramatically"},{"title":"Sound Reports","url":"/sound-reports/","description":"Witnesses report humming, buzzing, whining, crackling, roaring, whooshing, and silence, with no sound appearing consistently.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises","level":2,"text":"Sound Reports What Sounds Do UFO Witnesses Actually Report? UFO Noises Sound Reports Sound Reports What Sounds Do UFO Witnesses Actually Report? Witnesses report humming, buzzing, whining, crackling, roaring, whooshing, and silence, with no sound appearing consistently. Witnesses report humming, buzzing, whining, crackling, roaring, whooshing, and silence, with no sound appearing consistently. dataset evidence The most common sound descriptions How reports differ across witnesses Why no single pattern dominates ufo noises sound reports Introduction People have reported unusual sounds in connection with UFO sightings for decades, but there is no recognised acoustic signature that consistently distinguishes one report from another. Witnesses have described everything from faint electrical humming and buzzing to roaring, crackling, whistling and complete silence. Even within the largest historical collections of reports, no single sound appears often enough to qualify as a defining characteristic. This variability is important because it limits the evidential value of sound descriptions alone. While recurring themes do exist, they are broad and often resemble familiar environmental or mechanical noises. Investigators therefore treat reported sounds as one observational detail among many, rather than as reliable indicators of an unidentified aerial phenomenon. The UFO Phenomenon Full Documentary 2021 7NEWS Spotlight Channel: 7 News Spotlight Open on YouTube The Most Common Sound Descriptions Across historical UFO catalogues and witness databases, several broad categories recur more frequently than others. Low frequency humming: Often compared with an electrical transformer, power station, or large generator. This is one of the most frequently mentioned sounds in specialised historical datasets involving close encounters and reported physical effects. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org UFO REPORTS INVOLVING VEHICLE INTERFERENCE Center for UFO StudiesUFO REPORTSNovember 3, 2025... Published: November 3, 2025 Buzzing: Frequently likened to high voltage equipment, bees, or an electric motor. Buzzing commonly appears in reports describing apparently hovering objects. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA Whining or whirring: Witnesses sometimes compare the sound to a turbine, spinning machinery or a jet engine operating at low power rather than full thrust. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA Whooshing or swishing: Usually associated with reports in which an object appeared to move rapidly overhead or depart suddenly. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA Crackling or sizzling: Less commonly reported, these descriptions are often compared with electrical discharge or static. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA Roaring: A minority of witnesses describe loud engine like or rushing sounds, although these reports are much less common than humming or buzzing. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org UFO REPORTS INVOLVING VEHICLE INTERFERENCE Center for UFO StudiesUFO REPORTSNovember 3, 2025... Published: November 3, 2025 Silence: One of the most striking recurring observations is that many witnesses report hearing nothing at all, even when they believe the object was relatively close. BUFORA estimates that roughly four out of five reported UFOs are described as silent. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA Although these descriptions recur, they overlap heavily with the sounds produced by aircraft, helicopters, drones , industrial equipment, weather phenomena and other familiar sources. How Reports Differ Across Witnesses The diversity of reported sounds is one of the strongest patterns in the literature. Several factors contribute to this variation: Different estimated distances. An object perceived as nearby may actually be much farther away, altering how—or whether—its sound reaches the observer. Changing environmental conditions. Wind direction, humidity, terrain and urban background noise all influence what listeners hear. Different phases of an event. Some witnesses report sounds only during approach or departure, while others hear nothing during the main observation. Individual comparison language. Witnesses typically describe unfamiliar sounds by comparing them with everyday experiences, producing descriptions such as \"like a transformer\", \"like bees\", \"like a jet\", or \"like electricity\" even if they heard broadly similar noises. Because eyewitnesses rarely have instruments to measure frequency, loudness or duration, most reports rely on subjective comparisons rather than objective acoustic data. 1:17:27 Unearthly Encounters ALIEN ABDUCTION: THE STRANGEST UFO CASE FILES Beyond Belief Channel: FilmIsNow Movies · Views: 1.2M · Uploaded: May 2024 · Length: 1 hour 17 minutes Open on YouTube Why No Single Pattern Dominates The strongest conclusion from historical collections is not that one sound repeats consistently, but that no sound does . One frequently cited analysis of UFO reports involving alleged vehicle interference classified reported sounds using witnesses' own words. Humming was the largest category, accounting for 34 of 90 sound reports (38% of reported sounds), followed by whining (17%), while buzzing, whooshing, whistling, sizzling and roaring all occurred less frequently. Importantly, the researchers concluded that humming appeared in only a small minority of the total cases examined and that no single sound could be regarded as typical of UFO events . [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org UFO REPORTS INVOLVING VEHICLE INTERFERENCE Center for UFO StudiesUFO REPORTSNovember 3, 2025... Published: November 3, 2025 BUFORA reaches a similar conclusion from a broader perspective. Its guide notes that most reported UFOs are described as noiseless, while only around one in five are said to emit buzzing, humming, cracking, roaring, swishing, whirring or whining sounds. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA Taken together, these independent summaries point to the same overall pattern: reported sounds are diverse, inconsistent and often absent altogether. What Silence Tells Investigators Reports of silence deserve separate attention because they occur so frequently. Witnesses sometimes describe apparently large or fast moving objects that produce no obvious engine noise or sonic boom. This observation has encouraged speculation about unconventional propulsion, but silence alone is not considered evidence for extraordinary technology. Investigators instead consider several ordinary possibilities, including inaccurate distance estimates, atmospheric effects, wind direction, and the fact that many observations occur at night when judging scale is particularly difficult. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA BUFORAGUIDE TO UFOS BUFORA From an investigative standpoint, silence functions much like any other witness observation: it is documented carefully but interpreted cautiously alongside visual, environmental and contextual evidence. Response and Analysis of Recent UFO Disclosures from the US Military Channel: Isaac Arthur Open on YouTube What the Overall Pattern Suggests Looking across decades of reports, the common pattern is not a unique acoustic fingerprint but a limited set of recurring descriptions centred on humming, buzzing, whining and silence. These themes appear often enough to be noteworthy but not consistently enough to identify a genuine \"UFO sound.\" For that reason, researchers generally avoid treating reported noises as diagnostic evidence. Instead, sound descriptions are most useful when combined with other independently verifiable information such as radar data, multiple witnesses, photographs, environmental conditions or confirmed aircraft activity. On their own, the reported sounds remain too varied, subjective and inconsistent to define a reliable acoustic signature for unidentified aerial phenomena. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to What Sounds Do UFO Witnesses Actually Report?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The UFO Handbook: A Guide to Investigating, Evaluating, and R... By Allan Hendry An explanation of the usual reasons for confusing IFOs (identified flying objects) with UFOs and a survey of the tools used to investigat... See on Amazon Book The UFO Experience: A Scientific Inquiry By Joseph Allen Hynek Cited by the New York Review of Books as \"the best brief for visitation,\" this classic study presents an analysis of UFO reports and conc... See on Amazon Book The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects By Edward J Ruppelt The Report on Unidentified Flying Objects is a book by Edward J. Ruppelt which described the study of UFOs by United States Air Force fro... See on Amazon Book Why You Hear"},{"title":"Reported Silence","url":"/reported-silence/","description":"Reported silence can seem extraordinary, but distance errors, wind, terrain and nighttime scale confusion can all reduce audible sound.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Sound Reports","level":3,"text":"Reported Silence Why Do Nearby UFO Reports So Often Mention Silence? Sound Reports Reported Silence Reported Silence Why Do Nearby UFO Reports So Often Mention Silence? Reported silence can seem extraordinary, but distance errors, wind, terrain and nighttime scale confusion can all reduce audible sound. Reported silence can seem extraordinary, but distance errors, wind, terrain and nighttime scale confusion can all reduce audible sound. mechanism How often witnesses report silence Ordinary reasons sound may not reach an observer Why silence is not proof of unusual propulsion ufo noises sound reports reported silence Introduction One of the most frequently repeated claims in UFO reports is that an object appeared surprisingly close, moved rapidly, or hovered overhead without producing any obvious sound. In UFO literature, silence is often presented as one of the phenomenon's most striking characteristics. However, silence alone is not unusual enough to demonstrate that an object used unconventional propulsion. Acoustics, human perception , and errors in judging distance all provide well understood reasons why an apparently nearby object may seem silent. Investigators therefore treat reported silence as an observation that deserves explanation rather than as evidence in itself. It becomes meaningful only when considered alongside independently verified information about an object's distance, speed, environment and the reliability of the witness's estimates. How Often Do Witnesses Report Silence? Historical collections of UFO reports consistently show that witnesses frequently describe seeing no accompanying engine or propulsion noise. The British UFO Research Association (BUFORA), for example, notes that a large majority of reported UFOs are described as silent, making silence one of the most commonly reported acoustic characteristics in the field. That consistency is interesting because it appears across many decades and many different types of reported objects. Yet the reports vary enormously in other respects. Some witnesses describe slow moving lights, others fast moving objects, and others stationary shapes. The only common factor is often the absence of an obvious sound rather than the presence of any distinctive one. The important limitation is that these accounts almost always rely on subjective estimates of distance and size. Without independent measurements, investigators cannot determine whether an object was genuinely nearby or only appeared so. Why Apparently Nearby Objects May Produce Little or No Audible Sound Several ordinary mechanisms can make an object seem much quieter than expected. Distance Is Commonly Misjudged Human observers are poor at estimating the distance of isolated lights against a dark sky. Without familiar visual references, a small distant light can appear to be a much larger nearby object. If the object is actually several kilometres away rather than a few hundred metres away, its sound may have weakened below the listener's hearing threshold before reaching the observer. This is especially relevant at night, when depth cues are reduced and apparent size becomes a poor guide to actual range. The UFO Phenomenon Full Documentary 2021 7NEWS Spotlight Channel: 7 News Spotlight Open on YouTube Wind and Atmospheric Conditions Sound does not travel uniformly in every direction. Wind can carry sound away from an observer, while temperature layers in the atmosphere can bend sound upward or downward. Terrain, trees and buildings also absorb or block sound. These effects explain why aircraft that are plainly visible sometimes seem unexpectedly quiet, while others sound much louder than their appearance would suggest. Aviation noise specialists have long recognised that weather, flight path and atmospheric conditions strongly influence what people hear on the ground. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation Administration Aircraft Noise Federal Aviation Administration Federal Aviation AdministrationAircraft Noise Federal Aviation AdministrationJanuary 31, 2025... Published: January 31, 2025 Modern Aircraft Can Be Quieter Than People Expect Many people's expectations are shaped by older jet aircraft or helicopters. Modern aircraft, electric drones and gliders may produce considerably less noise under some conditions than observers anticipate. An aircraft travelling towards an observer at altitude may also appear visually prominent before its sound arrives, while one moving away can become difficult to hear much sooner than expected. Aircraft noise is therefore not determined solely by engine power but also by direction, altitude, atmospheric conditions and the listener's location. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation Administration Aircraft Noise Federal Aviation Administration Federal Aviation AdministrationAircraft Noise Federal Aviation AdministrationJanuary 31, 2025... Published: January 31, 2025 Background Noise Masks Weak Sounds Witnesses often recall a dramatic visual event but overlook the existing acoustic environment. Wind through trees, road traffic, rivers, insects, surf or urban noise can mask quieter sounds. Conversely, rural settings that feel \"very quiet\" are rarely acoustically silent. Low level environmental sounds can make distant engines harder to distinguish than intuition suggests. Night Time Perception Can Exaggerate the Mystery Many UFO reports occur after dark, when estimating speed, altitude and distance becomes substantially more difficult. Several perceptual effects contribute: Bright lights against a dark background often appear closer than they are. Without landmarks, observers can overestimate angular speed and underestimate distance. A slow moving distant object may appear to be a fast moving nearby one. Brief observations leave little opportunity to compare visual and auditory cues. If the observer unconsciously places the object much closer than it actually is, silence naturally seems far more surprising. Why the US government won't explain what's in our skies Reality Check Channel: NewsNation Open on YouTube Why Silence Does Not Demonstrate Unusual Propulsion It is tempting to argue that a genuinely nearby fast moving object should necessarily produce loud engine noise. That conclusion depends on assumptions that are rarely established in eyewitness reports. For silence to become strong evidence of something unusual, investigators would need reliable confirmation of several facts simultaneously: the object's true distance; its actual speed; whether it possessed any conventional propulsion system; whether atmospheric conditions favoured sound transmission; whether competing environmental noise masked quieter sounds. Eyewitness reports seldom provide enough objective information to establish all of these factors independently. Consequently, silence by itself cannot distinguish between an ordinary but misidentified object and an unidentified one. This does not mean every report is explained. Rather, it means that the reported absence of sound is not diagnostically unique. The same observation can arise through several ordinary physical and perceptual mechanisms. What Investigators Can Learn From Silent Reports Although silence is not proof of unconventional technology, it still has value as part of a complete witness account. Investigators compare reported silence with other observations, including estimated range, weather conditions, radar information, known aircraft movements and the testimony of multiple independent witnesses. When several observers at different locations report the same event, differences in what each person heard can sometimes reveal more about local acoustic conditions than about the object itself. For that reason, silence is best treated as one observational detail rather than a defining characteristic. It remains one of the most frequently reported features of UFO sightings, but its evidential weight depends on whether the surrounding circumstances support the claim that the object was genuinely close enough that audible sound would reasonably have been expected. Alien Revelations in Our Solar System Marathon Unidentified: Inside America's UFO Investigation Channel: HISTORY Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Do Nearby UFO Reports So Often Mention Silence?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The UFO Handbook: A Guide to Investigating, Evaluating, and R... By Allan Hendry An explanation of the usual reasons for confusing IFOs (identified flying objects) with UFOs and a survey of the tools used to investigat... See on Amazon Book The UFO Experience: A Scientific Inquiry By Joseph Allen Hynek Cited by the New York Review of Books as \"the best brief for visitation,\" this classic study presents an analysis of UFO reports and conc... See on Amazon Book UFOs Explained By Philip J. Klass Analyzing several UFO cases, the"},{"title":"Humming Reports","url":"/humming-reports/","description":"Humming is the largest sound category in one historical dataset, yet it still appears in only a minority of the cases examined.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Sound Reports","level":3,"text":"Humming Reports Is Humming Really the Most Common UFO Sound? Sound Reports Humming Reports Humming Reports Is Humming Really the Most Common UFO Sound? Humming is the largest sound category in one historical dataset, yet it still appears in only a minority of the cases examined. Humming is the largest sound category in one historical dataset, yet it still appears in only a minority of the cases examined. dataset evidence How witnesses describe low frequency humming What the historical sound counts actually show Why humming remains weak diagnostic evidence ufo noises sound reports humming reports Introduction Electrical or generator like humming is one of the most frequently reported sounds in historical UFO case collections, but its importance is often overstated. Within one of the best known historical datasets focused on reports involving alleged electromagnetic effects, humming forms the largest single sound category. Even so, it appears in only a minority of the relevant cases and in an even smaller fraction of the overall reports examined. The key lesson is therefore not that UFOs \"usually hum\", but that humming is simply the most common description among a highly varied set of reported sounds. That distinction matters because it limits the value of humming as evidence for identifying or classifying an unexplained aerial event. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org UFO REPORTS INVOLVING VEHICLE INTERFERENCE Center for UFO StudiesUFO REPORTSNovember 3, 2025... Published: November 3, 2025 How witnesses describe low frequency humming When witnesses report humming, they rarely describe it as a conventional engine. Instead, they typically compare it with familiar electrical or industrial sources , using phrases such as: a large electrical transformer a power station or generator high voltage equipment a deep electrical vibration a steady low frequency drone These comparisons appear repeatedly because witnesses generally describe unfamiliar sounds by analogy rather than by measured acoustic properties. Most reports contain no instrumental recordings, frequency measurements or sound pressure readings, only subjective impressions. Historical case collections also show that humming is often described as diffuse rather than directional. Some witnesses report difficulty identifying where the sound originated, saying that it seemed to fill the surrounding area instead of radiating clearly from a visible object. This characteristic appears in several close range reports but remains based on eyewitness testimony rather than objective acoustic measurement. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org UFO REPORTS INVOLVING VEHICLE INTERFERENCE Center for UFO StudiesUFO REPORTSNovember 3, 2025... Published: November 3, 2025 51:24 Breaking Down UAP Footage with the Head of The Pentagon’s UAP Taskforce, Dr. Jon Kosloski Channel: StarTalk · Views: 1.5M · Uploaded: August 2025 · Length: 51 minutes Open on YouTube What the historical sound counts actually show The strongest quantitative evidence comes from a historical analysis of UFO reports involving alleged vehicle interference and other claimed electromagnetic effects. Within that dataset: humming accounted for 34 of 90 classified sound reports (38%) whining represented about 17% buzzing and whooshing each accounted for roughly 6% roaring, sizzling and explosive sounds were less common around 17% of descriptions were too vague or unique to classify reliably. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org UFO REPORTS INVOLVING VEHICLE INTERFERENCE Center for UFO StudiesUFO REPORTSNovember 3, 2025... Published: November 3, 2025 The important qualification is found in the study's own interpretation. Although humming was the largest category among reported sounds, it occurred in only a small proportion of the entire collection of electromagnetic effect cases. The authors explicitly concluded that no single sound could be regarded as a typical characteristic of a UFO event . [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org UFO REPORTS INVOLVING VEHICLE INTERFERENCE Center for UFO StudiesUFO REPORTSNovember 3, 2025... Published: November 3, 2025 Independent reviews point in the same direction. The UK Ministry of Defence's Project Condign found that reports including any sound were themselves uncommon, and that among unidentified cases the reported sounds were usually described as humming, whining or crackling. Even so, these represented only a very small percentage of the total reports analysed. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com UFO TransparencyProject Condign, Unidentified Aerial Phenomena in the UK Air Defence Region, Volume 1 (Executive Summary, Chapters 1–5, A... Taken together, these studies suggest that humming is best understood as the leading category within a relatively rare subset of sightings rather than as a defining feature of UFO reports generally. Why humming appears so often in witness accounts Several factors may explain why electrical humming recurs in eyewitness descriptions. First, low frequency sounds are difficult to describe precisely. People naturally compare unfamiliar noises with common experiences, and electrical transformers, generators and industrial machinery provide readily recognised reference points. Second, many reports involving humming also include claims of electrical interference with vehicles, radios or lights. Whether those accompanying observations have ordinary explanations or remain unexplained, the presence of both themes encourages witnesses to frame the sound as electrical in character. Historical investigators noted this association but did not establish a causal relationship between the reported sound and the alleged interference. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org UFO REPORTS INVOLVING VEHICLE INTERFERENCE Center for UFO StudiesUFO REPORTSNovember 3, 2025... Published: November 3, 2025 Third, environmental acoustics can alter how sounds are perceived. Wind, terrain, atmospheric conditions and background noise can transform distant aircraft, helicopters, power infrastructure or industrial equipment into a broad humming sound that is difficult to localise. Because nearly all historical reports lack acoustic recordings, investigators cannot reliably distinguish between these possibilities after the fact. Listening for UFOs The Galileo Project's Search for UAP with Andy Mead Merged EP0115 Channel: Merged Podcast Open on YouTube Why humming remains weak diagnostic evidence Despite its prominence in historical catalogues, humming has limited value as a diagnostic feature. Several reasons explain why: It is not unique. Electrical substations, transformers, industrial machinery, wind turbines, drones and some aircraft can all produce similar low frequency sounds. Many reported UFOs are silent. Several major catalogues contain large numbers of reports in which witnesses heard nothing at all, even when they believed an object was nearby. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com UFO TransparencyProject Condign, Unidentified Aerial Phenomena in the UK Air Defence Region, Volume 1 (Executive Summary, Chapters 1–5, A... Descriptions are subjective. Terms such as \"generator like\", \"electrical\" or \"buzzing\" reflect personal comparisons rather than measured acoustic characteristics. The largest dataset still shows substantial variation. Even in a collection specifically focused on reports with alleged physical effects, humming accounts for well under half of the classified sound descriptions. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org UFO REPORTS INVOLVING VEHICLE INTERFERENCE Center for UFO StudiesUFO REPORTSNovember 3, 2025... Published: November 3, 2025 For these reasons, investigators generally treat reported humming as one observational detail that may help reconstruct a witness's experience but not as evidence that an event belongs to a distinct class of unidentified aerial phenomena. What humming reports ultimately tell us Historical evidence supports a narrower conclusion than popular culture often suggests. Electrical humming is indeed the most frequently reported sound in one influential dataset of UFO cases involving alleged electromagnetic effects, but it remains a minority characteristic rather than a universal one. Most sightings include no distinctive acoustic signature, and many include no reported sound at all. The recurring image of a transformer like hum is therefore best understood as a pattern within eyewitness testimony rather than a verified acoustic fingerprint. It highlights how people consistently describe unusual experiences using familiar comparisons while also illustrating the limits of relying on subjective sound reports as evidence for identifying unexplained aerial events. [cufos.org] cufos.org UFO REPORTS INVOLVING VEHICLE INTERFERENCE Center for UFO StudiesUFO REPORTSNovember 3, 2025... Published: November 3, 2025 Searching for Solid Evidence of UAPs Dr. Massimo Teodorani, Galileo Project Channel: The DemystifySci Podcast Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading"},{"title":"Movement Sounds","url":"/movement-sounds/","description":"Whooshing, whining and roaring are often linked to movement phases, while the same object may be reported as quiet when hovering.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Sound Reports","level":3,"text":"Movement Sounds Do UFO Sounds Change When Objects Start Moving? Sound Reports Movement Sounds Movement Sounds Do UFO Sounds Change When Objects Start Moving? Whooshing, whining and roaring are often linked to movement phases, while the same object may be reported as quiet when hovering. Whooshing, whining and roaring are often linked to movement phases, while the same object may be reported as quiet when hovering. event time window Sounds reported during approach Noise changes during hovering and departure How motion and distance complicate witness accounts ufo noises sound reports movement sounds Introduction Witnesses who report hearing sounds during UFO sightings often describe those sounds as changing with the apparent movement of the object rather than remaining constant throughout the event. A recurring pattern across historical case collections is that an object may be reported as silent while hovering, then produce a whooshing, whining, humming or roaring sound as it approaches or departs. Although no single acoustic pattern has been established, the timing of reported sounds is one of the more consistent themes in eyewitness accounts. Investigators therefore pay attention not only to what a witness heard, but also when the sound occurred during the observation. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Vehicle interference case compendium Center for UFO StudiesAN UPDATE ON VEHICLEOctober 31, 2025... Published: October 31, 2025 This pattern does not demonstrate a specific propulsion system or confirm that the sound originated from the observed object. Instead, it highlights how movement, changing distance, atmospheric conditions and human perception can all influence what witnesses report. Sounds Reported During Approach Many reports describe the first audible indication of an unidentified object as it comes closer. Rather than an immediate loud noise, witnesses often describe a gradual increase in sound, using comparisons such as: A low electrical hum becoming more noticeable. A rising whine similar to a turbine winding up. A rushing or whooshing sound accompanying rapid movement overhead. An engine like roar that fades as the object slows or changes direction. These descriptions are not unique to UFO reports. Similar changes naturally occur with conventional aircraft, helicopters and drones as their apparent distance and speed change. Consequently, investigators treat movement related sounds as descriptive observations rather than diagnostic evidence. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Vehicle interference case compendium Center for UFO StudiesAN UPDATE ON VEHICLEOctober 31, 2025... Published: October 31, 2025 Some historical close encounter reports illustrate this sequence. In one vehicle interference case preserved by the Center for UFO Studies (CUFOS), a witness reported first hearing a loud swishing sound before observing a domed disc overhead, followed by a loud humming noise while the object remained nearby. Once the object departed, both the sound and the reported vehicle malfunction ended. Such accounts demonstrate that witnesses often divide an event into distinct acoustic phases rather than describing one continuous sound. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Vehicle interference case compendium Center for UFO StudiesAN UPDATE ON VEHICLEOctober 31, 2025... Published: October 31, 2025 Listening for UFOs The Galileo Project's Search for UAP with Andy Mead Merged EP0115 Channel: Merged Podcast Open on YouTube Noise Changes During Hovering and Departure One of the more striking patterns in UFO literature is the contrast between motion and apparent stillness. Historical British UFO Research Association (BUFORA) guidance notes that many reported UFOs are described as completely silent, even when observers believe they are relatively close. At the same time, reports that do include sound frequently associate it with movement rather than prolonged hovering. Earlier summaries of BUFORA case material describe whooshing and rushing sounds as being more common during rapid travel than during stationary observation. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAABOUT BUFORA BUFORAABOUT BUFORA Recent analyses of selected historical UAP reports also found repeated references to apparently silent hovering objects. A 2023 review of 301 carefully selected reports identified multiple cases in which witnesses described objects hovering or travelling unusually while explicitly noting an absence of sound. Triangle shaped objects, in particular, were frequently reported as hovering quietly. [DOI.org] doi.org , 1947 2016 ZenodoDecember 7, 2023... Published: December 7, 2023 Conversely, departures are often described as the loudest stage of an event. Witnesses have reported: A sudden rushing or swishing sound as an object accelerates away. A brief rise in humming or whining immediately before departure. A rapidly diminishing roar that disappears as the object recedes. A return to complete silence once the object is no longer visible. Not every report follows this sequence, but the arrival–hover–departure pattern appears repeatedly enough that investigators recognise it as a recurring narrative structure in eyewitness testimony. How Motion and Distance Complicate Witness Accounts The apparent relationship between movement and sound does not necessarily reflect changes in the object itself. Several ordinary factors can alter what a witness hears. Changing Distance Sound intensity decreases rapidly with distance. An object approaching an observer naturally becomes easier to hear, while the same object moving away becomes progressively quieter. If the true distance has been misjudged—a common issue in aerial observations—the perceived change in sound may be attributed to the wrong cause. UAP Flight Characteristics: The Five Observables Kevin Knuth Channel: Tim Ventura Open on YouTube Atmospheric Conditions Wind direction, temperature layers, humidity and surrounding terrain all affect how sound travels . Under some conditions, noise may seem to appear suddenly after travelling through the atmosphere, while under others it may be weakened or masked altogether. These environmental effects can make an object's acoustic behaviour appear inconsistent even if its sound output remains unchanged. Human Perception During Dynamic Events Witnesses often remember unusual moments more vividly than steady ones. A dramatic approach or sudden departure may therefore receive greater attention than a quieter hovering phase. In addition, unfamiliar sounds are usually described through analogy—\"like a jet\", \"like electricity\", \"like rushing air\"—rather than through objective measurements, making comparisons between reports inherently subjective. [Cambridge University Press] cambridge.org Cambridge University PressPsychological aspects in unidentified anomalous phenomena (UAP) witnesses International Journal of Astrobiolo... Why the Pattern Remains Difficult to Interpret Although reports frequently associate whooshing, whining and roaring with approach or departure, no recognised acoustic signature has emerged across UFO databases. Similar descriptions occur in reports ultimately attributed to conventional aircraft, helicopters, drones, atmospheric effects and other familiar sources. Equally important are the many reports in which witnesses describe no sound at all. Recent government released UAP case summaries also include observations of apparently hovering luminous objects accompanied by explicit statements that no sound was heard, illustrating that silence remains a common element in modern reports as well as historical ones. [AARO] aaro.mil Next AARO UAP Report DocumentsMay 8, 2026... Published: May 8, 2026 For that reason, investigators generally view changes in reported sound as supporting context rather than independent evidence. The timing of a sound—whether it occurs during approach, hovering or departure—can help reconstruct a witness's account, but it does not by itself distinguish an unidentified object from more ordinary explanations. Searching for Solid Evidence of UAPs Dr. Massimo Teodorani, Galileo Project Channel: The DemystifySci Podcast Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Do UFO Sounds Change When Objects Start Moving?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The UFO Handbook: A Guide to Investigating, Evaluating, and R... By Allan Hendry An explanation of the usual reasons for confusing IFOs (identified flying objects) with UFOs and a survey of the tools used to investigat... See on Amazon Book The UFO Experience: A Scientific Inquiry By Joseph Allen Hynek Cited by the New York Review of Books as \"the best brief for visitation,\" this classic study presents an analysis of UFO reports and conc... See on Amazon Book Fundamentals of Acoustics By Lawrence E. Kinsler, Austin R. Frey et al. Dargestellt werden die physikalischen und mathematischen Konzepte zur Beschreibung der"},{"title":"Witness Language","url":"/witness-language/","description":"People translate unfamiliar sounds into familiar comparisons such as bees, transformers, turbines or electricity, creating wide variation in reports.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Sound Reports","level":3,"text":"Witness Language Why Can the Same UFO Sound Get Different Descriptions? Sound Reports Witness Language Witness Language Why Can the Same UFO Sound Get Different Descriptions? People translate unfamiliar sounds into familiar comparisons such as bees, transformers, turbines or electricity, creating wide variation in reports. People translate unfamiliar sounds into familiar comparisons such as bees, transformers, turbines or electricity, creating wide variation in reports. population context Everyday comparisons used by witnesses How memory and expectation shape wording Limits of reports without acoustic measurements ufo noises sound reports witness language Introduction People who report unusual UFO sounds often seem to disagree about what they heard, even when describing what may have been the same kind of event. One witness might recall a sound \"like bees\", another \"like a transformer\", and another \"like an electric motor\". This variation does not necessarily mean they heard completely different noises. Instead, it reflects how people describe unfamiliar sounds using familiar experiences, how memory reconstructs unusual events, and the fact that almost no reports include objective acoustic measurements. Within historical UFO case collections, investigators have long noted that witnesses rely on analogies rather than technical descriptions. That makes sound reports valuable as personal observations, but less reliable as precise evidence for identifying a distinctive \"UFO noise\". The wording often reveals as much about human perception and language as it does about the sound itself. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAINVESTIGATIONS BUFORA BUFORAINVESTIGATIONS BUFORA Why unfamiliar sounds become familiar comparisons Humans rarely describe a sound by its exact frequency, pitch or acoustic spectrum. Instead, they compare it with something they already know. This is a normal feature of perception rather than something unique to UFO reports. When witnesses encounter an unfamiliar noise, they often reach for comparisons such as: Bees or insects, to convey a steady buzzing. An electrical transformer, to suggest a low continuous hum. A turbine or jet engine, to indicate a rising mechanical whine. High voltage electricity, to describe crackling or sizzling. A distant generator or industrial machinery, to communicate a persistent low frequency vibration. These comparisons are not technical measurements. They are metaphors intended to help another person imagine an unfamiliar sound. Two listeners could hear broadly similar acoustic characteristics yet choose different comparisons because their personal experiences differ. Someone familiar with industrial equipment may think of electrical substations, while another person with little exposure to heavy machinery may think instead of insects or household appliances. Investigators from organisations such as BUFORA have repeatedly noted that witnesses often explain unusual experiences by translating them into familiar language rather than reporting measurable acoustic properties. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAINVESTIGATIONS BUFORA BUFORAINVESTIGATIONS BUFORA These Illusions Fool Almost Everyone Channel: Veritasium Open on YouTube Vocabulary shapes what gets reported A person's background strongly influences the labels they choose. For example: An engineer may describe a \"50 Hz transformer hum \". A pilot may compare the same impression to an aircraft auxiliary power unit. Someone living in the countryside may think of bees or power lines. Another witness may simply report \"a strange vibration\" because no familiar comparison seems adequate. This does not automatically imply different underlying sounds. It illustrates how language compresses complex sensory experiences into everyday references that other people are likely to understand. How memory and expectation influence wording Descriptions are also affected by how memory reconstructs unusual events. Psychological research shows that memory is not a perfect recording. People actively reconstruct experiences using prior knowledge, expectations and existing mental categories. For unfamiliar sounds, listeners naturally fit what they heard into the closest pattern already stored in memory. Research on auditory expectation demonstrates that predictability and prior experience influence both how sounds are perceived and how well they are remembered. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Information Theoretic Properties of Auditory Sequences Dynamically Influence Expectation and Memory PubMedJanuary 25, 2017... Published: January 25, 2017 In eyewitness research, another relevant finding is the role of verbalisation. Translating an unusual sensory experience into words can subtly reshape later recall, because language often captures only selected features while omitting others. Studies of \"verbal overshadowing\" show that describing complex sensory experiences may alter subsequent memory rather than preserving it exactly. Although most research concerns faces and voices rather than unexplained aerial events, the underlying principle highlights why later descriptions should not be treated as exact acoustic records. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Eyewitness memory Eyewitness memory Expectation can also influence which aspects of a sound become most memorable. A witness who believes an object resembles advanced technology may focus on electrical qualities, while someone expecting conventional aircraft may remember engine like characteristics. These influences affect wording without necessarily implying deliberate exaggeration or fabrication. YouTube Audio Visual Illusion in Speech Perception: McGurk Effect Psychology 101 Audio Visual Illusion in Speech Perception: McGurk Effect Psychology 101 Open on YouTube Why reports rarely agree in precise acoustic terms Unlike scientific acoustic investigations, almost all UFO reports are based entirely on human hearing. Witnesses seldom record: Frequency or frequency range. Sound pressure level (volume). [silicon steels.com] silicon steels.com March 27, 2026 — A COMPREHENSIVE GUIDE TO IDENTIFYING AND TROUBLESHOOTING TRANSFORMER FAULTS USING SOUND 27 Mar 2026 Reading Volume: In... Published: March 27, 2026 Duration measured by a clock. Directional measurements. Spectral analysis. Changes over time. [pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Source details in endnotes. Without these measurements, investigators cannot determine whether two reported humming sounds were objectively similar or only appeared similar after witnesses chose comparable words. Environmental conditions further complicate interpretation. Wind, echoes from buildings or hillsides, background traffic, vegetation and atmospheric conditions all modify how sound reaches different listeners. Even people standing only a short distance apart may notice different elements of the same acoustic event. For that reason, investigators generally treat reported sounds as supporting observations rather than diagnostic evidence. The absence of instrumental acoustic data means comparisons between cases remain largely qualitative rather than quantitative. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAINVESTIGATIONS BUFORA BUFORAINVESTIGATIONS BUFORA YouTube Psychoacoustics: Why do you hear sounds that don't exist? Hearing Psychoacoustics: Why do you hear sounds that don't exist? Hearing Open on YouTube What this means for comparing UFO sound reports The wide variety of sound descriptions does not necessarily indicate that witnesses experienced completely different phenomena. Nor does it demonstrate that a unique acoustic signature exists. Instead, the historical record suggests three consistent patterns: Witnesses usually describe unfamiliar sounds by comparing them with familiar objects or technologies. Individual memory, vocabulary and expectations influence which comparison they choose. Without objective acoustic measurements, different verbal descriptions cannot reliably be interpreted as evidence of different underlying sounds. This helps explain why one historical catalogue may contain references to humming, buzzing, turbines, electricity and bees without implying several distinct categories of UFO noise. Many descriptions may simply represent different linguistic attempts to communicate broadly similar auditory impressions. Consequently, researchers examining reported UFO sounds generally place greater weight on patterns across many independent reports than on the exact wording used by any individual witness. The comparisons themselves remain informative, but they are best understood as human descriptions of unusual listening experiences rather than precise acoustic measurements. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Can the Same UFO Sound Get Different Descriptions?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The UFO Handbook: A Guide to Investigating, Evaluating, and R... By Allan Hendry An explanation of the usual reasons for"},{"title":"Sound Explanations","url":"/sound-explanations/","description":"Aircraft, drones, weather and industrial machinery can mimic reported UFO sounds, so investigators compare acoustics with location and context.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Sound Reports","level":3,"text":"Sound Explanations Could a Familiar Source Explain That UFO Noise? Sound Reports Sound Explanations Sound Explanations Could a Familiar Source Explain That UFO Noise? Aircraft, drones, weather and industrial machinery can mimic reported UFO sounds, so investigators compare acoustics with location and context. Aircraft, drones, weather and industrial machinery can mimic reported UFO sounds, so investigators compare acoustics with location and context. policy intervention Common aircraft and mechanical lookalikes Environmental effects that alter perceived sound Evidence needed to rule out ordinary sources ufo noises sound reports sound explanations Introduction Many reported UFO sounds can be matched to ordinary sources once investigators examine the event in context. A humming noise may come from a distant aircraft, a buzzing sound from a drone, or a low frequency rumble from industrial machinery carried unusually far by atmospheric conditions. For this reason, experienced investigators rarely treat sound descriptions on their own as persuasive evidence. Instead, they compare the reported noise with aircraft movements, weather records, terrain, local infrastructure and other observations before deciding whether an event remains unexplained. This approach does not assume that every report has a mundane explanation, but it recognises that human hearing is easily influenced by distance, environment and expectation, making careful elimination of familiar sources an essential first step. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Real time identification of aircraft sound events PubMed Central (PMC)Real time identification of aircraft sound events PMCSeptember 10, 2020... Published: September 10, 2020 Could a Familiar Source Explain That UFO Noise? The first question investigators ask is not \"What made the sound?\" but \"What ordinary sources were present at that place and time?\" Unlike visual sightings, sounds can travel around obstacles, reflect from buildings or hills, and arrive from a different direction than their source. A witness may therefore hear an aircraft without seeing it, or hear machinery that appears much closer than it really is. This makes location, timing and environmental conditions as important as the sound itself. [NOAA Institutional Repository] repository.library.noaa.gov NOAA Institutional RepositoryTheory and application of a Radio Acoustic Sounding System... A typical investigation compares the reported characteristics against known sound sources rather than relying on subjective descriptions such as \"electrical\", \"metallic\" or \"otherworldly\". Common Aircraft and Mechanical Lookalikes Aircraft are among the most common explanations considered because their sounds change dramatically with altitude, engine power and atmospheric conditions. Investigators typically check: Commercial aircraft operating along scheduled air routes. High altitude jets may appear visually distant while engine noise reaches observers several seconds later. Helicopters , whose blade slap can resemble rhythmic beating, humming or pulsing noises. Military aircraft , which may follow irregular routes or produce unfamiliar engine sounds. Small propeller aircraft , especially during climbing turns or low altitude operations. Drones , whose electric motors often produce high pitched buzzing or whining similar to many witness descriptions. Today, investigators frequently compare reports with ADS B (Automatic Dependent Surveillance–Broadcast) flight tracking data, airport activity and air traffic records. Modern acoustic research also combines aircraft position data with recorded sound to identify whether particular aircraft produced measured noise, demonstrating how synchronising flight information with acoustics can distinguish aircraft from unrelated environmental sounds. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Real time identification of aircraft sound events PubMed Central (PMC)Real time identification of aircraft sound events PMCSeptember 10, 2020... Published: September 10, 2020 However, flight tracking tools are not perfect. Not every aircraft broadcasts publicly available ADS B data, some military flights are intentionally absent from public tracking services, and coverage varies by region. Consequently, the absence of a tracked aircraft is not considered proof that no aircraft were present. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation Administration ADS B Privacy Federal Aviation Administration Federal Aviation AdministrationADS B Privacy Federal Aviation AdministrationMarch 22, 2025... Published: March 22, 2025 Beyond aviation, investigators also examine nearby: electrical substations and transformers, wind turbines, factories and processing plants, railways, heavy road traffic, construction equipment, agricultural machinery. Many produce continuous low frequency sounds that witnesses later describe as mysterious humming or vibration. What is a Sonic Boom and Why Do They Happen? Channel: On The Shoulders of Science Open on YouTube How Weather Changes What People Hear Environmental conditions can make ordinary sounds seem extraordinary. Temperature inversions—layers of warm air above cooler air—can bend sound waves back towards the ground, allowing aircraft or industrial noise to travel much farther than normal. Humidity, wind direction and atmospheric stability also influence how loudly and clearly distant sounds are perceived. These well established acoustic effects are one reason investigators obtain weather observations alongside witness testimony. [NOAA Institutional Repository] repository.library.noaa.gov NOAA Institutional RepositoryTheory and application of a Radio Acoustic Sounding System... Terrain also matters. Valleys can channel sound over long distances, while cliffs, forests and urban buildings reflect or scatter it. A witness may therefore perceive a sound as stationary when the source is moving, or believe it originated overhead when reflections altered its apparent direction. Because of these effects, investigators avoid assuming that perceived direction accurately identifies the sound source. Why Witness Descriptions Alone Are Not Enough Most reports describe sounds through comparison rather than measurement: \"like a transformer\"; \"like bees\"; \"like a jet\"; \"like electricity\"; \"like an enormous fan.\" These comparisons are understandable but subjective. Two witnesses hearing the same sound may choose completely different analogies depending on personal experience. Investigators therefore focus on measurable features where possible: approximate duration, whether the pitch changed, whether loudness increased or faded, whether the sound appeared to move, whether multiple witnesses independently described similar characteristics, whether recordings exist. Objective information generally carries greater evidential value than descriptive labels. What Evidence Helps Rule Out Ordinary Sources? The strongest investigations combine several independent lines of evidence instead of relying on memory alone. Useful information includes: Accurate time and location , allowing comparison with aircraft movements and weather records. Multiple independent witnesses , particularly if they were separated geographically. Audio or video recordings , which permit spectral or timing analysis. Meteorological observations , including wind, cloud cover and temperature profiles. Information about nearby infrastructure , such as airports, industrial sites or railway lines. Visual observations , showing whether a visible object's movement matches the reported sound. Modern proposals for scientific UAP research go further by recommending integrated sensor networks that combine optical cameras, microphones, environmental sensors, radar and radio receivers. Using several independent instruments makes it easier to recognise ordinary aircraft, environmental noise or recording artefacts before classifying an event as anomalous. Acoustic Scene Analysis, Complex Modulations, and a New Form of Filtering – Les Atlas (UW) 2008 Channel: Center for Language & Speech Processing(CLSP), JHU Open on YouTube When Does a Sound Remain Unexplained? After familiar explanations are examined, some reports may still lack an obvious source. That does not necessarily imply an extraordinary cause. An unexplained sound usually means only that available evidence is insufficient to identify it confidently. Missing timing information, incomplete flight records, lack of recordings or uncertain witness estimates often prevent a definitive conclusion. Investigators therefore distinguish between unidentified and unexplainable . A sound can remain unidentified because the evidence is incomplete, not because known physical explanations have been ruled out. This cautious distinction reflects the broader approach used in serious investigations of reported UFO noises : eliminate the ordinary explanations that can be tested,"},{"title":"Silent Sightings","url":"/silent-sightings/","description":"Many apparently silent UFOs may be farther away, smaller, or differently positioned than witnesses initially estimate.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises","level":2,"text":"Silent Sightings Why Do So Many UFO Reports Describe Silence? UFO Noises Silent Sightings Silent Sightings Why Do So Many UFO Reports Describe Silence? Many apparently silent UFOs may be farther away, smaller, or differently positioned than witnesses initially estimate. Many apparently silent UFOs may be farther away, smaller, or differently positioned than witnesses initially estimate. mechanism How distance changes apparent sound Nighttime size and altitude errors Aircraft, balloons, and quiet viewing angles ufo noises silent sightings Introduction One of the most striking features of many reported UFO or unidentified aerial phenomenon (UAP) encounters is not an unusual sound, but the complete absence of one. Witnesses frequently describe objects that appear close enough to hear, moving at speeds or performing manoeuvres that would normally produce engine noise, rotor wash, or sonic booms—yet they report silence. This apparent contradiction has become known as the silent UFO paradox : if an object is genuinely nearby and moving as observed, why is it not audible? Within the broader subject of UFO noises , silent sightings are especially important because they challenge expectations rather than reinforce them. However, they do not by themselves demonstrate exotic technology. Modern UAP investigations have not identified a verified acoustic signature associated with unexplained aerial objects, and government reviews continue to conclude that silence alone is not evidence of an extraordinary origin. [U.S. Department of War] defense.gov U.S. Department of WarDOD Report Discounts Sightings of Extraterrestrial Technology U.S. Department of Defense Defense Department New... (https://www.defense.gov/News/News Stories/Article/Article/3701297/dod report discounts sightings of extraterrestrial technology/) Why silence seems so unusual People intuitively associate flight with sound. Helicopters generate distinctive rotor noise, propeller aircraft create a persistent engine note, and jet aircraft produce both engine roar and, at high speed, sonic booms. Even relatively quiet electric drones emit audible propeller sounds at modest distances. Against this background, reports of apparently silent objects stand out because witnesses often claim one or more of the following: The object appeared low overhead. It seemed large enough that conventional aircraft would have been clearly audible. It accelerated or changed direction abruptly. It departed without any increase in sound. The paradox therefore depends on the witness's estimate of distance, size and speed. If any of those estimates are inaccurate—as can easily happen when observing an unfamiliar object against a dark or featureless sky—the expectation of noise may also be incorrect. Why eyewitnesses may hear nothing Silence has several possible explanations that do not require an unconventional aircraft. Distance is often misjudged Without reliable visual references, people frequently underestimate how far away lights in the sky actually are. A bright aircraft, satellite flare, atmospheric phenomenon or drone may appear much closer than it really is. If an object is kilometres away rather than hundreds of metres away, little or no sound may reach an observer. Sound and light do not arrive together Light reaches an observer almost instantly, whereas sound travels through air at roughly 343 metres per second under normal conditions. An aircraft producing a loud sound may therefore be seen first, with the sound arriving several seconds later. If the object changes direction, disappears behind terrain or leaves the observer's field of view before the sound arrives, the experience may be remembered as silent. Atmospheric conditions can suppress sound Temperature inversions, wind direction, humidity and terrain can all affect how sound propagates. Under some conditions, aircraft noise is carried away from an observer rather than towards them. In others, sound is refracted, producing unexpectedly quiet conditions nearby while remaining audible much farther away. These well established acoustic effects help explain why observers sometimes report unusually quiet aircraft or explosions. 1:02:46 UAP Independent Study Event Post Meeting Media Teleconference (May 31, 2023) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 30.3K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 1 hour 2 minutes Open on YouTube Expectations influence perception Witnesses concentrating on an unexpected visual event may not notice background sounds or may later remember the event differently. Cognitive psychology has shown that memory reconstructs experiences rather than replaying them exactly, particularly during surprising or emotionally significant events. This does not imply that witnesses are dishonest. Instead, it illustrates why silence should be treated as one observational detail rather than a decisive physical measurement. Silent sightings in historical UFO reports Silence has been reported throughout the modern history of UFO claims, but it has never been universal. Many classic case files include descriptions such as: Disc shaped objects hovering without audible engines. Triangular objects gliding silently overhead. Bright lights moving steadily with no apparent sound. Objects disappearing rapidly without sonic booms. At the same time, numerous other reports describe humming, buzzing, crackling or low frequency vibration. Even historical UFO researchers noted that the \"silent UFO\" image became so influential that investigators sometimes gave less attention to cases involving reported sounds, despite both types appearing in witness testimony. [Nicap] nicap.org section 8 section 8January 1, 1964... Published: January 1, 1964 The historical record therefore contains inconsistency rather than a single recurring acoustic pattern. Modern UAP investigations and the silence question Recent government investigations illustrate an important distinction between witness description and investigative conclusion . Some cases published by the U.S. All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) include eyewitness statements that observed lights or orbs were \"silent\" or that no sound was heard during prolonged observations. These descriptions are preserved because they form part of the original witness accounts . [AARO] aaro.mil Next AARO UAP Report DocumentsMay 8, 2026... Published: May 8, 2026 However, AARO does not treat silence as evidence of advanced propulsion or non human technology. Instead, reports are analysed alongside radar information, infrared imagery, weather conditions, aircraft activity and other sensor data where available. The office has repeatedly stated that unresolved cases generally reflect insufficient information rather than evidence for extraterrestrial craft. [U.S. Department of War] defense.gov U.S. Department of WarDOD Report Discounts Sightings of Extraterrestrial Technology U.S. Department of Defense Defense Department New... (https://www.defense.gov/News/News Stories/Article/Article/3701297/dod report discounts sightings of extraterrestrial technology/) This distinction is central to understanding the paradox: silence is documented as a reported characteristic, but not as a validated diagnostic feature. Why the paradox remains unresolved The silent UFO paradox persists because two competing interpretations remain possible in many individual cases. One interpretation argues that genuinely nearby objects performing extraordinary manoeuvres without sound would imply propulsion unlike conventional aviation. The other argues that the apparent contradiction disappears if one or more assumptions are wrong—for example: the object was much farther away than believed; its speed was overestimated; it was an ordinary aircraft viewed under unusual atmospheric conditions; the witness observed an optical or atmospheric phenomenon rather than a physical craft. Without independent measurements of range, velocity and acoustic environment, these competing explanations are difficult to separate. 59:23 Replay! NASA's Release of the Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena Report Channel: VideoFromSpace · Views: 71.9K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 59 minutes Open on YouTube What silence can and cannot tell us Silence is an interesting observational feature, but by itself it has limited evidential value. It can indicate that: the observer perceived no audible engine or propulsion noise; environmental conditions may have affected sound transmission; the event differs from expectations for nearby conventional aircraft. It cannot , on its own, establish that: an object was using unknown propulsion; the estimated distance or speed was correct; the object was extraterrestrial or technologically exotic. Scientific investigations place greatest weight on multiple independent sources of evidence—such as calibrated sensors, radar, imagery and corroborating observations—rather than on any single reported characteristic, including silence. [U.S. Department of War]"},{"title":"Sound Delay","url":"/sound-delay/","description":"A fast or high aircraft can be visible well before its sound arrives, creating a brief but convincing impression of complete silence.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Silent Sightings","level":3,"text":"Sound Delay Can Aircraft Noise Arrive After the UFO Passes? Silent Sightings Sound Delay Sound Delay Can Aircraft Noise Arrive After the UFO Passes? A fast or high aircraft can be visible well before its sound arrives, creating a brief but convincing impression of complete silence. A fast or high aircraft can be visible well before its sound arrives, creating a brief but convincing impression of complete silence. mechanism How sound travel time creates a delay When altitude and speed make the gap noticeable Why witnesses may fail to link the later noise ufo noises silent sightings sound delay Introduction A witness who sees an aircraft but hears nothing at first may understandably conclude that the object is unusually quiet or even silent. In many cases, however, the apparent silence is a consequence of timing rather than an absence of sound. Light reaches the observer almost instantaneously, while sound travels through air at only about 343 metres per second under typical conditions. As a result, an aircraft can be seen well before its engine noise arrives, especially if it is distant, flying at altitude or moving quickly. This delay can briefly create the impression of a silent unidentified object, and when the sound finally arrives it may no longer seem connected to what was seen. This mechanism is a well understood aspect of acoustics and is an important conventional explanation when evaluating reports of apparently silent UFOs. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation AdministrationFundamentals of Noise and Sound Federal Aviation AdministrationMarch 29, 2022... Published: March 29, 2022 Can Aircraft Noise Arrive After the UFO Passes? Yes. The delay between seeing an aircraft and hearing it is a normal consequence of the different speeds of light and sound. For nearby, low flying aircraft the gap is often so short that people perceive the sight and sound as occurring together. As the distance increases, however, the delay becomes increasingly noticeable. An aircraft several kilometres away may be visible for many seconds before its engine noise reaches the observer. During that interval, the object can appear to move silently across the sky. This effect becomes especially persuasive when there are few visual references. If an observer believes an aircraft is only a few hundred metres away when it is actually several kilometres distant, the lack of immediate engine noise can seem extraordinary rather than expected. How Sound Travel Time Creates a Delay The mechanism is straightforward. Electromagnetic radiation, including visible light, effectively reaches human observers instantly over atmospheric distances. Sound must physically travel through the air, making its arrival much slower. Approximate delays illustrate the effect: At 1 kilometre, sound takes roughly 3 seconds to arrive. At 3 kilometres, the delay is close to 9 seconds. At 5 kilometres, the delay approaches 15 seconds. At 10 kilometres, the delay can exceed 29 seconds. These values vary slightly with air temperature because the speed of sound changes with atmospheric conditions, but the principle remains the same. A witness may therefore watch an aircraft cross a substantial portion of the sky before hearing the engines. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation AdministrationFundamentals of Noise and Sound Federal Aviation AdministrationMarch 29, 2022... Published: March 29, 2022 5:44 The sonic boom problem Katerina Kaouri Channel: TED Ed · Views: 5.1M · Uploaded: February 2015 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube When Altitude and Speed Make the Gap Noticeable Several factors can lengthen the perceived separation between sight and sound. High Altitude Aircraft cruising at several thousand metres altitude are physically much farther away than many observers realise. Even if they appear directly overhead, the sound must travel the full slant distance through the atmosphere before reaching the ground. Because engine noise also weakens with distance, the arriving sound may be much quieter than expected, making it easier to overlook or dismiss. Modern commercial aircraft are also significantly quieter than earlier generations, further reducing the chance that observers immediately associate a delayed sound with the aircraft they saw. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation Administration Aircraft Noise Federal Aviation Administration Federal Aviation AdministrationAircraft Noise Federal Aviation AdministrationJanuary 31, 2025... Published: January 31, 2025 Fast Motion A fast moving aircraft changes position while its earlier produced sound is still travelling towards the observer. Imagine an aircraft travelling several hundred kilometres per hour. By the time engine noise produced at one point reaches the ground, the aircraft itself may already have travelled a considerable distance. The observer therefore sees the aircraft in one location while hearing sound generated when it occupied another. This separation can make the noise seem to come from the wrong direction or appear unrelated to the visible object. Wind and Atmospheric Conditions Wind does not simply make sounds louder or quieter; it can also influence how efficiently sound reaches different locations. A tailwind carrying sound towards an observer may reduce the apparent delay, while wind blowing the opposite way can increase it or weaken the sound enough that it becomes difficult to notice. Temperature gradients and atmospheric layering can also refract sound, bending acoustic waves upward or downward and changing where engine noise is most easily heard. These effects can reinforce the impression that an otherwise ordinary aircraft is unexpectedly silent. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation AdministrationFundamentals of Noise and Sound Federal Aviation AdministrationMarch 29, 2022... Published: March 29, 2022 Measuring the Speed of Sound GCSE Physics Channel: Physics Online Open on YouTube Why Witnesses May Fail to Link the Later Noise The human brain naturally assumes that sounds occurring together have a common source. When several seconds separate a visual event from its accompanying noise, that connection becomes much less obvious. Several psychological factors contribute: Attention shifts. Once the object has moved on or disappeared, the observer may already be looking elsewhere. Direction mismatch. The delayed sound may appear to originate behind the aircraft's current position because it reflects where the aircraft was when that sound was generated. Expectation bias. People generally expect large, nearby aircraft to produce immediate engine noise. When that expectation is violated, they may interpret the silence as evidence that the object was unconventional. Background masking. Traffic, wind, insects or urban noise can obscure the first faint arrival of engine sound, making the eventual noise seem detached from the original sighting. These factors make delayed sound particularly easy to misinterpret during brief or unexpected observations. Why This Matters in Silent UFO Reports The delayed arrival of aircraft noise does not explain every report of a silent unidentified object, but it provides a simple and well established mechanism that investigators routinely consider before invoking more unusual explanations. A report describing an apparently silent object that is followed several seconds later by distant engine noise is consistent with ordinary acoustic propagation, especially if the object's distance or altitude was uncertain. The later sound should not automatically be dismissed as unrelated simply because it arrived after the visual observation. For this reason, careful investigation benefits from reconstructing the geometry of the sighting, estimated altitude, likely aircraft routes and the expected sound travel time. A delay that initially appears mysterious may be entirely consistent with the known physics of sound propagation through the atmosphere. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation AdministrationFundamentals of Noise and Sound Federal Aviation AdministrationMarch 29, 2022... Published: March 29, 2022 The sound barrier, shockwaves, sonic booms and the shock wave cone. Channel: Zak's Lab Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Can Aircraft Noise Arrive After the UFO Passes?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Fundamentals of Acoustics By Lawrence E. Kinsler, Austin R. Frey et al. Dargestellt werden die physikalischen und mathematischen Konzepte zur Beschreibung der Erzeugung, Weiterleitung und Wahrnehmung von Schal... See on Amazon Book The Science of Sound By Thomas D. Rossing, Paul A. Wheeler et al. Written for students without a background in mathematics or physics, this textbook provides an introduction to the study of acoustics. It... See on Amazon Book Stick and Rudder: An Explanation of the Art"},{"title":"Balloons","url":"/balloons/","description":"Balloons can drift slowly and silently while distance errors make them appear lower, larger and more structured than they are.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Silent Sightings","level":3,"text":"Balloons Why High Balloons Can Look Like Giant Silent UFOs Silent Sightings Balloons Balloons Why High Balloons Can Look Like Giant Silent UFOs Balloons can drift slowly and silently while distance errors make them appear lower, larger and more structured than they are. Balloons can drift slowly and silently while distance errors make them appear lower, larger and more structured than they are. entity How balloon altitude defeats size judgement Why wind drift resembles hovering or slow flight Visual checks that separate balloons from powered craft ufo noises silent sightings balloons Introduction High altitude balloons are among the most convincing conventional explanations for some reports of large, silent unidentified objects in the sky. Unlike aircraft, they have no engines and therefore produce virtually no sound of their own. At the same time, they often fly at altitudes where the human eye has few reliable clues for judging distance. When observers underestimate how high a balloon really is, they can mistakenly conclude that it is a huge object flying much lower than reality. The combination of silence, apparent slow movement and uncertain scale can create the impression of a giant structured craft rather than a scientific or meteorological balloon. This mechanism fits naturally within the broader problem of silent UFO sightings caused by errors in estimating distance, but it has its own distinctive visual characteristics and historical examples. [National Weather Service] weather.gov National Weather Service Radiosonde Observation National Weather Service Radiosonde Observation How Balloon Altitude Defeats Size Judgement A person looking at an isolated object against a clear sky has few visual references. Without nearby buildings, hills or clouds, the brain has difficulty estimating how far away the object really is. If an object is assumed to be a few hundred metres away when it is actually tens of kilometres above the ground, its apparent size is dramatically inflated. This effect is especially important for high altitude weather balloons. A typical National Weather Service radiosonde balloon climbs beyond 35 kilometres (115,000 feet) while drifting hundreds of kilometres from its launch site. During ascent, the balloon itself expands from roughly 1.5 metres in diameter to between six and eight metres before bursting. Although physically modest, it can appear surprisingly prominent because sunlight reflects strongly from its surface against a dark blue sky. [National Weather Service] weather.gov National Weather Service Radiosonde Observation National Weather Service Radiosonde Observation Human perception is also known to contain systematic biases when judging distance and height, particularly where reliable reference points are absent. Experimental psychology has repeatedly shown that people do not estimate spatial distances accurately under such conditions, making aerial observations especially vulnerable to mistaken assumptions about size and altitude. [scholarworks.wm.edu] scholarworks.wm.edu Open source on wm.edu. The result is a familiar pattern in UFO reports: a supposedly enormous object appears to move unusually slowly; it seems unexpectedly close yet remains silent; its size is inferred from the false assumption that it is flying at low altitude; ordinary balloon movement is interpreted as controlled flight. 10:35 Congress Fell for a BALLOON? MICK WEST Breaks Down The Hellfire Missile UFO Channel: The Zach Show · Views: 302 · Uploaded: October 2025 · Length: 10 minutes Open on YouTube Why Wind Drift Resembles Hovering or Slow Flight Unlike powered aircraft, high altitude balloons simply travel with atmospheric winds. This creates behaviour that can seem highly unusual to ground observers. Because winds differ greatly between atmospheric layers, a balloon may appear to: remain almost stationary for several minutes; drift gently despite strong surface winds; change apparent direction as it enters a different wind layer; move with remarkable smoothness, lacking the banking or acceleration expected from an aeroplane. Observers often interpret this as deliberate hovering. In reality, the balloon is moving at the speed and direction of the wind surrounding it rather than following a powered flight path. Depending on viewing geometry, motion directly towards or away from the observer may produce very little apparent sideways movement, reinforcing the illusion that the object is fixed in place. This differs from conventional aircraft, whose engines, contrails or predictable navigation lights usually provide additional clues to their identity. A balloon offers few such cues while remaining effectively silent. Why Silence Reinforces the Illusion Silence is one of the strongest psychological reasons balloons become UFO candidates. Scientific weather balloons and many research balloons have no propulsion system. Apart from the brief sound of launch, they simply float. At cruising altitude they produce essentially no audible noise detectable from the ground. For an observer who believes the object is only a short distance away, this creates an apparent contradiction: a seemingly massive craft should be producing engine noise, rotor noise or jet exhaust. Because no sound is heard, witnesses may conclude they are seeing an unconventional vehicle rather than questioning the assumed distance. The explanation is usually simpler. The object is not exceptionally quiet for its apparent size; it is much farther away than it appears. Chinese Balloon Shows Why UFO Sightings Matter Channel: Bloomberg News Open on YouTube Historical Examples of Balloon Misidentification Balloons have featured prominently in the history of UFO investigations because their appearance can convincingly imitate unusual aerial objects under the right viewing conditions. The best known example is the 1947 Roswell incident. Later US Air Force investigations concluded that debris recovered near Roswell originated from the classified Project Mogul programme, which used long trains of high altitude balloons carrying acoustic equipment intended to detect Soviet nuclear tests. Although debate surrounding Roswell continues in popular culture, the case illustrates how unusual balloon equipment could be mistaken for something far more extraordinary, particularly when the underlying project remained secret. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia Project Mogul Project Mogul More recently, widespread attention surrounding large surveillance and research balloons has demonstrated how unfamiliar high altitude objects can rapidly generate UFO speculation before their identity becomes established. Public discussion following the 2023 Chinese balloon incident also highlighted how many scientific, commercial and military balloons routinely occupy the upper atmosphere without attracting notice under normal circumstances. [AP News] apnews.com AP News China balloon: Many questions about suspected spy in the sky airspace and was ultimately shot down by the Air Force over the Atlantic, sparking a diplomatic dispute and widespread social media atten... These incidents do not show that every unexplained sighting involves a balloon. Rather, they demonstrate that balloons remain an important class of conventional object capable of producing genuinely puzzling observations. Visual Checks That Separate Balloons from Powered Craft Although a single observation is rarely enough for certainty, several characteristics consistently favour a balloon explanation over a powered aircraft or genuinely unusual object. Movement follows the wind. Balloons typically drift with prevailing air currents rather than making purposeful turns or rapid accelerations. No visible propulsion. There are no exhaust plumes, rotor discs or persistent engine lights associated with propulsion. Gradual rather than abrupt motion. Changes in direction usually occur slowly as the balloon encounters different wind layers, not through sharp manoeuvres. Changing appearance with sunlight. Reflective surfaces may flash brightly, fade dramatically or seem metallic depending on the Sun's angle, even though the object itself has not changed. Extended visibility. A balloon may remain in view for a long period because its movement relative to the observer is slow, unlike fast moving aircraft crossing the sky. These indicators become more persuasive when combined with weather data, upper air wind information and known balloon launch schedules. 5:32 The SHOCKING Math Error Behind Viral UFO Videos Mick West Channel: The Zach Show · Views: 126 · Uploaded: October 2025 · Length: 5 minutes Open on YouTube Why Balloons Remain a Credible Explanation High altitude balloons explain a distinctive subset of silent UFO reports because they naturally combine three features that often puzzle witnesses: silence, slow movement and misleading apparent size. Their silence requires no exotic technology, only the absence of engines. Their apparent hovering is an"},{"title":"Hover Illusion","url":"/hover-illusion/","description":"An aircraft flying toward or away from a witness can show little sideways motion, making ordinary flight look like silent hovering.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Silent Sightings","level":3,"text":"Hover Illusion When a Head On Aircraft Looks Like a Hovering UFO Silent Sightings Hover Illusion Hover Illusion When a Head On Aircraft Looks Like a Hovering UFO An aircraft flying toward or away from a witness can show little sideways motion, making ordinary flight look like silent hovering. An aircraft flying toward or away from a witness can show little sideways motion, making ordinary flight look like silent hovering. population context Why head on motion is hard to judge How landing lights reinforce the hovering effect Clues that reveal an approaching or departing aircraft ufo noises silent sightings hover illusion Introduction One of the most common conventional explanations for apparently silent, hovering UFOs is surprisingly simple: the object is an ordinary aircraft flying almost directly towards or away from the observer. When an aircraft's motion is aligned with the viewer's line of sight, it produces very little sideways movement across the sky. Combined with bright landing lights and uncertain distance perception , this geometry can create a powerful illusion that the aircraft is stationary, hovering, or moving in impossible ways. If the aircraft is still several kilometres away, its engine noise may be faint, delayed or masked by the surrounding environment, reinforcing the impression of an eerily silent object. This combination of perspective, lighting and acoustics has been recognised both in aviation safety literature and in investigations of mistaken aerial sightings. [Skybrary] skybrary.aero Visual Illusions SKYbrary Aviation Safety Visual Illusions SKYbrary Aviation Safety... Why Head On Motion Is Hard to Judge Human vision is far better at detecting movement across the field of view than movement directly towards or away from the observer. A car driving across a road is obviously moving because its position changes rapidly against the background. A car driving straight towards you, however, changes very little in apparent position until it becomes quite close. The same principle applies to aircraft. An airliner approaching on a landing path may travel at well over 200 km/h while appearing almost motionless because its angular position in the sky changes only slowly. Instead of sweeping sideways, it simply grows gradually larger. Without reliable reference points such as mountains, buildings or clouds, the brain struggles to estimate: how far away the aircraft actually is; whether it is approaching or departing; how fast it is travelling; whether it is climbing, descending or maintaining altitude. These limitations are well understood in aviation, where visual illusions affecting judgement of distance, movement and alignment are extensively documented because they can influence even trained pilots. [Skybrary] skybrary.aero Visual Illusions SKYbrary Aviation Safety Visual Illusions SKYbrary Aviation Safety... 2:46 How Runway Illusions affect pilot’s perception SIMPLY explained. Channel: flight club · Views: 43.4K · Uploaded: February 2023 · Length: 2 minutes 46 seconds Open on YouTube How Landing Lights Reinforce the Hovering Effect Landing lights are designed to be extremely bright and highly focused so that aircraft are visible over long distances during approach and departure. Viewed nearly head on, these lights can dominate everything else: the fuselage becomes difficult to distinguish; navigation lights may be overwhelmed by the white beam; the aircraft's outline disappears into darkness; the observer effectively sees only one or two brilliant points of light. This creates a misleading impression. Because the illuminated point changes position only slowly, the light appears suspended in space even though the aircraft is steadily closing the distance. At night this effect becomes stronger because the observer loses most of the depth cues available during daylight. A single bright light floating against a dark sky provides little information about its true distance or speed. A further complication is that brighter objects are often perceived as being closer than they really are. If a witness subconsciously assumes the aircraft is nearby, the absence of loud engine noise may seem extraordinary even though the aircraft is actually several kilometres away. Aviation guidance recognises that bright lighting and sparse visual references can distort judgement of distance and position during night operations. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation Administration Chapter 8. Medical Facts for Pilots Federal Aviation Administration Chapter 8. Medical Facts for Pilots Why the Aircraft May Seem Silent The apparent silence is usually not because the aircraft is unusually quiet, but because the observer has overestimated how close it is. Several ordinary factors contribute: Distance. Modern aircraft become much quieter at several kilometres' range than many people expect. Sound delay . Light reaches the observer essentially instantaneously, while sound travels at roughly 343 metres per second under typical conditions. An approaching aircraft may therefore be seen well before noticeable engine noise arrives. Wind and atmosphere. Wind direction, temperature layers and background environmental noise can weaken or redirect aircraft sound. Expectation. If the object is mistakenly believed to be only a few hundred metres away, the lack of obvious engine noise appears mysterious even though it is entirely consistent with a much greater distance. These acoustic factors complement the visual illusion rather than creating it independently. 13:58 3 Ways your BRAIN Tricks You into Thinking AIRPLANES HOVER! Channel: Mentour Pilot · Views: 201.0K · Uploaded: September 2021 · Length: 13 minutes Open on YouTube Clues That Reveal an Approaching or Departing Aircraft Although the hovering impression can be convincing, there are usually subtle clues that distinguish a conventional aircraft from an unidentified object. Useful indicators include: Gradual brightening. The landing lights steadily increase in intensity as the aircraft approaches. Increasing apparent size. Even if sideways movement is minimal, the lights slowly separate and the aircraft eventually becomes visible. Navigation light pattern. As the viewing angle changes, the standard red, green and white aviation lights become easier to identify. Predictable track. The object continues along a straight approach or departure corridor rather than making abrupt manoeuvres. Eventual engine noise. As the aircraft draws nearer or passes abeam, engine sound often becomes noticeable and aligns with the visual observation. Once the aircraft begins to pass the observer rather than remain nearly head on, its sideways motion becomes much more obvious and the hovering illusion rapidly disappears. Night Time Perception Can Add Further Illusions A head on aircraft can be mistaken not only for a hovering object but also for one that changes direction unexpectedly. One contributing factor is autokinesis , a well known visual illusion in which a small light against a dark, featureless background appears to drift if stared at for several seconds. Aviation authorities teach pilots to avoid prolonged fixation on isolated lights because this effect can create false impressions of movement. [Skybrary] skybrary.aero Autokinetic Effect SKYbrary Aviation Safety Autokinetic Effect SKYbrary Aviation Safety... This illusion does not mean the aircraft is actually manoeuvring. Instead, the observer's visual system lacks stable reference points and begins interpreting tiny involuntary eye movements as movement of the light itself. When combined with the genuine perspective effects of a head on approach, autokinesis can make an otherwise ordinary aircraft seem to hover, wobble or drift unpredictably. Why This Explanation Fits Many Silent UFO Reports Not every silent aerial sighting can be identified with certainty, and some reports lack enough information for a definitive explanation. However, a head on aircraft approach provides a particularly strong explanation for a recurring pattern of observations: a bright stationary light; [ufouap.net] ufouap.net Autokinesis: Why a Stationary Light Seems to Move Global UFO ArchiveJuly 11, 2026 — Evidence / Updated 2026 07 11 / 7 min read AUTOKINE... Published: July 11, 2026 little or no sideways movement; apparent hovering for several minutes; little immediate engine noise; eventual growth in brightness before normal aircraft features become visible. This combination follows directly from ordinary viewing geometry rather than unusual aircraft behaviour. It illustrates how perspective can conceal true speed and direction, making a conventional aircraft appear to defy expectations without requiring any unconventional propulsion or genuinely silent flight. Aviation research into visual perception consistently shows that judging the distance and motion of isolated lights at night is one of the least reliable tasks for human observers, making this a"},{"title":"Night Lights","url":"/night-lights/","description":"At night, isolated navigation or landing lights can make a distant aircraft look much closer, larger and quieter than it really is.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Silent Sightings","level":3,"text":"Night Lights Why Night Lights Make Silent UFOs Look Nearby Silent Sightings Night Lights Night Lights Why Night Lights Make Silent UFOs Look Nearby At night, isolated navigation or landing lights can make a distant aircraft look much closer, larger and quieter than it really is. At night, isolated navigation or landing lights can make a distant aircraft look much closer, larger and quieter than it really is. population context How darkness removes distance cues Why brightness distorts perceived range Ways landmarks and flight data can correct the illusion ufo noises silent sightings night lights Introduction Many reports of apparently silent UFOs occur after dark, when witnesses see only one or more bright lights rather than the outline of an aircraft. Under these conditions, people lose many of the visual cues needed to judge distance, altitude and size. A distant aircraft can therefore appear surprisingly low, unusually large and almost silent, even though its engine noise is entirely consistent with its true range. This is one of the best understood mechanisms behind some night time UFO reports and fits within the broader pattern of silent sightings caused by distance misjudgement rather than by unusual propulsion. Research on human vision and aviation has long recognised that darkness, isolated lights and featureless surroundings make distance estimation particularly unreliable. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation Administration Chapter 8. Medical Facts for Pilots Federal Aviation Administration Chapter 8. Medical Facts for Pilots How Darkness Removes Distance Cues During daylight, the brain estimates distance using many overlapping clues. The apparent size of an aircraft can be compared with clouds, terrain, trees, buildings or the horizon. Surface texture, atmospheric haze and shadows also provide depth information. At night, many of these references disappear. An observer may see only a few navigation lights, flashing anti collision strobes or bright landing lights suspended against a black sky. Without a visible aircraft body or reliable background, the brain must infer distance from incomplete information. These inferences are often wrong because there is no dependable scale against which to judge the lights. Aviation training material warns pilots that darkness and featureless terrain greatly increase susceptibility to visual illusions involving height, distance and orientation. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov 12 afh ch11 Federal Aviation AdministrationAirplane Flying Handbook (FAA H 8083 3C)... This explains why many witnesses sincerely believe an object is only a few hundred metres away when it may actually be several kilometres distant. Why Bright Lights Can Look Much Closer Than They Are Brightness is a powerful but imperfect cue for distance. In everyday life, nearby objects often appear brighter than distant ones, so the visual system naturally associates intense light with proximity. At night, however, aircraft landing lights are designed to be visible from long distances and can remain conspicuous even when the aircraft itself cannot be seen. A bright forward facing landing light viewed head on may therefore create several misleading impressions simultaneously: the aircraft appears closer than it is; the object seems larger because the light dominates the view; engine noise seems unexpectedly weak; forward motion appears unusually slow because the aircraft is still far away. The effect is strongest when the aircraft is approaching the observer, since its relative motion across the sky is small. Instead of sweeping sideways, the light appears almost stationary while gradually increasing in brightness, making it seem to hover or creep silently towards the observer. FAA guidance also notes that bright lighting can distort perceived range in operational settings, illustrating how light intensity alters human judgement of distance under low light conditions. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation Administration Chapter 8. Medical Facts for Pilots Federal Aviation Administration Chapter 8. Medical Facts for Pilots 12:21 Drones, Airplanes, Or UAPs? How To Tell The Difference Business Insider Channel: Business Insider · Views: 262.1K · Uploaded: December 2024 · Length: 12 minutes Open on YouTube Why an Aircraft Can Seem Low Yet Produce Little Noise The expectation that a low aircraft should be loud is generally correct. The illusion arises when the aircraft is not actually low. If an observer assumes an aircraft is only 300 metres away instead of several kilometres away, the lack of loud engine noise appears mysterious. In reality: sound intensity decreases rapidly with distance; wind direction may carry engine noise away from the observer; modern turbofan aircraft are quieter than many people expect, especially during stable flight; the observer's estimate of altitude may be based entirely on the apparent position of lights rather than the aircraft's true location. Because the perceived distance is incorrect from the beginning, the apparent silence becomes part of a misleading but internally consistent interpretation of the sighting. Why Approaching Aircraft Often Look Like Hovering Objects Many well known UFO descriptions involve a bright object that appears almost motionless before suddenly changing position or passing overhead. A conventional explanation is simple geometry. An aircraft flying almost directly towards an observer changes its bearing only slowly despite travelling at high speed. The light therefore appears fixed against the background while steadily becoming brighter. Only after the aircraft passes to one side does its true lateral motion become obvious. At that point, observers often recognise the navigation lights or hear engine noise that was previously faint or delayed by distance. This head on viewing geometry is especially misleading when only the landing lights are visible and the aircraft's outline remains invisible against the night sky. Night Time Visual Illusions That Add to the Effect The absence of reference points can trigger additional perceptual effects that reinforce distance errors. 8:14 Are they aliens? Channel: ReasonTV · Views: 20.7K · Uploaded: October 2022 · Length: 8 minutes Open on YouTube Autokinesis When a person stares at a single light against a dark background for several seconds, the light may appear to drift or move even though it is stationary. This phenomenon, known as autokinesis, is well documented in aviation because it can mislead pilots during night flight. An isolated aircraft light may therefore appear to execute subtle movements that encourage interpretations of unusual manoeuvring. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov 12 afh ch11 Federal Aviation AdministrationAirplane Flying Handbook (FAA H 8083 3C)... Featureless Surroundings Dark countryside, open water or overcast skies remove visual texture that would normally help estimate altitude and distance. Aviation authorities warn that such \"featureless terrain\" increases errors in judging height and range, even for trained pilots. The same conditions affect ground observers trying to estimate how high an aircraft is flying. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation Administration Chapter 8. Medical Facts for Pilots Federal Aviation Administration Chapter 8. Medical Facts for Pilots 12:52 UFO Videos Explained: Mick West's Expert Analysis Channel: TWiT Tech Podcast Network · Views: 2.5K · Uploaded: February 2025 · Length: 12 minutes Open on YouTube Using Landmarks and Flight Data to Test the Impression Night time distance illusions can often be checked using independent evidence rather than relying solely on visual impressions. Useful cross checks include: Fixed landmarks. Compare the lights with hills, towers, buildings or known terrain features. If the object passes behind a distant ridge, it cannot be nearby. The horizon. A visible horizon or cloud layer provides a better estimate of elevation than isolated lights alone. Navigation light patterns. Red, green and white position lights, together with regular anti collision strobes, strongly indicate a conventional aircraft viewed from a particular angle. Flight tracking services. Public ADS B flight tracking data can often identify commercial and many private aircraft in the area, allowing observers to compare the reported position, heading and altitude with what they saw. None of these methods explains every unidentified sighting, but they frequently resolve reports in which a supposedly low, silent object proves to have been a much more distant aircraft viewed under poor depth perception conditions. Why This Mechanism Matters in Silent UFO Reports Night time aircraft lights demonstrate how ordinary perception can produce extraordinary impressions without requiring unusual aircraft behaviour. Darkness removes reliable depth cues, bright landing lights exaggerate apparent proximity, and"},{"title":"Range Checks","url":"/range-checks/","description":"Investigators compare witness estimates with tracking data, landmarks, radar and multiple viewpoints before treating silence as unusual.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Silent Sightings","level":3,"text":"Range Checks How Investigators Check Whether a Silent UFO Was Distant Silent Sightings Range Checks Range Checks How Investigators Check Whether a Silent UFO Was Distant Investigators compare witness estimates with tracking data, landmarks, radar and multiple viewpoints before treating silence as unusual. Investigators compare witness estimates with tracking data, landmarks, radar and multiple viewpoints before treating silence as unusual. dataset evidence Why witness range estimates receive low confidence Which external records can establish distance How confirmed range changes sound expectations ufo noises silent sightings range checks Introduction Reports of silent UFOs often become far less unusual once investigators establish how far away the reported object actually was. Human observers are generally poor at estimating the range of isolated objects in the sky, particularly at night or when there are no familiar landmarks. For that reason, experienced investigators rarely treat the absence of sound as meaningful until they have independently reconstructed the object's likely distance. If an object that appeared to be a few hundred metres away was actually several kilometres distant, faint or absent engine noise may be entirely consistent with ordinary aircraft acoustics. Conversely, if reliable evidence places an object genuinely close to witnesses while remaining silent, that observation deserves closer scrutiny. The central task is therefore not explaining silence first, but testing the distance estimate on which the claim depends. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation Administration Noise Federal Aviation Administration Federal Aviation AdministrationNoise Federal Aviation AdministrationDecember 13, 2024... Published: December 13, 2024 Why Witness Range Estimates Receive Low Confidence Investigators begin from the assumption that eyewitness estimates of aerial distance are provisional rather than precise. Unlike ground objects, airborne targets often lack reliable visual cues for judging scale. A bright light may appear closer than it is, while an unfamiliar aircraft silhouette can seem either much larger or much smaller depending on atmospheric conditions and viewing angle. Distance estimates are especially vulnerable to several recurring errors: No fixed reference points. Without buildings, hills or known terrain beneath the object, observers cannot use normal depth perception . Unknown object size. An observer cannot calculate range if the true dimensions of the object are unknown. Night time observations. Darkness removes texture, horizon cues and contrast, leaving only isolated lights. Perspective illusions. Objects travelling towards or away from an observer may appear nearly stationary despite covering large distances. For these reasons, modern investigations usually separate what witnesses actually observed from what they inferred. Statements such as \"it was only 200 metres away\" are treated as hypotheses to be tested rather than established facts. Which External Records Can Establish Distance The strongest investigations attempt to replace subjective estimates with independently verifiable measurements. No single source is decisive in every case, so investigators compare multiple datasets to see whether they converge on a consistent reconstruction. Flight Tracking and ADS B Records Many civilian aircraft continuously broadcast their identity, position and altitude through Automatic Dependent Surveillance–Broadcast (ADS B). Historical ADS B archives can reveal whether a conventional aircraft occupied the reported location at the relevant time. If a tracked aircraft was, for example, eight kilometres away rather than the witness's estimated one kilometre, expectations about engine noise change dramatically. Modern investigations increasingly combine ADS B data with radar information because the two systems complement one another and improve positional accuracy. [Ukrainian Scientific Journals] journals.uran.ua Ukrainian Scientific JournalsMethod for determining coordinates of airborne objects by radars with additional use of ADS B receivers Ea... 1:01:40 Videogame Science and UFOs with Mick West Channel: Center for Inquiry · Views: 7.1K · Uploaded: April 2023 · Length: 1 hour 1 minute Open on YouTube Primary and Secondary Radar Where radar data are available, investigators can estimate an aircraft's range independently of witness testimony. Primary surveillance radar determines an object's distance by measuring the return time of reflected radio pulses, while secondary surveillance radar supplements this with transponder derived identification and altitude for cooperative aircraft. These measurements provide objective spatial information that can either support or contradict visual estimates. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Open source on faa.gov. Radar evidence is not universal, however. Small objects, balloons , drones , stealthy designs, terrain masking or limited radar coverage may all reduce detectability. Therefore, the absence of a radar return does not automatically imply that no object was present. Landmarks, Maps and Viewing Geometry When radar is unavailable, investigators reconstruct geometry using photographs, videos and known landmarks. Common techniques include: identifying buildings, hills or towers visible beneath the object; calculating sightlines from the witness position; comparing elevation angles with topographic maps; estimating whether the reported altitude is physically consistent with surrounding terrain. Even approximate geometric reconstruction can show that an object believed to be overhead was actually much closer to the horizon and therefore much farther away. Multiple Independent Witnesses Independent observers located in different places provide one of the most useful opportunities for estimating range. If two witnesses observed the same object from different locations at roughly the same time, investigators can compare viewing directions. Where those sightlines intersect, they provide a form of triangulation that often constrains the object's position far more effectively than either witness alone. Modern scientific proposals for studying unidentified aerial phenomena similarly emphasise simultaneous observations from multiple sensors and locations because they permit direct estimation of position and motion rather than relying on subjective judgement. [arXiv] arxiv.org The Scientific Investigation of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena (UAP) Using Multimodal Ground Based ObservatoriesMay 29, 2023... Published: May 29, 2023 How Confirmed Range Changes Sound Expectations Once investigators establish a more reliable distance, they reassess whether silence remains unusual. If reconstructed evidence indicates the object was several kilometres away, weak or absent sound may be expected because: sound intensity decreases rapidly with distance; atmospheric conditions can refract or absorb sound; wind direction can carry engine noise away from observers; background environmental noise can mask faint aircraft sounds. The Federal Aviation Administration notes that aircraft noise varies substantially with distance, aircraft operation and environmental conditions, making simple assumptions about how loud an aircraft \"should\" sound unreliable. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation Administration Noise Federal Aviation Administration Federal Aviation AdministrationNoise Federal Aviation AdministrationDecember 13, 2024... Published: December 13, 2024 Conversely, if multiple independent measurements indicate an object passed at genuinely low altitude and short range, investigators then examine whether the reported silence remains compatible with known aircraft types, balloons, drones or environmental masking before considering more unusual explanations. 7:03 Quantum physicists REanalyze GOFAST UFO video footage released by the US Navy and Pentagon Channel: NYU Quantum Technology Lab · Views: 22.0K · Uploaded: June 2021 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube A Practical Investigation Workflow Experienced investigators typically follow a sequence designed to reduce uncertainty before interpreting the acoustic evidence. 10:35 Congress Fell for a BALLOON? MICK WEST Breaks Down The Hellfire Missile UFO Channel: The Zach Show · Views: 302 · Uploaded: October 2025 · Length: 10 minutes Open on YouTube 1. Record the witness account without altering it. 2. Identify the precise observation point and viewing direction. 3. Collect photographs, videos or sketches made at the time. 4. Check historical aircraft movements using flight tracking records. 5. Compare available radar, air traffic or other official records. 6. Reconstruct geometry using landmarks and maps. 7. Compare independent witness accounts for consistency. 8. Re evaluate whether the reported silence matches the reconstructed distance. This approach deliberately delays conclusions about anomalous silence"},{"title":"The Hum","url":"/the-hum/","description":"Persistent local Hums have inspired UFO theories, although investigations point to multiple industrial, environmental, and auditory causes.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises","level":2,"text":"The Hum Is The Hum Connected to UFOs? UFO Noises The Hum The Hum Is The Hum Connected to UFOs? Persistent local Hums have inspired UFO theories, although investigations point to multiple industrial, environmental, and auditory causes. Persistent local Hums have inspired UFO theories, although investigations point to multiple industrial, environmental, and auditory causes. population context Famous communities affected by The Hum External and internal sound explanations Why UFO theories persist ufo noises the hum Introduction The Hum is one of the most persistent acoustic mysteries linked to UFO folklore. It refers to a low frequency humming, buzzing or rumbling sound reported by small numbers of people in specific communities, often without an obvious source. Because the sound can be difficult to locate, is sometimes heard only by a minority of residents, and has occasionally resisted straightforward investigation, it has inspired speculation about extraterrestrial craft, secret military technology and other extraordinary explanations. Despite these claims, there is no credible evidence connecting The Hum to UFOs or unidentified aerial phenomena. Instead, investigations have repeatedly found that different cases appear to have different causes. Some have been traced to industrial equipment or infrastructure, while others may reflect internal auditory conditions such as low frequency tinnitus. Rather than pointing to a single global mystery, current evidence suggests that \"The Hum\" is a label applied to several distinct phenomena that can produce remarkably similar experiences. [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the ‘Windsor Hum Study’ Results... Famous communities affected by The Hum Although reports of unexplained humming have emerged worldwide, a handful of locations have become closely associated with the phenomenon and, by extension, with UFO speculation. Taos, New Mexico The best known case began in the early 1990s near Taos, New Mexico. Residents described hearing a continuous low frequency hum resembling a distant diesel engine or heavy machinery. The sound was unusual because: only a small proportion of residents reported hearing it; many described it as louder indoors than outdoors; extensive acoustic investigations failed to identify a consistent external source. The inability to isolate a clear cause transformed the Taos Hum into a cultural mystery. It appeared in documentaries, paranormal books and UFO discussions, even though no investigation uncovered evidence linking it to unidentified aircraft or extraterrestrial activity. Scientific attention instead shifted towards understanding environmental acoustics and individual hearing differences. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Manifestations of a low frequency sound of unknown origin perceived worldwide, also known as \"the Hum\" or the \"Taos Hum\" PubMed... Kokomo, Indiana In Kokomo, Indiana, complaints about a persistent hum emerged in the late 1990s. Unlike Taos, investigators eventually identified significant industrial contributors, including cooling towers and air compressors operating at low frequencies. After modifications were made, many residents reported improvements. The Kokomo case illustrates an important lesson: a mysterious sound can initially appear inexplicable but later prove to have an ordinary industrial origin once sufficiently detailed acoustic measurements are performed. [CiteSeerX] citeseerx.ist.psu.edu Journal of Scientiﬁc Exploration, Vol. 18, No. 4, pp. 571–595, 2004November 1, 2025... Published: November 1, 2025 Windsor, Ontario Beginning in 2011, thousands of residents around Windsor, Canada, reported an intermittent low frequency vibration that was often described as being felt as much as heard. Multiple scientific studies detected genuine low frequency acoustic activity and repeatedly pointed towards the heavily industrialised Zug Island area across the Detroit River as the most likely source. Researchers characterised the Hum's acoustic signature and localised it geographically, although obtaining direct access to all suspected industrial facilities proved difficult. This case demonstrates that some \"Hum\" reports involve measurable external sound rather than purely subjective perception . [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the ‘Windsor Hum Study’ Results... United Kingdom communities Reports have also come from Bristol, Halifax in West Yorkshire, Omagh in Northern Ireland and other British communities. Several investigations have ultimately associated local hums with ventilation systems, industrial fans or other infrastructure, while some cases remain unresolved because no single source could be confirmed conclusively. [thetimes.co.uk] thetimes.co.uk Omagh hum mystery solved The Fermanagh and Omagh district council engaged a noise consultant who identified a specific location as the probable origin. However, d... Still no explanation for the mysterious Windsor hum haunting Ontario city, southeastern Michigan Channel: Click On Detroit Local 4 WDIV Open on YouTube External and internal sound explanations One reason The Hum remains controversial is that there is probably no universal explanation. Different cases appear to arise through different mechanisms. External environmental sources Many investigations have identified plausible physical causes capable of producing persistent low frequency noise: industrial machinery; large ventilation systems; compressors and cooling equipment; distant road or rail traffic; electrical infrastructure; atmospheric conditions that carry low frequency sound over long distances. Low frequency sound behaves differently from higher pitched noise. It travels farther, penetrates buildings more easily and is difficult for people to locate accurately. This makes ordinary infrastructure seem strangely directionless or omnipresent, encouraging the impression that the sound has no obvious origin. [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the ‘Windsor Hum Study’ Results... Internal auditory explanations Not every reported Hum corresponds to an external sound. Increasing evidence suggests that many people experience an internally generated perception. Research published in the International Tinnitus Journal found that numerous Hum hearers displayed characteristics more consistent with an unusual form of tinnitus than with exposure to an unidentified environmental signal. Features such as changes when turning the head or interactions with other sounds suggested that the perceived hum often originated within the auditory system. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Manifestations of a low frequency sound of unknown origin perceived worldwide, also known as \"the Hum\" or the \"Taos Hum\" PubMed... More recent research has strengthened this interpretation. A 2026 study concluded that, for many participants, the most likely explanation was low frequency subjective tinnitus rather than exceptional hearing sensitivity or an unidentified external acoustic source. Importantly, the researchers did not argue that every reported Hum has the same cause; instead, they concluded that multiple mechanisms probably exist. [The Hearing Review] hearingreview.com The Hearing ReviewStudy Suggests \"The Hum\" Is Most Likely a Form of Tinnitus The Hearing ReviewJune 23, 2026... Published: June 23, 2026 Why UFO theories persist Although scientific investigations have not found evidence connecting The Hum with UFOs, the phenomenon continues to feature prominently in UFO discussions. Several factors help explain why. The sound seems unnatural Many hearers describe the Hum as mechanical, rhythmic or electrical rather than resembling familiar environmental noise. Because it often lacks an obvious direction, listeners may conclude that it comes from an unusual airborne or underground source. The Haunting Hymn of the Taos Hum Channel: Great Big Story Open on YouTube Not everyone hears it One of the most puzzling characteristics is that family members or neighbours standing together may disagree about whether any sound exists. For those who hear it, this can reinforce the belief that the source possesses unusual properties beyond ordinary acoustics. In reality, hearing sensitivity varies considerably between individuals, particularly at very low frequencies, and internal auditory phenomena affect only some people. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Manifestations of a low frequency sound of unknown origin perceived worldwide, also known as \"the Hum\" or the \"Taos Hum\" PubMed... Mystery attracts extraordinary explanations Whenever investigations fail to identify a single cause, alternative theories naturally emerge. Over the years, suggestions have included: hidden military technology; underground facilities; electromagnetic transmissions; secret industrial projects; extraterrestrial spacecraft. While such ideas circulate widely online and in popular culture, none has been supported by verifiable evidence."},{"title":"Hum Tinnitus","url":"/hum-tinnitus/","description":"Head movement, sound interactions and hearing tests suggest that many Hum experiences may begin inside the auditory system rather than outside it.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / The Hum","level":3,"text":"Hum Tinnitus Could The Hum Be Coming From Inside the Ear? Hum Tinnitus Hum Tinnitus Could The Hum Be Coming From Inside the Ear? Head movement, sound interactions and hearing tests suggest that many Hum experiences may begin inside the auditory system rather than outside it. Head movement, sound interactions and hearing tests suggest that many Hum experiences may begin inside the auditory system rather than outside it. mechanism Clues that point to an internal sound How low frequency tinnitus differs from ordinary noise Why one explanation cannot fit every Hum report ufo noises the hum hum tinnitus Introduction Many reports of The Hum are described as an external low frequency sound, yet medical research suggests that a significant proportion may instead originate within the listener's own auditory system. In these cases, the experience resembles a rare form of low frequency subjective tinnitus —the perception of sound without a corresponding external acoustic source. This possibility is especially relevant when the sound is heard by only one person, changes with head movement, interacts with other sounds, or cannot be detected by sensitive acoustic measurements. At the same time, not every Hum report fits this pattern. Well documented cases such as Windsor, Ontario demonstrate that genuine environmental low frequency noise can also occur. The evidence therefore points towards multiple mechanisms rather than a single explanation. [PLOS] journals.plos.org On the potential sources of a low frequency sound percept that only a few can perceive PLOS OneMarch 27, 2026... Published: March 27, 2026 Clues That Point to an Internal Sound One of the strongest arguments for an internal origin comes from patterns that would be difficult to explain if the sound were produced entirely by an external source. Researchers studying people who identify as \"hearers\" found that many describe distinctive behaviours, including: the Hum disappearing or changing after turning the head; the perceived tone forming beats or interference patterns with external sounds; delays before the sound returns after air travel or changes in pressure; hearing the Hum when nearby people cannot, even in otherwise quiet environments. These observations are consistent with changes occurring within the ear or auditory pathways rather than with a fixed environmental sound. In a questionnaire study involving more than 160 people reporting the Hum, around three quarters displayed at least one of these characteristic features, leading the author to argue that many cases represent an unusual form of tinnitus rather than an unidentified external signal. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Manifestations of a low frequency sound of unknown origin perceived worldwide, also known as \"the Hum\" or the \"Taos Hum\" PubMedJu... Head movement is particularly interesting because altering the position of the neck, jaw or inner ear can temporarily change sensory input reaching the brain. Many forms of tinnitus are known to vary with movements of the jaw or head, reflecting interactions between hearing pathways and nerves controlling muscles and balance rather than changes in the outside environment. Although this does not prove tinnitus in every Hum case, it provides a biologically plausible explanation for experiences that seem to move or disappear with body position. [PLOS] journals.plos.org On the potential sources of a low frequency sound percept that only a few can perceive PLOS OneMarch 27, 2026... Published: March 27, 2026 The Hum ( Taos Hum for 12 Hours ) Channel: crysknife007 Open on YouTube How Low Frequency Tinnitus Differs From Ordinary Noise Most people associate tinnitus with high pitched ringing, but tinnitus can also occur as a deep humming, rumbling or droning sound. Low frequency tinnitus is less common and can therefore be mistaken for distant machinery, electrical equipment or even aircraft. Several features distinguish it from ordinary environmental noise: It often lacks a clear direction. People may be unable to point towards where the sound is coming from. Only one person may hear it. Others in the same room may hear nothing at all. The loudness may fluctuate without changes in the surroundings. Quiet indoor environments often make it more noticeable , not because the sound becomes stronger but because there is less competing background noise. Unlike genuine low frequency environmental noise, subjective tinnitus cannot usually be recorded with microphones because the sound exists as a perception generated somewhere along the auditory system. This distinction explains why extensive acoustic surveys sometimes fail to detect anything matching a sufferer's experience. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsA Classification of Environmental “Hums” and Low Frequency Tinnitus R.E. Walford, 1983... What Hearing Tests Can and Cannot Show Medical assessment aims to determine whether the sound is more likely to be internal or external rather than assuming either explanation from the outset. Audiologists may use: conventional and low frequency hearing tests; tinnitus pitch matching, where patients compare their perceived sound with generated tones; examination for hearing loss, ear disease or middle ear disorders; assessment of whether jaw movement, neck movement or pressure changes alter the sound. Earlier work comparing people reporting environmental Hums with patients already diagnosed with low frequency tinnitus found substantial overlap in the frequencies they perceived. While this did not prove that all Hum reports were tinnitus, it demonstrated that many complaints closely resembled recognised clinical cases. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsA Classification of Environmental “Hums” and Low Frequency Tinnitus R.E. Walford, 1983... Recent research has strengthened this view. A 2026 study examined people reporting persistent low frequency sound percepts, measuring unusually sensitive hearing and spontaneous otoacoustic emissions—tiny sounds naturally generated by the inner ear. The researchers found little evidence that exceptional low frequency hearing or self generated ear emissions explained most cases. Instead, they concluded that subjective low frequency tinnitus is often the most likely explanation when no external sound source can be identified , while emphasising that genuine external sounds remain possible in some situations. [PLOS] journals.plos.org On the potential sources of a low frequency sound percept that only a few can perceive PLOS OneMarch 27, 2026... Published: March 27, 2026 What is Tinnitus? Causes & Treatment Strategies Channel: Fauquier ENT Open on YouTube Why One Explanation Cannot Fit Every Hum Report The tinnitus hypothesis explains many personal Hum experiences, but it cannot account for every well investigated case. Some communities have documented measurable low frequency sound or vibration affecting multiple residents simultaneously. In those situations, investigators have identified industrial equipment, ventilation systems or other infrastructure as likely contributors. Such findings demonstrate that authentic environmental Hums exist alongside medically generated auditory perceptions. Conversely, many reports share features far more typical of tinnitus: only a minority of residents perceive the sound; the sound changes with head or jaw movement; extensive environmental measurements fail to detect a matching acoustic signal; clinical examination finds patterns compatible with auditory dysfunction. Rather than forcing every report into one category, researchers increasingly favour a mixed model in which \"The Hum\" serves as an umbrella label for several different phenomena that produce remarkably similar subjective experiences. [sagepub.com] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsA Classification of Environmental “Hums” and Low Frequency Tinnitus R.E. Walford, 1983... What This Means for UFO Speculation Within discussions of unexplained aerial phenomena, the Hum has sometimes been presented as evidence of hidden aircraft, experimental propulsion systems or extraterrestrial technology. The existence of low frequency tinnitus offers a more ordinary explanation for many reports that appear mysterious at first. Importantly, recognising an internal auditory mechanism does not imply that every Hum complaint is imagined or psychological. Subjective tinnitus is a genuine neurological perception that can be persistent, intrusive and distressing. At the same time, documented environmental Hum cases show that external low frequency sound also occurs in the real world. The most evidence based approach is therefore to investigate each report individually. If acoustic measurements repeatedly fail to identify an external source and the perceived sound varies with head movement, hearing tests or other recognised tinnitus characteristics, an internal auditory origin becomes increasingly plausible. Where measurable low frequency sound is present"},{"title":"UFO Claim Test","url":"/ufo-claim-test/","description":"Persistent sounds, missing visual sightings and ordinary causes in solved cases leave UFO claims without positive evidence connecting craft to the Hum.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / The Hum","level":3,"text":"UFO Claim Test Does The Hum Provide Any Real UFO Evidence? UFO Claim Test UFO Claim Test Does The Hum Provide Any Real UFO Evidence? Persistent sounds, missing visual sightings and ordinary causes in solved cases leave UFO claims without positive evidence connecting craft to the Hum. Persistent sounds, missing visual sightings and ordinary causes in solved cases leave UFO claims without positive evidence connecting craft to the Hum. dataset evidence What a genuine UFO connection would need to show How solved Hum cases weaken extraordinary claims Why unexplained does not mean extraterrestrial ufo noises the hum ufo claim test Introduction The Hum is frequently mentioned in UFO discussions because it is mysterious, sometimes difficult to measure and, in a few locations, has remained only partly explained. However, none of those characteristics amount to evidence that unidentified flying objects or extraterrestrial technology are responsible. A sound that lacks an immediate explanation is not, by itself, evidence for an extraordinary cause. Across the best studied Hum cases, investigations have either identified plausible terrestrial sources, found evidence consistent with hearing related conditions, or concluded that available data are insufficient to determine a single cause. In no major investigation has researchers produced positive evidence linking the Hum to unidentified aerial phenomena (UAP). [gc.ca] international.gc.ca Global Affairs Canada Summary of the ‘Windsor Hum Study’ Results... Understanding this distinction is important because the Hum often appears in UFO folklore through association rather than evidence. Coincidences, personal beliefs and unresolved questions can encourage speculation, but scientific investigations require testable links rather than unexplained observations alone. What a genuine UFO connection would need to show For the Hum to constitute credible evidence of UFO activity, investigators would need more than reports of strange sounds. They would need independent evidence demonstrating that the sound originated from an unidentified craft rather than from environmental, industrial or biological causes. Such evidence would typically include: Simultaneous, well documented visual observations of an unidentified object and the Hum. Instrumental measurements showing the sound originated from an airborne source rather than ground based infrastructure. Repeatable acoustic signatures uniquely associated with the same unidentified phenomenon. Elimination of conventional explanations through systematic investigation. No major Hum case has met these standards. Reports occasionally describe unusual lights or aircraft near the time people heard a Hum, but these accounts remain anecdotal and lack corroborating measurements that establish a causal relationship. Correlation alone cannot demonstrate that one event caused the other. [Live Science] livescience.com Live Science What is the Taos Hum? Live Science Live Science What is the Taos Hum? Live Science How solved Hum cases weaken extraordinary claims Several of the best known Hum investigations illustrate why mystery alone should not be treated as evidence for UFOs. ‘The Hum’: The Unexplained Noise 2% of People Can Hear Channel: The Atlantic Open on YouTube Kokomo demonstrates that ordinary sources can initially appear mysterious Residents of Kokomo, Indiana, reported a persistent low frequency Hum that seemed inexplicable. Detailed acoustic investigations eventually identified two industrial sources : an air compressor and a cooling tower operating at frequencies capable of producing the reported disturbance. Noise control measures reduced the problem for many residents. The significance extends beyond Kokomo itself. It demonstrates that a phenomenon may appear mysterious for years before careful engineering studies identify an entirely conventional explanation. This makes caution essential before attributing unresolved Hum reports to extraordinary causes. [Live Science] livescience.com Live Science Mysterious Hum Driving People Around the World Crazy Live Science Live ScienceMysterious Hum Driving People Around the World Crazy Live ScienceJuly 25, 2013... Published: July 25, 2013 Windsor showed measurable sound without any UFO evidence The Windsor Hum in Ontario became famous because researchers confirmed that an external low frequency sound existed and deployed sophisticated monitoring equipment to characterise it. Rather than uncovering evidence of unidentified craft, investigators concluded that the most likely source was industrial activity associated with blast furnace operations on Zug Island. Although obtaining definitive access to every suspected facility proved difficult, the investigation pointed towards heavy industry rather than aerial phenomena. [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the ‘Windsor Hum Study’ Results... These findings are particularly important because Windsor represents one of the strongest documented cases involving measurable external sound. Even there, the evidence supports a terrestrial explanation instead of a UFO hypothesis. Why unexplained does not mean extraterrestrial One of the most common reasoning errors in paranormal debates is the argument from ignorance: assuming that because something has not yet been explained, an extraordinary explanation becomes more likely. Science does not work that way. Many natural phenomena—including lightning, earthquakes and certain diseases—remained unexplained for long periods before ordinary mechanisms were identified. An incomplete explanation is simply a gap in knowledge, not evidence supporting a specific alternative. The Taos Hum illustrates this point well. Despite extensive investigation, researchers did not identify one definitive external source. Yet they also found no evidence indicating UFOs, alien technology or unidentified aircraft. The case remains unresolved rather than supportive of any particular extraordinary explanation. [Live Science] livescience.com Live Science What is the Taos Hum? Live Science Live Science What is the Taos Hum? Live Science An unresolved question therefore has several possible outcomes: A conventional environmental source may eventually be identified. Multiple unrelated causes may be producing similar experiences. Some reports may involve hearing or neurological factors. Some cases may remain permanently uncertain because sufficient evidence was never collected. None of these possibilities logically requires an extraterrestrial explanation. The Taos Hum: Unraveling New Mexico’s Mysterious Sound Channel: Dive Into Discovery Open on YouTube Human perception complicates the picture Another reason the Hum does not support UFO claims is that not every reported Hum appears to originate from an external sound. Research has suggested that many people experiencing the Hum display characteristics consistent with an unusual form of tinnitus or other auditory phenomena. In some cases, head movement alters the perception , or the reported sound behaves in ways inconsistent with a stable external source. These findings imply that different people may experience superficially similar sensations through different mechanisms. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Manifestations of a low frequency sound of unknown origin perceived worldwide, also known as \"the Hum\" or the \"Taos Hum\" PubMed... This does not mean that all Hum reports are imaginary. Some cases involve measurable environmental sound, while others may involve internal auditory perception. The important point is that this diversity of mechanisms argues against a single global cause, let alone a worldwide network of hidden UFO activity. The Haunting Hymn of the Taos Hum Channel: Great Big Story Open on YouTube Why UFO folklore persists despite weak evidence The Hum occupies an unusual place in UFO culture because it possesses several features that encourage speculation: It is often difficult to locate. Only some people report hearing it. It can persist for months or years. Investigations do not always reach definitive conclusions. These characteristics make the phenomenon memorable and easy to incorporate into broader narratives about secret technology or extraterrestrial visitation. However, the persistence of a story within folklore is not the same as accumulating empirical evidence. Media coverage, documentaries and internet discussions also tend to connect unrelated unexplained phenomena into a single narrative. A local acoustic mystery may therefore become associated with UFO reports despite there being no documented physical link between them. [Live Science] livescience.com Live Science What is the Taos Hum? Live Science Live Science What is the Taos Hum? Live Science The evidence based assessment The available evidence does not support the claim that the Hum provides proof of UFO activity. The strongest investigations point instead towards a mixture of industrial noise, local"},{"title":"Kokomo Case","url":"/kokomo-case/","description":"Kokomo showed how detailed measurements and equipment changes can turn an apparently unexplained hum into a solvable industrial noise problem.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / The Hum","level":3,"text":"Kokomo Case How Kokomo Found the Machines Behind Its Hum The Hum Kokomo Case Kokomo Case How Kokomo Found the Machines Behind Its Hum Kokomo showed how detailed measurements and equipment changes can turn an apparently unexplained hum into a solvable industrial noise problem. Kokomo showed how detailed measurements and equipment changes can turn an apparently unexplained hum into a solvable industrial noise problem. event time window The complaints that triggered the investigation Cooling towers and compressors identified as contributors What changed after industrial modifications ufo noises the hum kokomo case Introduction The Kokomo Hum is one of the few well known \"Hum\" cases in which engineers identified specific industrial noise sources through systematic measurement rather than leaving the phenomenon entirely unexplained . Because mysterious low frequency sounds are sometimes linked to UFOs, secret technology or other extraordinary ideas, the Kokomo investigation is especially significant: it demonstrated how detailed acoustic fieldwork can separate measurable environmental noise from broader claims that are much harder to verify. While the investigation did not resolve every resident's experience, it showed that at least part of the reported Hum originated from identifiable industrial equipment, providing one of the strongest examples of an apparently mysterious Hum yielding to engineering analysis. [ICBEN] icben.org Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Cowan.docJune 16, 2008... Published: June 16, 2008 The complaints that triggered the investigation By the early 2000s, residents across Kokomo, Indiana, were reporting a persistent low frequency humming or droning sound. Many described it as resembling a distant diesel engine that was most noticeable at night or indoors. Some residents also associated the Hum with headaches, disturbed sleep and difficulty concentrating, while others living nearby reported hearing nothing at all. These contrasting experiences made the problem particularly difficult to investigate. [OUCI] ouci.dntb.gov.ua OUCIModern Day Acoustical Scares: From ‘The Hum’ to ‘Wind Turbine Syndrome’ OUCIModern Day Acoustical Scares: From ‘The Hum’ to ‘Wind Turbine Syndrome’ Rather than dismissing the reports, the City of Kokomo commissioned an independent acoustical investigation. The consulting team began by interviewing affected residents, holding public meetings and documenting where complaints occurred throughout the city. According to the investigation, 126 residents were formally identified as Hum hearers, although investigators believed additional people chose not to participate publicly. [ICBEN] icben.org Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Cowan.docJune 16, 2008... Published: June 16, 2008 The study was deliberately designed to test multiple possible explanations instead of assuming a single cause. Engineers measured: low frequency airborne sound; ground vibration; electromagnetic fields, because some residents suspected electrical causes; and the timing and locations of reported Hum events. Ground vibration measurements proved especially important because some people believed machinery was transmitting vibrations through the earth. Measurements at twelve sites found vibration levels more than ten times below recognised human perception thresholds, allowing investigators to rule out ground borne vibration as the primary explanation. [ICBEN] icben.org Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Cowan.docJune 16, 2008... Published: June 16, 2008 The HUM Sample (Kokomo Hum Taos Hum Bristol Hum) Channel: AVL Designs Open on YouTube Cooling towers and compressors identified as contributors The breakthrough came from detailed low frequency acoustic measurements rather than ordinary sound surveys. Investigators detected two unusually strong tonal signals that repeatedly appeared where residents experienced the Hum: a 10 Hz tone together with harmonics extending to about 60 Hz ; and a distinct 36 Hz tone. These tones stood roughly 20–40 decibels above the surrounding low frequency background , making them stand out clearly from ordinary environmental noise. [ICBEN] icben.org Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Cowan.docJune 16, 2008... Published: June 16, 2008 The next challenge was determining where these sounds originated. Conventional listening was not enough because low frequency sound is difficult for humans to localise. Instead, engineers used a rotating dual microphone array that compared the phase and intensity of incoming sound from different directions. This directional analysis traced: the 10 Hz tone to large industrial air compressors at the Haynes International facility; and the 36 Hz tone to a cooling tower at the DaimlerChrysler casting plant. Both sources generated low frequency tonal noise that remained detectable over distances exceeding one kilometre under suitable conditions. [ICBEN] icben.org Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Cowan.docJune 16, 2008... Published: June 16, 2008 Importantly, the investigators did not stop after identifying the first plausible sources. Residents also pointed towards additional industrial sites, including tunnelling operations and ventilation equipment. These facilities were examined as well, but measurements showed they either produced different frequencies or were not operating during the periods when most Hum complaints occurred. That process of elimination strengthened confidence that the compressor and cooling tower were genuine contributors rather than coincidental nearby machinery. [ICBEN] icben.org Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Cowan.docJune 16, 2008... Published: June 16, 2008 What changed after industrial modifications After the acoustic sources had been identified, managers at both industrial facilities agreed to install noise control measures and modify the equipment responsible for the identified tones. According to the engineering report, the companies voluntarily implemented silencing and equipment changes intended to reduce emissions at the measured frequencies. The investigation became notable because it did not simply identify a probable source—it resulted in practical engineering interventions designed to test the hypothesis directly. [ICBEN] icben.org Microsoft Word Microsoft Word Cowan.docJune 16, 2008... Published: June 16, 2008 Reports after the modifications were mixed. Many summaries of the Kokomo case note that residents reported noticeable improvements following the equipment changes, making Kokomo one of the strongest examples of an environmental Hum being substantially reduced through industrial mitigation. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Research Gate(PDF) Deployment of a short term geophysical field survey to monitor acoustic signals associated with the Windsor Hum Research Gate(PDF) Deployment of a short term geophysical field survey to monitor acoustic signals associated with the Windsor Hum However, not every complaint disappeared. Some residents continued to report hearing the Hum, and later commentary argued that the industrial tones alone could not explain every reported experience. Acoustic consultant Jim Cowan himself noted that some reported sensations appeared to involve issues extending beyond the measurable industrial noise identified during the investigation. Subsequent writers have therefore cautioned against presenting Kokomo as a complete solution to every Hum report. [CiteSeerX] citeseerx.ist.psu.edu Journal of Scientiﬁc Exploration, Vol. 18, No. 4, pp. 571–595, 2004... This distinction is important. The investigation successfully demonstrated that genuine, measurable industrial low frequency noise existed and that it reached residential neighbourhoods. Whether every individual report stemmed from those sources remains a separate question. The Kokomo Hum: An Acoustical Investigation Channel: AVL Designs Open on YouTube Why the Kokomo case matters Within discussions of the Hum phenomenon—and particularly in conversations where mysterious sounds become linked to UFOs or secret technologies—Kokomo serves as a valuable counterexample. Instead of relying on speculation, investigators followed a methodical engineering process: The Kokomo Hum Is Back Channel: AVL Designs Open on YouTube 1. document complaints systematically; 2. measure sound rather than relying solely on witness descriptions; 3. test alternative explanations such as ground vibration; 4. localise individual frequencies to specific equipment; 5. modify the suspected machinery; and 6. evaluate whether residents' experiences changed afterwards. That approach showed that at least some apparently mysterious Hum events can arise from ordinary industrial infrastructure whose low frequency output is difficult to detect without specialised instruments. At the same time, Kokomo also illustrates that identifying measurable noise sources does not necessarily explain every reported perception, leaving room for multiple mechanisms—including environmental acoustics and individual hearing"},{"title":"Taos Hum","url":"/taos-hum/","description":"The Taos Hum became famous because only some residents heard it and repeated investigations failed to isolate a consistent external source.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / The Hum","level":3,"text":"Taos Hum Why Could Investigators Not Find the Taos Hum? The Hum Taos Hum Taos Hum Why Could Investigators Not Find the Taos Hum? The Taos Hum became famous because only some residents heard it and repeated investigations failed to isolate a consistent external source. The Taos Hum became famous because only some residents heard it and repeated investigations failed to isolate a consistent external source. entity What residents reported in Taos How acoustic investigations searched for a source Why unresolved findings encouraged UFO speculation ufo noises the hum taos hum Introduction The Taos Hum became one of the world's most famous unexplained sound mysteries not because investigators confirmed an unknown acoustic source, but because repeated scientific investigations failed to find one. Beginning in the early 1990s, a small minority of people around Taos, New Mexico, reported hearing a persistent low frequency hum resembling a distant diesel engine or heavy machinery. Extensive measurements using acoustic, seismic, electromagnetic and vibration sensors found no consistent external signal matching the descriptions. Rather than proving an extraordinary cause, the investigations highlighted how difficult it can be to distinguish between environmental sound, individual hearing differences and other sensory phenomena. That unresolved gap helped transform the Taos Hum into a recurring feature of UFO folklore, despite the absence of evidence connecting it to unidentified aerial phenomena. [auditory.org] auditory.org 5a NS2 A perceived low frequency sound in Taos, New Mexico 5aNS2 A perceived low frequency sound in Taos, New Mexico.November 28, 1994... Published: November 28, 1994 What residents reported in Taos Accounts from residents shared several recurring features, but they also revealed important inconsistencies that complicated scientific investigation. People who reported the Hum commonly described: a continuous low frequency droning or rumbling similar to an idling diesel engine; a sound that was often stronger indoors than outdoors; greater intensity at night, when background environmental noise was lower; difficulty identifying a direction or specific location for the sound; sleep disturbance, irritation and occasional headaches associated with prolonged exposure. [washingtonpost.com] washingtonpost.com TAOS HUM' DISTURBS ARTISTIC COLONY'S GOOD VIBRATIONS The Washington PostJune 24, 1993... Published: June 24, 1993 A striking feature was that only a small proportion of residents reported hearing the Hum. Friends, neighbours and even family members living in the same homes frequently heard nothing unusual. This selective perception immediately distinguished the Taos case from conventional community noise complaints, where most nearby people detect the same sound. [washingtonpost.com] washingtonpost.com TAOS HUM' DISTURBS ARTISTIC COLONY'S GOOD VIBRATIONS The Washington PostJune 24, 1993... Published: June 24, 1993 The descriptions also varied. When investigators asked affected residents to reproduce the sound using audio equipment, the frequencies they selected differed from person to person rather than converging on a single acoustic signature. Most matched sounds between roughly 40 and 80 Hz, but there was no single frequency that every participant identified. [washingtonpost.com] washingtonpost.com TAOS HUM' DISTURBS ARTISTIC COLONY'S GOOD VIBRATIONS The Washington PostJune 24, 1993... Published: June 24, 1993 The Unexplained Taos Hum: New Mexico's Enduring Mystery Channel: Animation Open on YouTube How acoustic investigations searched for a source The complaints eventually prompted an unusually broad scientific investigation after members of New Mexico's congressional delegation requested assistance. Researchers from the University of New Mexico, Sandia National Laboratories, Los Alamos National Laboratory and the Air Force Phillips Laboratory carried out coordinated field measurements during 1993. Instead of relying solely on microphones, the team simultaneously monitored several types of physical signals, including: airborne sound; ground vibration (seismic activity); electric fields; magnetic fields; electromagnetic signals. The reasoning was straightforward: if the reported sensation originated from industrial machinery, geological activity or some unusual electromagnetic process, at least one of these instruments might detect a corresponding physical signal. [auditory.org] auditory.org 5a NS2 A perceived low frequency sound in Taos, New Mexico 5aNS2 A perceived low frequency sound in Taos, New Mexico.November 28, 1994... Published: November 28, 1994 Investigators concentrated particularly on low frequency sound because witness reports consistently suggested a deep humming or rumbling sensation. Their equipment was sensitive enough to detect extremely weak airborne signals, including sounds below normal human hearing thresholds in parts of the target frequency range. Despite this sensitivity, they found no persistent acoustic signal matching what witnesses described. [auditory.org] auditory.org 5a NS2 A perceived low frequency sound in Taos, New Mexico 5aNS2 A perceived low frequency sound in Taos, New Mexico.November 28, 1994... Published: November 28, 1994 Just as importantly, they failed to discover a consistent seismic or electromagnetic anomaly that could explain the reports. The inability to identify any corresponding physical measurement meant there was no single environmental source that investigators could isolate, map or eliminate. [auditory.org] auditory.org 5a NS2 A perceived low frequency sound in Taos, New Mexico 5aNS2 A perceived low frequency sound in Taos, New Mexico.November 28, 1994... Published: November 28, 1994 Why investigators could not isolate a physical source The Taos Hum resisted acoustic investigation because several factors worked against the normal methods used in environmental noise studies. First, the reports lacked a stable geographic pattern. Although complaints clustered around northern New Mexico, no precise location consistently produced stronger instrument readings than neighbouring areas. Without an identifiable hotspot, investigators could not work backwards to locate a source. Second, the reported sound appeared highly individual. Conventional noise complaints usually become easier to solve when multiple observers independently identify the same direction, timing and intensity. In Taos, different listeners often disagreed about these characteristics, making triangulation impossible. [washingtonpost.com] washingtonpost.com TAOS HUM' DISTURBS ARTISTIC COLONY'S GOOD VIBRATIONS The Washington PostJune 24, 1993... Published: June 24, 1993 Third, low frequency sound itself presents measurement challenges. Such sounds travel long distances, penetrate buildings efficiently and are difficult for humans to localise accurately. A listener may perceive a low frequency sound without being able to determine where it originates, even when it is genuine. However, the Taos investigation faced an additional problem: even instruments capable of detecting very weak low frequency signals failed to reveal a matching source. [auditory.org] auditory.org 5a NS2 A perceived low frequency sound in Taos, New Mexico 5aNS2 A perceived low frequency sound in Taos, New Mexico.November 28, 1994... Published: November 28, 1994 Finally, the phenomenon was intermittent from the perspective of many witnesses. Reports of changing loudness, occasional disappearance and individual variation meant researchers could not simply record a continuously present signal and compare it with testimony. [washingtonpost.com] washingtonpost.com TAOS HUM' DISTURBS ARTISTIC COLONY'S GOOD VIBRATIONS The Washington PostJune 24, 1993... Published: June 24, 1993 The Taos Hum: Unraveling New Mexico’s Mysterious Sound Channel: Dive Into Discovery Open on YouTube Could the Hum have been internal rather than external? One consequence of the unsuccessful field investigations was increased interest in explanations involving human hearing rather than environmental acoustics. Subsequent research has suggested that many Hum reports may reflect an unusual form of very low frequency tinnitus or another internally generated auditory phenomenon in at least some individuals. Studies have identified recurring features among many people who experience the Hum, including changes associated with head movement or interactions with external sounds, patterns that are difficult to explain if a single external acoustic source is responsible. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Manifestations of a low frequency sound of unknown origin perceived worldwide, also known as \"the Hum\" or the \"Taos Hum\" PubMed... This hypothesis does not imply that every reported Hum has the same cause. Other well known Hum cases, such as Windsor, Ontario, have been linked to measurable industrial low frequency sound. Instead, the Taos investigations helped demonstrate that similar"},{"title":"Windsor Data","url":"/windsor-data/","description":"Windsor researchers recorded real low-frequency activity and repeatedly localized its likely origin toward the industrial area around Zug Island.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / The Hum","level":3,"text":"Windsor Data What Did Scientists Measure During the Windsor Hum? The Hum Windsor Data Windsor Data What Did Scientists Measure During the Windsor Hum? Windsor researchers recorded real low frequency activity and repeatedly localized its likely origin toward the industrial area around Zug Island. Windsor researchers recorded real low frequency activity and repeatedly localized its likely origin toward the industrial area around Zug Island. dataset evidence How residents described the vibration What monitoring revealed about frequency and direction Why Zug Island remained the leading source ufo noises the hum windsor data Introduction The Windsor Hum stands apart from many other reported “Hum” cases because researchers did not simply collect witness accounts —they recorded measurable low frequency acoustic activity and carried out multiple scientific attempts to determine where it came from. Beginning in 2011, residents in Windsor, Ontario, described a deep rumble or vibration that was often felt as much as heard. Subsequent investigations by Natural Resources Canada, the University of Windsor and Western University confirmed that an external physical signal was present during at least some reported events. While the studies stopped short of identifying one specific machine, they produced the strongest instrumental evidence of any major Hum case and repeatedly narrowed the likely origin to heavy industrial activity across the Detroit River, particularly around Zug Island. [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the ‘Windsor Hum Study’ Results... How residents described the vibration Witness reports showed remarkable consistency before extensive measurements even began. People commonly compared the sensation to: A distant idling diesel engine. A blast furnace or industrial machinery. A low, pulsating rumble. A vibration that could be felt through buildings rather than simply heard. Complaints began in early 2011 and were concentrated in southern Windsor and nearby LaSalle. Many reports described episodes lasting from minutes to several hours, with the sound appearing intermittently rather than continuously. The fact that residents independently described similar low frequency characteristics gave researchers a useful starting point for designing monitoring equipment capable of detecting frequencies below those typical of ordinary environmental noise. [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the ‘Windsor Hum Study’ Results... What monitoring revealed about frequency and direction The investigations progressed through several increasingly sophisticated stages, each answering a different question about the Hum. Still no explanation for the mysterious Windsor hum haunting Ontario city, southeastern Michigan Channel: Click On Detroit Local 4 WDIV Open on YouTube Natural Resources Canada established that it was airborne One of the earliest government studies used seismic monitoring between June and August 2011. An important result was that the suspected signal behaved like an acoustic wave travelling through the air rather than a seismic vibration propagating through the ground. The measurements also identified a dominant tone near 35 Hz , a very low frequency consistent with many residents' descriptions. Triangulation suggested that the source lay in the general direction of Zug Island. [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the ‘Windsor Hum Study’ Results... Long term monitoring captured the Hum directly The University of Windsor then deployed permanent and portable environmental noise monitoring stations throughout Windsor Essex. Because the Hum occurred unpredictably, continuous unattended monitoring was essential. Researchers succeeded in recording several genuine Hum events, allowing them to identify an acoustic signature instead of relying solely on subjective reports. They found that: the signal was intermittent rather than constant; its strongest energy lay in the low frequency range; recorded events matched the timing and character of many public complaints; the phenomenon appeared only on a limited number of monitoring days, making source identification much more difficult. This represented a major advance over earlier Hum investigations elsewhere, where researchers often failed to capture any clear external signal. [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the ‘Windsor Hum Study’ Results... Beamforming attempted to pinpoint the source After characterising the signal, the researchers employed acoustic beamforming—a technique that combines measurements from multiple microphones to estimate the direction from which sound arrives. The project was hampered by an unfortunate coincidence: during the periods when the beamforming equipment was deployed on the river, the Hum largely failed to occur. As a result, the team could not produce the definitive directional image needed to identify one specific industrial installation. Even so, the overall acoustic evidence remained consistent with an origin from the heavily industrial area around Zug Island rather than from residential neighbourhoods in Windsor itself. [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the ‘Windsor Hum Study’ Results... Why Zug Island remained the leading source The evidence pointing towards Zug Island did not depend on a single experiment. Instead, several independent observations converged. Natural Resources Canada's initial measurements placed the apparent source in that direction. Later acoustic monitoring by the University of Windsor concluded that the measured characteristics were most consistent with industrial operations on Zug Island, particularly blast furnace activity. Researchers emphasised that they had not identified a single \"smoking gun\" machine, but regarded the industrial complex as the most likely origin based on the available evidence. [gc.ca] international.gc.ca Global Affairs Canada Summary of the ‘Windsor Hum Study’ Results... An important limitation was access. Canadian researchers could not freely instrument suspected facilities on the American side of the river. Without microphones and vibration sensors placed directly on the industrial equipment, they could not isolate one individual source with engineering certainty. Consequently, the final reports described the conclusion as a well supported localisation rather than definitive proof of one machine. [Global Affairs Canada] international.gc.ca Summary of the ‘Windsor Hum Study’ Results... THE HUM: The Windsor Hum (Part 1) Channel: NightDocs Open on YouTube Why not every study reached exactly the same conclusion The Windsor Hum illustrates how difficult low frequency acoustics can be to investigate in a complex industrial landscape. A separate infrasound array study by Western University detected low frequency signals but found that some measured arrival directions did not consistently point towards Zug Island. Researchers noted that local sound propagation could be distorted by atmospheric conditions, terrain, reflections from the river, ground effects and surrounding industrial structures. They therefore concluded that, although they detected relevant low frequency signals, they could not conclusively geolocate the source during their relatively short deployment. [ResearchGate] researchgate.net Open source on researchgate.net. Rather than contradicting the broader investigation, this highlighted the practical difficulty of tracing intermittent low frequency sound outdoors. The Hum was not continuously active, and environmental conditions could alter the apparent direction from which the sound arrived. The Windsor Hum: Audio recording Channel: WXYZ TV Detroit Channel 7 Open on YouTube What the measurements mean for UFO speculation Within discussions of unexplained sounds and alleged UFO noises , the Windsor measurements are significant because they demonstrate that at least one famous Hum involved a measurable external acoustic phenomenon rather than solely individual perception. However, the studies do not support an extraordinary explanation. Instead, they indicate: a real low frequency airborne sound existed during documented Hum events; the signal was repeatedly associated with the direction of an industrial zone; no evidence connected the phenomenon with unidentified aerial phenomena or extraterrestrial activity; the remaining uncertainty concerns which specific industrial process produced the sound, not whether the sound itself existed. [gc.ca] international.gc.ca Global Affairs Canada Summary of the ‘Windsor Hum Study’ Results... The Windsor case therefore represents one of the strongest examples of scientists successfully measuring an otherwise mysterious Hum. Although the exact machine responsible was never conclusively identified, the investigations substantially narrowed the explanation from an unexplained mystery to a probable industrial source supported by multiple independent measurements. [Global Affairs"},{"title":"Drones","url":"/drones/","description":"The pulsing buzz of multirotor drones can resemble many modern UFO sound descriptions, especially at night or at a distance.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises","level":2,"text":"Drones Could That Buzzing UFO Be a Drone? UFO Noises Drones Drones Could That Buzzing UFO Be a Drone? The pulsing buzz of multirotor drones can resemble many modern UFO sound descriptions, especially at night or at a distance. The pulsing buzz of multirotor drones can resemble many modern UFO sound descriptions, especially at night or at a distance. entity Why drone rotors produce a pulsing buzz How distance and darkness alter identification Clues that separate drones from unknown objects ufo noises drones Introduction A pulsing buzzing sound is one of the most common reasons people suspect they have heard a UFO. In many modern cases, however, the sound is entirely consistent with a multirotor drone. Consumer and professional drones produce a distinctive acoustic signature created by rapidly spinning propellers and electric motors. At night, when visual cues are limited and judging distance is difficult, that characteristic buzz can seem to come from an unidentified object rather than a familiar aircraft. This does not mean every unexplained aerial report is a drone, but it does mean drones have become an increasingly important conventional explanation for many reports of buzzing lights or hovering objects. Understanding how drone noise behaves can help distinguish a likely drone from an object that genuinely remains unidentified. [Civil Aviation Authority] caa.co.uk Civil Aviation Authority Environmental Research and Consultancy Department Civil Aviation AuthorityEnvironmental Research and Consultancy DepartmentMarch 1, 2019... Published: March 1, 2019 Could That Buzzing UFO Be a Drone? The growth of recreational, commercial and public sector drone use has changed the landscape of UFO reports. Small quadcopters and larger multirotor aircraft are now common in urban, suburban and rural environments, especially after dark when navigation lights are more visible than the aircraft itself. Unlike helicopters or fixed wing aircraft, multirotor drones often remain stationary or move slowly while producing a continuous, rhythmic buzzing. To someone unfamiliar with drone acoustics, this combination of hovering lights and an unusual sound can appear highly unusual, particularly if the operator is out of sight. The result is that many modern reports of \"buzzing UFOs\" involve characteristics that are entirely compatible with drone flight, although sound alone is never sufficient to identify an object conclusively. 🚁🔊 Drone Noise Explained Channel: Salford Acoustics Open on YouTube Why Drone Rotors Produce a Pulsing Buzz The familiar drone sound is generated primarily by its propellers rather than by the electric motors themselves. Each rotor blade repeatedly displaces air, producing pressure pulses known as blade passing frequencies. With several propellers spinning simultaneously, these frequencies combine into the recognisable buzzing or humming associated with quadcopters and similar aircraft. [Civil Aviation Authority] caa.co.uk Civil Aviation Authority Environmental Research and Consultancy Department Civil Aviation AuthorityEnvironmental Research and Consultancy DepartmentMarch 1, 2019... Published: March 1, 2019 Several factors shape the sound: Rotor speed: Higher revolutions per minute produce a higher pitched buzz. Multiple propellers: Four, six or eight rotors create overlapping harmonics that give drones their characteristic pulsing quality. Flight manoeuvres: Climbing, descending or fighting wind causes rapid changes in motor speed, making the buzz rise and fall. Aircraft size: Larger drones generally produce deeper, louder sounds because they require more thrust. Research into drone acoustics has also found that people often perceive drone noise as more noticeable than conventional aircraft noise at similar sound levels because its tonal and fluctuating character attracts attention. [Civil Aviation Authority] caa.co.uk Civil Aviation AuthorityCAP2962: The Effects of Emerging Technology Aviation Noise on Humans UK Civil Aviation AuthorityAugust 27, 2024... Published: August 27, 2024 How Distance and Darkness Alter Identification Night time conditions make both visual and acoustic identification less reliable. Without clear landmarks, observers frequently misjudge how high or how far away a light is. A relatively small drone may appear much larger if its navigation lights dominate the view. At the same time, the sound can seem disconnected from the visible object because of wind, echoes from buildings or delayed arrival of sound compared with light. Other effects that complicate identification include: Wind direction , which may carry drone noise towards or away from the listener. Reflections , particularly in urban areas where sound bounces off buildings. Background silence , making moderate buzzing seem unusually prominent. Lack of visual references , increasing errors in estimating speed and distance. These factors explain why a drone can appear mysterious without requiring any unusual technology. They also explain why witnesses may disagree about whether an object seemed close, distant, silent or loud. How to Recognize Drones by Sound MilTech Trends 2025 Channel: JetSoftPro: software development one stop shop Open on YouTube Clues That Separate Drones from Unknown Objects Although no single feature is decisive, several observations increase or decrease the likelihood that a buzzing object is a drone. Signs consistent with a drone include: A rhythmic, mechanical buzz that changes pitch during climbing, descending or hovering. The ability to hover almost motionless before making relatively slow directional changes. Red, green or white navigation lights visible at night. Operation below the typical maximum recreational flight altitude in many jurisdictions. Gradual acceleration rather than instantaneous changes in speed. [UK Civil Aviation Authority] register drones.caa.co.uk Open source on caa.co.uk. Features that would be difficult to reconcile with known multirotor drones include: Complete silence despite an apparently nearby low altitude object. Sustained extreme acceleration without any corresponding change in sound. Abrupt right angle turns at high speed. Flight behaviour far beyond the endurance or manoeuvrability of current drone technology. Even then, unusual observations do not automatically indicate an extraordinary explanation. Misjudged distance, lighting conditions and incomplete observations can still produce misleading impressions. Why Buzzing Alone Is Weak Evidence Buzzing is among the least distinctive characteristics in a UFO report because many familiar technologies produce similar sounds. Besides drones, possible sources include: Electrical infrastructure. Small helicopters at greater distances. Industrial ventilation equipment. Agricultural machinery. Insects or wildlife under certain conditions. Investigators therefore treat sound as supporting information rather than primary evidence. A report becomes more informative when audio is combined with reliable visual observations, photographs, radar data or multiple independent witnesses describing the same event. Drone Propeller Aeroacoustic Simulation CFD + CAA Noise Analysis (TCAE) Channel: CFD Support Open on YouTube The Practical Takeaway Modern drones have introduced a new source of genuine ambiguity into reports of strange aerial sounds. Their pulsing rotor noise, ability to hover and bright navigation lights can closely resemble descriptions that, decades ago, might have been considered much more unusual. For that reason, a buzzing sound should now prompt a practical question before a speculative one: does the combination of sound, movement, lighting and location match the behaviour of a multirotor drone? In many contemporary cases the answer is yes, making drones one of the most plausible explanations for reports of buzzing \"UFOs\". When key observations cannot be reconciled with known drone performance, the object may remain unidentified—but the buzzing itself is not evidence of an extraordinary origin. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Could That Buzzing UFO Be a Drone?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Drone Pilot's Handbook By Adam Juniper The perfect companion for anyone buying (or thinking of buying) a drone, whether it's just for fun, to race against friends, or to give t... See on Amazon Book Drones For Dummies By Mark LaFay The fast and easy way to pick out, set up, and learn to fly your drone Ready to soar into the world of unmanned aircraft? Drones For Dumm... See on Amazon Book Make: Drones: Teach an Arduino to Fly By David McGriffy Make: Drones will help the widest possible audience understand how drones work by providing several DIY drone projects based on the world... See on Amazon Book Small Unmanned Aircraft: Theory and Practice By Randal W. Beard, Timothy W. McLain Includes bibliographical references (p. [291] 298) and index. See on Amazon Browse more"},{"title":"Flight Patterns","url":"/flight-patterns/","description":"Stable hovering, gradual acceleration and slow directional changes are more consistent with multirotor flight than extraordinary motion.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Drones","level":3,"text":"Flight Patterns Does the Object Move Like a Drone? Drones Flight Patterns Flight Patterns Does the Object Move Like a Drone? Stable hovering, gradual acceleration and slow directional changes are more consistent with multirotor flight than extraordinary motion. Stable hovering, gradual acceleration and slow directional changes are more consistent with multirotor flight than extraordinary motion. mechanism Recognizable hovering behaviour Gradual turns and acceleration Movements that exceed normal drone performance ufo noises drones flight patterns Introduction A buzzing sound can suggest that an unidentified light is actually a drone, but movement patterns often provide even stronger clues. Modern multirotor drones are designed to hover with remarkable stability, make controlled directional changes and accelerate smoothly under computer assisted flight control. When an object remains almost stationary, pivots gradually, or moves away in a measured and predictable manner, its behaviour is frequently consistent with a conventional drone rather than an unknown aircraft. At the same time, movement alone is not enough to identify what has been seen. The most reliable assessment comes from combining flight behaviour with sound, lighting, apparent distance and the surrounding environment. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation AdministrationWhat To Know About Drones Federal Aviation AdministrationJanuary 14, 2025... Published: January 14, 2025 Does the Object Hover Like a Drone? One of the defining capabilities of a multirotor drone is sustained hovering. Unlike fixed wing aircraft, which must keep moving forward to remain airborne, quadcopters and similar designs continuously adjust the speed of individual rotors to maintain a near stationary position. In calm conditions, a modern GPS assisted drone can appear almost motionless from the ground. Even in light wind, it often makes tiny, continuous corrections rather than drifting freely. These subtle adjustments may be difficult to notice at a distance but are a normal consequence of the flight controller balancing the aircraft against changing air currents. Research into multirotor flight control consistently treats precise hover as a fundamental operating mode because the aircraft's software is designed to maintain stability automatically. [NASA Technical Reports Server] ntrs.nasa.gov NASA Technical Reports ServerHandling Qualities of Multirotor RPM Controlled Electric Vertical Take Off and Landing (eVTOL) Aircraft for... Observers sometimes interpret prolonged hovering as unusual because conventional aircraft cannot remain fixed in one location. In reality, sustained hovering is one of the most recognisable characteristics of multirotor drones. Recognisable hovering behaviour Movement consistent with a drone typically includes: Remaining in approximately the same position for many seconds or even several minutes. Small side to side or vertical corrections rather than perfectly motionless flight. Brief drift caused by wind, followed by an immediate return towards the previous position. Stable hovering while lights remain pointed in the same general direction. These behaviours become more convincing when accompanied by the familiar pulsing rotor noise discussed elsewhere in the broader comparison between buzzing UFO sounds and drone acoustics. Drones The complete flight dynamics Channel: Sabin Civil Engineering Open on YouTube Why Drone Turns Look Smooth Rather Than Instantaneous Drone turns are normally deliberate and progressive rather than abrupt. When changing direction, the flight controller alters motor speeds to rotate the aircraft before producing horizontal movement. Even highly responsive consumer drones usually yaw—the rotation about the vertical axis—smoothly instead of snapping instantly through large angles. This computer controlled stability gives drone movement a characteristic appearance. A drone may pause, rotate towards a new heading, then accelerate away. Alternatively, it may combine a gentle turn with forward flight while maintaining altitude. These transitions often appear mechanical and controlled because onboard software constantly limits instability and overcorrection. [arXiv] arxiv.org arXiv Simple physics behind the flight of a drone arXiv Simple physics behind the flight of a drone From the ground, especially at night, these gradual movements can seem strangely deliberate. However, they are exactly what would be expected from an electronically stabilised multirotor. Gradual turns and acceleration Patterns commonly associated with drones include: Slow rotation before moving in a new direction. Smooth acceleration instead of explosive starts. Controlled deceleration before stopping to hover again. Repeated hover turn move sequences while surveying an area. These movements are especially common during aerial photography, inspections or recreational flying, where the pilot intentionally avoids sudden manoeuvres to keep the camera stable. Why Distance Can Make Drone Movements Look Stranger Estimating aerial motion is surprisingly difficult without reliable depth cues. A nearby drone may appear to make dramatic sideways jumps simply because observers misjudge its distance, while a more distant drone can seem completely stationary even though it is travelling steadily. Night conditions increase these perceptual errors. Bright navigation lights may be visible long before the body of the aircraft, making it difficult to judge orientation or speed. If only one light is visible, a gradual yaw rotation can even be mistaken for a sudden change in direction as different lights come into view. Wind adds another complication. Although drones actively resist drift, stronger gusts can produce small corrections that, from certain viewpoints, appear as irregular movements rather than automatic stabilisation. For these reasons, apparent flight behaviour should always be interpreted alongside sound, lighting configuration and environmental conditions rather than in isolation. How a Quadcopter Works Flight Mechanics, Components, & Sensors (2) Channel: EngineersEscape Open on YouTube What Would Be Unusual Even for a Drone? Recognising drone like movement also means recognising behaviour that exceeds normal multirotor performance. Most consumer drones remain subject to the same physical limits as any other aircraft. They require measurable time to accelerate, decelerate and change direction. Their turns are constrained by inertia, available thrust and flight control software. Aviation regulators also require operators to fly responsibly and maintain visual awareness during most routine operations, reinforcing the expectation of controlled rather than reckless manoeuvres. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Federal Aviation AdministrationSmall Unmanned Aircraft Systems (UAS) Regulations (Part 107) Federal Aviation AdministrationJuly 6, 2026... Published: July 6, 2026 Movement that would be difficult to reconcile with ordinary multirotor flight includes: Instantaneous changes of direction without any visible transition. Immediate acceleration from stationary hover to extremely high speed. Sharp right angle turns at high velocity with no slowing beforehand. Sustained motion that appears to ignore inertia or aerodynamic forces. Abrupt disappearance without any obscuration, lighting change or loss of visibility. Reports containing these features deserve careful scrutiny, but they do not automatically demonstrate extraordinary technology. Errors in estimating distance, perspective, viewing angle, atmospheric conditions or multiple objects can all produce misleading impressions. Using Flight Patterns as One Piece of the Puzzle Hovering and turning patterns are most valuable when considered alongside other observations. A stable hover, gentle yaw, gradual acceleration and repeated controlled repositioning are all hallmarks of multirotor flight and therefore strengthen the case that a buzzing unidentified object is a drone. Conversely, flight behaviour that genuinely appears incompatible with known drone performance should be examined carefully using all available evidence rather than movement alone. Sound, lighting, weather conditions, witness location and apparent distance all contribute to a more reliable assessment than any single characteristic in isolation. How Drones Fly and Maneuver – Basic Physics Explained Channel: Applied Pi Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Does the Object Move Like a Drone?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Drone Pilot's Handbook By Adam Juniper The perfect companion for anyone buying (or thinking of buying) a drone, whether it's just for fun, to race against friends, or to give t... See on Amazon Book Drones For Dummies By Mark LaFay The fast and easy way to pick out, set up, and learn to fly your drone Ready to soar into the world of"},{"title":"Lights and Altitude","url":"/lights-and-altitude/","description":"Red, green and white lights combined with low-altitude hovering can make an unknown object more consistent with lawful or unlawful drone activity.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Drones","level":3,"text":"Lights and Altitude Do the Lights and Height Match a Drone? Drones Lights and Altitude Lights and Altitude Do the Lights and Height Match a Drone? Red, green and white lights combined with low altitude hovering can make an unknown object more consistent with lawful or unlawful drone activity. Red, green and white lights combined with low altitude hovering can make an unknown object more consistent with lawful or unlawful drone activity. policy intervention Common drone navigation light colours Estimating low altitude flight at night How flight rules inform identification without proving it ufo noises drones lights and altitude Introduction When a buzzing object with bright lights appears at night, it is tempting to assume that its lighting pattern or apparent height can identify it immediately as either a drone or something more unusual. In practice, neither clue is conclusive on its own. Navigation lights, anti collision strobes and estimated flight altitude can make a drone explanation more or less plausible, but they cannot positively identify an aircraft. Modern drone regulations also vary by jurisdiction, and many drones can legally carry additional lights that differ from traditional aviation lighting. Understanding how lighting conventions and legal altitude limits work helps place a sighting into context without overstating what the evidence can prove. Do the Lights Match a Drone? Many consumer and professional drones are equipped with multiple LEDs for orientation, visibility or regulatory compliance. The colours and flashing patterns often resemble familiar aircraft lighting, but manufacturers are not required to use one universal configuration across every model. Common features include: Red and green orientation lights: Some multirotor drones use red and green LEDs on opposite arms to help the pilot determine the aircraft's direction. White forward or downward facing lights: These may illuminate the ground for landing, assist onboard sensors or improve visibility rather than serving as certified navigation lights. Flashing anti collision lights: Bright strobes are increasingly common, particularly for night operations, because they improve the aircraft's visibility to both the operator and other airspace users. Because many drones are viewed only as small points of light at night, observers often cannot distinguish whether several coloured lights belong to one aircraft or whether one flashing light dominates the view. Atmospheric haze, mist and distance can also blend colours together. Importantly, the presence of red, green and white lights does not automatically mean an object is a drone. Manned aircraft also use navigation lighting, and aftermarket lighting kits allow drone operators to modify the appearance of many consumer models. Lighting therefore provides supporting evidence rather than definitive identification. [Civil Aviation Authority] caa.co.uk Open source on caa.co.uk. 3:01 How to spot the difference: Drones vs. planes Channel: 13News Now · Views: 3.3K · Uploaded: December 2024 · Length: 3 minutes 1 second Open on YouTube Estimating Height After Dark Is Surprisingly Difficult Witnesses frequently describe a hovering object as being \"just above the trees\" or \"a few hundred feet up\". These estimates are often much less reliable than they seem. At night, people lose many of the visual references normally used to judge altitude, including terrain detail, shadows and object size. A small drone with bright LEDs may appear much farther away than it really is, while a conventional aircraft approaching head on may seem almost stationary and deceptively low. Several factors increase estimation errors: Bright lights appear larger than the aircraft carrying them. A dark sky removes depth cues. Wind blown sound can make a drone seem closer or farther than its actual position. Hovering flight reduces the motion cues people normally use to judge distance. For this reason, statements such as \"it was definitely below 100 metres\" or \"well above the legal drone limit\" should be treated cautiously unless the observation includes reliable reference points such as buildings, towers or known terrain features. How Legal Height Limits Inform Identification Many countries impose a maximum routine operating height for small drones in standard recreational or low risk commercial operations. In the UK Open Category, for example, drones are generally limited to 120 metres (400 feet) above the nearest point of the ground, with some authorised exceptions. The purpose is to reduce conflict with manned aircraft, which usually operate higher but may legally fly lower in specific circumstances such as emergency services, military operations or agricultural work. [Civil Aviation Authority] caa.co.uk Open source on caa.co.uk. This rule is useful when evaluating sightings, but only as contextual evidence. If an observer can confidently demonstrate that an illuminated object remained thousands of feet above the ground, an ordinary consumer drone becomes a less likely explanation. Conversely, an object hovering apparently below rooftop level or treetop height is broadly consistent with typical drone operations. However, several important qualifications remain: Human estimates of altitude are frequently inaccurate at night. Professional operators may have authorisations that differ from standard recreational rules. Terrain affects legal altitude because the limit is measured from the nearest point of the surface, not always from the observer's position. A drone operating above legal limits is still physically capable of doing so; illegality does not make it impossible. Therefore, exceeding a regulatory altitude does not automatically eliminate the drone hypothesis, nor does remaining below the limit prove that an object was a drone. 9:01 Can you fly a drone at night? You MUST KNOW these 2 THINGS in 2024 Channel: Altitude University · Views: 27.2K · Uploaded: January 2022 · Length: 9 minutes Open on YouTube Night Flying Rules Add Useful Context Night operations have become increasingly regulated because visibility is reduced for both operators and other airspace users. Current UK Civil Aviation Authority guidance requires drones flying at night in the Open Category to display a green flashing light throughout the flight to improve visibility and help distinguish unmanned aircraft from manned aircraft while maintaining visual line of sight. The rule is intended to increase safety rather than to create a unique visual signature that members of the public can use for identification. [Civil Aviation Authority] caa.co.uk Open source on caa.co.uk. This highlights an important limitation in interpreting sightings. A witness may report seeing a flashing green light and reasonably conclude that the object could be a legally operated drone. Equally, the absence of such a light does not prove the object was something unusual. The aircraft may have been operating unlawfully, the light may have been obscured by viewing angle, or the observer may simply have been too far away to perceive its colour accurately. 7:31 Flying YOUR Drone at Night in 2026: What YOU Need to Know! Channel: UAV Coach · Views: 57.5K · Uploaded: September 2023 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube Using Lights and Height Together Within reports of buzzing unidentified objects, lighting and apparent altitude become most useful when considered alongside other observations rather than in isolation. A combination of features that is broadly consistent with drone activity includes: A continuous electric buzzing sound. Slow movement or prolonged hovering. Bright flashing or coloured LEDs visible at relatively low apparent altitude. Flight remaining within a limited local area. By contrast, if the reported sound, movement, lighting behaviour and apparent height consistently conflict with known drone capabilities, investigators have stronger reasons to examine alternative explanations. Even then, discrepancies should first be checked against common sources of observational error before concluding that the object cannot have been a conventional aircraft. The key point is that navigation lights and legal altitude rules are valuable contextual tools. They help assess whether a drone explanation is plausible, but they cannot identify an aircraft by themselves or determine whether a reported UFO is genuinely unexplained . Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Do the Lights and Height Match a Drone?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Drone Pilot's Handbook By Adam Juniper The perfect companion for anyone buying (or thinking of buying) a drone, whether it's just for fun, to race against friends, or to give t... See on Amazon Book Remote Pilot Test Prep 2022: Study & Prepare: Pass Your Part... By Asa Test Prep Board eBundle: printed book and software download code Operating a drone for non hobby operations requires a Remote"},{"title":"Pitch Changes","url":"/pitch-changes/","description":"Changes in rotor speed can make a drone's buzz rise, fall or pulse as it hovers, climbs, descends or fights the wind.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Drones","level":3,"text":"Pitch Changes Why a Drone's Buzz Changes in Flight Drones Pitch Changes Pitch Changes Why a Drone's Buzz Changes in Flight Changes in rotor speed can make a drone's buzz rise, fall or pulse as it hovers, climbs, descends or fights the wind. Changes in rotor speed can make a drone's buzz rise, fall or pulse as it hovers, climbs, descends or fights the wind. mechanism How rotor speed changes pitch What hovering and climbing sound like When fluctuating noise supports a drone explanation ufo noises drones pitch changes Introduction One of the strongest clues that a buzzing \"UFO\" may actually be a multirotor drone is that its sound does not remain constant. A drone's buzz often rises, falls or develops a subtle pulsing quality while it appears to hover in place or begins to climb. These pitch changes are not random. They result from the flight controller making rapid adjustments to the speed of individual propellers to keep the aircraft stable, maintain altitude and respond to wind. Because these changes occur many times per second, listeners may perceive the sound as \"breathing\", \"warbling\" or periodically increasing in pitch. Understanding why this happens helps distinguish a conventional drone from reports of unexplained aerial sounds whose acoustic behaviour is genuinely inconsistent with known aircraft. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Stochastic analysis of a single rotor to quantify the effect of RPS variation on noise of hovering multirotors ScienceDirect... How Rotor Speed Changes the Pitch The dominant tones produced by a multirotor drone come from its spinning propellers rather than from the electric motors themselves. Every time a blade passes a fixed point, it generates a pressure pulse. The rate of these pulses, known as the blade passing frequency, is determined by the propeller's rotational speed and the number of blades. When rotor speed increases, the blade passing frequency rises, producing a noticeably higher pitched buzz. When speed decreases, the perceived pitch falls accordingly. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A Method to Measure and Model Acoustic Emissions of Multicopters PMC... Unlike a single rotor helicopter, a quadcopter or hexacopter continuously varies the speed of individual rotors to maintain balance. Even during what appears to be perfectly stationary flight, each motor speeds up or slows down slightly in response to tiny disturbances. Rather than producing one perfectly stable tone, the aircraft emits several closely related tones whose frequencies shift continuously. These overlapping changes create the characteristic fluctuating buzz associated with drones . [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Stochastic analysis of a single rotor to quantify the effect of RPS variation on noise of hovering multirotors ScienceDirect... What Hovering and Climbing Sound Like Hovering Is Constantly Active To an observer, a hovering drone may appear motionless, but its flight controller is making hundreds of corrections every second. Small gusts of wind, changes in battery output and tiny attitude adjustments all require slight changes in rotor speed. Research has shown that the amount of rotor speed variation increases when the aircraft experiences greater disturbance, such as higher payloads or larger tilt corrections. Those speed fluctuations alter the strength and exact frequency of the drone's tonal components, producing a subtle wavering sound instead of a perfectly steady hum. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Stochastic analysis of a single rotor to quantify the effect of RPS variation on noise of hovering multirotors ScienceDirect... Can You Explain Drone Pitch, Roll, And Yaw Basics? Channel: RC Toy Central Open on YouTube Climbing Produces a Higher Pitched Buzz When a drone climbs vertically, it must generate more lift than is needed merely to hover. The simplest way to do this on a conventional multirotor is by increasing the average speed of all rotors. As the propellers accelerate: the blade passing frequency increases; [researchgate.net] researchgate.net PDF) A Method to Measure and Model Acoustic Emissions of Multicopters PDF) A Method to Measure and Model Acoustic Emissions of Multicopters harmonic tones shift upward; the overall buzz often becomes brighter and slightly louder. To someone listening from the ground, the drone may seem to \"rev up\" before or during the climb, much like an electric fan speeding up. Once the aircraft reaches a new altitude and resumes hovering, the average rotor speed generally falls back, and the pitch correspondingly decreases. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)A Method to Measure and Model Acoustic Emissions of Multicopters PMC... Wind Can Produce Repeated Pitch Changes A drone holding position in windy conditions rarely maintains identical rotor speeds for long. Instead, it repeatedly increases thrust to resist gusts and reduces thrust when the disturbance passes. These corrections can produce: rhythmic rises and falls in pitch; irregular pulsing rather than a smooth tone; brief increases in loudness as thrust rises. The resulting sound can seem unusual to listeners expecting a constant mechanical hum, particularly if they cannot see the aircraft itself. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Stochastic analysis of a single rotor to quantify the effect of RPS variation on noise of hovering multirotors ScienceDirect... How Do Pitch, Roll, And Yaw Control A Drone? Channel: RC Toy Central Open on YouTube Why the Noise Sometimes Seems to Pulse The pitch heard by an observer is influenced by more than simple rotor speed. Multiple propellers operate at very similar—but not always identical—frequencies. As these tones overlap, listeners may perceive beating or modulation, where the sound appears to swell and fade even though the aircraft remains nearby. In addition, rapid control inputs shift the blade passing frequencies and their harmonics by small amounts. Studies of hovering multirotors have found that uncertainty and variation in rotor revolutions per second broaden and reshape these tonal peaks, helping explain why drone noise often sounds less stable than that of simpler rotating machinery. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Stochastic analysis of a single rotor to quantify the effect of RPS variation on noise of hovering multirotors ScienceDirect... Beginners Guide: Throttle Yaw Pitch and Roll Channel: JD Quad Open on YouTube When Fluctuating Noise Supports a Drone Explanation Pitch variation alone cannot identify an aerial object, but it can be an important consistency check when evaluating reports of buzzing UFOs. A drone explanation becomes more plausible when observers report several of the following together: the buzz rises as the object climbs and falls as it settles into a hover; the pitch changes repeatedly while the object appears stationary in windy conditions; the sound fluctuates in a smooth, mechanical manner rather than changing abruptly or discontinuously; the object makes short vertical movements accompanied by corresponding increases in the intensity or pitch of the buzz. These behaviours match how multirotor flight controllers regulate lift through continual rotor speed adjustments rather than maintaining a fixed engine speed. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com Stochastic analysis of a single rotor to quantify the effect of RPS variation on noise of hovering multirotors ScienceDirect... What This Means for Identifying Buzzing UFO Reports Witnesses sometimes interpret changing pitch as evidence that an object is using unfamiliar propulsion. In reality, moderate rises and falls in a buzzing tone are expected characteristics of modern multirotor drones. Hovering is an active process rather than a passive one, and climbing requires additional thrust that naturally raises rotor speed and the blade passing frequency. This does not mean every buzzing aerial object is a drone. However, when a report combines a hovering light with a buzz that subtly rises during climbs, falls after manoeuvres and fluctuates while holding position in the wind, those acoustic characteristics are well aligned with the known behaviour of multirotor aircraft rather than representing an inherently anomalous sound source. [sciencedirect.com] sciencedirect.com Stochastic analysis of a single rotor to quantify the effect of RPS variation on noise of hovering multirotors ScienceDirect... Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why a Drone's Buzz Changes in Flight. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Drone Pilot's Handbook By Adam Juniper The perfect companion for anyone buying (or thinking of buying) a drone, whether it's just for fun, to race against friends, or to give t... See on Amazon Book Make: Drones By David McGriffy Make: Drones will help the widest possible audience understand how drones work by providing several DIY drone projects based on the"},{"title":"Drone Limits","url":"/drone-limits/","description":"A nearby silent object or one making sustained high-speed right-angle turns would be difficult to reconcile with known multirotor drones.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Drones","level":3,"text":"Drone Limits When the Drone Explanation Stops Fitting Drones Drone Limits Drone Limits When the Drone Explanation Stops Fitting A nearby silent object or one making sustained high speed right angle turns would be difficult to reconcile with known multirotor drones. A nearby silent object or one making sustained high speed right angle turns would be difficult to reconcile with known multirotor drones. dataset evidence Why nearby drones should usually be audible Motion claims beyond multirotor capability How distance errors can imitate impossible performance ufo noises drones drone limits Introduction Most buzzing lights in the night sky can be explained by ordinary drones once distance, lighting and acoustics are taken into account. However, the drone explanation becomes increasingly difficult to sustain when reports combine two particular features: an object that appears genuinely close yet remains completely silent, or an object that performs manoeuvres well beyond the physical limits of known multirotor aircraft. Neither feature automatically proves that an unidentified object is extraordinary, because human observers can misjudge distance, speed and direction. Even so, these are among the strongest reasons investigators hesitate before attributing a sighting to a conventional drone. The key question is not whether a witness reported silence or extreme motion, but whether those observations remain consistent after considering known limitations of perception and the performance envelope of real aircraft. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsAcoustic signature measurement of small multi rotor unmanned aircraft systems N Kloet, S Watkins, R Clothier, 2017February... Why Nearby Drones Should Usually Be Audible Small electric drones are quieter than helicopters, but they are not silent. Their characteristic sound comes from rapidly rotating propellers, producing a tonal, pulsing buzz that changes as motor speed varies. Acoustic measurements consistently show that sound level falls rapidly with distance—roughly 6 dB for each doubling of range under ideal conditions—but a drone that is genuinely nearby should normally still be detectable unless environmental factors interfere. [Sage Journals] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsAcoustic signature measurement of small multi rotor unmanned aircraft systems N Kloet, S Watkins, R Clothier, 2017February... Silence therefore becomes noteworthy when several conditions are met simultaneously: The object is estimated to be within tens of metres rather than hundreds. Background noise is low enough that ordinary environmental sounds are easily heard. The witness has an unobstructed line of sight. Wind direction or shielding cannot reasonably explain why sound failed to reach the observer. Even then, caution is required. Modern research shows that drone noise is highly directional and depends on the aircraft's orientation, flight mode and the observer's position. A drone viewed from certain angles may sound noticeably quieter than one flying the same path from another direction. Wind, vegetation, buildings and terrain can further reduce or redirect the sound reaching a listener. [DOI.org] doi.org May 20, 2026... Published: May 20, 2026 This means that \"I heard nothing\" is not, by itself, decisive evidence against a drone. Instead, it becomes significant only when supported by reliable estimates that place the object close enough that its absence of sound would genuinely be unexpected. 12:21 Drones, Airplanes, Or UAPs? How To Tell The Difference Business Insider Channel: Business Insider · Views: 262.1K · Uploaded: December 2024 · Length: 12 minutes Open on YouTube Motion Claims Beyond Multirotor Capability Consumer and professional multirotor drones are remarkably agile. They can hover precisely, climb rapidly, accelerate briskly and reverse direction much faster than conventional aircraft. These capabilities explain why inexperienced observers sometimes describe drone flight as unusual. There are, however, reported behaviours that exceed what current multirotor designs can plausibly achieve. Examples commonly cited in UFO reports include: Instantaneous acceleration from stationary to extreme speed without visible transition. Sharp right angle turns at high speed without slowing. Abrupt stops that appear to involve no braking distance. Sustained hypersonic looking motion within the atmosphere. Rapid oscillations or repeated high speed directional changes that would impose enormous inertial loads. Such descriptions do not fit the known flight mechanics of battery powered quadcopters or similar multirotor systems. Real drones must obey aerodynamic limits, thrust limits, battery constraints and structural loads. High speed turns require banking, reduced speed or increased turning radius; they cannot simply ignore inertia. Even specialised racing drones, while exceptionally manoeuvrable, remain constrained by these physical requirements. That does not automatically invalidate witness testimony. Instead, it shifts attention towards whether the reported motion reflects the object's actual movement or the observer's interpretation of what they saw. How Distance Errors Can Imitate Impossible Performance One of the largest sources of error in aerial observations is uncertainty about distance. A light with no familiar background reference can appear much closer or farther away than it really is. Because perceived speed depends directly on estimated distance, even modest range errors can dramatically alter apparent performance. For example: A distant aircraft crossing the sky can appear to \"shoot away\" if the observer assumes it is nearby. A small change in viewing angle may look like a sharp right angle turn when the object is actually following a smooth curved path. Two separate lights switching attention between one another may be remembered as a single object making impossible jumps. Brief losses of visual contact behind cloud, trees or glare can create the impression of sudden acceleration or disappearance. Psychologists studying visual perception have long recognised that humans estimate angular motion far more reliably than true speed or distance. Without accurate range information, even experienced observers can greatly overestimate acceleration and manoeuvrability. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Open source on faa.gov. This is particularly important at night, when the object itself may be visible only as a bright point of light with few surrounding visual references. 57:40 How UAP Achieve \"Impossible\" Speeds & Acceleration Kevin Knuth Channel: Tim Ventura · Views: 20.0K · Uploaded: November 2021 · Length: 57 minutes Open on YouTube When Silence and Extreme Motion Together Deserve Closer Attention A report becomes more difficult to reconcile with an ordinary drone when multiple independent observations point in the same direction. For example, investigators would pay closer attention if: Several observers at different locations independently reported the object as silent despite apparent close proximity. Video, radar or other sensor data supported unusually rapid manoeuvres rather than relying on visual impressions alone. Environmental conditions made acoustic masking unlikely. The reported motion remained inconsistent with known drone performance after careful reconstruction of distance, perspective and timing. By contrast, a single witness estimating both extraordinary speed and complete silence from an unknown distance provides much weaker evidence. Human memory, perception and estimation errors can produce exactly this combination without requiring any unconventional aircraft. A Balanced Interpretation Silence and extreme manoeuvring are among the strongest observational challenges to a routine drone explanation, but neither feature is conclusive in isolation. Drone acoustics vary with direction, altitude and weather, while apparent speed and acceleration depend heavily on uncertain distance estimates. Research into drone noise confirms that real multirotors normally produce detectable acoustic signatures at close range, although those signatures are not equally audible from every direction or under every environmental condition. [sagepub.com] journals.sagepub.com Sage JournalsAcoustic signature measurement of small multi rotor unmanned aircraft systems N Kloet, S Watkins, R Clothier, 2017February... The most persuasive cases are therefore those in which careful investigation cannot reconcile multiple lines of evidence with known drone behaviour. Reports based solely on subjective impressions of silence or seemingly impossible motion remain intriguing, but they require corroboration before they can confidently be said to fall outside the capabilities of ordinary multirotor aircraft. 8:59 Quantum physicists analyze pentagon US navy UFO video footage Channel: NYU Quantum Technology Lab · Views: 6.8K · Uploaded: June 2021 · Length: 8 minutes Open on YouTube Amazon book picks"},{"title":"Night Distance","url":"/night-distance/","description":"Darkness, wind and reflections can make a nearby drone look distant while its buzzing seems to come from somewhere else.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Drones","level":3,"text":"Night Distance Why Drone Lights and Buzz Seem Misaligned Drones Night Distance Night Distance Why Drone Lights and Buzz Seem Misaligned Darkness, wind and reflections can make a nearby drone look distant while its buzzing seems to come from somewhere else. Darkness, wind and reflections can make a nearby drone look distant while its buzzing seems to come from somewhere else. population context Why darkness distorts size and distance How wind and buildings redirect sound Clues that reconnect the light with the noise ufo noises drones night distance Introduction When people report a bright light in the night sky but insist that its buzzing seemed to come from somewhere else, the mismatch can feel mysterious. In many cases, however, the combination of darkness, limited visual references and complex sound propagation provides a conventional explanation. A nearby drone may appear much farther away than it really is, while wind, echoes and the directional nature of rotor noise make the buzzing seem detached from the visible light. These perception errors are well documented in research on spatial hearing and aviation acoustics, and they are especially relevant when assessing reports of buzzing \"UFOs\" that turn out to be ordinary drones . [caa.co.uk] caa.co.uk Civil Aviation AuthorityCAP2962: The Effects of Emerging Technology Aviation Noise on Humans UK Civil Aviation AuthorityAugust 27, 2024... Published: August 27, 2024 Why darkness distorts size and distance At night, observers lose many of the visual cues that normally help estimate the position of an aircraft. Trees, buildings, hills and cloud layers become difficult to judge, leaving only a small cluster of lights against a dark background. This creates several common perceptual errors: Size becomes ambiguous. A bright navigation light can appear to belong to a much larger object if the aircraft itself cannot be seen. Distance is underestimated or overestimated. Without foreground landmarks, a drone tens of metres away may be perceived as hundreds of metres distant, or vice versa. Height becomes difficult to judge. A low flying drone can appear to hover high overhead when the observer lacks a reliable horizon. Movement is misread. Slow sideways motion or hovering can appear unusual because there are few fixed reference points against which to compare it. Vision normally helps calibrate how humans interpret three dimensional space. Research on auditory distance perception has shown that visual information plays an important role in maintaining accurate estimates of where sounds originate. When visual references are degraded or absent, localisation errors become more likely. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Partial visual loss disrupts the relationship between judged room size and sound source distance PubMed... For reports involving alleged UFO noises , this matters because the apparent position of the light may already be incorrect before the sound is considered. How wind and buildings redirect sound Even if the light is correctly identified, the accompanying buzz may not appear to come from the same direction. Unlike light, sound travels relatively slowly through air and is strongly influenced by the environment. Rotor noise from multirotor drones contains prominent tonal components that interact with surrounding terrain and structures in ways that can alter where listeners believe the sound originates. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Quantification of the Psychoacoustic Effect of Noise from Small Unmanned Aerial Vehicles PMCAugust 24, 2021... Published: August 24, 2021 12:15 Spatial Disorientation Explained: Deadly Flight Illusions Every Pilot Must Know Channel: Epic Flight Academy · Views: 9.1K · Uploaded: December 2025 · Length: 12 minutes Open on YouTube Wind can shift the apparent source Wind affects sound far more than it affects light. A listener standing downwind from a drone may hear the buzzing more clearly because the wind carries acoustic energy towards them. Someone standing upwind may hear very little despite looking directly at the aircraft. As wind strength and direction change, the perceived source of the buzz may seem to drift independently of the visible light. These changes do not move the aircraft itself; they only alter how efficiently its sound reaches the observer. Echoes create misleading directions Urban and suburban environments introduce another complication. Buildings, walls, parked vehicles and even large industrial structures reflect sound. If reflected sound reaches the listener nearly as strongly as the direct sound, the brain may attribute the source to the reflected direction instead of the drone's true position. The effect is most noticeable when: the drone is flying between buildings; the listener is close to reflective surfaces; direct sound is weakened by wind or obstacles; surrounding ambient noise is otherwise low. The result can be a convincing impression that the buzzing comes from behind, beside or above the visible light. Rotor noise is not equally loud in every direction Drone propellers do not radiate sound uniformly. Their tonal blade passing frequencies and harmonics vary with aircraft orientation, rotor speed and manoeuvres, producing directional changes in perceived loudness. A drone banking or turning may therefore sound stronger from one position than another without changing location dramatically. [nih.gov] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Quantification of the Psychoacoustic Effect of Noise from Small Unmanned Aerial Vehicles PMCAugust 24, 2021... Published: August 24, 2021 Why the light and buzz seem disconnected Observers often assume that a nearby sound must belong to a nearby object. Under night time conditions, that assumption is not always reliable. Several effects combine to separate the perceived light from the perceived sound: Visual distance errors make the illuminated object appear farther away than it really is. Acoustic reflections redirect the buzzing. Wind changes which listener hears the strongest sound. Low ambient noise makes intermittent buzzing seem unusually prominent. Expectation encourages the brain to link whichever sound is most noticeable with the brightest visible object. Importantly, this does not imply that witnesses are inventing their observations. Instead, it illustrates that human perception naturally integrates imperfect visual and acoustic information into a single interpretation, even when the two signals have been distorted by the environment. [Federal Aviation Administration] faa.gov Open source on faa.gov. 7:34 Night Illusions (Private Pilot Lesson 15d) Channel: Cyndy Hollman · Views: 43.9K · Uploaded: November 2020 · Length: 7 minutes Open on YouTube Clues that reconnect the light with the noise When investigating whether a buzzing light is likely to be a drone rather than something unidentified, a few practical observations can reduce the chance of being misled by night time perception. Look for patterns rather than relying on a single impression: Observe whether the buzzing becomes stronger when you move a short distance. Large changes over only a few metres often suggest reflections or shielding by nearby structures. Compare the object's apparent motion against fixed landmarks such as rooftops or trees instead of judging it against empty sky. Notice whether the sound changes during climbs, descents or turns. Drone rotor speed commonly varies during these manoeuvres, producing changes in pitch and loudness. If multiple observers are present, compare descriptions from different locations. People standing only tens of metres apart may report different apparent sound directions because of local reflections and wind. These checks cannot identify every aerial object, but they help distinguish genuine anomalies from the well understood ways darkness and acoustics separate visible lights from their associated sounds. What this means for buzzing UFO reports Night time reports of buzzing aerial lights deserve careful evaluation, but a perceived mismatch between where a light is seen and where a buzz is heard is not, by itself, evidence of an unconventional object. The combination of poor night time depth perception, ambiguous visual scale, wind driven sound propagation, directional rotor noise and echoes provides a plausible explanation for many cases in which a drone appears visually distant while its buzzing seems to originate elsewhere. Recognising these perception limits helps avoid treating an ordinary acoustic illusion as evidence that the light and the sound must have come from different objects. [caa.co.uk] caa.co.uk Civil Aviation AuthorityCAP2962: The Effects of Emerging Technology Aviation Noise on Humans UK Civil Aviation AuthorityAugust 27, 2024... Published: August 27, 2024 Lecture 28 : Meteorological Conditions and Sound Propagation Channel: NPTEL IIT Kharagpur Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides"},{"title":"Myths","url":"/myths/","description":"No verified noise proves extraterrestrial technology, and unexplained audio usually reflects limited evidence rather than a distinctive craft.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises","level":2,"text":"Myths Can Any Sound Prove a UFO Is Extraordinary? UFO Noises Myths Myths Can Any Sound Prove a UFO Is Extraordinary? No verified noise proves extraterrestrial technology, and unexplained audio usually reflects limited evidence rather than a distinctive craft. No verified noise proves extraterrestrial technology, and unexplained audio usually reflects limited evidence rather than a distinctive craft. dataset evidence The myth of a signature UFO noise What unexplained really means Why additional evidence is essential ufo noises myths Introduction Can a strange sound by itself prove that a UFO is an extraordinary craft? Based on the available evidence, the answer is no. Witnesses have reported humming, buzzing, whistling, roaring and complete silence in association with unidentified aerial objects for decades, but no sound has been shown to be unique to an unidentified aerial phenomenon (UAP), let alone to extraterrestrial technology. The strongest evidence instead shows that unusual audio is often ambiguous: it may be genuine, remain temporarily unexplained , and still provide no reliable basis for concluding that an object is extraordinary. Sound becomes meaningful only when it can be corroborated by additional evidence such as multiple independent observations, instrument recordings, radar data or physical measurements. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies Frequently Asked Questions Center for UFO StudiesFrequently Asked Questions Center for UFO Studies... The Myth of a Signature UFO Noise A persistent belief in UFO folklore is that genuine UFOs produce a distinctive acoustic signature—a low electrical hum, an eerie vibration or an otherworldly mechanical tone that immediately identifies the craft. Historical reports certainly contain recurring descriptions, but recurrence is not the same as uniqueness. One of the largest specialised analyses of reports involving alleged electromagnetic effects found that humming was the most commonly described sound. However, it appeared in only a minority of cases, while witnesses also described whining, buzzing, whistles, roaring, whooshing and many one off sounds. The authors concluded that no single sound could be regarded as characteristic of UFO events. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org UFO REPORTS INVOLVING VEHICLE INTERFERENCE Center for UFO StudiesUFO REPORTSNovember 3, 2025... Published: November 3, 2025 This diversity creates an important logical problem. If an alleged phenomenon can sound like many different things—or make no sound at all—it cannot be identified acoustically. A hum may resemble electrical infrastructure, distant aircraft, industrial machinery or atmospheric vibration just as easily as an unidentified object. Even organisations that investigate UFO reports from an open but analytical perspective caution against treating any single witness description as diagnostic. Their emphasis is on evaluating the entire event rather than relying on one reported feature. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAABOUT BUFORA BUFORAABOUT BUFORA What \"Unexplained\" Really Means An unexplained sound is not the same as evidence for an extraordinary cause. Investigators often distinguish between three different situations: Identified: the source is confidently determined, such as an aircraft, drone, industrial process or meteor. Unidentified: insufficient information exists to determine the source. Extraordinary: evidence demonstrates something beyond currently understood technology or natural phenomena. Only the first category reaches a firm conclusion. The second simply reflects missing information rather than proof of an exotic explanation. This distinction matters because sound alone usually provides relatively little information. Human listeners have difficulty estimating the distance, direction and speed of unfamiliar sounds, especially at night or when low frequencies are involved. Acoustic perception is also influenced by terrain, weather, echoes and expectations. Consequently, many unusual sounds remain genuinely unidentified without supporting the conclusion that they originated from an extraordinary vehicle. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org Center for UFO Studies Frequently Asked Questions Center for UFO StudiesFrequently Asked Questions Center for UFO Studies... Why Additional Evidence Is Essential Modern scientific approaches to studying UAP increasingly rely on multiple independent measurements rather than eyewitness testimony alone. Projects designed to investigate anomalous aerial phenomena recommend combining visual imaging with radar, radio frequency monitoring, environmental sensors and microphones. The purpose of including acoustic sensors is not because sound can identify a UFO by itself, but because it becomes more informative when matched with other independently recorded data. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. For example, an unusual hum recorded simultaneously with: calibrated video from multiple viewpoints, precise timing, radar observations, weather measurements, and aircraft traffic records would allow investigators to test competing explanations far more effectively than an isolated witness account. Without that broader context, even a high quality recording often leaves fundamental questions unanswered, including how far away the source was, what produced it and whether the sound actually originated from the object being observed. 8:14 Are they aliens? Channel: ReasonTV · Views: 20.7K · Uploaded: October 2022 · Length: 8 minutes Open on YouTube Why Witnesses Often Reach Extraordinary Conclusions People naturally connect events that occur at the same time. If an unfamiliar light appears overhead while an unusual sound is heard nearby, it is understandable that observers assume both came from the same source. Yet coincidence and perceptual uncertainty complicate that conclusion. Several factors can reinforce this association: low frequency sounds are notoriously difficult to locate; echoes can make sounds appear to originate elsewhere; darkness reduces visual reference points; unusual experiences become more memorable than ordinary ones; later recollection may strengthen perceived links between separate events. None of these effects imply dishonesty. Instead, they illustrate why eyewitness reports remain valuable observations while still requiring independent verification. The \"Hum\" as a Case Study in Misinterpretation The long running phenomenon known as The Hum illustrates how an unexplained sound can generate extraordinary speculation despite limited supporting evidence. Communities in several countries have reported persistent low frequency humming or droning that some residents hear while others do not. Because the source is often difficult to isolate, various theories have linked the phenomenon to secret technology, underground activity or UFOs. However, investigations have identified numerous conventional possibilities depending on location, including industrial equipment, transportation infrastructure, ventilation systems, environmental vibration and, in some cases, physiological factors affecting hearing. There is no credible evidence that The Hum represents a universal phenomenon with a single extraordinary cause, nor has any consistent connection with UFO sightings been established. [Wikipedia] Wikipedia List of unexplained sounds List of unexplained sounds The lesson extends beyond The Hum itself: persistent uncertainty does not automatically increase the probability of an exotic explanation. How Investigators Assess Reported UFO Sounds Experienced investigators generally avoid asking whether a sound \"matches\" previous UFO reports. Instead, they ask questions that can be tested. Typical considerations include: Was the sound recorded or only remembered? Were multiple independent witnesses present? Did anyone observe the source directly? Could aircraft, drones , weather or industrial activity account for it? Was the timing consistent across all observations? Is there supporting radar, photographic or environmental evidence? These questions shift the focus from recognising supposedly mysterious sounds to evaluating the quality of the evidence itself. Organisations dedicated to systematic UFO investigation likewise stress careful documentation and critical evaluation rather than assuming that unusual characteristics indicate extraordinary origins. [BUFORA] bufora.org.uk BUFORAINVESTIGATIONS BUFORA BUFORAINVESTIGATIONS BUFORA 1:01:40 Videogame Science and UFOs with Mick West Channel: Center for Inquiry · Views: 7.1K · Uploaded: April 2023 · Length: 1 hour 1 minute Open on YouTube What the Available Evidence Supports Current evidence does not support the claim that any particular sound proves the presence of an extraordinary aerial craft. Historical reports include genuine descriptions of humming, buzzing, whining, roaring, crackling and complete silence, but none consistently distinguish unidentified objects from ordinary"},{"title":"Multi Sensor Tests","url":"/multi-sensor-tests/","description":"Audio becomes more useful when its timing can be compared with calibrated video, radar, weather records and aircraft traffic data.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Myths","level":3,"text":"Multi Sensor Tests What Evidence Makes a UFO Sound Convincing? Myths Multi Sensor Tests Multi Sensor Tests What Evidence Makes a UFO Sound Convincing? Audio becomes more useful when its timing can be compared with calibrated video, radar, weather records and aircraft traffic data. Audio becomes more useful when its timing can be compared with calibrated video, radar, weather records and aircraft traffic data. policy intervention Matching audio with independent instruments Testing timing and source consistency How combined data eliminates ordinary explanations ufo noises myths multi sensor tests Introduction A recorded hum, buzz or roar does not become convincing evidence of an unidentified aerial phenomenon (UAP) simply because it sounds unusual. Modern investigations instead ask a more demanding question: does the sound agree with independent measurements collected at the same time? This multi sensor approach is central to current scientific and government led UAP research. Rather than treating microphones as proof on their own, investigators compare audio with calibrated video, radar or radio observations, weather measurements, aircraft tracking data and precise timestamps. When several independent systems tell the same physical story, confidence increases. When they disagree, the sound is often explained as a recording artefact, environmental noise or a conventional aircraft. This shift from isolated witness accounts to synchronised sensor networks represents one of the biggest changes in how UFO sound claims are evaluated today. [Galileo Project] galileo.hsites.harvard.edu Galileo ProjectThe Scientific Investigation of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena (UAP) Using Multimodal Ground Based Observatories The Gali... Matching Audio with Independent Instruments The strongest investigations begin by asking whether every sensor observed the same event. A microphone may capture a low frequency rumble while a camera records a distant light. That coincidence alone is not enough. Investigators test whether the timing, direction and expected physics all match. If the sound arrives too early, too late or from a different direction than the object appears to be travelling, the simplest explanation is that the two observations are unrelated. Modern multi sensor observatories therefore synchronise all instruments using highly accurate clocks. Each frame of video, acoustic waveform and environmental measurement receives a precise timestamp so that investigators can reconstruct the sequence of events rather than relying on human memory. This allows analysts to distinguish between genuine simultaneous observations and unrelated background sounds that merely happened nearby. [Galileo Project] galileo.hsites.harvard.edu Galileo ProjectThe Scientific Investigation of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena (UAP) Using Multimodal Ground Based Observatories The Gali... Scientific UAP observatory proposals typically combine: Wide field and narrow field calibrated cameras. Microphones covering infrasonic, audible and ultrasonic frequencies. Passive radar or radio frequency receivers. Weather stations measuring wind, pressure and humidity. Electric and magnetic field sensors. Position and timing systems that keep every instrument synchronised. No single instrument is expected to identify an extraordinary object. Instead, each contributes one independent measurement that can either reinforce or contradict the others. [Galileo Project] galileo.hsites.harvard.edu Galileo ProjectThe Scientific Investigation of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena (UAP) Using Multimodal Ground Based Observatories The Gali... 4:00:26 Public Meeting on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (Official NASA Broadcast) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 358.7K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 4 hours Open on YouTube Testing Timing and Source Consistency The most valuable test is whether the recorded sound behaves as expected for a real physical source. Sound travels much more slowly than light. If an aerial object is filmed several kilometres away, any genuine acoustic signal should arrive after a predictable delay determined by atmospheric conditions. A sound that appears before the visual event, or with an impossible delay, suggests that the recording captured something else entirely. Investigators also compare: Direction: Does the sound originate from the same bearing as the visual target? Movement: Does the apparent motion of the sound source match the object's measured trajectory? Frequency changes: Are pitch shifts consistent with expected Doppler effects for the measured speed? Duration: Does the sound persist for the same interval as the observed object? Intensity: Is the recorded loudness physically plausible for the estimated distance? Because these comparisons rely on independently measured quantities, they are far more informative than witness impressions such as \"the sound seemed to follow the object\". A mismatch in even one of these areas can eliminate an otherwise persuasive claim. [Galileo Project] galileo.hsites.harvard.edu Galileo ProjectThe Scientific Investigation of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena (UAP) Using Multimodal Ground Based Observatories The Gali... 51:24 Breaking Down UAP Footage with the Head of The Pentagon’s UAP Taskforce, Dr. Jon Kosloski Channel: StarTalk · Views: 1.5M · Uploaded: August 2025 · Length: 51 minutes Open on YouTube How Combined Data Eliminates Ordinary Explanations Most unusual aerial sounds have several ordinary possibilities. Multi sensor evidence allows investigators to test those possibilities directly instead of relying on speculation. For example, a mysterious humming noise may initially seem remarkable. However, if aircraft transponder data (such as ADS B broadcasts), calibrated video and the acoustic spectrum all point to a distant aircraft climbing behind cloud, the mystery largely disappears. Likewise, weather measurements can identify conditions that bend or reflect sound over unexpectedly long distances. Temperature inversions, wind gradients and humidity all affect how sound propagates. A microphone alone cannot distinguish these effects, but environmental sensors can reveal whether unusual atmospheric conditions were present. [Galileo Project] galileo.hsites.harvard.edu Galileo ProjectThe Scientific Investigation of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena (UAP) Using Multimodal Ground Based Observatories The Gali... Independent datasets are also checked against: Civil and military aircraft traffic records. Satellite and rocket activity. Meteorological observations. Known drone operations where available. Local industrial or infrastructure noise sources. Each successful match removes one conventional explanation from consideration. Only events that survive multiple independent checks remain candidates for further investigation. Why Calibration Matters More Than Dramatic Audio A dramatic recording is far less valuable than a calibrated one. Consumer recordings often contain automatic gain control, wind noise reduction, compression and microphone limitations that alter the original sound. Phones also rarely preserve detailed information about microphone response or absolute sound pressure levels. Research observatories instead calibrate microphones before deployment. Calibration establishes how accurately an instrument measures different frequencies and sound levels, allowing later analysis to distinguish genuine acoustic signatures from equipment artefacts. The same principle applies to cameras. Lens distortion, exposure settings and pointing direction are characterised before observations begin. Because every instrument's limitations are understood, unexpected measurements become easier to interpret scientifically rather than anecdotally. [Galileo Project] galileo.hsites.harvard.edu Galileo ProjectThe Scientific Investigation of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena (UAP) Using Multimodal Ground Based Observatories The Gali... Cross Checking Sensor Disagreements Disagreement between sensors is often as informative as agreement. Suppose a witness reports a loud humming object overhead, but: calibrated cameras record nothing, radar detects no corresponding target, nearby microphones do not record the hum, weather data show strong winds capable of carrying distant industrial noise. Rather than strengthening the UFO interpretation, these inconsistencies encourage investigators to examine perceptual errors, local environmental effects or recording limitations. Conversely, if multiple independent sensors simultaneously detect the same object's location, motion and physical characteristics, confidence that a real aerial object existed increases substantially—even if its exact identity remains unknown. Importantly, \"real object\" and \"extraordinary object\" are separate conclusions. Multi sensor agreement demonstrates that an event occurred; it does not by itself establish exotic technology or extraterrestrial origin. [Galileo Project] galileo.hsites.harvard.edu Galileo"},{"title":"Sound Signature","url":"/sound-signature/","description":"Large report collections contain hums, whistles, roars, buzzes and silence rather than one sound that reliably identifies a UFO.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Myths","level":3,"text":"Sound Signature Is There Really a Signature UFO Noise? Myths Sound Signature Sound Signature Is There Really a Signature UFO Noise? Large report collections contain hums, whistles, roars, buzzes and silence rather than one sound that reliably identifies a UFO. Large report collections contain hums, whistles, roars, buzzes and silence rather than one sound that reliably identifies a UFO. dataset evidence The range of sounds in historical reports Why recurring descriptions are not unique markers What a true acoustic signature would require ufo noises myths sound signature Introduction Decades of UFO and unidentified aerial phenomenon (UAP) reports provide an unusually large body of witness descriptions from which to ask a straightforward question: do these reports reveal a distinctive sound that reliably identifies a UFO? The available evidence says no. Although witnesses have described humming, buzzing, whistling, roaring, rushing air noises, crackling sounds and complete silence, no single acoustic pattern appears consistently enough to qualify as a unique signature. Instead, historical datasets show remarkable variation, with silence often reported more frequently than any particular sound. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New HamNovember 7, 2025... Published: November 7, 2025 That conclusion matters because an acoustic signature is a specific scientific concept. It is not merely a sound that appears repeatedly, but one that can distinguish a phenomenon from all plausible alternatives. Current UFO report collections do not meet that standard. They document recurring descriptions, but not a sound that is both consistent across cases and unique to unidentified objects. The Range of Sounds in Historical Reports The largest catalogues of UFO witness accounts include sound as one descriptive feature among many rather than treating it as a defining characteristic. The diversity is striking. The UFOCAT database, maintained by researchers associated with the Center for UFO Studies, includes a dedicated sound field for cases where audio was reported. Its coding guide lists dozens of different descriptions, including humming, buzzing, hissing, whistling, roaring, rumbling, whooshing, high pitched tones, engine like noises and many miscellaneous descriptions. More importantly, the single most common entry is \"NoSound\" , indicating that the witness reported complete silence. Humming and whistling are among the more frequently named sounds, but each still represents only a small minority of the overall catalogue. [Center for UFO Studies] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New HamNovember 7, 2025... Published: November 7, 2025 More recent attempts to organise historical UAP reports into structured datasets reach a similar conclusion. A 301 case analysis covering reports from 1947 to 2016 treated sound as one measurable characteristic alongside reported shape, estimated size, flight behaviour and electromagnetic effects. Rather than identifying one characteristic noise, the study found that reported silence was common, while audible cases covered multiple unrelated sound types. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com UFO TransparencyThe Reported Shape, Size, Kinematics, Electromagnetic Effects, and Presence of Sound of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena fro... Older catalogues reached much the same result. Analyses of close encounters frequently grouped reported sounds into broad families such as: low frequency hums or buzzing, whistles or high pitched tones, roaring or explosive sounds, rushing air or swishing noises, intermittent beeps or pulses. These categories overlap substantially with sounds produced by ordinary aircraft, atmospheric phenomena, machinery and other familiar sources. [UfoCui] ufocui.it Ufo Cui Effects of UFOs Upon People Effects of UFOs Upon PeopleOctober 4, 2025... Published: October 4, 2025 Listening for UFOs The Galileo Project's Search for UAP with Andy Mead Merged EP0115 Channel: Merged Podcast Open on YouTube Why Recurring Descriptions Are Not Unique Markers The fact that witnesses repeatedly mention humming or buzzing does not make those sounds diagnostic. For an acoustic feature to identify a specific phenomenon, it must satisfy two conditions: Consistency: it should appear in most genuine cases. Specificity: it should rarely occur in unrelated situations. The historical record satisfies neither requirement. Humming illustrates the problem. It appears often enough to be recognisable as a recurring description, yet many witnesses report no sound at all. Others describe whistles, roaring, crackling or entirely different noises. Meanwhile, low frequency humming is also associated with electrical transformers, industrial equipment, helicopters, drones , atmospheric conditions and many ordinary aircraft operating at distance. A witness hearing a hum therefore has no reliable basis for distinguishing an unidentified aerial object from numerous conventional possibilities using sound alone. [cufos.org] cufos.org USER'S GUIDE UFOCAT 2023 Concord, New HamNovember 7, 2025... Published: November 7, 2025 The same reasoning applies to reported silence. Silent flight may sound unusual when observers believe an object should be noisy, but silence itself cannot function as an identifying signature. Electric aircraft, gliders, distant drones, balloons , viewing geometry and atmospheric conditions can all reduce perceived sound. Conversely, many witnesses who report dramatic manoeuvres also report hearing nothing at all. The absence of sound is therefore no more unique than the presence of a hum. [UFO Transparency] ufotransparency.com UFO TransparencyThe Reported Shape, Size, Kinematics, Electromagnetic Effects, and Presence of Sound of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena fro... What a True Acoustic Signature Would Require Scientific acoustics defines a signature as a repeatable, measurable pattern rather than a subjective description. A convincing UFO acoustic signature would require evidence such as: calibrated audio recordings from multiple independent microphones; known microphone positions to estimate source location; synchronised video showing the same object; environmental measurements accounting for wind, terrain and echoes; elimination of aircraft, vehicles and other conventional sound sources; repeated detection of the same frequency structure across unrelated events. Historical witness reports rarely satisfy these conditions. Most consist of personal recollections recorded after the event, without instrumental measurements that would allow detailed acoustic analysis. Recognising this limitation, modern scientific proposals for studying UAP recommend multimodal observation systems. These combine optical cameras with radar, radio frequency monitoring, environmental sensors and microphones. In this framework, microphones are valuable because they can corroborate other observations—not because sound alone can identify an anomalous object. [arXiv] arxiv.org The Scientific Investigation of Unidentified Aerial Phenomena (UAP) Using Multimodal Ground Based ObservatoriesMay 29, 2023... Published: May 29, 2023 What Does a UFO Sound Like? Actual Recordings of UAP sounds Channel: Out of the Dark Investigations Open on YouTube Why Witnesses Hear Different Things The diversity of reported sounds is not necessarily surprising. Several factors can alter how an observer experiences an unfamiliar airborne sound: atmospheric temperature layers can bend sound waves; wind direction can amplify or suppress certain frequencies; echoes from terrain or buildings may distort the original sound; low frequency vibrations are difficult for people to locate accurately; distance between object and witness may be misjudged. Human memory introduces additional variation. Witnesses naturally compare unfamiliar sounds with familiar ones, producing descriptions such as \"like a transformer\", \"like a swarm of bees\" or \"like a jet\". These comparisons help communicate experience but do not constitute precise acoustic measurements. Consequently, recurring descriptive language is expected even when underlying physical causes differ. What the Historical Record Actually Supports Taken together, large report collections support a more restrained conclusion than popular UFO folklore often suggests. They indicate that: witnesses sometimes report distinctive sounds; humming, buzzing and whistling recur more often than many other descriptions; complete silence is at least as prominent as any individual sound category; no reported sound consistently appears across the majority of cases; every commonly reported sound also occurs in ordinary natural or technological settings. The available evidence therefore does not reveal a unique acoustic signature for UFOs. Instead, decades of reports show that sound is highly variable and, by itself, cannot reliably distinguish unidentified aerial objects from conventional explanations. Acoustic"},{"title":"False Linking","url":"/false-linking/","description":"Timing, darkness, echoes and poor low-frequency localization can make an unrelated sound seem to come from an unfamiliar light.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Myths","level":3,"text":"False Linking Did That Strange Sound Really Come From the UFO? Myths False Linking False Linking Did That Strange Sound Really Come From the UFO? Timing, darkness, echoes and poor low frequency localization can make an unrelated sound seem to come from an unfamiliar light. Timing, darkness, echoes and poor low frequency localization can make an unrelated sound seem to come from an unfamiliar light. population context How simultaneous events become mentally linked Why low sounds and echoes confuse direction Ways investigators test whether light and sound matched ufo noises myths false linking Introduction When witnesses report both an unusual light and an unusual sound, it is natural to assume they came from the same source. However, research on human perception shows that events occurring at roughly the same time are often mentally linked even when they are physically unrelated. This does not mean witnesses are being dishonest. Rather, the brain automatically combines visual and auditory information to create a coherent account of what happened. In UFO reports, this tendency can make a distant aircraft, electrical hum, animal call or industrial noise seem to belong to an unfamiliar light overhead. Understanding this mechanism helps explain why sound reports vary so widely and why investigators treat sound–light associations as a hypothesis to test rather than as evidence that the light itself produced the noise. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)Broad generalisation of the ventriloquism aftereffect across sound frequencies PMCMarch 7, 2026... Published: March 7, 2026 How simultaneous events become mentally linked Humans are exceptionally good at detecting patterns, especially when unexpected events occur together. If an unfamiliar light appears in the sky at the same moment a person notices a buzzing or rumbling sound, the brain tends to assume they share a common cause unless there is evidence to the contrary. Psychologists studying multisensory perception have shown that vision often influences where people believe a sound originated. This is known as visual capture , or the ventriloquism effect. The classic example is a ventriloquist's puppet: although the sound comes from the performer, observers perceive it as coming from the moving puppet. The same principle applies more generally whenever visual information is judged to be more reliable than auditory location cues. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)An f MRI Study of the Ventriloquism Effect PubMed Central (PMC)An fMRI Study of the Ventriloquism Effect PMC... This mechanism is particularly relevant during unexpected night time observations because: the visual object may be unusual or emotionally salient; the sound source may be hidden by darkness; there is little surrounding information to judge distance or direction accurately; and attention becomes focused on the unusual light, encouraging the brain to bind nearby sensory events into a single experience. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)An f MRI Study of the Ventriloquism Effect PubMed Central (PMC)An fMRI Study of the Ventriloquism Effect PMC... Importantly, this is a normal feature of perception rather than a flaw unique to UFO witnesses. Visual Illusions: Catching the Brain Creating Reality World Science Festival Channel: World Science Festival Open on YouTube Why low sounds and echoes confuse direction Even without an unusual light, humans are not equally good at locating every type of sound. Low frequency sounds present a particular challenge because the brain relies heavily on tiny timing differences between the ears to estimate direction. Those cues become less precise when sounds are distant, diffuse or mixed with environmental reflections. Narrowband sounds are also harder to localise than broadband sounds that contain a wider range of frequencies. [nature.com] nature.com March 7, 2026... Published: March 7, 2026 Night time environments introduce further complications: Echoes and reverberation: Buildings, hills, forests and clouds can reflect sound, making it appear to come from somewhere other than its original source. Temperature inversions: Certain atmospheric conditions can bend sound over long distances, allowing traffic, trains or industrial machinery to be heard unexpectedly far from their source. Missing visual references: Without landmarks, estimating whether a sound is nearby or kilometres away becomes much more difficult. These factors mean a witness may sincerely perceive a distant rumble as coming from a bright object overhead when the sound and light are unrelated. Why the timing can seem convincing A common reason witnesses become confident that a sound belonged to a UFO is that the sound appears to change as the light moves or disappears. Several ordinary situations can create this impression: a vehicle emerges from behind terrain just as an aircraft or bright planet is noticed; wind carries intermittent sounds that seem to strengthen or fade with the observer's attention; a witness notices a background hum only after becoming alert to the unusual light; separate events begin or end close together by coincidence. Because people naturally construct coherent narratives, the sequence \"I saw the light, then heard the sound\" can easily become \"the light made the sound\", even when no direct physical connection exists. This tendency is strongest when neither the sound nor the light can be independently identified. Hearing lips and seeing voices: The science of the McGurk Effect Channel: Language of Mind Open on YouTube How investigators test whether the sound really matched the light Experienced investigators generally avoid assuming that simultaneous observations had the same source. Instead, they examine whether the physical evidence supports the witness's interpretation. Typical questions include: Was the sound consistent with the light's apparent distance? A nearby loud hum is difficult to reconcile with an object estimated to be many kilometres away. Did the timing make physical sense? If the object appeared distant, investigators compare the expected travel time of sound with the witness's account. Could another sound source explain the report? Aircraft, helicopters, drones , road traffic, electrical infrastructure, wildlife and industrial activity are all considered. Do independent witnesses agree? If observers at different locations describe different sounds while seeing the same light, this suggests separate sound sources. Is there instrumental evidence? Audio recordings, radar data, aircraft tracking and weather information can help determine whether the reported sound plausibly originated from the observed object. Modern recommendations for studying unidentified aerial phenomena emphasise collecting synchronised measurements from cameras, microphones and other sensors precisely because human perception alone cannot reliably establish that a particular sound belonged to a particular light. [ScienceDirect] sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect Sound Localization Sound Localization an overview ScienceDirect Topics... What this means for interpreting UFO sound reports The existence of a reported hum, buzz or roar does not automatically strengthen the case that an unidentified light represented an unusual craft. Instead, it introduces another observation that must be independently evaluated. The available evidence from perception research suggests that witnesses can honestly and accurately report both seeing a strange light and hearing an unusual sound while still being mistaken about the connection between them. Darkness, poor sound localisation, echoes and the brain's natural tendency to integrate simultaneous sensory information all make such false linking entirely plausible. Recognising this distinction helps explain why sound descriptions in UFO reports are so varied and why investigators look for independent confirmation before concluding that a reported noise truly originated from the observed object. [nature.com] nature.com March 7, 2026... Published: March 7, 2026 The Brain with David Eagleman 1. What Is Reality (2016) Channel: George Kalarritis, Clinical Psychologist Open on YouTube Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Did That Strange Sound Really Come From the UFO?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Demon Haunted World By Carl Sagan, Ann Druyan Rating: 4.5/5 from 43 Google Books ratings NEW YORK TIMES BESTSELLER • From the renowned astronomer and author of Cosmos comes a “powerful [and] stirring defense of informed ration... See on Amazon Book Thinking, Fast and Slow By Daniel Kahneman No Marketing Blurb See on Amazon Book Spatial Hearing By Jens Blauert The field of spatial hearing has exploded in the decade or so since Jens Blauert's classic work on acoustics was first published in Engli... See on Amazon Book The"},{"title":"Audio Limits","url":"/audio-limits/","description":"Even clear audio may not reveal a source's distance, direction or connection to a visible object without synchronized supporting data.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Myths","level":3,"text":"Audio Limits Why Clear UFO Audio Can Still Mislead Myths Audio Limits Audio Limits Why Clear UFO Audio Can Still Mislead Even clear audio may not reveal a source's distance, direction or connection to a visible object without synchronized supporting data. Even clear audio may not reveal a source's distance, direction or connection to a visible object without synchronized supporting data. dataset evidence What recordings capture and what they omit Distance, direction and source ambiguity How synchronization changes evidential value ufo noises myths audio limits Introduction A clear audio recording of an alleged UFO event is often more valuable than a witness's memory because it preserves the sound for repeated analysis. Even so, the recording rarely identifies what produced the sound on its own. A microphone captures changes in air pressure at one location, not a complete three dimensional picture of the event. Without accurate timing, location data, multiple recording points or synchronised visual evidence, investigators usually cannot determine whether the sound came from the visible object, from something outside the camera's view, or from an entirely unrelated source. Modern UAP research therefore treats audio as one piece of a broader evidence set rather than as a standalone identifier. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMCJuly 10, 2024... Published: July 10, 2024 What a recording captures – and what it leaves out An audio recording faithfully stores the signal that reached a microphone, but it does not automatically preserve the context needed to interpret that signal. A single recording typically documents: The timing of the sound reaching the microphone. Its frequency content and loudness at the recording position. Changes in pitch, rhythm or duration that may later be analysed. However, it usually does not reveal: The exact location of the source. The true loudness at the source. Whether the sound originated from the visible object or another nearby source. Whether reflections from buildings, terrain or clouds altered the sound before it reached the microphone. This distinction is important because the microphone records only what arrives at one point in space after the sound has travelled through the environment. The recording is therefore evidence of an acoustic event, not automatically evidence of the object's identity. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMCJuly 10, 2024... Published: July 10, 2024 24:46 Breakdown of the Pentagon UFO videos with Mick West Channel: Cool Worlds · Views: 365.2K · Uploaded: April 2020 · Length: 24 minutes Open on YouTube Distance, direction and source ambiguity People often assume that an unusual sound in a recording must have come from the object visible in the video. In reality, determining a sound's origin from a single recording is surprisingly difficult. Human listeners estimate direction using tiny timing and loudness differences between both ears. A single microphone lacks those binaural cues, making localisation much less reliable than ordinary hearing. Even people using both ears regularly make front versus back mistakes or misjudge distance when echoes, background noise or unfamiliar sounds are involved. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov PubMed Central (PMC)An Overview of the Major Phenomena of the Localization of Sound Sources by Normal Hearing, Hearing Impaired, and Aide... Distance is even more uncertain. A quiet nearby source and a loud distant source may produce nearly identical recordings because the microphone cannot know how powerful the original sound was. Wind, humidity, terrain and atmospheric conditions can further alter how sound travels before it is recorded. [PubMed] pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Auditory distance perception in humans: a review of cues, development, neuronal bases, and effects of sensory loss PubMed... As a result, an apparently dramatic hum, buzz or roar may have originated from: an aircraft outside the camera's field of view; industrial equipment; road traffic reflected by surrounding terrain; a drone; an unrelated environmental source. The recording alone often cannot distinguish between these possibilities. Why matching sound to a visible object is difficult Even when a video appears to show an unidentified object while unusual audio is heard, coincidence remains a genuine possibility. A camera's field of view is narrow compared with the surrounding environment. A microphone, by contrast, records sound arriving from many directions simultaneously. An aircraft behind the camera, a helicopter several kilometres away or machinery outside the frame may all contribute to the recorded soundtrack. Another complication is timing. Light reaches the camera almost instantly over ordinary viewing distances, whereas sound travels through air at roughly 343 metres per second under typical conditions. If an object is distant, the sound associated with it should arrive noticeably later than the image. Without careful synchronisation and accurate distance estimates, investigators cannot simply assume that the recorded sound belongs to the object shown on screen. [PubMed Central (PMC)] pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov Pub Med Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PubMed Central (PMC)Auditory localization: a comprehensive practical review PMCJuly 10, 2024... Published: July 10, 2024 How synchronisation changes evidential value The value of an audio recording increases dramatically when it is synchronised with independent measurements. Modern proposals for scientific UAP observation therefore combine microphones with multiple other sensors rather than relying on audio alone. These systems are designed so that every measurement shares accurate timing, allowing investigators to compare whether different instruments detected the same event simultaneously. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. A much stronger case can be built when investigators have: precisely timestamped audio; calibrated video from more than one viewpoint; known microphone locations; environmental measurements such as wind and weather; radar or radio frequency observations; aircraft tracking information. When these independent records agree, competing explanations can be tested rather than assumed. Conversely, if the sound fails to match the timing or location implied by other sensors, it may indicate that the audio originated elsewhere rather than from the observed object. [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. 21:25 Gimbal UFO A New Analysis Channel: Mick West · Views: 204.1K · Uploaded: March 2022 · Length: 21 minutes Open on YouTube What investigators can still learn from audio Although audio rarely identifies an unknown object by itself, it remains scientifically useful. Spectral analysis can reveal whether a recording resembles known sources such as propellers, jet engines or electrical equipment. Investigators may also examine background characteristics, repeated frequency bands and transient sounds to determine whether the recording is internally consistent or has likely been edited. However, these analyses usually answer narrower questions than many people expect. They may show that a recording is authentic, that it contains identifiable environmental sounds or that it resembles a particular class of machinery. They generally cannot demonstrate that an unidentified aerial object was extraordinary simply because the sound is unusual. [arXiv] arxiv.org arXiv Multi Band Acoustic Monitoring of Aerial Signatures arXiv Multi Band Acoustic Monitoring of Aerial Signatures Listening for UFOs The Galileo Project's Search for UAP with Andy Mead Merged EP0115 Channel: Merged Podcast Open on YouTube The key limitation A recording preserves sound more accurately than human memory, but preservation is not the same as identification. Without reliable information about where the microphone was, where the sound originated, how the environment affected transmission and whether other sensors observed the same event, even a remarkably clear recording may prove very little about the source itself. For that reason, the strongest investigations treat audio as corroborating evidence rather than decisive proof. A mysterious recording may document that an unusual sound occurred, yet still leave unanswered the central question: what actually produced it? [arXiv] arxiv.org Open source on arxiv.org. Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Why Clear UFO Audio Can Still Mislead. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book The Demon Haunted World By Carl Sagan, Ann Druyan Rating: 4.5/5 from 43 Google Books"},{"title":"Unexplained","url":"/unexplained/","description":"A sound can remain unidentified because evidence is missing without providing proof of exotic technology or an extraordinary craft.","breadcrumb":"UFO Noises / Myths","level":3,"text":"Unexplained Does Unexplained Mean an Extraordinary UFO Sound? Myths Unexplained Unexplained Does Unexplained Mean an Extraordinary UFO Sound? A sound can remain unidentified because evidence is missing without providing proof of exotic technology or an extraordinary craft. A sound can remain unidentified because evidence is missing without providing proof of exotic technology or an extraordinary craft. mechanism Identified, unidentified and extraordinary claims Why missing information is not positive evidence What would justify a stronger conclusion ufo noises myths unexplained Introduction An unexplained sound linked to a reported UFO is not, by itself, evidence of an extraordinary craft. In investigations of unidentified aerial phenomena (UAP), the word unidentified has a precise meaning: the available information is insufficient to determine the source. That is fundamentally different from demonstrating that the source lies beyond known technology or natural processes. Modern UAP investigators, including government and scientific programmes, repeatedly emphasise that many reports remain unresolved because the evidence is incomplete, not because the evidence points towards an exotic explanation. [aaro.mil] aaro.mil AARO FAQ... Understanding this distinction is essential when evaluating reports of unusual humming, buzzing, roaring or other sounds. A missing explanation is an evidence gap, not positive evidence for an extraordinary claim. Identified, unidentified and extraordinary claims Confusion often arises because these three categories are treated as though they mean the same thing when they do not. Identified: Investigators can confidently attribute the sound to a known source, such as an aircraft, drone, atmospheric phenomenon, industrial equipment or another ordinary cause. Unidentified: The available evidence is too limited or contradictory to determine what produced the sound. Extraordinary: Reliable evidence demonstrates behaviour that cannot reasonably be explained by known natural phenomena, human technology or measurement error. Only the third category would justify claims that something genuinely exceptional has occurred. Merely remaining in the second category does not move a case closer to the third. This distinction is reflected in current UAP investigations. The U.S. All domain Anomaly Resolution Office (AARO) explains that many cases remain unidentified because available sensors or observations did not collect enough information for a positive attribution. That lack of information is treated as an investigative limitation rather than evidence for an extraordinary explanation. [AARO] aaro.mil AARO FAQ... 1:02:46 UAP Independent Study Event Post Meeting Media Teleconference (May 31, 2023) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 30.3K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 1 hour 2 minutes Open on YouTube Why missing information is not positive evidence One of the most common reasoning errors in UFO discussions is treating an absence of explanation as evidence for a preferred explanation. If a witness hears an unusual low frequency hum at night but there is: no recording, no confirmed location, no reliable estimate of distance, no independent witnesses, no visual documentation, and no environmental measurements, then investigators have little objective information to analyse. The report may honestly remain unexplained, but the unexplained status reflects missing evidence rather than unusual evidence. This is an example of the logical distinction between: \"We do not know what caused it.\" \"Therefore it was an extraordinary craft.\" The second statement does not follow from the first. In logic, the absence of a known explanation is not itself proof that an extraordinary explanation is correct. NASA's UAP guidance similarly stresses that there is currently no credible evidence that UAP are extraterrestrial, while also acknowledging that some reports cannot yet be fully resolved because the available data are inadequate. [NASA Science] science.nasa.gov Science UAP FAQs NASA ScienceUAP FAQs NASA Science... Why sound alone often leaves cases unresolved Sound is one of the weakest forms of evidence for identifying an unknown aerial object. Unlike calibrated radar or precisely timed video, human hearing provides limited information about: exact distance, true direction, altitude, object size, speed, whether multiple sounds are coming from different sources. Low frequency sounds are especially difficult because they travel long distances, reflect from terrain and buildings, and may appear to originate somewhere other than their true source. Night time conditions further reduce confidence. Without visual reference points, listeners may unintentionally combine unrelated events into a single narrative—for example, associating a distant mechanical hum with an unrelated light in the sky. None of these limitations imply that witnesses are dishonest. Rather, they illustrate why sincere observations may still be insufficient to support firm conclusions. 4:00:26 Public Meeting on Unidentified Anomalous Phenomena (Official NASA Broadcast) Channel: NASA Video · Views: 358.7K · Uploaded: May 2023 · Length: 4 hours Open on YouTube An unresolved report is not evidence of hidden technology Public discussion sometimes assumes that every unresolved UFO report represents evidence withheld from science. Investigative practice takes a different view. Current UAP investigations typically maintain unresolved cases in active files until additional information becomes available. New photographs, radar tracks, weather records or other sensor data may later allow a previously unidentified report to be attributed to an ordinary source. AARO has stated that many archived reports lack sufficient scientific data for analysis and may be revisited if better evidence emerges. At the same time, it reports having resolved hundreds of cases as balloons , birds, drones, satellites and conventional aircraft. [U.S. Department of War] defense.gov Open source on defense.gov. (https://www.defense.gov/News/News Stories/Article/Article/3965403/dod examining unidentified anomalous phenomena/) This demonstrates an important principle: some reports remain unidentified not because they display extraordinary characteristics, but because investigators lack enough reliable information to identify them confidently. Are we Being Visited by Aliens? Neil deGrasse Tyson on UFO's Channel: Science Time Open on YouTube What would justify a stronger conclusion? An unexplained sound would become significantly more persuasive only if it were supported by multiple independent forms of evidence pointing towards the same event. Examples include: synchronised audio recorded on calibrated equipment; high quality video from several viewpoints; radar observations consistent with the recorded sound; precise timing allowing comparison with aircraft and weather data; independent witnesses reporting matching details without influencing one another; elimination of known environmental and technological sources through systematic investigation. Even then, investigators would first test conventional explanations before concluding that the phenomenon represented something genuinely anomalous. Modern scientific proposals for studying UAP therefore emphasise multi sensor observation rather than relying on witness testimony alone. The goal is to replace ambiguity with measurable data, allowing competing explanations to be tested instead of assumed. [arXiv] arxiv.org Initial Results From the First Field Expedition of UAPx to Study Unidentified Anomalous PhenomenaDecember 1, 2023... Published: December 1, 2023 The practical lesson for evaluating UFO sounds When a reported UFO sound cannot be explained, the most defensible conclusion is often simply that the available evidence is insufficient. That outcome may feel unsatisfying, but it is consistent with scientific reasoning. Investigators distinguish between uncertainty and evidence for extraordinary claims because the two are fundamentally different. An unexplained sound marks the limit of what the evidence currently supports; it does not, by itself, establish the existence of exotic propulsion, advanced secret technology or extraterrestrial craft. As additional observations become available, some unidentified cases may eventually receive ordinary explanations, while others may remain unresolved without ever becoming evidence for something extraordinary. [aaro.mil] aaro.mil AARO FAQ... Amazon book picks Further Reading Books and field guides related to Does Unexplained Mean an Extraordinary UFO Sound?. Use these as the next step if you want deeper reading beyond the article. Book Escaping the Rabbit Hole By Mick West Revised and updated for the first time in 2023—Now includes strategies for debunking conspiracies regarding the coronavirus pandemic, ele... See on Amazon Book The Demon Haunted World By Carl Sagan, Ann Druyan Rating: 4.5/5 from 43 Google Books ratings NEW"}]};
